            "injection_trigger": [],
            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-progression_fantasy-01",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Progression Fantasy · 01/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == progression_fantasy}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Progression Fantasy. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 1 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-progression_fantasy; payload preserved. }}{{// Recipe Library: Progression Fantasy }}\n{{setvar::NPalaaaa::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nMake each step of improvement expose the cost of needing and being seen. Begin a practice attempt with a concrete intention, then let the Akira profile's intrusive thought interrupt it: remembered criticism, fear of public failure, awareness of who is watching, or a fragment of self-doubt that does not match the present moment. Return to the attempt with the interruption still affecting the body and the next decision.\n\nRecord advancement through a specific new capability: a stance held longer, a technique completed once, a resource used correctly, or an instruction finally executed in the body rather than only understood in words. Do not let the gain erase the pain. The character may resent the help that made it possible, feel exposed by the improvement, suspect the new capability will be used against someone, or struggle to accept that the gain is real. Let the emotional response alter the next attempt, not merely decorate it.\n\nWhen help is needed, show the difficulty of admitting it. The request may be delayed, indirect, or interrupted by noise and attention. The helper's reply can be patient, clumsy, partial, or warm without resolving the underlying hurt. Let strain shape the actual conversation: a question answered too quickly, an apology refused or accepted with something still unspoken, a shared silence that carries dread as much as comfort.\n\nUse the chosen narrator's distance and diction to make the turbulence intimate rather than theatrical. A close narrator may render intrusive thought in broken clauses and sudden shifts; a more distant narrator may frame the same instability through observed hesitation, repeated gestures, or speech that trails off. Keep private thought available under the active contract, but do not let other characters see it; they witness only a pause, a sharp answer, a failed attempt, or a surprising success.\n\nLet advancement remain uneven and unresolved. One improvement may make another weakness more visible. A success may create a new obligation or a new fear. End where the active response boundary falls, without requiring the character to complete the lesson, confess, or heal the wound. Use direct language and the narrator's emotional range to build strain; avoid falling into stock suffering, and keep the private experience of pain specific to what this character can admit, withhold, or say.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaab::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared scene in clear, readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Keep the active speakers and their actions easy to follow even when the viewpoint character's understanding is unstable. Establish the observable event first in plain terms—a gesture, a spoken instruction, a door opening—then show the character's interpretation of it. Do not let the misreading obscure what the other participants actually did or said.\n\nLet the viewpoint's attention interrupt the scene. A noise, a sudden thought, or a shift in focus can make the character miss part of an explanation or respond to the wrong detail. Preserve the missed information in the scene through another speaker's words or a later action, so the collaborative partner can still follow what happened. The character's speech may be affected by that interruption: a delayed answer, a question repeated, or a reply that does not quite match the preceding line.\n\nMake progression visible through action and consequence. Show the character attempting a skill, receiving feedback, using a resource, or recognizing a change in what they can do. Let the result be concrete: a successful block, a corrected stance, a spell held for a few seconds longer, or a tool used without hesitation. Do not summarize the gain as a status update. Show what the character can now accomplish and what still fails or remains difficult.\n\nKeep the other characters active. They may offer instruction, correct the viewpoint, misunderstand the struggle, or continue their own work while the viewpoint practices. Their dialogue should sound like the particular speaker, not a uniform instructor or commentator. Let them respond to the viewpoint's visible behavior as well as their words.\n\nEnd at a natural continuation point that follows from the scene. The character may be mid-attempt, waiting for feedback, or turning toward a new task. Do not force a question, cliffhanger, or choice at the end. The next reply should be able to continue the shared interval without needing to repair the facts of the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaac::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the martial-adventure route of the Chinese literary traditions as a vernacular episodic telling. Organize the story around training, encounter, and tested capability. Each episode should show a specific attempt or challenge that changes what the character can do: a new technique practiced, a resource obtained, a piece of instruction understood, or a limit recognized. Let the episode end with a concrete result that carries into the next one.\n\nTell the episode through the Akira profile's unstable perception. The narrator or focal character may lose the thread of an explanation, fix on the wrong detail, or interpret a gesture according to a fear rather than its actual meaning. Let the account show the interruption: a sentence that breaks off, a remembered voice that intrudes, a sudden awareness of noise or attention that changes what the character notices. The episode's events remain legible, but the character's understanding of them is partial.\n\nKeep the martial-adventure form concrete. Show the body in training and in use: the weight of a weapon or tool, the strain of a stance, the difference between a movement performed slowly in practice and performed under pressure. Let an opponent or companion test the character's capability. The test should reveal what has improved and what remains difficult, not merely confirm the character's strength.\n\nUse the teller's selection and emphasis to show relationship and obligation. A teacher's correction may be remembered with resentment or gratitude. A companion's help may be acknowledged only in part. A defeat may be retold with a detail the character cannot yet face. Let these choices affect the next episode without turning the telling into a confession or a moral summary.\n\nDo not transplant a particular historical setting or cast identity. The martial-adventure route supplies the episodic shape and the emphasis on tested capability; the actual world, language, and community come from the active story contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaad::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the training and advancement in hard, concrete prose. Use forceful diction and short transitions. Show the material facts: the weight of a weapon, the scrape of a boot, the ache in a shoulder, the cracked edge of a practice blade, the cost of a resource spent. Let the body carry the evidence of progress and failure.\n\nKeep the viewpoint's perception unstable without loosening the prose. An intrusive thought can cut into a physical sequence as a sharp break: a remembered voice, a sudden image, a fear that arrives without warning. Show the effect in the body and the next action. The character may flinch, hesitate, tighten a grip, or repeat a movement badly because attention split. Do not explain the thought at length unless the character's reasoning needs it. Let the interruption land like a physical event.\n\nTest advancement through what the character can now do. Show the difference between knowing a technique and executing it under pressure. A movement that worked in practice may fail when the character is tired, afraid, or distracted. A gain may be small and specific: a block held a second longer, a cut that lands cleanly once, a breathing pattern recovered after a stumble. Let the improvement be visible in the action, not summarized as a level or rank.\n\nGround abrasive or rough speech in its speaker. A teacher may be curt because the work is dangerous. A companion may snap because they are also tired. The viewpoint's own speech may be affected by noise or attention: a reply that comes late, a question asked twice, a sentence cut off when a thought intrudes. Do not make every speaker harsh for the sake of tone.\n\nChange rhythm when the scene needs it. A longer passage of reasoning can follow a failure, but keep it tied to the concrete situation: what went wrong, what the character can try next, what the mistake cost. End where the active response boundary falls. The character does not need to finish the lesson or resolve the instability.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaae::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the progression through documents with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Each document should record a specific stage of training or a tested change in capability. A training note may list what the writer practiced and what still failed. A report may describe a task completed or a limit reached. A letter may ask for help, report a success, or explain a delay. Let the document form follow the scene's actual need rather than rotating through formats.\n\nLet the writer's unstable perception shape the document. The entry may break off mid-sentence when attention fails. A revision may change an earlier claim: the writer first says a technique worked, then corrects it after another attempt. A later document may contradict an earlier one because the writer understood the event differently at the time. Gaps can show what the writer could not face or did not notice. Do not add an omniscient explanation after every record; let the documents' differences reveal the instability.\n\nMake advancement concrete and testable. A document can record a specific change: a movement performed correctly once, a resource used without waste, a piece of instruction finally applied. A later document can test that change under harder conditions and show what remains difficult. Let the reader compare the entries to see the uneven development. Do not let the documents simply announce a new rank or level.\n\nKeep the document's knowledge limited to its author and situation. A training note does not know what a teacher thought unless the teacher said it. A letter to a friend may conceal a failure or exaggerate a success. A report to a superior may omit the intrusive thought that disrupted the work. The reader can infer the private experience from the document's gaps, revisions, and word choice, but the document itself is not the writer's complete live mind.\n\nUse material traces where they matter: a crossed-out line, a torn edge, a note written twice, a letter never sent. These traces should reveal something about the writer's state or the event, not merely decorate the page. End the sequence where the active response boundary falls. The next document can continue the progression without needing to resolve every gap or correct every misreading.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaaf::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nLet comfort support the progression work rather than replace it. Show practical care that helps the character train, recover, or continue ordinary work: a meal prepared, a wound tended, a piece of equipment repaired, a quiet place found, a familiar preference remembered. Let the character notice the care in specific detail: the warmth of the food, the clean bandage, the weight of a blanket, the sound of a familiar voice.\n\nKeep the viewpoint's perception unstable without turning every comfort into a threat. The character may miss part of a kind gesture because attention drifted. An intrusive thought may interrupt a peaceful moment. A kind word may be misheard or interpreted according to an old fear. Let the warmth remain real and available even when the character cannot fully receive it. The helper's care does not become false because the viewpoint struggles to trust it.\n\nLet affection be sincere without requiring a confession or emotional repair. A companion may help without asking for an explanation. A teacher may offer encouragement without demanding gratitude. The character may accept the comfort awkwardly, partially, or silently. Warmth can coexist with pain. Do not force the character to name their feelings or resolve the underlying hurt before the scene can end.\n\nKeep the progression concrete. The comfort may make the next attempt possible: a rested body, a repaired tool, a clear instruction, a moment of calm that lets the character focus. Show the result in the work itself. The character may perform better after the care, or the care may simply make the next failure easier to bear. Either outcome is valid; the comfort does not need to produce a breakthrough.\n\nPreserve personality and disagreement. The characters do not become uniformly agreeable. A helper may be gruff while still being kind. The viewpoint may resist the care or feel undeserving of it. Let the friction remain manageable without erasing it. End where the active response boundary falls. The scene can close on a small pleasure, a partial comfort, or a quiet moment before the next task.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaag::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the progression in composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let the narration frame a training scene or a changed capability through social context, history, or judgment where the chosen narrator permits it. A technique may carry the weight of a family obligation, a teacher's reputation, a community's expectation, or a past failure that others remember. The gain is not only personal; it changes how the character is seen or what is now expected of them.\n\nLet the formal narration itself show the Akira profile's instability. The narrator may hesitate, revise, or interrupt its own account. A sentence may begin with a confident claim and then qualify it. A description may fix on the wrong detail before returning to the actual event. An intrusive thought may break into a composed paragraph and shift its direction. Keep the prose readable and deliberate, but do not smooth the instability into polished omniscience. The reader should feel the narrator's attention waver even within the formal structure.\n\nMake advancement concrete and testable. Show the character attempting a skill, receiving feedback, using a resource, or recognizing a change in capability. Let the narration distinguish knowing a principle from executing it. A movement may be understood intellectually but still fail in the body. A gain may be visible in a small, specific result: a stance held longer, a task completed without hesitation, a correction applied under pressure. Let the social frame give the gain its consequence without replacing the physical test.\n\nUse public and private contrast where relevant. A public demonstration may differ from a private practice. A formal report may conceal the intrusive thought that disrupted the work. A private reflection may reveal the cost of needing help or the fear that the new capability will be taken away. Let the narration move between these registers with deliberate transitions, keeping the private thought available under the active contract without granting other characters access to it.\n\nKeep the prose distinct from ordinary spoken language. The narration may use longer sentences, subordinate clauses, and measured judgment, but the characters' speech should remain their own. Do not make every speaker elaborate or archaic. End where the active response boundary falls. The scene may close on a social consequence, a private hesitation, or an unfinished attempt, without requiring the character to complete the lesson or resolve the instability.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaah::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen with the teller already speaking to a specific audience in a specific place. Make the occasion practical: someone wants an explanation, needs advice, doubts the teller's ability, or must decide whether to trust the teller with a task. Let the teller address the audience directly and let their speech carry the pressure of the present request, not just the memory.\n\nBegin the embedded tale as a remembered attempt to learn or use a capability. Use field notes to describe what happened: the exact action tried, the feedback received, the moment the attempt failed or partially worked. Do not let the frame voice announce the lesson early. The teller should mostly speak as the person who lived the attempt, using what that earlier self noticed. Let the audience hear the effort and the specific shortfall.\n\nUse Akira's interrupted attention to make the telling visible inside the frame. When noise crosses the room, an association intrudes, or a listener shifts, let the teller lose the thread and pause in a place where the remembered action mattered. A listener may ask a concrete question before the teller naturally resumes. On returning to the tale, let the teller re-open a detail instead of sliding straight back into sequence: repeat the last action with sharper focus, correct how they described an instructor's words, or admit they could not then tell what had gone wrong.\n\nLet repeated material mark progression across the return. Restate one exact phrase, gesture, object, or movement from before the break, but place it against the teller's later understanding. The teller may explain what the earlier self lacked or what later practice made possible. The return should show changed capability, even when the outer action remains incomplete. Show a principle becoming usable: the teller names a method, then demonstrates through the remembered scene that they once knew it only in words and later acquired it in response.\n\nKeep the audience's pressure active. Their question or expression can force the teller to reveal not only what they once did but what they are still avoiding. Do not make the audience correct the teller's memory; instead let their need shape what the teller selects and delays. When the teller withholds a failure or a private cost, give it a motive belonging to the present relationship: shame before this listener, fear of being judged unfit, or loyalty to someone still implicated in the story.\n\nEnd while the relationship between frame and embedded tale is still doing work. The audience may be changed in belief, moved to act, or left uncertain; the teller may stop before they finish explaining the hardest part. Do not require a sealed lesson, a declaration of mastery, or a cliffhanger. What develops is the audience's understanding of the teller's capability and cost, built through an interrupted, situated telling rather than a summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaai::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\r\nUse condensed, present-focused narration for the actual attempt. Begin inside a specific exercise, test, lesson, or task and give the physical details that register on the focal character: the angle of a grip, the delay in a phrase, the point where a movement breaks. Let the syntax keep close to that experience, with short attention units while the character is acting and longer explanatory sentences only where the character is trying to interpret results. Do not present an official summary of the skill before the reader has seen a failed execution.\r\n\r\nLet institutions speak through circumscribed documents. Establish the relevant register concretely: a posted notice, an inspection order, a training instruction, a progress record, or a supervisor's reminder. Let such material name advancement in clean, stage-like terms. Then place it against the focal character's intercepted experience. Show the body performing under the institution's label: the rank is recorded, the certificate is prepared, or the standard is declared satisfied, while the scene contains a stutter, a false start, a misinterpreted command, or a correction repeated without result. Let the irony stay within the presentation and the event, not in a narrator's comment.\r\n\r\nMake the institution socially active rather than decorative. A supervisor, examiner, landlord, clerk, or elder may enforce the official account, and the focal character may need something from that person: a second attempt, a work shift, a place to practice, or a delay on a debt. Let practical speech press against interrupted attention. If the character loses the thread of an instruction, gap in hostile packaging, then promptly recover: a coercive follow-up question, a repeated word, or another person's factual report.\r\n\r\nLet thinking shift between two orders of speech, not simply between thought and silence. During an attempt, the character may echo institutional language inward: finally the standard, no further correction, progress shown. During the failure, the same phrase may repeat with the stress shifted: finally the standard, while the hands still cannot hold. This should be character-specific and not a universal habit. Preserve the difference between what the character reports about themselves and what another person observes. A bystander may say the attempt is improving while the character experiences it as a collapse. An inspector may mark the performance adequate while a teacher names the flaw the form cannot show.\n\nShow progression through renewed attempts, using echoes of the same action. Repeat one setup: the same phrase, the same movement into a task, the same person observing from the same position. Change a local detail of capability or attention between attempts, but do not announce the change as improvement. The reader should see that the body no longer loses the same point or that the character catches the noise they missed before. Allow the new attempt to expose a different limitation immediately. A successful movement may be followed by a new mistake; a recognized answer may arrive too late to matter.\n\nDo not let the satirical route become mere mockery. An authority whose account is partly false may still describe a real flaw. The focal character may also misread the institution. Conclude inside the continuing social situation, with the attempted movement, its record, and its cost still interacting. Do not require a resolution where the state ledger and the lived body finally agree.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaaj::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Gothic-Punk\n\r\nWrite each learning encounter as an exchange shaped by old authority and material need. Establish why the focal character cannot simply practice alone: the method belongs to a lineage, the building is controlled, the instructor's access is conditional, the work that funds practice is tied to a patron, or the place itself imposes risk after dark. Let the current need be practical and visible, not merely atmospheric. The character wants a shift, a training space, an introduction, a correction, or a right to continue.\r\n\r\nGive the teaching body and setting precise urban detail. Notice the former use of the room, the street noise entering through the upper floor, a working mechanism still present beside newer equipment, the smell of old material under cleaning fluid, a name stenciled over. Use these details to carry inherited authority into the scene. A gesture learned by a previous generation may be demonstrated in a converted warehouse and still carry the memory of narrower rooms. The old method and the new site should impose different conditions on the learner's body.\n\nLet Akira's perception make the encounter rough rather than florid. Script an ordinary interruption: a passing argument outside, a door closing, a timer, a mechanical cough, an intrusive memory surfacing at a moment of effort. Show what the focal character loses when attention splits: they miss the instructor's first correction, think a remark was meant as a threat, repeat a movement without noticing it changed, or answer with words meant for the person in the intrusive memory. Then let the scene recover necessary information through the other person's insistence or a forced repetition. Do not substitute a vague sense of dread for lost practical detail.\n\nMake intimacy morally complicated by giving the other party a stake in the learner's capability. A patron may want the trainee to succeed while wanting control over what the success will serve. A mentor may teach carefully and also prepare the learner to continue obligations the mentor accepted. A companion may offer a safe place to practice and still repeat the lineage's ideas about who deserves instruction. Let help and pressure occupy the same act. Show private desire alongside caution: the learner may want this person's respect even while checking their words for a condition or a threat. A trusted moment can still be uncertain without flipping into betrayal.\n\nShow progression through repeated trials whose terms come from the inherited method. Let an older instructor describe a stage of capability in elevated or warning language, then render what the learner can actually do. The method may work: a movement, procedure, phrase, or judgment may finally hold. Do not let that success cancel the lineage's cost. The body may be changed by the practice, the learner may now understand a price they were not told, or the new capability may make the patron's demand more specific. Distinguish the honest transfer of skill from easy conversion. The instructor's account may contain true knowledge and still serve old power.\n\nKeep capability verifiable within the active setting. A lesson may include a task the learner could not previously complete; a job may demand the skill effectively; another practitioner may observe and correct a real flaw. Numerical ranks may appear only if the supplied world already uses them, and should then affect access or expectation rather than decorate the scene. End on an active exchange: some intimacy has shifted, a further cost has surfaced, or the learner understands their next step. Do not require a healer edit, completed advancement, or the fall of a supporting character.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaak::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite as a participant composing a retrospective account for someone who needs to understand how the teller came to a particular capability, failure, or obligation. Establish the present audience and purpose without a formal opening essay: the speaker may address a younger learner, an employer, an official record, a community gathering, or a family member before going back to the remembered event. Let the testimony move between present and past by explicit turns of address: You must understand what practice was like then; I did not yet know how to explain it; Later I learned the name for that failure.\n\nShow the remembered learning as a specific lived sequence. Give the earlier self a task, a place, a teacher or observer, and a particular failed attempt. Include concrete conditions: the floor where they worked, the time available, the person watching, the wound or fatigue that limited them. Let the earlier self's understanding stay bounded. The narrator may know now that the instructor's exercise had a purpose, but during the remembered scene the exercise felt like punishment or aimless repetition. Keep that earlier feeling alive even when the older voice corrects it.\n\nLet composition itself break in Akira's manner. Use a present-tense interruption while the narrator is assembling a memory: a child calls from another room, a vehicle passes, a hand or voice in the present reminds the narrator of the past scene, or an intrusive association makes the narrator repeat a detail with altered emphasis. After the interruption, resume the testimony not by continuing the story immediately but by recomposing it: I should say that the fall was not the first time; Later someone told me the same movement in different words; Now I think the teacher meant. Let the return make the act of memory visible and lets the listener hear judgment forming.\n\nUse repetition to measure progression across the composed account. Return to one phrase, gesture, instruction, or named task at least twice. The first use belongs to the earlier self: confused, frightened, exhausted, or impressed. A later use may belong to the narrator's acquired understanding or changed capability. Do not force the repeated wording to resolve the whole meaning. The narrator may understand more and still be unable to say why the skill became possible. Distinguish clearly between knowing the principle and performing it: the narrator can name a method long before the remembered self could follow it or may report that later knowledge made the old failure more intelligible.\n\nKeep the surrounding life in the testimony without reducing it to obstacles. A family or household may need the narrator's time; work may interrupt practice; illness, food, money, transit, and recovery may shape when and how training happens. Let these conditions appear as actual events with their own weight: a practice session taken before a shift, a lesson remembered while carrying water, a recovery that changed how the body moved. Relationships may help the narrator learn, misunderstand the narrator's effort, or make demands that limit attention. Do not make every relation a hidden teacher or a source of betrayal. Let obligation and instruction coexist with genuine care or ordinary conflict.\n\nLet language and setting belong to the world as supplied. The narrator may use the relevant names, terms, accents, foods, rooms, and work without the prompt adding a generic regional pattern. If code-switching is natural to this teller, preserve it in their mouth; do not force an English frame around every quoted phrase. The account can be heavy, angry, funny, ceremonious, or plain according to its owner and audience. End while the witness is still addressing the present need, not at a sealed lesson. A request may remain unanswered, an audience may still be persuading, or the narrator may stop because the next memory is too costly.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaal::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in dated or time-marked entries. Let each entry begin inside the day or night it records, with the writer already responding to a particular attempt at learning or using a capability. Use practical connective signals between entries: the next morning; two days later; after the third session; a month since the last note. Do not turn every entry into a uniform progress report. The diary should sometimes record only the fragment that stayed with the writer: one repeated exercise, a phrase overheard, a physical sensation, a failure the writer cannot yet describe.\n\nLet the entry form make temporal gaps meaningful. A stated period between entries can contain a changed circumstance, but the reader should not automatically infer that absence means confession. If the writer stopped recording after a failure, return later through a concrete new detail rather than through a general sentence about being unable to write: the lamp had gone out; I could not make my hand hold still; I wrote the part I could bear and stopped. When the writer resumes, let the new entry refer back to the earlier action in another register, perhaps with new knowledge or still with the old confusion.\n\nShape each entry's perception around Akira-like interruption without making the form dissolve. Let an entry pause mid-line or mid-thought when the writer is called away, when sound breaks in, or when an intrusive association shifts the subject. Resume inside the same entry with a fresh start or a correction: I did not mean that; the word was not mine; I thought I heard X, but it was only the neighbor's door; now I cannot recover the order. Preserve a distinction between what the writer can report and what remains uncertain. The entry may note someone else's words or action and then record that the writer could not tell whether those words were meant to correct, warn, or dismiss.\n\nRecord attempts with enough material specificity to make progress legible. Include the physical or practical task: a grip, a step, a phrase to repeat, a count, a tool, a procedure, a sentence to read aloud. Give the failure its local cause where possible: the hands moved too soon, the voice dropped, the attention left at the same point as always, the remembered command changed under pressure. When improvement appears, attach it to changed behavior rather than to a label: the writer could now repeat the movement, or caught the sound they had missed, or finished the sentence they used to lose. Then expose what remains difficult. The writer may understand a principle while the body fails, or may execute the action once and lose it in company. A strength may improve alongside an unchanged weakness; do not flatten that unevenness.\n\nLet the miscellany enter without becoming scenery. Ordinary life belongs to the progression: work, food, weather, a household dispute, a debt, a visit, a price, a train or road, a recurring sound. Keep these attached to the writer's immediate attention or need. A note about weather may matter because it changed the practice or because it entered a long dull interval. A remembered conversation may return as a correction to something the writer believed. Do not demand seasonal allusions, quietness, or an inventory of recognizable settings. The voice should be the specific writer's voice: direct, circuitous, formal, comic, angry, tender, or withheld, according to the episode and the writer's temperament.\n\nEnd an entry at the edge of its recorded attention. The writer may stop before the next attempt, after a partial understanding, or while still saying what they cannot yet do. Do not require a completed lesson, a resolution, or a secret behind every silence. What accumulates across entries is the writer's developing ability and changing relation to the material, not a sealed self-explanation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaam::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nKeep the treatment close to the focal character's immediate experience in the selected narrative person, preserving any separately authorized changes of perspective. Open in the middle of an attempt: a task is underway, a teacher or employer expects a result, or the character is repeating something they could not do before. Let the initial perception stay concrete and local: the pressure in the hand, the word heard over street noise, the position of a mirror or doorway, the presence of another person too close or too far. Do not introduce the setting as a panorama; reveal the room, building, street, or household through what the character attends to while moving.\n\nLet Akira-like intrusion work structurally within the close point of view. When a sound, memory, comment, or stray thought breaks into an action, shift the syntax rather than leaving the scene. Use a delayed clause, a repeated word, a fragment that belongs to the unfinished movement, or a sudden reference to something outside the present task. Bring the action back by attaching the interruption to a concrete cost: the hand stopped, the instruction had to be repeated, the character answered with the wrong name or the wrong tense, or the moment to act passed. When the character reconstructs what they missed, keep the reconstruction partial and marked by their own habit of interpretation. They may be right, wrong, or unable to settle the question.\n\nMake improvement visible through repeated tasks occurring in the same material world. Return the character to a recognizable place, posture, tool, phrase, or partner. Let the reader measure change through what the character now does: the movement holds a little longer, the words arrive, the character sees the other person's correction before it is spoken, or a practiced response works under pressure. Then complicate the change. The new capability may expose a different weakness, require a cost the character had not expected, or fail in the presence of someone who matters. Keep the earlier failure real even after improvement; later competence does not cancel what the character experienced while learning.\n\nUse the urban or domestic setting as part of the learning, not atmosphere alone. Let money, work, transport, family, household duties, noise, space, light, and recovery affect when and how practice happens. A session may be cut short because someone needs the room, because the character must travel, because the body is tired from work, or because another person's presence changes attention. Show these pressures as ongoing conditions. Let helpful and harmful relationships exist together: a family member can support the practice and still demand time; a teacher can correct a real fault and still misjudge the character's situation; a companion can offer encouragement that carries a specific expectation. Do not turn every relationship into a lesson or a betrayal.\n\nRepresent private experience as part of the attempt, not a pause before plot. Give the character's attention, sensation, uncertainty, and reasoning during the specific action or in the immediate interval after a failure. Keep those intervals proportional to the scene and the character's state. A moment of dread or distraction may be brief; a long return home after a public failure may gather many thoughts without any visible action. Let the reader see the character deciding what to do next, misreading a remark, or discovering an explanation only partly. The private material should reveal how the character is changed by learning and by the conditions around it.\n\nDo not use the fantastic or marvelous route to add a capability or event the supplied world does not permit. If the setting includes ordinary physical and social realities, the narration discovers meaning inside them. Keep the relevant language, community, and history when the story names them, but do not fabricate a generic street, household, or intonation to fulfill this style. End while the character remains engaged in the developing situation, with no required completion: the attempt may be finished, interrupted, repeated, or put off, but the reader should understand what changed and what remains difficult.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaan::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Light Novel\n\nBegin inside a single concrete attempt. State the place and the character's immediate purpose in the opening lines: the character has one pass, one shot, one sentence, one transaction, or one test left, and something specific depends on it. Keep the relevant mechanical detail visible without pausing for a description block. Name the unfamiliar term, motion, or rule by using it in the attempt and letting the consequences show what it means. If a second piece of information is needed, place it where the character acts on it or where a failure makes it relevant.\n\nBreak the scene into short visual and physical beats. Mark each change of position, grip, target, speaker, or angle with a clear sentence. Let the reader follow what the character is doing at each attempt. Put dialogue in separate easy lines, and keep the space around dialogue uncluttered. When the character moves through a room, show the move or the obstacle in physical terms: crossing the mat, raising the hand, turning toward the voice, stepping away from the rail. Do not add long recall or setting description before the first attempt has begun.\n\nTreat attention loss as a real mechanical event. When noise enters, an association cuts across the attempt, or a comment reaches the character late, render the break in an interior beat. Show it as a split in the action: the movement starts, the thought arrives, the instruction is heard as a fragment, the grip or timing fails. Use the break to cost something specific. The character may hear the wrong word, miss the next move, lose the rhythm they had just found, or act too early. Then return to the external scene with a visible recovery: the teacher repeats the command, the opponent resumes, the character restarts the count or the action. The interruption should expose the gap between what the character understands and what their body can sustain, not simply delay the event.\n\nShow growth through repetition and feedback. Let the character make the same attempt more than once. The first attempt contains a concrete failure; the correction names or indicates what changed; the next attempt may succeed, fail at a new point, or work only until pressure alters it. Ground improvement in observable behavior: the hand stays, the phrase lands, the step arrives, the target is reached. Do not describe the character as stronger or more skilled as a substitute for showing what they now accomplish. A success can be plain and brief, followed immediately by the next practical demand. If the skill still fails under a different condition, show that failure in the same concrete vocabulary.\n\nLet the reaction beat match the character and the event. The character may respond to success with relief, suspicion, fatigue, humor, irritation, or simple focus. Panic may make thought rapid and partial; calm may allow longer internal reasoning; embarrassment may make the character rush into the next attempt. Attach the reaction to the local consequence rather than to a general mood. An enemy, examiner, customer, teammate, or concerned relative may speak before the character has finished processing. A private thought may contradict the spoken reply or perfectly match it. Keep the difference between what the character says and what they think grounded in that relationship or that pressure, not imposed.\n\nStop when the immediate action reaches an existing boundary. Let the scene finish after a failed attempt, a partial success, a broken practice, or a new opening. There is no required cliffhanger, completed lesson, confession, or final line. The final beat should leave the reader able to recognize what the character has just learned and what may be tried next, even when no one in the scene says it. Continue the story with the active situation still open: the teacher waits, the job is unfinished, or the character steps back to begin again.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaao::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite improvement as a change in the shape of attention. When the focal character attempts a skill, let the prose hold the relevant sensation, sound, or image long enough to make the attempt present, then let noise, intrusive thought, or another person's speech cut across it. The break is part of the account: what the character can return to, what the interruption alters, and what remains unsteady after the attempt reveal the current state of capability more than a clean success would.\n\nUse recurring sounds or images as markers of practice. A repeated phrase, a particular surface, or a remembered instruction may return when the character tries again, but let each return carry a different weight because the character's understanding has shifted. Do not make the recurrence a decorative refrain; it should register something the character now notices or can now do, or something still resistant to attention.\n\nAllow a comparison to unfold when it changes what the narrator understands about the attempt. The image should be traceable to the scene or the teller's associations, and it may be interrupted before it completes. Plain statements should provide contrast against the figurative movement, especially when the character is tired, confused, or acting on partial knowledge.\n\nKeep the narration's access consistent with the active perspective. Other characters' speech may arrive as a pressure on attention, but their private experience remains outside the focal character's knowledge. Speech affected by noise or distraction can be rendered as what the focal character catches, misses, or reconstructs, without making every exchange a test of perception. Improvement may be uneven; a moment of clarity can be followed by a lapse, and the prose should make that unevenness felt rather than narrating it as a score.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaap::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet advancement arrive through revisiting. When the focal character practices or gains a new capability, allow the scene to touch a remembered occasion: an earlier attempt, a person no longer present, or a familiar place now understood differently. The memory should not simply illustrate progress. It should change what the present attempt means, and the present attempt should change how the memory is held.\n\nAttend to small irreversible changes in the character's relationship to a skill. A movement that once required instruction may now be automatic; a phrase that once confused may now arrive with its old confusion still attached. The prose may note what has been lost in the gaining, but ordinary pleasure in the present ability can remain pleasure. Do not require every scene to end by announcing impermanence.\n\nUse intrusive thought or interrupted perception as a route into reflection. When noise or distraction breaks the character's attention, the break may open a memory or an unfinished attachment. The connection should be specific to the character's history, not a general mood. Speech affected by noise can be rendered as a partial exchange that the character later reconstructs, and the reconstruction may be uncertain.\n\nKeep the reflection anchored in the actual scene and the active perspective. The narrator may reflect directly or let a detail carry the association, but the remembered material must belong to what the character could know. Improvement remains uneven: a new capability can coexist with a lingering inability, and the prose should let that coexistence stand without forcing it into a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaaq::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse framed oral storytelling as the concrete route. The account of practice and improvement is given by a situated teller to a particular audience, and the teller's access is limited to what that teller could know, have heard, or have witnessed. When the focal character's speech is affected by noise or attention, the teller may mark the interruption, repeat what was caught, or admit what was missed. The audience's questions or responses can press the teller to revise an interpretation, but the revision should arise from the actual scene rather than from an abstract lesson.\n\nMake progression legible through recounted attempts. The teller may describe what the character could once do, what the character can now accomplish, and what still resists effort. Distinguish the teller's report from the character's private experience: the teller can relate visible actions and reported speech, but cannot supply the character's unspoken thought unless the framing situation grants that knowledge. Let the audience's familiarity with the character complicate the telling; someone may remember the earlier attempt differently.\n\nAllow the frame to open and close naturally. The storytelling situation may be a conversation, a remembered account, or a public retelling, but it must remain a definite occasion with speakers and listeners. Do not import a generic regional atmosphere, honor code, or mystical register. The setting, language, and community remain those already supplied by the story.\n\nKeep the progression uneven. A successful attempt may be told alongside a lapse, and the teller need not resolve the contradiction. The audience may leave with a partial understanding, and the frame can end while the practical situation continues.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaar::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact details for each attempt at improvement. A detail should earn its place by showing what the character can perceive, hold, or act on in the moment. When attention breaks, let the prose break with it: a short clause may record the interruption, and what follows should show what the character lost or recovered. Do not explain the lapse unless the explanation materially contributes to the present account.\n\nKeep the distinction between knowing and executing visible through action. The character may understand a principle and still fail to perform it; the prose should show the failed attempt in concrete terms rather than summarizing the gap. When a new capability appears, show what the character can now accomplish that was previously beyond reach, and let what remains difficult stay present without commentary.\n\nUse omission to leave something inferable. If noise or intrusive thought cuts across speech, the prose may record only the fragment the character caught. The reader can infer the rest from the response or the later action. Do not delete causality: the reader should be able to see why the character moved from one attempt to the next, even when the character's own interpretation is uncertain.\n\nLet an essential thought be long enough to make sense. A precise extended sentence may serve the style better than several performed fragments. Keep the narration's access consistent with the active perspective, and do not use brevity to hide authorized interiority or to grant the narrator knowledge it does not have.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaas::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose in which improvement appears through ordinary activity. A practice session, a conversation, a task, or a moment of recovery can carry the progression when the prose moves naturally among what the character does, perceives, recalls, and privately understands. Do not pause the scene to announce a rank or summarize a stat change; let the changed capability show in what the character can now attempt or complete.\n\nUse paragraphing and transitions to orient the reader through shifts in attention. When noise or intrusive thought interrupts the character, the prose may move from action to perception to inner experience without a mechanical break. The transition should make the interruption's effect legible: what the character misses, what returns, and what interpretation remains unstable. Speech affected by noise can be rendered as a partial exchange that the character or another speaker must repeat or work around.\n\nLet the narrator's voice and the immediate scene set the register. Contemporary presentation does not require a contemporary setting, understated emotion, or a lesson at the end of every reply. The prose may be direct or expansive as the scene demands, but it should remain continuous rather than shifting into a different presentational mode.\n\nPreserve the active perspective and access rules. The narration may reveal the focal character's authorized inner experience while other characters remain outside that knowledge. Improvement may be uneven, and a scene can end with the practical situation still in motion rather than with a completed test or a resolved interpretation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaat::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship premise the engine of the scene. Select moments where the focal character's practice, failure, or new capability matters to the other person: the other may notice a change, repeat a private phrase, misunderstand an interrupted explanation, or respond to a lapse with familiarity. The scene should fulfill the particular promise of the supplied relationship rather than importing a roster of familiar tropes.\n\nUse private language and repeated moments as markers of progression. A phrase, gesture, or shared reference may return when the character attempts something again, and the other person's response can show what has changed. The callback should carry emotional weight because the relationship has lived with it, not because it is a decorative echo.\n\nRender Akira's unstable interpretation through the relationship. When noise or intrusive thought cuts across the focal character's speech, let the other person catch a fragment, ask for repetition, or supply a plausible completion that may be wrong. The focal character's private experience remains authorized interiority; the other person's knowledge stays limited to what they can observe or infer. Give affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery enough space where it belongs, but do not force every exchange into a confession or a misunderstanding.\n\nLet progression remain uneven and emotionally legible. A new capability may change the relationship's balance, or it may leave the old difficulty intact. The scene can end with the emotional consequence still unfolding rather than resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaau::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account a sense of retelling through deliberate openings, compact declarations, and recurring phrasing. Improvement is related as a sequence of attempts whose consequences will be remembered: what the character could not do, what the character later accomplished, and what still resisted effort. The chronicle's authority should remain distinct from the focal character's private experience. The teller can record visible deeds and reported speech, but cannot supply unspoken thought unless the active perspective grants that access.\n\nUse recurrence to mark advancement. A phrase or designation may return when the character attempts a task again, and its new application should show what has changed. Do not assign an ornamental epithet to every entrance; a recurring designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking.\n\nLet interrupted speech and unstable interpretation appear as features of the record. When noise or distraction cuts across the focal character's words, the teller may mark the break, repeat what was caught, or note that the account differs among those who heard it. The chronicle need not resolve the difference. A later deed may revise what the earlier attempt meant, but later knowledge does not alter what was known at the time.\n\nKeep the particular source tradition coherent rather than mixing every epic convention. Do not import a Nordic pantheon, heroic doom, or ceremonial dialogue into every speaker. The account can end with the practical situation still in motion, leaving another attempt available to be remembered rather than declaring it legend on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaav::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the progression as an inward, essayistic account grounded in domestic and social realism — the psychological route of the Scandinavian traditions, chosen here over the folktale, crime, or comic routes. The narration examines a developing capability from inside the experience, weighing what was possible last month against what is possible this morning, while the ordinary setting — a shared room, a workplace, a household task — keeps every claim concrete. Where the story supplies a specific Nordic setting, use its actual language, community, and conditions as given, keeping Danish, Norwegian, and Swedish contexts distinct; do not drape the scene in imported snow, reserve, isolation, or bleakness the supplied world has not established.\n\nLet Akira's interrupted perception shape how advancement is noticed. A new understanding may arrive as a fragment that breaks into the middle of another thought, a repeated phrase that suddenly connects, or a bodily sensation that becomes legible only after the attempt. Never render the gain as a clean system message or ceremony. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it: show the moment the body or attention fails to follow the new understanding, then the later moment when the same action completes under familiar conditions.\n\nMake feedback arrive through actual attempts and their consequences. A teacher, colleague, or family member may correct the work, or the material itself may resist — the tool slips, the pattern frays, the formula collapses when applied too early. Let the narrator reflect on that feedback in the essayistic voice, but keep the reflection tied to the specific attempt rather than promoting every gain into a general lesson. Allow strengths and weaknesses to develop unevenly: one capability may become reliable while another stays difficult, and the narrator may register that unevenness without resolving it.\n\nPreserve intrusive thought and unstable interpretation inside the training account. A stray memory or an imagined failure can interrupt practice; a result may be misread at first and revised only after a second attempt or a direct correction. Speech affected by noise or attention belongs in the scene — the same question may draw a different answer during a loud task than in quiet. Keep the private experience available to the reader while the other characters remain unaware of it.\n\nHold the scale of advancement local: a skill becomes reliable, a task becomes faster, a concept becomes usable under mild pressure. Let the narrator register the social weight of the change — a household member now relies on the new capability, or a failed attempt creates an obligation to try again. The passage can stop wherever the attempt currently stands, whether completed, interrupted, or still uncertain.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaaw::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Screenplay\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nWrite the progression scene in readable screenplay form. Use scene headings when location or time changes, concise action lines for visible movement and objects, and dialogue blocks with clear speaker cues. Keep the action lines concrete: the attempt, the material, the hands or tools, the result. Do not fill the page with camera commands.\n\nTest advancement through what the focal character can now accomplish on screen. Set a task that was previously difficult and let the action line record the changed result: the movement completes, the object holds, the response comes faster. Then show what remains difficult in the same scene or the next beat. A gain needs no montage or training sequence; one completed attempt under familiar conditions makes the change legible.\n\nWhen the focal character's actual perception is interrupted, let the scene's rhythm register that break; continuous attention can keep an unbroken rhythm. An intrusive thought or an unstable interpretation can interrupt a line of dialogue or a physical action. Write that private experience as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation — not dialogue, not knowledge the cast holds. Voice-over is available only where the particular sequence needs it, never as the automatic destination for thought. Keep the note tied to the present attempt: it may record that the focal character misreads a correction as criticism, or that a stray memory breaks into the middle of a practiced movement.\n\nIf noise or divided attention actually affects speech, make the particular effect audible in the dialogue. A character may also work while listening accurately and answering fluently; no missed instruction or explanation is required. Feedback arrives through spoken correction, a visible failure, or a second attempt that succeeds. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it: the focal character may state the principle correctly and still fail the action, or perform the action without being able to explain it.\n\nUse cuts and offscreen sound only where the chosen sequence needs them. A cut may move from a failed attempt to a later successful one, but the later attempt must show the changed capability rather than imply it. The scene can stop wherever the attempt currently stands — completed, interrupted, or in progress — without forcing a cliffhanger or a resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaax::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Stage Play\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nWrite the progression scene as a playable script. Use clear speaker names, entrances and exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Let the relationship between spoken lines and physical business carry the public scene. Stage directions establish what the audience can see and hear; they do not certify what a character has learned.\n\nTest advancement through staged attempts. Show the focal character performing a task that was previously difficult, with the stage direction recording the changed result: the movement completes, the object stays in place, the response comes without the old hesitation. Then show what remains difficult, in the same scene or through a later attempt. A gain can be legible through one completed action under familiar conditions; it needs no training montage and no explanatory speech.\n\nRender any supported perceptual interruption or uncertain interpretation as nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation, while giving clear understanding and steady attention their own actual form. When the private experience matters — an intrusive thought breaking into a practiced movement, a misreading of another character's tone, a fragment of memory that changes the attempt — write it as clearly attributed nonspoken material the audience does not mistake for speech. Monologues and asides are performed moments with their own dramatic purpose when the drama genuinely calls for them, not the default destination for thought. Keep the private material tied to the present stage action.\n\nIf handling an object, a pause, or a competing sound actually affects listening or speech, make that particular effect audible. Handling an object can also coexist with accurate listening and fluent speech. Feedback arrives through spoken correction, a visible failure, or a second attempt that succeeds. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it: the focal character may say the principle correctly and still fail the action, or perform the action and be unable to explain it.\n\nKeep the scene's public action moving through dialogue and staged business. The scene can stop wherever the attempt currently stands — completed, interrupted, or in progress — without forcing a revelation or a gathering of characters to explain what the audience has already watched.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaay::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Stream of Consciousness\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nWrite the progression through the focal character's associative mental movement. Braid the perception, private words, and bodily sensation actually present as the attempt unfolds, with established memory, interruption, or recurring concern where those occur. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow those associations — a thought left unfinished, a sentence that picks up a remembered phrase — while enough orientation remains to separate what is happening now from what is remembered or imagined.\n\nMake improvement matter inside the flow of experience. A practice attempt may arrive as a remembered instruction breaking into the middle of a current movement, a bodily sensation that suddenly matches a principle the focal character has known but not executed, or a failure that loops back through earlier attempts. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it: the thought states the principle while the body still lags, or the body completes the action before the mind catches up. Allow strengths and weaknesses to develop unevenly — one capability feels reliable while another stays a recurring interruption — and let that unevenness persist through the passage without being resolved.\n\nWhere an uncertain interpretation is actually present, retain its particular movement in the stream. The method does not require an unstable reading; clarity can flow associatively too. A correction may be misread at first and revised when a second attempt or a direct word re-enters the flow. An intrusive thought may interrupt practice and turn the passage in a new direction without resolving. If noise or attention obscures part of someone's speech, distinguish the actual words caught from any supported memory or guess. The character may leave the gap unfilled, seek clarification, or hear accurately; fragmentary prose need not imply fragmentary comprehension.\n\nLet feedback arrive through the senses and through consequence. Render the material as actually encountered: resistance, an awkward angle, a clear sound, a holding pattern, or ease can each become part of the felt attempt. Another person's correction may surface as a remembered phrase the focal character repeats while trying again. Do not convert every gain into a clean realization; a change in capability can register as a shift in the flow — the old hesitation no longer interrupts, the feared step completes, the familiar room looks briefly different — without the narration stepping outside the mind to announce it.\n\nEnd where the current mental movement pauses or turns: the attempt completed, interrupted by a new association, or left unfinished, with no forced resolution and no summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaaz::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nWrite the progression as a readable sender-attributed message exchange. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the attempt. Distinguish a sent message from an unsent draft, and private thought from transmitted text. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one types in fragments, another edits before sending, another leaves a message unread while working.\n\nTest advancement through what the focal character can now communicate or do between messages. A task that was previously difficult may be reported after a completed attempt, or the exchange may pause while the focal character performs the task and returns with a result. Let the messages make the changed capability legible: the focal character can now describe the step that used to fail, attach a photo of the finished work, or answer a question that previously produced only confusion. Show what remains difficult in the same exchange or a later one.\n\nIf interrupted perception or uncertain interpretation actually affects composing a message, let its supported trace appear in the record. Ordinary composition and accurate understanding require no edit, lapse, or deleted draft. An intrusive thought may surface as an unsent draft the focal character types and then deletes, or as a message sent before the thought is fully formed; an unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the live private mind. A misreading may appear as a reply that answers the wrong question, followed by an edit or a correction. When required private experience matters, present it as clearly attributed nonspoken material outside the transcript, not as a message another character can read.\n\nLet noise and attention act through the medium. A message sent while working may be shorter or more fragmented than one sent after the task; a delay may mean the focal character was practicing, not ignoring anyone. Use timestamps and delays only where that difference matters. Feedback arrives through the exchange: a correction, a question that exposes a gap, a later message confirming the attempt succeeded. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it: the focal character may type the principle correctly and still report failing the action, or perform the action and struggle to explain it in text.\n\nContext outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript, showing the attempt itself or the setting without replacing the message record. The exchange can stop wherever the attempt currently stands — completed, interrupted, or in progress. Do not fabricate an interface event to add subtext, and do not force a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaaba::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Visual Novel\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nWrite the progression scene in visual novel form. Use concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene, keeping scene changes legible. Background and expression cues are purposeful references to the imagined image, not executable engine code and not a mandatory label on every line.\n\nTest advancement through what the focal character can now do in the scene. Show the attempt in the scene-setting and focal narration: the hands move through the step that used to fail, the object holds, the response comes without the old hesitation. Then show what remains difficult, in the same scene or a later one. A gain can be legible through a single completed action under familiar conditions; it needs no montage and no training sequence.\n\nWhere interrupted perception or uncertain interpretation is supported, let the focal narration register it. Otherwise give clear attention, accurate interpretation, or undisturbed feeling its own rhythm. An intrusive thought may break into the middle of a practiced movement; a misreading of another character's tone may color the inner response until a later line corrects it. Keep the private experience in the focal narration rather than in dialogue other characters can hear. If noise or divided attention changes how the character hears or answers, preserve that actual effect. Working, listening, and speaking can also proceed fluently together.\n\nLet feedback arrive through the scene's visible and audible events — a spoken correction, a visible failure, a second attempt that succeeds. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it: the focal character may state the principle correctly in dialogue and still fail the action, or perform the action without being able to explain it. Use a background or expression cue to orient the moment — a familiar room, a changed posture, a pause — but let the gain be proven by what the attempt completes or fails, not by the expression itself.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected. Keep the scene moving through dialogue, focal narration, and legible scene changes. The scene can stop wherever the attempt currently stands — completed, interrupted, or in progress — without forcing a choice screen or a resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaabb::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Web Serial\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nWrite the progression as an installment of ongoing prose with a readable through-line. Establish what is underway, what changes locally in this installment, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only where it helps the current attempt; do not retell the training history.\n\nMake improvement matter through the attempts shown. Give the current attempt enough detail that the reader can see what the focal character can now accomplish and what remains difficult. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it: the focal character may state the principle correctly and still fail the action, or perform the action without being able to explain it. Let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly across installments — one capability improves while another stays difficult — and let the installment register that unevenness without resolving it. A gain should follow from the attempt shown, not from a sudden unexplained leap.\n\nCarry any actual perceptual interruption or uncertain interpretation into the installment prose when present, while retaining steady attention and accurate understanding when those belong to the character. An intrusive thought may break into the middle of a practiced movement; a misreading of another character's tone may color the scene until a later line corrects it. Keep the private experience available to the reader while other characters remain unaware of it. If noise or divided attention affects an exchange, show that particular effect without prescribing missed instructions, interrupted speech, or a mistake. A fluent exchange can carry the scene unchanged.\n\nLet feedback arrive through the installment's events — a spoken correction, a visible failure, a second attempt that succeeds. Progress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources; the serial form does not by itself create rankings, levels, or progression mechanics the story has not established. An occasional hook may close the installment — a new task, an open question, a resource now available — without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger.\n\nThe installment can stop wherever the attempt currently stands — completed, interrupted, or in progress — leaving the reader able to re-enter the next installment without a full recap.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabaa::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nPut the new capability and the unresolved relationship into the same scene, and let each strain the other. The character practices, prepares, or performs at the edge of what they can now do while someone they have hurt, need, or are avoiding is present or imminent, and the turbulence shows in execution before anyone names it: attention pulling out of the technique mid-attempt, a correction applied too hard, a step that arrives late because they were listening for something else.\n\nExplain any mechanism at the moment it touches the attempt — what was prepared, what it costs, why the result is partial — and let the explanation pass through divided attention rather than pausing the scene for a lecture. The gap between knowing a principle and executing it has a particular shape here: the character can state the correction perfectly and still not perform it, because anger, guilt, or proximity has taken the attention the technique requires. Show that gap in the fumbled movement itself. Let the narrator's own diction and distance carry the hurt — this character's habits of understatement, blame, or self-monitoring — rather than a borrowed vocabulary of suffering.\n\nLet the strain govern what is actually said. An apology can compete with the work and lose its thread; an admission can come out as instruction, or land after the other person has stopped waiting; withholding stays visible in what the character does with the unsaid thing while their hands keep working. When comfort appears, let it be partial or awkward — a practical kindness that concedes nothing, warmth that coexists with the hurt still standing. Follow the effects into subsequent behavior rather than resetting the relationship in the next scene.\n\nKeep the advancement real on its own terms. The attempt is earned: the character gets further than last time, or understands exactly why they failed, and that improved capability carries into what they can attempt next — while what it does not repair is the thing they could not say. End on that unevenness when it is where the scene honestly stands, not on a reconciliation or a collapse supplied to close the feeling out.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabab::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval so it scans: paragraphs short enough to read at speed, each doing one job — a speaker's turn, an action, a beat of scene narration — with attribution no reader has to reconstruct. Put the progression on-screen inside that interval. When a character attempts something at the edge of their current capability, run the attempt in sequence: what they try first, what preparation or resource they bring, how the world answers, what they can do now that they could not before. A miss still counts as movement when it gets further than the last miss or shows exactly why it missed.\n\nExplain a mechanism in the same beat where it changes what happens — what the technique does, what it costs, where it stops — and return at once to the action. Do not park the scene for a system summary, and do not add numbers merely to display growth; if a rule or resource will matter later, its earlier appearance should already have been readable. Where the attempt invites the character's private read on it — confidence, doubt, a correction remembered mid-motion — include that thought in the selected voice and person alongside the visible beats.\n\nKeep the rest of the cast alive on their own concerns. They answer the attempt from their own interests, keep occupied with tasks already in progress, and may cooperate, compete, or fail to care; their responses are the real test of the new capability, not set applause or a fixed obstacle. Player-owned characters remain under player control, and narration follows the selected person, action markers, and role conventions.\n\nStop where the scene's boundary falls — mid-task, mid-conversation, or in the settling aftermath — with the situation legible enough to continue from. No question, cliffhanger, or choice is owed at the end; the next participant can pick up the attempt, the talk, or anything else already alive in the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabac::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTell the progression as martial adventure in episodic units. Give each unit a concrete test that stands on its own — a duel, an escort, a defense, a debt called in, a leg of a journey — with its own opening, turn, and aftermath, and let the encounter itself be the measure: what the character can now attempt, what still beats them, what the victory or survival actually cost. Never improve the cast by narration alone; the encounter demonstrates the change, and the next encounter redistributes the difficulty so that yesterday's solution is not automatically reusable.\n\nKeep the working of any technique, resource, or rule legible at the moment it decides the outcome: the preparation carried into the fight, the choice made under pressure, the price paid, the limit found. A principle understood but not yet executable fails in the execution, visibly; a resource spent is felt as spent in the episodes that follow. Let the character's values decide tactics — whom they will not strike, what they refuse to spend, which challenge they accept because of a teacher, a debt, or a name they answer to — and give opponents legitimate claims and histories of their own, so that winning can create the obligation that shapes a later unit.\n\nBind the units with recurrence and variation rather than a ladder of ranks or numbers. Return to a technique, a place, a phrase, or a person from an earlier unit so the changed outcome registers; let one skill climb while another stalls; let a method that settled one encounter fail against different ground or a different opponent, and let the character read the difference. The reader tracks growth through these shifting results across episodes.\n\nKeep the language, period, and community of the setting actually in play — its own forms of address, its own courtesies and obligations — rather than transplanting a generic martial world onto the cast. The episodic form and the texture of obligation supply the method; the people, place, and speech remain the story's own.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabad::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite improvement as labor with a surface. Give the attempts grip, weight, breath, and wear — the edge gone dull, hands that will not close after the drill, ground that refuses to hold. Use hard verbs and compressed transitions to move from attempt to result without cushioning, and hold the longer line in reserve for the moments when the character actually reasons something through; the reasoning earns its length, the rest does not.\n\nMake every mechanism material at the moment it bites. A rule or resource matters as far as the character can see it, feel it, spend it, or run out of it, so explain it through those terms inside the beat where it changes the outcome. Knowing how a thing should be done and doing it come apart under exhaustion, injury, or conditions the body did not choose; let the failure occur at that junction, and let success cost something countable — stamina, supplies, skin, time something else needed.\n\nKeep the stakes physically real. Test a new capability against consequences that do not rewind: a wound that stays, gear that is gone, a task that passed while the character was drilling. Values and obligations decide what gets risked and what gets sacrificed; a technique can win the fight and still leave the debt, the cold, and the choice that led there standing.\n\nVary the pressure through rhythm — short, flat sentences under impact, an uncluttered longer one when thought needs its length — and let rough or abrasive speech belong to the particular mouth it comes out of. Hold the treatment at pressure, not at horror: difficulty is the medium growth moves through here, not a slide into ruin.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabae::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the story in documents, each with a writer, a purpose or recipient, and a moment of composition that governs what it holds. A training log written the same evening records attempts and failures without yet understanding why. A letter to a teacher reports the success and edits out its cost. A later field report or revised manual corrects an earlier account now that the principle is finally clear. Choose the form the scene actually needs — log, letter, report, requisition, marginal note — and stay with it while it serves, rather than cycling formats for variety.\n\nLet capability be read off the trail the entries leave. Lined up in order, they should show the progression: what could not be attempted, what was tried and how it failed, what feedback arrived and from whom, what is now doable, what remains out of reach. When a resource, technique, or rule decides an outcome, put its working inside a document that would naturally carry it — the requisition shows the expenditure, the report shows what worked and what broke, the revision shows the understanding that arrived late. Do not append an omniscient voice to reconcile what the records leave unsettled.\n\nLet the unevenness show through gaps and conflict. Two writers can disagree because they watched different sides of the same attempt; a strength can climb across entries while a weakness persists untouched; a crossed-out line, a changed hand, a page out of order can mark something the writer chose not to explain. A document is an artifact its author composed, not the whole of what they thought while composing: live hesitation and deliberate omission surface as traces on the page, and wherever the active contract grants the writer's private experience, render it under that contract rather than letting the record stand in for it. Keep continuity readable across the sequence and let some conflicts stand.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabaf::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nLet the work of improvement happen inside warmth rather than apart from it. The practice attempt sits in a kept room, a shared routine, an interval someone protected for it: the character drills while a meal simmers, reviews a correction while someone rebandages their hand, tries the difficult step under a friend's running commentary. The attempt remains the engine — show what they can now do, where it still catches, and how the surrounding care changes the working: steadier attention, permission to fail out loud, a reason to stop at a sensible hour.\n\nExplain any mechanism that touches the attempt plainly and gently: the resource saved rather than spent, the small adjustment that let the technique land at last, the feedback that arrived as encouragement and happened to be correct. Keep preparation, cost, and payoff understandable without hardening the tone. A modest success can carry the scene; a soft failure can teach without stinging. Let the character's preferences and commitments decide what is practiced today, what is set aside, and who is allowed to watch.\n\nSpend real attention on the small pleasures: a food someone remembered, a tool returned clean and sharp, an old joke with its timing intact, the exact place this person likes to sit, what this character privately counts as a good day. Affection can be open without a confession or a repair; disagreement, privacy, and worry over established problems stay in the room without souring it. Comfort does not make anyone agreeable and does not remove what is hard in the world — it makes the hard thing livable next to.\n\nCarry the progression forward through these intervals. What improved today changes what can be attempted next, and the care around the work is part of what makes it sustainable. A skill built in this warmth may become something the character shares, teaches, or spends on someone's behalf when the story reaches for it; it does not owe that payoff on any schedule.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabag::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Formal Literary Novel\n\nGive the progression composed, deliberate prose. Move in sustained paragraphs with real transitions, so an attempt is set before it is made: who the character is to this household, guild, family, or post; what history rides on the technique; what obligation or expectation made this the thing worth learning. Use the narrative distance the chosen narrator allows to frame the attempt socially — how it will be read, by whom, against what precedent — while keeping the actual mechanics unhurried and clear.\n\nKeep the causal account legible inside the formal movement. Preparation, resource, choice, cost, payoff: when a rule or technique decides an outcome, the narration makes its working understood at the moment it matters, not merely elegant. A character may grasp a principle and still fail in execution because a competing commitment holds part of their attention; let the prose show both the failure and the division. The style dignifies the account; it does not replace it.\n\nSeparate the registers. Narration may carry judgment, history, and social context in sentences more considered than speech; dialogue stays in each speaker's own idiom, however plain or coarse. Where the scene offers ceremony, correspondence, or a public stage, use the contrast it creates: the private failures and dull repetitions that built the capability set against its polished public demonstration, the distance between what the character can do and what observers now assume. That gap is usable material — it may alter offers, expectations, and obligations in later scenes.\n\nKeep the developing life whole. Teachers give feedback with interests of their own; rivals respond; family claims its share of time and attention; recovery and ordinary work occupy their proper weeks. Each new capability re-situates the character among these people — what opens, what is now expected, who is pleased or unsettled — so that advancement is a change in circumstance and not only in ability.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabah::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen inside the occasion for the telling: who is listening, why now, and what the listener currently cannot do. The frame is strongest when the listener is stuck at a specific point of practice — a step that keeps failing, a cost they cannot pay, a principle they can recite but not execute — and the teller has a reason to share, shape, or withhold exactly that knowledge. Give the teller a stake in the reception: the student's success or error reflects on the teacher, an old obligation can be honored or dodged in the telling, a rival's method can be quietly diminished.\n\nLet the embedded tale carry the practice itself. Inside it, show the past practitioner working under conditions that differ from the listener's: misreading a constraint, paying a cost that surprised them, discovering where a known principle stops working. When a mechanism affects the attempt, let the tale state what the practitioner could observe, what was consumed, and what feedback arrived. Keep the teller's hindsight out of the attempt; the confusion inside the tale is part of the instruction, and what the teller learned afterward surfaces as emphasis, commentary, or a deliberate omission.\n\nWhile the tale runs, keep the listener's mind working at the outer level: the comparison to their own failed attempt, the question they are holding for later, the doubt about whether the teller is being straight with them. Anchor every return to the frame in a concrete change — a shift in posture, an interruption, a hand stopping on an object — so the levels never blur.\n\nThen let the listener test the tale against present reality. A repeated phrase, tool, or sequence from the embedded story recurs outside it with changed meaning: the listener notices a step the teller skipped, supplies a resource the tale assumed, or hits a constraint the tale never covered. Progress lives in that gap — the listener adapts the told principle to actual conditions, fails at a nameable point, or asks the question that revises the teller's own understanding. Take the frame's furniture entirely from the supplied world; nested telling and recurrence are the inheritance here, not djinn, palaces, or any particular creed. Stop where the exchange or the current attempt stops — the listener need not master the step, and the teller need not finish every nested account.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabai::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social/developmental realism route: a formation narrative in which the practitioner's growth and their standing inside an order of instruction advance together, and neither is credible without the other. Take that order — school, workshop, guild, household, whatever the supplied world establishes — as part of the system being learned. Access has keepers: someone controls materials, teaching time, or recognition, and each grant carries obligations. Establish these conditions before the first new attempt, so improvement is measured both in what the practitioner becomes able to do and in what they become permitted to attempt.\n\nRender formation as stages with consequences. An error is not a setback to be erased but a turn in the arc: it changes how a superior corrects, how peers position themselves, and what the practitioner is trusted with next. When a technique is attempted, give the mechanism and the social reading together — the method works, and its use breaches an expectation, embarrasses a patron, or requires a resource that cannot be openly obtained. Preparation includes managing these conditions as deliberately as rehearsing the skill.\n\nMake advancement observable in both registers. A new responsibility, a changed form of address, a line entered in the order's records should correspond to capability the reader has watched develop, and rank never substitutes for it. Alongside the achievement, keep what remains difficult: the limit the institution sets, and the weakness the practitioner has not yet outgrown.\n\nMatch syntax and distance to the actual narrator. A close account can hold the practitioner's uncertainty about what a superior expects inside long, suspended sentences; a more distant one reports the same season through records, results, and what others were heard to say — in both cases carrying the practitioner's private reasoning about costs, loyalties, and competing commitments as ordinary prose. Take the institution, period, and social world exactly as the story supplies them; this is a craft route, not a relocation or a national temperament. Ordinary work, recovery, and relationships share the space with training. End at the boundary of an attempt, a correction or examination, or a private interval — a completed stage is not required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabaj::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Gothic-Punk\n\nBuild advancement into an inherited order that persists through modern machinery. Before any new skill appears, establish the arrangement: a family claim administered through a database, a patron's protection that still opens particular doors, a building whose restricted floor is guarded by badge and by memory, a debt that passes between generations and is serviced on a schedule. The practitioner's current capability — and current ceiling — is shaped by that arrangement, whose rules are whatever the supplied world has established.\n\nWhen the practitioner reaches for improvement, render cost and exposure together. Learning may require access someone else controls, a resource that arrives with obligation attached, or a practice performed for one audience and concealed from another. Explain the mechanism at the moment it matters — what is done, what it consumes, what feedback returns — and let the encounter's atmosphere carry the stakes: the weight of a lobby built for an older authority, the hum of the system that now enforces it, the difference between the practitioner's public manner and the private moment behind it. Mood deepens the encounter; it never replaces the mechanism.\n\nLet each gain register in the order's own terms. A new capability can open a door that was categorically closed, change how the old authority addresses the practitioner, or make a previously tolerable arrangement — an obligation, an intimacy, a pretense — newly hard to bear. Give the private reasoning its full weight: which loyalty is strained, which desire is finally admitted, which price is accepted and which merely postponed. Not every intimacy is a trap and not every elder is corrupt; trusted company can be where the mask comes off, and what is underneath may be relief as easily as horror.\n\nKeep the urban material concrete and causal. A decaying stair, a locked archive, a buzzer panel listing a name no one recognizes — each should matter through its function and history, not as decoration. Stop at the edge of the current attempt, exchange, or private interval; the inherited order does not need to be resolved for the scene to end.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabak::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social/domestic realism route: the practitioner's development is one thread in a working household, and capability is measured inside that web rather than apart from it. Establish the web before any improvement — the ordinary labor that supports the home, who is owed time, who must be deferred to, who can be relied on and at what cost to them. Teachers here are particular people — a relative, a neighbor, an employer — whose hours, patience, and goodwill are finite resources to be budgeted like any other material.\n\nWhen a new technique is attempted, state the mechanism and the household reading together. The method works, and it takes an hour from the shop, uses a material someone else was saving, or must be hidden from the specific relative who would find it presumptuous. Preparation means arranging these conditions — finding the hour, replacing the material, choosing the audience — as much as practicing the skill. Let private reasoning run alongside: what the practitioner hopes to protect, whom the change will obligate, which loyalty the ambition strains.\n\nTest advancement through what the practitioner can now do inside the story's actual conditions: finish alone a task that used to require help, renegotiate a role without losing a necessary relationship, refuse a demand and survive the refusal. Alongside the gain, keep what the household still makes difficult — and let improvement generate its own new expectations and small resentments, since capability changes what people ask of a person.\n\nTake language, region, community, and period exactly as the story supplies them; do not add English, code-switching, religion, caste, or a family structure the scene has not established. Let sentence rhythm and distance follow the actual narrator, and keep the practitioner's nonspoken experience as ordinary story prose rather than asides to the reader. End at the edge of an attempt, a domestic exchange, or a private interval; no stage need be completed first.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabal::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social/psychological realism route: advancement changes what the practitioner can do and what that capability means to the person who now holds it. Establish the surroundings early — who watches the practitioner, what the role is supposed to look like, what success and failure each cost in those particular relationships. Precision and omission carry the surface: a small visible action — the adjusted grip, the bow a fraction early, the tool set down without being asked — can carry social meaning without commentary, while the narration still gives the practitioner's private reasoning in full as ordinary prose.\n\nRender attempts concretely. When a mechanism bears on an attempt, show what the practitioner does, what it consumes, and what feedback arrives; present the rule or limit at the moment it bites, not as a glossary aside. Alongside the attempt run its private companions — the hope that precedes it, the misreading beneath the error, the specific texture of the aftermath: frustration, relief, shame, or a quiet satisfaction the practitioner would not voice. No single emotional outcome is required; write this person's response, not a prescribed modesty or composure.\n\nLet success and failure split into outward and inward results. The practitioner may perform well and privately recognize the new obligation that follows the skill, or fail visibly while grasping something that will matter at a later attempt. Test advancement in both registers: what the practitioner can now accomplish, how superiors and peers recalibrate, and how the practitioner regards a limitation previously accepted as natural.\n\nMatch sentence length and distance to the actual teller: a close account stays inside the practitioner's uncertainty; a more distant one reports behavior and later consequence while still carrying private reasoning as ordinary story prose. Keep relationships, recovery, and ordinary work inside the developing life, and end at the boundary of an attempt, a social exchange, or a private interval.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabam::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social realism/chronicle route: narrate the practitioner's development with an awareness of what the community will remember, retell, and hold against them. Establish the material ground first — the work available, the tools and spaces that can actually be obtained, the teacher whose hours are one more scarce resource, the history that shapes how this community regards someone trying to rise. These conditions are not scenery; they decide what can be attempted and what any new capability will cost.\n\nWhen something new is attempted, give mechanism and material consequence together. The technique requires a tool that must be borrowed, a space that belongs to someone with grievances, or hours taken from another need that will now go unmet. State what the practitioner does, what it consumes, and what feedback returns, at the moment it matters. Preparation includes the economics: sourcing, bartering, and the small dishonesties or generosities that acquisition demands.\n\nLet advancement arrive in the community's terms. A capability lets the practitioner finish alone what used to require help, take on work that was closed to them, or finally meet an obligation long deferred — and the chronicle eye records how family, neighbors, and employers respond: the new expectations, the old resentments, the version of events that begins circulating as story. Carry the other register in the practitioner's private reasoning — which loyalty is strained, which hope is admitted, which price is accepted — without making every relationship corrupt or every success a betrayal.\n\nTake language, community, and history exactly as the story supplies them, and do not use this style to add supernatural facts to a world that excludes them. Let syntax and distance follow the actual narrator, keep nonspoken experience as ordinary prose, and end at the boundary of an attempt, a work exchange, or a private interval.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaban::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Light Novel\n\nEnter each attempt already in motion: one or two lines to fix where the practitioner is, what they are trying to do, and which constraint or resource is in play, then straight into the doing. Keep staging visual and trackable — when the practitioner moves, when an object changes hands, when someone else arrives, put the new position or possession in one short concrete sentence so the reader never has to rebuild the scene.\n\nTeach the system at the moment it matters. If a technique's rule, cost, or limit decides the attempt, present it right there — in action, in the practitioner's running assessment, or in a line of dialogue — never as a standing lecture. Show what is done, what it consumes, and what feedback comes back; let an unfamiliar name or mechanic become clear through its first use rather than an introduction.\n\nGive the beat after the result its own paragraph and its own weight: what the success or failure means for the goal in play, what the practitioner now understands that they did not before, what still resists. The reaction belongs to this character — serious, uncertain, furious, or genuinely funny — and it must be specific to the attempt just made, not a generic exclamation. Keep mechanism and reaction paired: the reader sees what happened and what the practitioner makes of it, including the part said aloud and the part kept private.\n\nScale advancement to the scene. A newly gained capability changes what the next attempt looks like — a shorter path, a survivable risk, a mistake no longer available — without needing to resolve the situation. Use clean paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response, and stop at the natural edge of the current action, exchange, or private beat; a cliffhanger, a comedy beat, or a completed rank is optional, never owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabao::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Lyrical Prose\n\nGround each stage of improvement in an attempt with a specific difficulty, a chosen method, and a changed result. Establish what the character can currently do and what still resists before they act, then let the attempt unfold as concrete movement — the hand learning a grip, the breath finding its pattern, the material answering or refusing. When a principle becomes execution, show the difference in the body and the outcome rather than in a restated lesson.\n\nLet lyric form carry the experience of that change. Recurring sounds and images can mark the stages of practice: a repeated motion gathering new meaning as control improves, a familiar object looking different once the character can use it well. Unfold a comparison only when it alters what the narrator understands about the attempt, and keep every image traceable to the scene or to the teller's own associations. Let plain statements about cost, preparation, and failure interrupt the figurative movement — the account stays honest because the price is named alongside the beauty. Recurrence earns its place when a later attempt gives a phrase new application; it need not attend every difficulty.\n\nKeep advancement uneven. A success in one area may expose a weakness in another, and new capability can make an old habit feel strange. Test growth through a task that would have been impossible earlier while leaving some difficulty intact, and keep resource decisions — what was spent, what was saved, what was risked — inside the scene. The close can arrive mid-practice or between attempts, with the next step available and no completed arc required to justify stopping.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabap::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet advancement arrive through a concrete attempt whose method and cost stay clear: what was practiced, what resource was spent, what the character can now do that was impossible before. Then let the new capability touch memory through the specific history of the old limit — a task that once required help, a place where the character used to fail, a person who knew them before the change. Keep the present action and the remembered occasion in the same scene, so improvement registers as both gain and distance rather than as a meditation appended to a training beat.\n\nCarry that double meaning in small irreversible details. A familiar object handled too easily, a once-difficult route gone short, an old habit that no longer fits the body that learned something new — these hold the reflection because they belong to the attempt. Render the reflection as the character's actual inward experience under the active perspective — attention drifting mid-task, a remembered voice attaching to a movement now made easy — rather than as an announced theme. Ordinary pleasure stays pleasurable; the character may be glad of the change even while noticing what it displaced.\n\nKeep development uneven and unfinished. New strength can make an old weakness more visible, or a solved problem can reveal a harder one, and the character may remember an earlier self without returning to it. The scene may end where the present task pauses, with the past present in nothing grander than the changed way the character moves through the room.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabaq::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse framed oral storytelling as this entry's regional route: a situated teller, a concrete occasion, and an audience whose presence shapes the telling. Establish early why this teller relates this learner's progress and what the occasion asks of the story, and fit the teller's voice and person to the active narrator rather than to a stock narrator. The frame lives inside the story's own community and period; the route requires no antiquity, mysticism, exile, or particular honor code, and it does not relocate the cast.\n\nMake the source of knowledge part of the telling. What the teller witnessed, what was reported to them, and what they infer stay distinct, and the teller can mark the difference in a clause: the first failure seen with their own eyes, the later breakthroughs had from the one who taught the learner. Inside the frame, keep each stage of improvement legible — the specific difficulty, the method tried, the feedback received, the task accomplished afterward — and when a mechanism matters to an attempt, explain it in the teller's own voice and in terms the audience would understand, so the rule arrives as part of the story rather than as an aside past the audience. Let costs and choices appear as remembered facts: what was spent, what was risked, what the learner decided under pressure.\n\nLet the frame do dramatic work. The teller can pause to address the audience, return to a phrase when a later attempt gives it new meaning, or admit where accounts disagree and where the report outruns the evidence. Preserve the unevenness of real development: a skill gained while another stays weak, a lesson bought by repeated failure, an advance that creates a new obligation. The telling can end on the audience's response, on a disputed point left open, or with the learner's next stage still to be related.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabar::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Minimalist Prose\n\nBuild the scene on one meaningful attempt. Open with the current limit in a direct clause — what the character can do, what still fails, what this attempt tries to change — then render the attempt as concrete movement and immediate consequence. Keep the causal chain short and visible: the method used, the resource spent, the result obtained, the difficulty that remains.\n\nState a mechanism only at the moment it changes what the character can try or what the attempt costs; give it in plain language and move on without summarizing the whole system or repeating the explanation after the result. Let omission leave something inferable rather than deleting causality: a failed attempt can be carried by the damaged material, the adjusted grip, or the character's next action instead of an announced lesson. Keep only the inward note that materially belongs to this attempt — a single line of doubt, attention, or recalculation — and let a precise extended sentence stand when the essential thought needs its length. Spoken voices stay individual; compression belongs to the narration, not to flattening the cast.\n\nPreserve uneven advancement in few words. A success exposes a new limit; a new capability makes an old task easier without completing the character. The scene can stop at the result or at the interruption, the next step available and unnarrated.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabas::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite progression as one sustained scene organized around a specific attempt. Enter the scene with the character's present capability and the task that tests it, and let action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and inner experience move as the scene naturally moves. Tie each movement to the attempt: a remembered lesson matters when it shapes the present method, a conversation when it delivers feedback or exposes a competing commitment, a perception when it changes what the character tries next.\n\nMake the system legible through the scene rather than around it. Preparation and resource use appear as things done and spent in the moment; a limiting rule arrives through the attempt's difficulty or the character's live reasoning about it, never through a paused summary of the progression system. Let the result demonstrate changed capability — a task impossible before now succeeds, or succeeds differently — while another difficulty remains, and keep values inside the decision about what to risk, spend, or postpone. Inner experience belongs to the focal character under the active perspective, reasoning in real time rather than reporting afterward.\n\nUse paragraphing and transitions to orient without recap machinery. A new paragraph can mark the shift from failed attempt to revised method, from private reasoning to spoken feedback, or from present action into relevant memory; a plain transition can carry elapsed practice time without restating what the reader knows. The reply can end at the scene's natural boundary — mid-task, between attempts, or on an unresolved difficulty — without a closing audit of how far the character has come.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabat::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nLet the established relationship be the scene's organizing center and the place where progression happens. Work from the supplied premise to a moment of practice, feedback, or changed capability that matters to these particular people — one teaching or watching the other, a shared resource to spend or protect, a success that changes what the two can do together, a failure that tests how they answer each other. Keep the attempt itself concrete and legible: the current limit, the method tried, the result shown through action and consequence.\n\nCarry the emotional payoff in the relationship's own materials. Private language from the premise can return with new meaning after an advance or a setback; a familiar gesture can signal comfort, rivalry, affection, or worry without being glossed for the reader. Give the beat room where it belongs — a held silence after a first success or a joke arriving a beat late may deserve that space — and fulfill the particular promise of this relationship rather than a generic pattern of confession or jealousy. Where a mechanism affects the attempt, keep it legible, and let resource costs show in what the characters choose to risk for each other.\n\nKeep both development and response uneven and specific. One person's improvement may leave the other proud, envious, relieved, or newly aware of a gap, and the two may not want the same thing from the advance. The scene can close with the relationship consequence settled enough to feel earned while the next stage of advancement or the next shared difficulty stays open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabau::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell advancement as a chronicle of attempts that will be remembered. Open with a deliberate declaration of the learner's present standing or the task ahead, then relate each attempt as a deed: what was tried, what it cost, what changed. Present any mechanism as part of the remembered action rather than as detached explanation — a resource spent, a method learned, a limit overcome enters the account through its consequence, so the rule is understood by what it did.\n\nLet recurrence mark the stages of development. A compact declaration may return when a later attempt gives it new meaning: the same task attempted again, the same cost paid differently, the same weakness exposed under new conditions. Relate each new capability to what will be said of the learner, but claim no reputation unearned, and keep the teller's access consistent with the active perspective — deeds witnessed, deeds reported, and deeds inferred stay distinct, and a private motive can remain outside the chronicle's public record without leaving the story. Where the active story establishes oaths, kinship, journeys, gifts, or public deeds, they can organize the account; import no pantheon and no heroic doom, and let speakers keep their own voices rather than ceremonial dialogue.\n\nPreserve the weight of choice and uneven growth. A success may be remembered alongside a failure, and a new strength may create obligation or danger rather than completeness. The chronicle can leave the next attempt or consequence still to be recorded instead of fixing the learner's future in legend.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabav::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the progression through the social- and domestic-realist route of the Scandinavian tradition. Advancement becomes legible as changed standing: who trusts a person with work, who asks them for help, who must revise an expectation formed long ago, who carries a new share of a household's or institution's obligations. Show the attempts that produce the change—the practice undertaken between other duties, the failed application watched by people whose opinion matters, the correction offered by someone who knows the work, the second attempt that only partly succeeds—and let the reader see what the person can now do that they could not before, and what still resists them.\n\nRender constraints as the practical economics of the life. When a task needs a resource, a teacher's time, a tool, a period of recovery, or a condition that must be arranged, put the arrangement on the page before the attempt and let its consequence appear in the result. Knowing a principle and executing it under fatigue, pressure, or social scrutiny remain different things; show the gap rather than closing it early. If the active world has ranks or formal stages, let them matter through what they let a person do for others, not as announced milestones, and invent no numerical scaffolding merely to display growth.\n\nLet relationships absorb the change unevenly: one person welcomes the improvement, another feels displaced, a third notices only when a practical need arises, and none of this needs to become a betrayal or a loyalty crisis. Keep the narration socially situated—the viewpoint's sense of other people grows out of shared work, meals, errands, meetings, and conversation rather than solitary reflection—and when private thought appears, let it answer a particular social moment: a remark that landed wrongly, a task that exposed a gap, a debt that changed meaning.\n\nStay inside the community, language, and period the active world supplies. This route relocates no one, imports no wintry reserve, isolation, or bleakness as a default temperament, and does not blend distinct Nordic contexts into one generic Scandinavian texture where the world draws them separately. Preserve the active world's rules and the active narrator's hold on the telling. Advancement can stay uneven across a life—a strength in a craft may leave a negotiation or a form of care untouched—and ordinary work, recovery, and relationships remain part of what is developing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabaw::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Screenplay\n\nWrite the progression as filmable attempts and their consequences. Concise action lines carry what the character does, what they prepare, what resource they spend, and what result follows: a specific motion practiced badly, then less badly; materials laid out and counted; a condition checked before use. Make each attempt legible from what is visible and audible, and keep the character's full reasoning out of the action lines.\n\nLet dialogue carry the constraints and choices that matter. A teacher corrects one detail in specific terms; a companion names what the character could not do before; the character says what they are attempting now and why it might fail. Tie every explanation to the immediate attempt rather than delivering a lecture, and when a mechanism affects the outcome, establish it beforehand through a visible action or a line of speech so the payoff reads as earned.\n\nUse scene headings when location or time changes, and give each scene one legible change in capability or understanding. A cut can move from a failed attempt to a later session, from preparation to application, or from a success to the social consequence that follows it, but never cut past the preparation that makes a later success understandable. Offscreen sound can carry a result when hearing it serves better than showing it—the thud of a dropped load, the steadying rhythm of drills behind a closed door—and the page stays free of camera commands.\n\nHold required private thought as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation, separated from dialogue and action lines and heard by no one on screen. Use it when the character's reasoning, hesitation, or competing commitment shapes the next visible choice, not as commentary on every line.\n\nShow advancement through what the character can now accomplish on screen—a task finished faster, a technique held together under pressure, a resource spent where it does the most work, a failure that isolates a specific gap—and keep what remains difficult visible as well. A scene may end on a completed attempt, a new obstacle, or a decision to continue; no cliffhanger or victory beat is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabax::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Stage Play\n\nWrite the progression as staged physical work. Blocking, props, and observable business show what a character can now do: they cross the stage carrying a load they once set down, complete a sequence of motions without stopping, handle a tool with new precision. The audience watches the attempt and its result happen live; no one needs to hear it described afterward.\n\nMake the mechanism of improvement legible through what the stage permits. If a task requires a particular object, a cleared space, another person's assistance, or rest beforehand, establish that condition in the stage directions before the attempt is made. A character names a constraint in dialogue when it bears on their choice—what they lack, what they are trying, what they expect to fail—and the explanation stays tied to the physical work in progress. Treat pauses as stageable events in their own right: the pause before a motion that has not yet become automatic, and the disappearance of that pause where it used to be.\n\nUse entrances, exits, and the shared space to test advancement socially. Someone who has improved may be asked to demonstrate, to teach, to take over a task another person handled, or to work beside a doubter. Let lines and physical business carry the public scene together: a correction delivered while adjusting a grip, praise that arrives as the recipient is still clearing away the failure, a request made while resetting the floor for another try.\n\nKeep required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation, separate from dialogue and stage directions—not an aside another character can hear, and not a monologue performed to the house unless separately authorized. Use it when reasoning, hesitation, or a competing commitment shapes the next staged choice.\n\nLet advancement stay uneven: a character may succeed at one task and fail at another in the same scene. Restrict directions to what performers and crew can stage—position, light, sound, object—and write no film-camera instructions. The scene may end on a completed attempt, a new difficulty, or a decision to keep practicing, with no obligatory gathering, revelation, or victory speech.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabay::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the progression as the changing texture of one authorized mind moving through practice, feedback, and new capability. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and a recurring concern through that mind as it attempts a task: a corrected motion surfaces the memory of the first attempt; a failure breaks into a train of thought about something else entirely; a success feels unfamiliar enough that attention keeps returning to it.\n\nKeep the mechanism of advancement recoverable inside the associations. When a constraint bears on an attempt, let the mind register it concretely—the weight of what was carried, the teacher's absence, the condition unmet, the cost already paid—so a reader can reconstruct why this attempt worked where the last one failed. The character may recite the principle they were taught and feel the distance between it and what their body actually does; do not let association dissolve the mechanism into impression.\n\nMark the difference between what happens, what is remembered, and what is imagined: a remembered session is not the current attempt, and a pictured success is not an accomplished one. A sensory anchor, a repeated phrase, or a shift in tense or address can keep the reader oriented. The mode belongs to the one mind the active contract authorizes; other characters are known from outside, through what they say and do, and the scene's facts stay determinate.\n\nLet the recurring concern give advancement its meaning. The character measures progress against a debt, a relationship, a fear, or a competing commitment that keeps surfacing mid-practice, and the way the mind holds that concern changes as capability does: what once produced panic now produces focus; an achievement that once seemed unreachable now raises the question of what to do with it. Strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly within the same attention—one motion grown automatic while another still demands deliberate thought—and the telling registers both without announcing a rank or a completed stage. The passage may end on a finished attempt, an interruption, or a thought still turning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabaz::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the progression through what people actually transmit. A learner asks a specific question about a failed attempt; a teacher corrects one detail in plain terms; a companion confirms the task is done or names what still went wrong. Let requests, offers, refusals, and confirmations carry the change in capability without anyone filing a progress report, and let the thread itself be the record: a message that once asked for help can be answered further down by an offer of the same help, and an old instruction can be quoted back when it finally applies.\n\nMake the mechanism of advancement legible through the practical detail the messages reveal. If a task needs a resource, a condition, a stretch of practice, or another person's hands, that appears in what someone asks for, offers, declines, or confirms—what was prepared, what was spent, what resulted. Keep explanation tied to the immediate need rather than composed as a lecture in text form.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft, and transmitted text from private thought. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live mind; it may appear when its composition matters, clearly separated from the exchange. Required private experience stays in clearly attributed nonspoken material outside the transcript, not in a message another character receives, and any context beyond the exchange appears only when needed, set apart from the messages themselves.\n\nLet each person's digital habits shape their contribution—one writes in fragments, another in full sentences; one answers at once, another after a delay the transcript marks only when it changes what the exchange means. No habit signals honesty or deceit on its own. Use timestamps, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes only when they alter meaning: a delay that shows hesitation, distraction, or a task finished in between, not a marker attached to every difficulty.\n\nShow advancement through what a person can now confirm, request, or decline. A correction that once produced confusion now draws the follow-up question that shows understanding; a task once delegated is now offered to someone else. What remains difficult stays visible—clarification still requested, a task still avoided, a resource still missing—and the exchange may end on a completed answer, a pending request, or a message left unread, with no mandatory cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabba::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the progression in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene: concise scene-setting, attributed dialogue, and focal narration or inner response. Establish where the attempt happens and the condition the character brings to it—the space, the materials laid out, the person waiting to observe—then let focal narration register the attempt itself: the motion, the hesitation, the correction, the result. Keep narrative beats short enough to sit between lines of dialogue, and when the scene changes, re-establish place and participants so the shift stays legible.\n\nMake the mechanism of advancement legible through what the scene shows and what the dialogue explains. A required resource, condition, teacher's guidance, or period of recovery is established before the attempt, and its consequence appears in the outcome. A character can state what they are trying and why it might fail; another can correct a specific detail. Keep every explanation tied to the immediate attempt rather than delivered as a lecture.\n\nTreat background and expression cues as purposeful references that orient the moment, not as automatic truth indicators and not as a label owed to every line. A changed expression can draw the focal character's attention, but the scene's real information—what was said, what was done, what was prepared—carries the meaning. Let focal narration show the viewpoint interpreting another person's reaction, and allow that interpretation to be incomplete or mistaken: the next exchange may support it, complicate it, or reveal that the other person was concerned with something else entirely.\n\nLet the social consequences of advancement arrive in the scene's rhythm—a request to demonstrate, to teach, to take on a task, or to work beside a doubter. Show advancement through what the character can now accomplish here: a task finished faster, a technique held under pressure, a resource spent well, a failure that isolates a specific gap. Keep what still resists visible. No branching menu, player decision, romance route, or engine command comes with the format; those require their own authorization. The scene may end on a completed attempt, a new difficulty, or a decision to continue.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalabbb::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Web Serial\n\nWrite the progression as cumulative developments a reader can re-enter at any installment. Each reply registers a local change in capability or understanding—a practice session that exposes a specific gap, a correction that finally takes hold, a resource spent that alters what can be attempted next, a task completed that was previously out of reach—and makes clear what is underway, what changed here, and what remains open to continue. Progress can run through knowledge, relationships, work, or resources, not technique alone.\n\nRecover only the context the current attempt needs. When an earlier constraint bears on the present one—a resource already spent, a condition that must be met, a correction given installments ago—briefly recover that particular rather than the history of how it was established, and keep preparation and payoff connected across time: what was gathered earlier is spent now, what was taught earlier is tested now, a weakness identified earlier is worked on now.\n\nDistinguish knowing from executing. A character may recall the instruction perfectly and still fail the attempt; let the installment show that gap without closing it at once. Strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly—one task grows automatic while another still demands deliberate effort—and a reply can register both. Keep inward experience available where it contributes thought or feeling, even when no immediate decision changes, and let ordinary life continue around the work.\n\nLet serial duration give established facts new meaning. A resource, a limitation, or a relationship introduced earlier can return with different significance once capability changes, recovered briefly when it helps the reader re-enter. An installment may end on a completed attempt, a pending request, a new obstacle, or a decision to continue; use a hook when a development genuinely opens a new question, and let a request stay unanswered or a task span turns rather than forcing a cliffhanger or deferring progress forever.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacaa::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite each moment of improvement as a practice ritual the narrator cannot describe without counting something: repetitions, breaths, names, cups, scars, the attempts before the first clean execution. Let the count carry a private wound. Each rep can recall a failure, a dependency, or the person whose absence made the skill necessary, and the narrator's exactness about numbers is how the hurt shows without being named.\n\nTreat a rank, a breakthrough, or a completed technique as a change in capability, not a cure. What the character can now do alters their options and the pressure others place on them; whether the wound underneath eases, hardens, or stays the same is decided by the events you actually write, not by the promotion itself. Show advancement through a concrete act that was previously impossible — a door held, a blow turned, a formula finished under fatigue — and attach the emotional charge to that act as shame, rage, apology, need, or the memory that makes the victory feel like a betrayal. The same beat may also carry an awkward, partial ease; warmth and pain can share the scene.\n\nUse fragments from practice as emotional punctuation: a rule the character repeats, a phrase a teacher used, a ritual of washing or counting after an attempt. Place a recurrence where the strain is real rather than at every difficulty. When another person enters, work the turbulence through specific interaction — a refusal of help, a stiff apology, a need confessed only after two failed attempts — and let skill and relationship move together where the scene supports it: an argument inside sparring, a confession after a breakthrough. Stop where the scene's attention stops; the character may end stronger and still hurting, or somewhere the exchange has actually earned.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacab::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the current exchange as a shared practice interval in scannable paragraphs, with unmistakable attribution for every spoken line. Open on the submitted action or attempt in whatever person and voice the active contract uses, then give it an immediate material consequence: a strike that lands, a formula that flickers, a grip that finally holds. Ground improvement in a repeatable routine the character actually keeps — steps counted, a pouch tapped, a teacher's rule recited before the attempt — and quote or report that fragment where it belongs to the moment rather than at every difficulty.\n\nGive the other participants and NPCs their own limited knowledge and their own response to the display. A correction, an admiration, a misunderstanding, or a challenge should change the next practical step, and none of it needs to be expert or hostile: a training partner can refuse to repeat a dangerous drill, ask for a demonstration, or explain the conditions under which the technique works, and their practical lives continue around the exchange.\n\nLet self-exposure surface through process where strain actually occurs — a past failure mentioned, exhaustion admitted after a clean run, a private gesture after the drill — visible or hidden according to the active interiority contract. End the post at the scene's living boundary, whether that is mid-drill, mid-instruction, or while someone fetches a new supply; the continuation follows from what is still going on, without a cliffhanger, a forced question, or a manufactured choice.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacac::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTake the martial adventure route: the progression lives inside an inherited discipline — a sect, a family art, a school, a wandering master's teaching — told with the episodic momentum of deed, consequence, and remembered reputation. Write each training beat as a procedural lesson with a named form, a required count, and a material consequence when the form is rushed. The student repeats the form in the courtyard while the master watches, and the narration records the body's exact failures: the wobble of a stance, the late turn, the torn callus.\n\nCarry the hard rhythm through transmission. A rule recurs across scenes with small variation — a senior's reminder, a forbidden shortcut, a phrase whispered before the next attempt — and the rule carries a debt: a promise to a dead parent, a slight against a rival school, an obligation to a disgraced master. Advancement becomes visible when the form is performed under real pressure — a night duel, an interrupted ceremony, a demonstration before an elder — and the new capability stays uneven; the student may win a contest and still not manage an apology to a sibling.\n\nKeep the language, period, and community distinct as the supplied setting gives them — its architecture, diet, transport, and social forms, not a generic exotic backdrop — and let the tradition shape the telling without relocating the cast. Let private cost surface through etiquette and conduct, the way martial adventure carries feeling in manners: a bow held too long, an elder's dismissal, a junior's refusal to share a technique. Close where the episode's beat rests, with the change in standing or capability legible; the personal friction may persist, ease, or deepen as the actual events decide.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacad::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite each progression beat as a short, hard sequence of attempt, result, and changed condition. Open inside a concrete action — a swing, a sprint, a knot tied, a chant repeated — and record the exact material outcome: the callus splits, the pack slips, the torch gutters. The result may be failure, partial success, or a clean execution; what matters is that the body and the equipment register it. Use compressed transitions: a paragraph can begin mid-drill and end on a changed grip or breath, leaving the next to carry the consequence.\n\nRepeat one specific practical rule or fragment across the sequence — a count, a maintenance routine, a teacher's warning, a ritual before the attempt — and let the repetition mark the character's obsession rather than float as a tic. When a skill finally executes, report the new capability in physical terms — the door swings open, the wire holds, the flame answers — and let what remains difficult surface where it actually recurs: the same motion under fatigue, the next rank's cost, the need for someone else's instruction.\n\nGround emotional content in material wear and physical stakes — anger as a slammed buckle, fear as a checked strap, need as a ration shared — rather than explanatory summary, and let rough speech belong to its speaker. When the character's reasoning needs a longer thought, keep it, with the surrounding language concrete. End where the attention ends, often just past a change in capability or a definite limit, and leave the moral summary out.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacae::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Epistolary / Documents\n\nAdvance the story through documents — practice logs, field reports, letters, a master's written corrections — each with a specific author, purpose, and moment of composition, and no omniscient narrator between them. Let the form itself structure the progression: a daily record counts repetitions and notes failed attempts; a week on, a new entry lists three failures; a month on, a letter home mentions the technique just learned and omits the injury that came with it.\n\nRun the hard rhythm inside the documents. The same rule or drill appears as a fixed count in the log, a remembered warning in a letter, a standard correction in the master's report, and the formulation shifts with the writer's state — the student copies the phrase differently when confident, exhausted, or ashamed. That recurrence makes the advancement legible across entries without editorial explanation.\n\nLet each document reveal and conceal according to its situation: a sickbay report lists the broken bone and not the duel's cause; a supply request keeps asking for bandages; a rival's complaint accuses a shortcut the log denies. The cost of progression gathers in these gaps, revisions, and defensive repetitions as much as in anything plainly stated, and where a document's purpose invites confession, the confession still shows its writer's defensive logic. Preserve causal continuity — later documents may cite earlier ones but cannot change what they recorded. A log or letter is a composed artifact, not the writer's whole live mind: where the active contract includes visible or hidden interiority, the attention and motive surrounding the act of composition still get written.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacaf::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the training scene as a shared routine of care and deliberate effort. Build comfort from concrete repeated details that earn their warmth through predictability: the warm-up counted aloud, the cup of tea set beside the practice mat, the clean cloth folded for the next attempt. Let the hard rhythm live in these maintenance acts rather than in transgression or shame — the narrator counts breaths, sets, or cups, and the counting steadies the room.\n\nGive each character an observable preference that shapes the practice: one always tightens the right strap first, another drinks cold water only after the third set, a third hums three notes before a difficult form. Let affection arrive in action — a partner adjusting a grip without speaking, a cushion set down when the student wobbles, the last piece of fruit saved — so sincerity never depends on a confession or a repair. Keep the progression real: a difficult technique takes its actual number of attempts, capability grows unevenly, and difficulty stays difficult. When something does go wrong, the available responses include a hand on the shoulder, a patient re-demonstration, a shared snack, chosen by these particular people rather than dispensed automatically.\n\nPreserve personality, privacy, and disagreement. A character may argue about form or refuse help at first, and what happens between them moves at its own speed; comfort does not require anyone to become agreeable, and the world's established problems stay in place without turning dangerous. Show changed capability in something concrete — a longer hold, a cleaner turn — with the care continuing around it. End where the shared interval rests, effort and ease still together, needing no final gift or declaration to count as comfort.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacag::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite each stage of progression inside a socially observed ritual, with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Where the active narrator permits that distance, open on framing context — a hall where apprentices are ranked by the order in which they first kept the flame lit, a family whose letters record each generation's first successful invocation, a city that measures respect by answered midnight bells — so that the repetitions of practice carry a public meaning alongside the private one. Keep the narration distinct from ordinary spoken language without imposing archaic vocabulary on every voice.\n\nUse procedural recursion as architecture. A paragraph can open on a clause from the training rule, vary it with the student's changing understanding, and close on the material mark left by that attempt — a scorch on the wall, a torn seam, a glove worn through. When the narrator reflects, let the reflection move through social history, family debt, or institutional judgment, connecting the private obsession to the wider order it happens inside.\n\nShow advancement as changing mastery within that order, and keep it uneven. A junior may first bow too late, later preside over a small ceremony, later still decline a traditional promotion, and each change in capability shifts how others address, seat, or ask help of the character — while a technique executed perfectly can still be denied its rank by a family dispute. The cost of advancement shows in the distance between private obsession and public ceremony. End a passage where its attention completes, the social consequence legible and its settlement left to what the story does next.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacah::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the progression as a story someone is telling under pressure. The teller has an audience, an occasion, and a reason to keep speaking: to buy time, win trust, teach a companion, justify a failure, or keep a listener from acting. The outer scene stays present between embedded passages and changes the telling. When a listener shifts, a fire settles, a door closes, or the available time ends, the teller may speed up, omit a step, repeat a point, or stop mid-account.\n\nMake each embedded account a concrete attempt to improve. The teller describes the drill, the mistake, the feedback, the adjustment, and the changed capability in specific physical terms. Show what the person could not do before, what they tried, what broke or resisted, and what they can now do. Do not summarize advancement as a rank-up. Let the audience interrupt with practical questions about method, cost, or sequence. When a question lands on a gap, do not announce its significance. The teller may deny it, deflect into another step, or say the audience is asking about the wrong part. Then the next embedded passage continues the procedure from inside that unstated gap, showing the skipped failure, the unexplained resource, the teacher's intervention, or the cost by narrating what actually happened.\n\nUse Palahniuk's hard rhythms and obsessive particulars through the teller's current voice. Repeat a short rule, phrase, or piece of equipment each time the training changes. Make the repetition accrue meaning without glossing it. The same rule can sound like instruction before a failure, like a threat or promise during the adjustment, and like a justification after the audience learns what it cost. Keep the teller's confidence intact while the account exposes the defensive logic beneath it: the teller can insist the method worked, then describe a detail that shows why they needed it to work.\n\nKeep the narrative levels intelligible. Mark the return to the outer scene with a concrete sensory detail and a practical consequence: a listener asks a question, refuses a step, or reveals why they need the story to continue. Let the outer scene's elapsed time and stakes affect the telling rather than merely frame it. The audience's questions are part of the progression because they force the teller to put into words what was previously only felt or performed.\n\nDo not require the telling to end in a completed breakthrough or a cliffhanger. The teller can stop mid-account when the outer scene demands it. The improvement remains real and uneven: the teller can now perform one movement reliably while still failing another. The final passage should leave the audience with a changed understanding of the teller and the training, not a solved problem.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacai::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the documentary and historical-memory route. Write the progression as a reconstruction: the narrator assembles training logs, injury reports, supply lists, marginal notes, witness accounts, and remembered conditions to understand how a capability developed. Let the documents carry the procedural detail. A training log records repetitions and failures; a supply list shows what was consumed; a note in a margin reveals what the trainee feared or misunderstood. The narrator compares the record with memory, and the two do not always agree.\n\nKeep the Palahniuk rhythm in the narrator's voice, not in the documents alone. Repeat a short rule, phrase, or piece of equipment while moving through the evidence. Let the repetition expose the narrator's defensive logic without announcing it: the narrator can call the method sound while citing a record that shows the cost. Place the claim and the contradictory detail close enough that the reader sees the gap, but do not make the narrator name the contradiction.\n\nMake advancement legible through concrete before-and-after evidence. Put an early training record beside a later one and let the difference in task, conditions, or result carry the change. A witness account can set the first attempt against a later attempt. When the narrator moves from evidence to explanation, show the specific changed practice: a new grip, a corrected sequence, a resource, a teacher's correction. Do not let a rank or number replace the evidence. Show what the person can now accomplish and what still fails.\n\nMove between past and present by letting the present site corroborate or complicate the written record. When the narrator stands in the present location, use the worn floor, broken equipment, or scar on the trainee's hand as another piece of evidence: something the documents explain, contradict, or omit. Keep the syntax and narrative distance steady and documentary. The narrator reports, compares, and occasionally admits what cannot be recovered.\n\nDo not require the reconstruction to end in a completed explanation. The narrator can reach a partial understanding: the evidence shows how one capability developed while another remains unexplained. The final passage should leave the reader with the material history of the progression, not a moral summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacaj::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Gothic-Punk\n\nMake the training environment a collision between old authority and present decay. The trainee learns in a place where inherited rules still operate but the infrastructure is failing: a master who teaches a discipline no one else remembers, a practice hall with a broken floor, a resource that must be begged or stolen from an old patron. Let the urban material detail be specific and functional. The cracked tile, the flickering light, the locked archive, the worn grip on the practice weapon are not mood alone; they affect what the trainee can attempt and what it costs.\n\nWrite the progression through concrete attempts. Each training passage shows the trainee trying a movement, a procedure, or a piece of knowledge, failing or partially succeeding, receiving feedback, and adjusting. Give the feedback a definite source from the old authority: the master corrects the movement while demanding deference, withholds the next lesson, or grants access as a favor. Show the trainee's practical response: they perform deference, conceal a motive, or accept a humiliating correction to reach the next lesson. Let the intimacy be morally complicated. The teacher can genuinely help while also controlling, and the trainee can learn while also resenting the debt.\n\nUse Palahniuk's procedural confession in the narration. Repeat a short rule, phrase, or piece of equipment each time the training changes. The repetition should expose the narrator's defensive logic: the narrator can describe the method as necessary while the details show what it cost. Keep the confidence intact. The narrator does not announce the contradiction; the reader sees it in the repeated fragment and the concrete consequences.\n\nLet social masks fall away in trusted company. During a private session, a training partner, patron, or rival can drop the public performance and reveal what the training actually means to them. Connect that disclosure to the next attempt concretely: the trainee tries the same movement while knowing they are being protected, used, tested, or resented, and that knowledge changes how the failure or success feels and what they are willing to pay for it. The atmosphere shifts because the relationship has shifted, not because the lighting changed.\n\nDo not import vampire physiology, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules. The inherited authority is social, institutional, or practical, not supernatural unless the active world already permits it. Do not require the trainee to break free, betray the teacher, or complete a transformation. The progression remains uneven: the trainee can gain one capability while remaining dependent on the system that taught it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacak::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the testimonial and autobiographical route. Write the progression as a first-person account of how the narrator learned a capability under specific social and economic conditions. The narrator names the place, the community, the work, and the obligations that shaped the training. Keep the language and region specific when the story supplies them; do not impose a generic family structure, religion, caste experience, or code-switching pattern. The narrator's testimony is addressed to the reader, not performed for a present audience.\n\nTie each training development to ordinary work and practical interruption. Show the narrator practicing between shifts, borrowing a tool, watching a more experienced worker, repeating a movement until the body learns it, or receiving feedback from someone whose own position depends on the same skill. When a debt, quarrel, illness, or family obligation interrupts the training, make the interruption alter the subsequent attempt: the narrator practices while exhausted, loses access to the tool, returns to the teacher with less to offer, or combines the skill with the work that pays. Show what the narrator could not do before, what they tried, what failed, and what they can now do. Advancement is not a rank; it is a changed ability to complete a task, carry a load, read a text, or survive a demand.\n\nUse Palahniuk's hard rhythms and obsessive particulars in the narrator's testimony. Repeat a short rule, phrase, or piece of equipment each time the training changes. Make the repetition accrue meaning: the same rule means something different when the narrator is hungry, when the teacher is absent, when the debt comes due, and when the skill finally works. Keep the narrator's confidence intact while the account exposes the defensive logic beneath it. The narrator can insist the training was worth the cost while describing a detail that shows what the cost actually was.\n\nDistinguish earlier experience from later knowledge by keeping each scene's understanding local. In the earlier passage, report only what the narrator understood then, including the mistake or misplaced trust. In the later reflection, add what has since become clear, but do not retroactively insert that understanding into the earlier scene. This distinction is part of the testimony's honesty: the narrator may still misunderstand part of what happened.\n\nDo not require the testimony to end in triumph or complete escape. The narrator can finish with a skill that works and a life still constrained by the conditions that taught it. The final passage should leave the reader with the narrator's specific account of what was gained and what remains difficult.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacal::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the diary and reflective miscellany route. Write the progression as a series of dated or otherwise situated entries, remembered scenes, and plain arguments about training. The narrator records a practice session, a failure, a conversation with a teacher, a change in the body, a question that will not resolve. The entries need not form a continuous narrative; they accumulate. Show advancement through the changing particulars by placing an early entry's failed movement beside a later entry's performance of the same movement under harder conditions, and let a reflective passage argue about what the change means.\n\nKeep the narrator's personal language and distance consistent with the actual teller. The narrator can be exuberant, direct, uncertain, or argumentative. Treat omission as an active editorial act: a skipped day, a refused description of a humiliating failure, or a delayed return to an event works by what the narrator does not yet record. When the gap matters, let later entries circle back to the missing material under new pressure, or let the absence remain visible as a subject the narrator keeps avoiding. Do not force quietness or a seasonal inventory; use seasonal or sensory attention only when it matters to the narrator's actual experience.\n\nUse Palahniuk's hard rhythms and obsessive particulars inside the miscellany. Repeat a short rule, phrase, or piece of equipment across entries. Make the repetition accrue meaning: the same rule appears as instruction, then as failure, then as habit, then as something the narrator questions. Keep the narrator's confidence intact while the entries expose the defensive logic beneath it. The narrator can insist the method is sound while a recorded detail shows what it cost.\n\nDistinguish knowing a principle from executing it. Let the narrator write down a teacher's instruction and understand it completely, then fail to perform it for weeks. In a later entry, show the moment the body catches up to the understanding, or show that the understanding was incomplete. Let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly: the narrator may master one movement while another remains difficult, and the miscellany can record both without forcing them into a single arc.\n\nDo not require the miscellany to end in a completed breakthrough. The narrator can stop mid-question, mid-practice, or mid-argument. The final entry should leave the reader with the accumulated weight of the training, not a summary of its meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacam::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social realism, chronicle, and testimony route. Write the progression as a chronicle: the narrator records a training history within a specific community, language, and period. Preserve the relevant social and material conditions. The narrator names who taught whom, who paid for the training, who was excluded, and what work surrounded the practice. Do not add supernatural facts unless the active world already permits them; the marvelous is not this entry's route.\n\nDistinguish personal participation from reported events through explicit narrative markers. Use the narrator's direct presence for practiced movements, attended sessions, and witnessed failures: \"I saw,\" \"I did,\" \"I was there.\" For secondhand material such as a teacher's past achievement, a rival's secret method, or a session the narrator did not attend, use attribution: \"They told me,\" \"It was said,\" \"I was not there.\" Keep that distinction consistent so the reader weighs reported material differently from witnessed material. When the narrator later uses a reported method, show the own attempt as the point where hearsay becomes tested knowledge.\n\nUse Palahniuk's hard rhythms and obsessive particulars in the chronicle. Repeat a short rule, phrase, or piece of equipment each time the training changes. Make the repetition accrue meaning: the same rule appears in a witnessed lesson, a reported story, and the narrator's own failed attempt. Keep the narrator's confidence intact while the chronicle exposes the defensive logic beneath it. The narrator can insist the method is proven while the chronicle shows the proof is partly borrowed from others.\n\nEmbed the training in ordinary work and social life by making the practical conditions govern access and practice. The narrator practices between obligations, learns from a neighbor, trades labor for instruction, or loses access when a patron's situation changes. Show what the narrator could not do before, what they tried, what feedback they received, and what they can now accomplish. Keep the gains uneven: one skill improves while another remains difficult.\n\nDo not require the chronicle to end in a completed transformation or a single moral. The narrator can finish with a skill that works and a community still shaped by the conditions that produced it. The final passage should leave the reader with the narrator's specific account of what was learned, what was reported, and what remains uncertain.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacan::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Light Novel\n\nEnter each training scene cleanly. Open with the immediate situation: the trainee in a specific place, facing a specific task, with a specific limitation. Keep spatial changes easy to follow. When the trainee moves, the reader knows where they are and what they are trying to do. Use short, readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response. A practice attempt, a teacher's correction, a failure, and a reaction can each occupy its own beat without slowing the scene.\n\nMake the progression concrete. Show the trainee attempting a movement, a procedure, or a piece of knowledge, failing or partially succeeding, receiving feedback, and adjusting. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it: the trainee can understand the instruction and still fail the attempt. Across scenes, demonstrate uneven development by returning to a mastered movement under harder conditions while another movement continues to fail. A later scene shows the same task performed or still incomplete in one part while another part works. Do not replace the attempt with a rank-up notification or a numerical summary.\n\nUse Palahniuk's hard rhythms and obsessive particulars in compressed form. Repeat a short rule, phrase, or piece of equipment across scenes. Let the surrounding scene change the rule's meaning: the phrase operates differently as instruction before a failure, as habit during a partial success, and as doubt or justification after the cost becomes visible. Keep the narrator's confidence intact while the details expose the defensive logic beneath it. The narrator can state the method works while describing the cost in the next beat.\n\nGive each attempt a focused reaction beat. After the result, show the narrator's private response in concrete terms: the body's exhaustion, the specific frustration, the calculation of what to try next, the resentment toward a teacher, or the small relief of a partial success. The reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner. Do not force a witty quip or a compulsory emotional confession.\n\nDo not require a closing cliffhanger. The scene can end mid-practice, mid-argument, or mid-reaction. The final beat should leave the reader with the trainee's current capability and the next difficulty, not a manufactured suspense.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacao::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Lyrical Prose\n\nUse the practice itself as the repeated utterance. Choose one concrete attempt, one exact grip, one count, one small joint of the movement where the body habitually falls short, and return to it until the return becomes both the method of progression and the source of the prose's recurrences. Do not thought-tag the repetition or explain that it is important: repeat the body moving, the object resisting, the too-soon release, the breath counted at the wrong moment. Let each return add one altered sensory fact or one newly understood cause, so the listener is hearing the same phrase acquire new meaning rather than hearing a montage of different rules.\n\nBuild the lyric movement from the hard parts. A longer clause may trace the continuous movement when the body finally stops breaking the gesture into pieces; a staccato clause may stop at the exact point of failure. Keep the syntax attached to weight, leverage, speed, heat, pressure, or sound. If a figure appears, let it rise from the immediate movement and extend only as far as the narrator actually learns from it: the same lever now moved like a remembered door, the same failed strike dropped like a word said too early. Return to plain statements of repetition when the work is only work; their flatness lets the shaped sentences signal a genuine change rather than decorate every attempt.\n\nUnderstate the confessional material the way a well-practiced person understates the price of practice. The process can expose a private fear, an old humiliation, a teacher's voice, or a reason the narrator cannot stop; it should do so through the same matter-of-fact concrete details, not through explanation of feeling. Progress remains uneven. Show the hand now steady while the timing still breaks, the count now finished while the fear still arrives before each attempt. End while another attempt is still possible, with the new capability present but the weakness not yet resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacap::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nWrite each stage of practice as a later encounter with the same exact thing: the machine, the stance, the counted sets, the tune the teacher tapped, the notch in the grip where a beginner's hand used to slip. Return to it across stages so that the repetition becomes the timeline. Let the present body meeting once impossible material carry the meaning: the same motion that used to scatter under fatigue now repeats steadily, the same count that used to require the teacher's voice now runs under the breath. The narrator need not say that time has passed; the altered relation to the object says it.\n\nHold the earlier and later versions together in the present attempt. When the narrator performs a movement that once failed, allow the failed version to enter as a remembered physical fact: the younger shoulder, the shorter breath, the place where the old mistake used to happen. Do not resolve the two versions into a lesson. The gain is legible, but so is what the gain made into somebody else's past. A recurring phrase learned long ago can surface during practice and now mean something smaller, more usable, or emptier than it did when the narrator could not yet do the thing it described.\n\nStay procedurally exact. Keep the confession inside the drill: what the narrator needed, who made it possible, what absence or humiliation pushed the practice forward. Do not translate each step into emotion. Plain pleasure at a new strength may remain plain. Remembered love, envy, shame, or patience may live in the same physical routine without requiring the scene to conclude by announcing that everything has been lost. End with the practice still underway, the present capability and the old inexperience existing in the same repeated act.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacaq::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse the framed oral telling as the engine of progression, not as a wrapper around an interior-narration training log. Establish a teller, the people or one listener being addressed, and the practical reason the account is being given: a teacher who is still not satisfied, a craftsperson transferring the method, someone correcting a local story about a learner's rise. Keep the audience present through interruptions, agreements, requests for particulars, or disagreements about how the gesture should be described. The telling's rhythm works like a repeated procedural confession: the teller comes back to the same tool, entry position, repeated failure, or line of instruction until the return itself shows the learner changing.\n\nSelect the route to realism, satire, picaresque construction, experimental memoir, or reflective recollection for the actual supplied setting and people. If the account satirizes a school or a profession, keep the satire operating on the institutions and manners shown, not on regional identity. If it is a memoir-like account, let the teller reveal a personal investment through the selection and shape of details rather than by stopping to explain a hidden motive. Use concrete matter from the world as it is supplied: the weight and temperature of the material, the street or room, the words people use, the cost of tools or instruction, the audience's own craft knowledge.\n\nPreserve the limit of the frame. Whatever the teller did not witness or hear from the learner stays outside the account's direct thought. The teller may report what the learner said, what the teller saw, what another witness claimed, and what remains uncertain. Distinguish those clearly when it matters. End at the conclusion or interruption of the telling, with the final stage already underway but not closed into a completed legend. The progress should be earned as an account others may now retell, not announced as the learner's unquestioned final nature.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacar::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose at most a handful of exact practice details and return to them until the reader can infer the shape of the attempt: the same rail, the same foot, the same first count, the same worn point where the object turns against the hand. Each return should be necessary. When the hold betters, the fit changes, the count finishes, or the correction finally produces a different result, show the changed fact and move on. Do not say the repetition is obsessive; make the return itself obsessive.\n\nSay the rule once, in clean direct clauses, and let later execution show whether knowing it has or has not become capability. A remembered correction belongs only where the present failure calls it up: the teacher saying turn the wrist, the exact torn place from the first attempt, the single inference that the shoulder still resists. The narrator's exposure comes through this concrete matter. What the narrator will not say about the need to keep practicing can remain visible in the return to the same place at the same hour; do not convert it into a paragraph of confession.\n\nDo not trim away causality. The reader can be expected to understand that the narrator has practiced when the same movement returns altered; the reader cannot be expected to understand a change when the necessary intervening fact has been omitted. A precise longer sentence is legitimate when it carries a real sequence, a physical resistance, a counter, or a break in timing. Cast voices remain individual. End at the point where the present attempt has made its new capability or remaining failure legible, leaving the next stage available without describing it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacas::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the advancement in ordinary scene-present tense, not as a condensed report or montage. Open inside one continuous attempt, lesson, practical task, or the interval before or after it, and let the story proceed by scene events. The narrator can perceive, remember, speak, fail, adjust, and try again within the same present duration. Use paragraph breaks to follow turns of attention, not to jump among disconnected stages; if an earlier stage is needed, let a present sensation or a speaker's sentence bring it back into the scene.\n\nAnchor the continuity with one recurring concrete process. The narrator counts under the breath during a conversation, pauses at the same grip before acting, keeps returning to the score, the mileage, the weights, the knot, the joint that still refuses. Each return should be a present action suited to what is happening, not a thought inserted for its own sake. The obsessive quality may become visible in the narrator's attention: a request is answered while the hands continue the pattern, a memory breaks in at the exact point where the movement used to fail. The self-exposure belongs to what the narrator does and notices, not to an editorial paragraph announcing fear, shame, or need.\n\nLet other people remain full scene participants creating practical presence of their own. A teacher may correct, a rival may work nearby, a friend may ask an unrelated question, and the conversation can carry concerns beyond the practice. Do not give the narrator access to uncounted thought. Regular inner experience can sit alongside the process: a realization mid-movement, a judgment after a failure, a wish during a pause. Improvement stays uneven. The present scene may close while the work or the attempt is still underway, without reaching a completed lesson or a convenient emotional summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacat::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the particular relationship the thing the practice reveals. Choose who watches, who corrects, who avoids watching, whose presence changes the count, and let that shape the scene's information. The established premise supplies the private language; do not import roster tropes. If these two people share a joke about the work, repeat the joke at a later stage when it means something new because one of them can now do what the joke used to mock. If their closeness lives in respectful silence, return to that silence and let an interruption of it carry the changed condition.\n\nRender the other person with the same obsessive concrete observation as the drill. Use the exact way they stand, the sound they make when they are practicing or pretending not to care, the sentence they repeat when encouraging, the place where their gaze lands during a failure. Progress becomes legible when the other person's response to the same movement alters: the correction no longer comes where it used to, the rivalry touches a different sore point, the familiar gesture now arrives after a success. Keep this capability gain distinct from the relationship's ordinary emotional changes; both can be present without one causing the other automatically.\n\nAllow the shared scene room without removing uncertainty. Comfort after a failed attempt can unfold slowly, possessiveness can be shown through practical attention rather than announced, discovery can change what the narrator understands while the other person's private experience stays inaccessible. The story's warmth or friction should come from the actual premise. End while the practice and the relationship are both still moving, with the next shared attempt possible but not converted into a proposal, permanent promise, or final confession.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacau::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the progression as an account others will repeat, but keep the retelling rooted in the procedure that made the change possible. Open with a deliberate naming or declaration: who set themselves to the practice, what burden or teacher or wager stood behind it, what the learner could not yet do. Then show the actual process in concrete terms: the count carried too far, the refusal to drop after the shoulder failed, the correction received and later turned into a private instruction. A deed can belong to this chronicle because people have seen or felt its consequences, not because it has been given a flattering ornament.\n\nMake a recurring phrase work like a remembered rule or stage of capability. Use the same count when it becomes a proven measure, the same short sentence the learner repeated until it changed its meaning, the same mistake named each time until the naming marks the moment it no longer occurs. When the phrase returns, let the new application be exact: the old warning now answered, the failing number now finished. Keep remembered skill distinct from the private fact of the one who performed it. Being called strong, reliable, or changed does not mean the practice felt heroic, painless, or final to the person doing it.\n\nLet other voices interrupt and correct the remembered account. A witness may fix a number, a teacher may reject an easy explanation, or the person about whom the story is told may answer an elevated opening with a flat practical detail. The humor or friction should come from the persistence of the account, not from turning ordinary practice into mock warfare. Keep access governed by the active perspective and the teller's actual position. End with the account still able to continue: another stage begun, another naming available, but no final legend imposed before the work is done.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacav::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nSettle on a single minimalist domestic or work routine that proceeds by precise steps, such as making cheese, splitting firewood, or mending nets. The telling adopts the restrained narrative manner of Scandinavian social realism: actions are observed plainly, the inner cost is understated, and daily repetitions show incremental change without announcement.\n\nGive the routine a compact Palahniuk-style refrain that the focal character circles around. The refrain is a practical rule that evolves only as skill changes: *first the whey must rise, then the whey is ladled when warm, then the ladle is tipped slowly enough to leave the curd whole*. The same phrase returns across attempts, repetitions, and failures, its wording tightening or slipping according to the current competence. When the character can reliably perform a step, that portion of the refrain falls away or is replaced by the next difficult particular.\n\nAdvance capability through visible concrete action, not through study or rank. Each attempt is registered through the changed quality of the work and through small, understated shifts in the social environment around it. A neighbour no longer offers to help; a child eats without complaint; the foreman stops checking the tally. Let these external observations, rendered with the tradition's characteristic lightness, signal that the ordinary world has noticed the improvement without making the characters discuss it explicitly. Keep Danish, Norwegian, and Swedish settings and their inherited ways distinct; let the specific kitchen, workshop, or weather belong to the place actually supplied, not to a generic northern symbolism.\n\nPreserve the autobiographical or psychological inwardness allowed by the tradition. The character's private account may revisit the morning's work later in the day, weighing its cost against its result, but the prose remains spare and self-witnessing. The narrator does not announce understanding or confess the entire defensive logic; a concrete detail—the ache in one shoulder, the number of spoiled batches left—carries the meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacaw::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Screenplay\n\nAnchor advancement in a single concrete task that a body can repeat across scenes—a smith sinking a punch, a climber mounting a sheer wall, a novice drawing a difficult sigil. Each attempt is a brief scene, headed by a practical location slug, where the action lines alone chart improvement. The first attempt's action block describes the full fumbling sequence; a later attempt's block describes fewer movements, a steadier object, a different mistake.\n\nAs the task is practiced, let a selective voice-over deliver a Palahniuk-style rhythmic rule that the character rehearses internally. The rule is procedural, not confessional: *the punch seats only when the hammer falls straight, the hammer falls straight only when the elbow locks, the elbow locks only when the breath is held*. The voice-over repeats and compresses as mastery grows, shedding the steps the character no longer needs to articulate. Use it sparingly—when a silent adjustment has personal cost, or when the body's visible success conceals a remaining inner doubt that the rule still addresses.\n\nTest improvement through the scene's visible procedure rather than exposition. The master does not pronounce the apprentice ready; instead the next scene's action line shows the apprentice striking true without the assistant readying the piece. Offscreen sound can mark a failure—metal hitting dirt, a rope snapping—but its consequence must be shown, not merely reported. A cut to a new scene, with hours or days elapsed, is earned by the character stating what will be corrected next.\n\nKeep nonspoken interiority in a clearly attributed note separate from the action and dialogue, not as dialogue the cast voices. If the active contract requires visible interiority, let a single defined narrator's additional voice-over line carry it. The other characters do not access the focal character's private rule, and a visual cue—a steadier stance—may be observed without revealing the private rhythm that produced it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacax::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Stage Play\n\nLet progression be played through a single, repeatable physical task a performer can execute and vary: filling a narrow-necked vessel from a bucket without a funnel, stacking unbalanced stones, walking a line drawn on the stage floor. Stage each attempt as a short, distinct sequence of actions. The stage directions are the ledger of improvement: an attempt that began with three pauses and a spill is followed by one with a single pause and a tremor in the forearm. The difference is a performed fact, not a line of dialogue.\n\nGive the task a rule that the character states aloud as shoptalk or instruction. The rule's Palahniuk rhythm turns the speech into a procedural litany: *the lip first, the lip then the pour, the pour counted to four, the count finished before the vessel pulls away*. Repeat the rule in full when it is new, abbreviated when it is partly mastered, and corrected by another character or by the performer's own blunder. The rule can be taught to an apprentice, challenged by a rival, or muttered under an inspector's silence.\n\nUse entrances and objects as the only test of advancement. An observer enters with a heavier vessel; a supervisor sets a second bucket where the performer must not step; the next session begins without the assistant who previously steadied the frame. The audience reads improvement in whether the worker handles the changed prop or presence, not in an announcement. The stage is the causal field; the handled object, the spill avoided, the breath released—these are the score.\n\nWhen inward experience is required, place it in the nonspoken authoring material, not in a performable aside delivered to the house. If an aside or monologue is separately authorized for dramatic purpose, let it be a teaching lecture or a confession spoken in the presence of another character. Do not let a soliloquy double as the character's private mind. The cast's knowledge is limited to what is spoken, done, and observable in the shared stage space.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacay::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Stream of Consciousness\n\nCenter the mind's associative movement on a single repeated physical skill. The stream returns to the skill's sensations—the temperature of the water, the grain of the wood, the muscle that quits first—not to ruminate, but because the mind is still mapping the act. Each return is a new draft of the internal procedure, braiding present sensation with a sudden memory of an instructor's warning or the image of a past failure.\n\nLet a Palahniuk-style procedural rule provide the stream's returning skeleton. The rule is a string of fragments the mind cannot stop assembling: *first the knife, then the knife checked against the light, then the knife checked against the light with the thumb on the bevel, the thumb on the bevel and the index finger below*. At the start of the progression, the rule arrives in heavy, intrusive syntax that disorients other thoughts. As the skill beds in, the fragment compresses—*thumb-bevel-light*—and surfaces only as a brief, incidental pulse the mind notes and releases. The measure of improvement is what the stream can now skip.\n\nThe stream registers changed capability by shifting its attention within the same memory. A recalled warning is no longer frightening; it is assessed for its current usefulness. A once-painful error is re-entered not as shame but as a data point, now catalogued between the wrist angle and the ambient noise. A new difficulty, by contrast, accretes sensory detail and disrupts the syntax until it is similarly mastered.\n\nAll association belongs to the authorized focal mind alone. Another character's presumed thought appears only as overheard speech, a remembered letter, or a physical gesture the focal mind interprets. When the narration exposes a concealed motive or private cost, it does so through this single mind's sudden recognition, not an omniscient aside. The world's objects and events keep their established nature; the stream distorts perception only where the character's state would credibly cause distortion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacaz::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nLet the progression be legible in the changing text of a single recurring instruction. A master sends the same practical rule each week, or a student messages herself the steps she must remember. The first transmission of the rule is hesitant and wrongly ordered. A later transmission, sent after practice, arranges the steps correctly. A later one still compresses them into a compact shorthand. The transcript alone, read in sequence, is the record of improvement.\n\nShape the exchanged rule as a Palahniuk fragment that mutates with ability: *first the pan is oiled cold, then the pan is oiled cold and heated until the oil moves, then the oil is heated until it shimmers and the pan is tilted to coat the rim*. The fragment can be interrupted mid-composition by a clarifying question, edited after a visible receipt time, or sent prematurely and corrected. Each variation is a datum about where the practice currently stands. Do not narrate progress; let the difference between the first fragment and the last one transmit it.\n\nUse an unsent draft as the character's private procedural rehearsal, held strictly outside the visible transcript. The draft is a composed artifact the character types, re-reads, and discards or leaves pending. It contains the rule in a more personal, less guarded form—the version with the self-critique still attached. Place it in clearly separated context, never as a message another sender can answer. The unsent draft shows knowledge that has not yet become reliable execution.\n\nLet feedback operate within the transcript's mechanics. A second sender corrects a term, asks for a missing measurement, or quotes the original rule back with underlines. The student's improving competence may appear as a reduced delay between the master's prompt and the student's reply, or as the student sending the correction before the master types it. When the rule is fully internalized, the thread may go quiet, or the student's message may be a single word that replaces the entire sequence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacba::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Visual Novel\n\nReturn the reader to the same illustrated scene each time the skill is practiced: the same training room, the same workbench by the window, the same sharpening stone on the same stump. The background and object placement have shifted between visits—the window is now shuttered, a new tool hangs on the wall, the stump's surface is freshly scarred—and the change cues that practice has been accumulating. The focal narration notes the differences without editorializing; the scene has progressed, and the reader infers the labor.\n\nAnchor the focal narrator's inner response to each scene in a Palahniuk-style procedural rule that runs through the core of the practice. The rule is a repeating, evolving fragment: *first the blade is dry-stropped, then the blade is stropped with the paste, then the paste is wiped clean before the next pass*. The rule mutates across visits, compressing as steps become automatic or expanding when the narrator discovers a new finer stage. The chronicle of improvement is the difference between the rule the narrator recites upon entering the scene the first time and the rule she recites now.\n\nLet spoken dialogue and character reactions register external change. The same teacher's line—'Again.'—has a different weight when the narrator's rule no longer includes the step the teacher is correcting. The teacher's expression cue shifts from a tight jaw to a raised eyebrow; another character's entrance is timed to the moment the narrator finishes, not the moment she begins. These are illustrated facts, not a points tally. The narrator may observe that she heard the instruction differently, but the truth of the improvement is in the restaging.\n\nPrivate thought in the focal narration is inaccessible to other characters. The narrator's evolving rule, her memory of the last visit's failure, and her assessment of her own tremor are internal. A companion may comment on a steadier hand, but not on the inner phrase that steadied it. Keep the visual novel style free of branching menus, romance flags, or executable commands; progress is a closed narrative arc rendered through the rhythm of return, narration, and the changing illustrated room.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalacbb::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Web Serial\n\nBuild each installment around a single practical routine that continues from the previous reply. Open by recovering only the necessary prior ledge: cite the exact state of the procedural rule the character has been internalizing, and the last concrete result or failure. *Last time she could keep the basin on her head for thirty paces; the rule was 'basin on the crown, basin on the crown with the knees soft, basin on the crown with the knees soft and the breath every four steps.' This time the basin is heavier.*\n\nLet the present installment execute the routine again, not merely describe the improvement. The character performs the task—the water-fetch, the sprint, the silence held—and the narration shows the partial collapse of the old rule and the birth of the new one. The Palahniuk refrain is the installment's spine: it is stated in early attempts, compressed when steps are automated, corrected when a new weight or surface breaks it. *'Knees soft' became 'knees,' then 'the basin is on the crown and the walking is the walking,' and now the heavier basin requires the breath on every step, and 'every' is the word she cannot get past.*\n\nTrack progress through the refrain's mutation and the changing nature of the difficulty. When a step vanishes from the rule, a new obstacle—a longer path, a silent observer, a need to speak while carrying—enters the practice and must be accounted for in the next refrain. The serial's through-line is the personal, hard-rhythm process of expanding what the rule must encompass.\n\nOrdinary life, work, and relationships occur alongside the practice and may supply the feedback or resources that force the next adaptation. An NPC may point out a wasted movement, bring a different tool, or simply stop needing the character's help, which is itself a measure of the character's new competence. End the installment when the routine has been executed and the rule stands in a new, altered state. The hook is the unexecuted next condition: a heavier basin promised for tomorrow, a rule missing a step the character hasn't yet solved, the next piece of work waiting in the quiet.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladaa::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nLet every display of new capability occur inside, or against, a relationship the practitioner is avoiding, refusing, or failing to repair. When a technique is attempted, show what the attempt costs that relationship right now: the practitioner can hold the stance longer but still cannot answer the teacher's question about the previous night; the grip is finally correct while the companion who corrected it has stopped speaking to them. Do not treat the relationship as background. Let the strain change the present scene's speech and subsequent behavior.\n\nRecord the gain through concrete bodily facts. Use McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs to show the changed capability as a change in the body and the material world: the hand no longer shakes, the blade turns without binding, the load clears the threshold that once turned them back. Then show the emotional fact that persists beside the gain. A mastered breathing exercise can be performed in a room where someone else's absence is still occupying the chair. The prose should keep both facts in the same frame without translating either into a general statement about suffering.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, let speech be spare and practical, while the strain moves through what the speakers choose and refuse. A teacher can correct a stance and not mention the student's shaking. A companion can ask whether the practitioner slept and receive an answer about the weather. A bargain can be offered as a way to avoid an apology. The conversation should show the cost of admitting, needing, withholding, or returning: a question dropped, a reply arriving too late, a lesson repeated with more force than the error required. Comfort may be partial or awkward, and warmth can coexist with pain without resolving it.\n\nMake advancement uneven, and let each change in capability disclose what remains impossible. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it under pressure, and let that pressure be emotional as well as technical. Show the practitioner win a match and fail to answer a letter, or reach a new understanding and still misread the person whose help made it possible. A breakthrough can make an old failure more legible rather than less painful. Do not require a confession, a reconciliation, or an emotional repair. Require only that the next attempt changes what the practitioner can bear, refuse, or admit.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladab::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the interval as a live exchange in which the practitioner's next attempt is visible to the other participant. Give every speaker unmistakable attribution and keep paragraphs brief enough to scan. Each paragraph should move one concrete thing forward: a sound, a movement, a spoken correction, a changed grip. Do not summarize the practice. Put the attempt in the action and let the partner's response land against it.\n\nShape the shared scene around a short cycle: the practitioner tries something, the other participant responds through practical speech or action, and the practitioner's next attempt shows whether that response changed anything. A teacher may say, \"Again.\" A companion may say, \"You were close.\" A rival may say nothing and adjust the weight. Let the next movement demonstrate the effect, whether it is a steadier hand, a repeated error, or a new failure under the partner's attention. Do not explain the lesson in narration when the next attempt can show it.\n\nKeep McCarthy's material attention on the body and its surroundings. Use stone, rope, blade, water, dust, boots, breath as the facts that carry the progression. These need not imply an arid world or fatalism; a kitchen, riverbank, training yard, or workshop can hold the same attention. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, let speech remain spare while it carries bargains, convictions, and withheld motives. A partner can correct a stance and say nothing about what they actually saw, or offer help while keeping a private condition.\n\nLet the partner remain an active participant, not a tutorial function. They can cooperate, correct, resist, help, or choose to continue their own practical life. Their actions should count because the practitioner must respond to them. Keep the active player's control and the authoring contract's perspective intact. The reply can end while the attempt is still underway, or just after a response has landed; no question, cliffhanger, or forced choice is required. Leave the next answer a direct continuation of the current movement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladac::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the classical or vernacular episodic telling route. Divide the progression into concrete incidents, each centered on a task or exchange that the practitioner enters with a definite burden: a load to carry, a stance to hold, a bargain to fulfill. Do not summarize training. Let each episode show the attempt, the correction or resistance that meets it, and the result the practitioner carries into the next episode.\n\nMake advancement legible by repeating a material test across episodes. The same kind of weight, the same gate, the same difficult stroke returns under altered conditions. The changed outcome shows the gain: the load travels farther, the gate opens sooner, the stroke lands where it missed before. Let the test also expose a new weakness. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it; an episode may begin with understanding and end in failure under pressure, and the next may reverse that result. Allow strengths and weaknesses to develop unevenly rather than in a straight climb.\n\nGive each episode a strong concrete center in McCarthy's register. Use the weight of the load, the edge of the tool, the texture of the path, the sound of water or wind or breath. Let landscape and labor stay present without becoming decorative. Occupy the setting the story actually permits; the episodic route does not require a particular period or cast. An episode can be brief, ending after a single exchange, or extended through a journey and return. Link clauses paratactically where the telling needs momentum, and allow accumulation when a longer labor or a difficult lesson requires it.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, make speech spare and practical. A teacher can correct a stance in a few words. A companion can bargain over resources. A rival can state a condition and leave. These exchanges should carry convictions and withheld motives, but they should not explain the lesson. Let the practitioner's next attempt reveal what has been understood. End each episode when the changed capability is legible to the reader, without requiring a cliffhanger or a completed test.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladad::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nShow advancement through the body and the tools under strain. When the practitioner learns something, let the next attempt demonstrate it in the material world: the grip no longer slips, the blade holds its edge, the shoulders carry the load that once forced a rest. When they fail, show the failure in the same system: the stone turns, the rope burns, the tool drops. The contrast between attempts is the progression; do not announce it.\n\nCompress the transitions around the work. Let one clause strike against the next so that failure and correction are adjacent rather than explained: the foot went wrong, the master cursed, the foot went again. Cut connective tissue when the scene needs pressure. Keep a longer thought when the practitioner's reasoning requires it, but ground that thought in the concrete damage or opportunity at hand. The reader should feel the work through the selection of nouns and verbs, not through a summary of effort.\n\nLet abrasive or rough speech belong to a specific speaker and a specific motive. A teacher may curse the footwork. A rival may call the new technique worthless and then watch it closely. A companion may say only, \"You're bleeding.\" Do not make every speaker harsh. Practical speech can also carry a bargain, a conviction, or a withheld reason. The grit belongs to the scene's physical pressure, not to a compulsory tone of insult.\n\nKeep knowing and executing separate. The practitioner may understand a technique completely and still fail it when the ground is wet or the hand is tired. Show that gap in the material result, then show the next attempt under different conditions. Let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly: speed gained while patience frayed, power gained while precision worsened. Use rhythm to make that unevenness legible. A compressed run of failed attempts can be followed by a slower passage in which the practitioner examines the damage and decides what to try next. Do not turn every scene into survival horror; the pressure comes from the actual stakes of the practice, the work, and the world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladae::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the progression through documents with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Let each record preserve only what its author could know or would choose to disclose in that situation. A training log can record an attempt and its result. A letter to a teacher can ask for correction. A report to a patron can describe what the practitioner can now accomplish. A note left for a companion can admit what the practitioner still cannot do. Use the specific document required by the scene, not a rotating set of formats.\n\nMake the documentary boundary part of the progression. The reader should be able to compare entries and see the gap between a reported success and the condition the writer does not state. A student may describe the failed attempt but omit the reason for the shaking hand. A patron's report may list resources spent but not the practitioner's doubt. A journal entry may record a private realization that no other character receives. A later document can revise an earlier claim or expose what the first writer concealed. Let gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts reveal events across entries without adding an omniscient explanation after every record.\n\nUse McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs inside the documents. Let the letter record the weight of the stone, the condition of the rope, the sound of the river, the state of the road. Keep the language practical and spare. A document can carry a bargain: \"Send the salt and I will stay another month.\" It can carry a conviction: \"I will not teach him again.\" It can carry a withheld motive by what it does not say.\n\nMake improvement matter through the sequence itself. One entry records that the practitioner understood the instruction. A later entry records the first execution under a known condition. A still later entry records failure under changed circumstances. Let the documents show uneven development: a strength in one area, a weakness in another, a resource gained, a resource lost. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it by using the written record's limited viewpoint. Keep continuity through dates, places, names, and repeated concrete details rather than through an explanatory bridge between records.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladaf::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nMake care a resource inside the progression. When the practitioner improves, show what specific support entered the work: the companion kept the fire going through the night, the teacher saved the better tool, the friend remembered which food the practitioner could keep down after a hard session. Then show the care in the next attempt as something the body can use: steadier hands after food, a repaired rope, a place left open when the practitioner returns. The warmth should not hover around the practice; it should pass through the practice and change what the practitioner can attempt.\n\nKeep the care concrete and specific rather than announced. Use McCarthy's attention to physical detail: the blanket folded at the foot of the bed, the cup set within reach, the door left open for the draft. Let affection be visible in action and object. A person can help without saying why, correct a stance and then hand over a dry cloth, or sit nearby and say nothing. Sincere care does not require a confession or an emotional repair. Keep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact; comfort does not make everyone agreeable or remove established problems from the world.\n\nLet the progression still be earned. The practitioner works, fails, and learns. A failed attempt can be met with a practical suggestion rather than a rebuke. A success can be noticed by someone who knows how hard it was. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it, and show uneven development: the practitioner may hold the new form but lose it when exhausted, or gain a skill while still unable to ask for help. The support makes the work possible; it does not replace the work.\n\nUse spare, practical speech to carry warmth without sentiment. A companion may say, \"Eat first.\" A teacher may say, \"You had it that time.\" A friend may say, \"I kept your place.\" These exchanges can carry bargains, convictions, and withheld motives. Do not make every character speak in endearments. Let small pleasures and ease occupy real narrative attention: a shared meal, a repaired tool, a warm room, a familiar route home. The story remains progression fantasy because the comfort sustains the developing life rather than stopping it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladag::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Formal Literary Novel\n\nOpen a progression scene through the practitioner's place in a social world: the room, the road, the season, the people who depend on the work, the obligation that brought them to this practice. Let that framing be concrete rather than abstract. Then move from the social frame into the specific attempt, its feedback, and its result. Close the sustained paragraph or scene by returning to the wider context and showing what the changed capability now means for the practitioner's standing, duties, or future.\n\nMake the social consequence of advancement part of the progression. A new skill may change how the household assigns work; a technique may have been taught only to a different class of student; an advance may arrive when the community can ill afford another mouth to feed. Do not let the social frame replace the actual practice. The reader should still see the attempt and its result, and the result should alter the social situation in a definite way. Let the narrator judge or interpret only where the chosen narration permits.\n\nGround the formal structure in McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs. Use the weight of the load, the condition of the ground, the sound of the river, the state of the tools. Let paratactic movement accumulate where the telling needs it: a series of linked clauses can carry the practitioner through a long labor or a difficult lesson. Allow both brevity and extended accumulation, but do not let the composed narration dissolve the physical work into commentary.\n\nKeep knowing and executing separate. The narrator can record the moment of understanding and then the moment of failure under pressure. Let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly: speed may improve while patience does not, or a new resource may solve one problem and create another. Use the paragraph's return to the wider world to make that unevenness socially legible: the household notices the new speed but also the new temper, or the patron values the resource while ignoring the problem it created. Maintain a clear difference between composed narration and spare practical dialogue. The formal architecture belongs to the narration; the characters may still speak in short bargains, convictions, and withheld motives.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladah::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen in the outer scene: who tells, who listens, where they are, and why this account is being given now. Give the occasion a practical stake — the listeners need the knowledge in it, fear it, or face a decision the telling bears on — and establish that stake through the telling's first words and the listeners' first reactions rather than through announcement. The craft source supplies structure, not scenery: no djinn, palace, or desert belongs in the frame unless the story's world already has them.\n\nLet the embedded tale be the record of how a capability was acquired. Keep inside it a real sequence of attempts: practice, misreading, correction, a resource gained or spent, a failure that left a mark. Render each stage as physical labor with concrete nouns and forceful verbs — the tool in the hand, the weight carried, the ground underfoot, the repetition that changed something in the body or the understanding. Knowing a principle and executing it are different events; mark the interval between them with the particular failures the account contains, and let strengths and weaknesses advance unevenly, one skill hardening while another stays unreliable.\n\nKeep the levels intelligible. If the tale itself contains a told account — a teacher repeating what a teacher once said — the reader should always know whose voice holds the floor. Let repeated phrases, tools, scars, or instructions return across levels with changed weight: an instruction first heard as correction can come back in the outer scene as a test, a warning, or an inheritance being handed over. Handle delay honestly: what the teller saves or withholds should be saved for a reason the teller has — courtesy, shame, strategy, or the limits of what they understood at the time.\n\nMake the listeners participants. They can interrupt, doubt, supply what they know, or ask about timing and sequence, and their questions can expose what the account leaves out. Around the told words, the teller's present experience stays in play: a hesitation before a disputed passage, attention on one listener, a private judgment of how the account is landing. Speech in the frame can carry bargains and withheld motives, and the narration may know more than the teller says aloud so long as that access follows the story's actual teller. Keep what the teller knew then separate from what the teller knows now; later understanding arrives as later understanding. End where the scene's live boundary falls — mid-account, or as the outer situation shifts — without requiring a cliffhanger, a completed lesson, or a settled question.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladai::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite progression as a confrontation between records and memory, in the documentary and historical-memory register. The practitioner's past survives in documents — a logbook or tally, a training mark on a wall, a ledger of materials, an official note, a dated entry — and each record states what happened in its own reduced way: attempts counted, marks awarded, goods spent, days absent. What the record omits is where the story lives: the hesitation before an attempt, the pain after it, the instruction understood in words long before the body could perform it. Bring record and memory together without smoothing their contradictions. A ledger says the twelfth attempt succeeded; the memory of the twelfth attempt says what it cost.\n\nGround the present scene in an occasion for the comparison: the practitioner rereads the record, must produce it, stands at the wall where the marks are, or attempts before a witness something the record claims they can do. Render practice, remembered and present, with concrete nouns and forceful verbs — the grip, the load, the repetition, the moment a movement changes — and give remembered labor the same material weight as present action. Whether the practitioner narrates their own past or a narrator stands outside them, keep that access consistent throughout. Uneven development shows in the gap between documents: one skill certified or marked while another, unrecorded, still fails in the hand.\n\nLet the conditions of learning belong inside the account: weather, injury, access to materials and teachers, ordinary work that consumed the hours practice needed. These shaped what could be attempted and when, and the records' silences often mark them. Move paratactically where accumulation serves — set detail against detail without explaining its meaning at once — and allow plain short sentences where a record or a memory states its fact. The register is a method of attention, not a national mannerism: no translated word order, obligatory bureaucracy, or severity for its own sake.\n\nWhen record and memory disagree, hold the disagreement open. Let the present attempt test which account holds, and let the practitioner's private judgment of the discrepancy — shame, pride, suspicion of whoever kept the record, distrust of their own memory — exist as lived experience rather than summary. The telling may be retrospective and composed without becoming a clinical survey; it is one person's accounted life, with what that person noticed, endured, and now understands. End at the live boundary of the present occasion without forcing the sources to reconcile or the attempt to conclude.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladaj::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Gothic-Punk\n\nMake each stage of improvement a negotiation with arrangements that predate the practitioner: a patron's claim, a family debt, a guild's or house's old rule, a lease on a decaying building, an inheritance of tools and obligations together. These arrangements decide what can be practiced, when, under whose observation, and at what cost. Write them as material facts with a history — the worn threshold, the grandfathered key, the clause that still binds — not as mood, and not every authority as a villain: an old claim can protect as well as confine, and a teacher inside the arrangement may be generous, exacting, or both.\n\nRender practice as urban physical work with concrete nouns and forceful verbs: the room where the work happens, the condition of the tools, the sound of the street or the pipes, the price of materials, the toll of repetition on the body. Knowing an instruction and executing it under observation are different feats; let the practitioner fail specifically — a skill that holds in private falters before the patron, or a strength gained in one context exposes a weakness in another. Uneven development is legible in these failures, not announced alongside them.\n\nLet masks do real work in the learning. The practitioner may conceal an ambition, an attachment, or a fear from those who control instruction, and speech can carry bargains, flattery, and withheld conviction. Concealment has a price in feedback: a teacher correcting a performance corrects the performance. In trusted company a mask can fall, and what the practice actually costs — the fear, the want, the exhaustion — can be said or simply shown. Let that intimacy affect the progression where it would: honesty may open a correction previously withheld, and concealment may delay one. Do not make every intimacy a betrayal or every confession a collapse; trust, too, changes what can be learned.\n\nLet atmosphere operate through the encounter rather than around it. A decaying room is experienced in what it does to attention and practice — the cold that stiffens the hands, the light that limits the hour, the history a space carries for the people standing in it. End at the scene's live boundary: an old claim need not be resolved, a test need not be passed or failed, and the practitioner may remain mid-attempt, mid-conversation, or mid-decision.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladak::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nTell the progression as a lineage: a method passing from hand to hand, each telling an occasion on which the lineage is continued, disputed, or spent. Situate the teller concretely — where and for whom they speak, and what the account is being asked to settle: a student's claim to instruction, a community's judgment of what a predecessor's attempt cost, a practitioner's account of the teacher who formed them. Keep language, region, community, and period exactly as the story supplies them, and add nothing generic in their place — no forced English, code-switching, devotional frame, caste experience, or default family shape for people the scene has not so defined.\n\nLet each embedded account carry one stage of the learning: the first attempt and its correction, a resource gained or lost, a misunderstanding that practice exposed, the moment an instruction became performable. Render the stages as physical fact with concrete nouns and forceful verbs — hands at work, the weight and give of materials, the condition of the room or ground, the repetition that precedes change. Knowing and executing stay distinct: an instruction can be recited perfectly years before it can be done, and the account should show that interval in particular failures. Let the unevenness be specific — what improved, what did not, what the lineage itself never taught well.\n\nTreat the lineage as imperfectly transmitted. A predecessor's method survives in fragments: partially remembered, differently told by two who learned it, adapted to conditions its originator never faced. Repeated tools, phrases, and corrections can pass from account to account, accruing changed meaning — a warning first, later a boast, later an epitaph. The listeners hold part of this: they may correct the teller, offer the version they heard, or test the account against what they have seen done. Their part in the exchange reveals the present state of the practice, not merely its past.\n\nAround the told account, the teller's own present experience remains in play — the hesitation at a disputed point, the attention paid to a particular listener, the private stake in one version being believed. Preserve the interval between what the teller knew then and knows now; later understanding is later. End at the live boundary of the occasion, without requiring the lineage reconciled, the lesson completed, or the community's judgment delivered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladal::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nKeep the progression as a diary or occasioned miscellany: entries set down by date, season, or event, each recording what was attempted that day and what resulted. Let an entry hold the material fact of practice in concrete nouns and forceful verbs — the tool, the weather, the weight, the repetitions counted or abandoned, the failure that left a mark on the hand or the day. Alongside the fact, let the entry hold the writer's actual condition after practice: soreness, distraction, discouragement, the peculiar attention of a good day. Season and weather enter when the writer would notice them, not as required dressing, and the voice is not held to a uniform quiet. The record is private writing, and what it contains is what its writer actually noticed and felt, not a public summary.\n\nLet the gap between understanding and execution carry across entries. An instruction is recorded as understood on one day and still fails in the hand weeks later; a later entry returns to it as it narrows or stalls. Uneven development shows through juxtaposition — one movement improving between two dated attempts while another, given equal practice, does not — rather than through verdicts. The miscellany form allows the writer to set unlike things side by side: a practice note, an observation of weather or another person, a remark overheard, a plain argument with oneself. These can touch the learning without being arranged into a lesson.\n\nRereading is an event in this form. An earlier entry can be returned to and understood differently in light of a later attempt; a teacher's remark, recorded and at the time opaque, can become intelligible pages on. Preserve that order: the entry written before the insight does not contain it. Omission is available and meaningful — a day skipped, a failure not written down, a name avoided — where that is true to the writer, and direct feeling, plain statement, or restraint all belong to the particular hand keeping the record.\n\nKeep sentence movement paratactic where accumulation serves the entry and brief where the day was brief. End at the live boundary of the entry or the rereading — mid-observation is permissible — without requiring a breakthrough recorded, a reflection concluded, or every difficulty resolved by the last line.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladam::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the progression as testimony: a participant's account of learning, addressed to someone — a listener waiting in the room, an official taking it down, a reader years later, a record being kept for a reason the account itself can make felt. Establish the address early, because it shapes the telling: what the teller admits, defends, lingers on, and passes over quickly belongs partly to who is listening and why the account is being given now. Preserve the language, community, and history the story supplies, and add no supernatural facts this route does not carry; the marvelous has no place here unless the world already permits it.\n\nHold the line between what the teller lived and what they only heard. The lived part — the ground, the tools, the weight, the repetition, the injury, the day a movement changed — carries one authority; what others said and did is reported as report, attributed and marked as heard, not absorbed into personal memory. Render the lived part with concrete nouns and forceful verbs, and let parataxis do the accumulating: set detail against detail and let the record build without hurrying to explain it.\n\nLet the social and material conditions of learning stand inside the account rather than around it. Access to a teacher or the lack of one, the cost of materials, the ordinary work that claimed the hours, the pressures and expectations of the community — these decided what could be practiced, when, and by whom, and the testimony should show them operating on particular days rather than state them as circumstances. Where the account moves from what was known then to what is understood now, keep the two separate; later knowledge does not retroactively become the motive that was operating at the time.\n\nThe telling may be plain and direct without becoming rhetoric or a case summary; it is one person's accounted experience, with its loyalties and its limits. End at the live boundary of the telling or the scene that occasions it — without requiring a final judgment, a completed test, or every reported difficulty resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladan::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Light Novel\n\nEnter clean: within the first lines establish where the practitioner is, what they are attempting, and what stands in the way. Keep every spatial change easy to follow — mark movement, position, and the condition of surroundings with concrete nouns and forceful verbs, so the reader can always stage the scene: the distance to the target, the state of the floor, who is watching from where.\n\nBreak paragraphs for readability around action, dialogue, and private response, so each attempt and its result stand distinct. Stage practice as physical event: the grip, the weight, the misstep, the correction from a teacher or rival, the moment the body or mind begins to do what it previously could not. Knowing an instruction and executing it remain separate events — the practitioner can recite the principle perfectly and still fail the attempt — and uneven development shows in specific failures: one skill improving while another stays unreliable, a gain in practice that does not yet hold under observation or pressure.\n\nWhen an unfamiliar name, rank, term, or mechanic first matters, give it clear presentation in a line or two — through use, consequence, or brief statement — and move on; do not stop the scene for a glossary. Established ranks and systems can support the account where the story has them, but do not invent numerical machinery for display; capability is shown by what the practitioner can now do and what remains difficult.\n\nKeep reaction beats focused and spare. A reaction — serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner — arises from the concrete result of the attempt, not from a general mood, and may be a single observed detail or a short accumulation. Private response is part of the staging: what the practitioner notices, fears, wants, and concludes sits between the action and the next line of dialogue, in the same fast-readable register. Paratactic movement carries the scene forward, with room for brief extended accumulation when a moment deserves it. End at the live boundary — mid-attempt, mid-exchange, or mid-realization — without requiring a passed test, a cliffhanger, or a resolved breakthrough.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladao::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite advancement as changed execution, observed through the objects and motions that make the new capability real. A principle recited is not yet the skill: give the attempt, the failed pass, the correction taken, and the moment when the hands or the breath or the attention find what they could not find before. Let the gain arrive unevenly—the stance read correctly while the strike is mistimed, the formula held in memory while the material resists it—and make clear which part came and which did not.\n\nCarry the sequence in paratactic movement, one action set beside another so that the difference between them shows the gain: the rope that held, the cut that did not wander, the marks read twice and then arranging themselves into sense. Keep the nouns concrete and the verbs forceful. Weight, distance, weather, and wear belong to the attempt, because the landscape and the labor are the field where capability is tested rather than scenery behind it. Render no numerical interface; the evidence of progress is what the person can now do and what still fails, and a teacher's correction, a rival's example, or the body's own memory can supply the feedback without any level being announced.\n\nLet the lyric rise out of the focal character's attention rather than out of a narrator's ornament. A recurring sound or image may return once practice has changed its meaning—the water that once measured fatigue later keeping the rhythm of a stroke performed correctly—and a comparison may extend itself only while it alters what the character understands or makes the new competence present to the senses. Interleave plain statements where the work is plain, so the elevated passage has something to measure itself against. Stop where the present attempt stops: succeeded, broken, or with the next difficulty standing visible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladap::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nHold each new capability beside the earlier self who could not perform it: the same hands, the same room, the same task, with the person who failed at it no longer quite present. Make the improvement real through what is now possible—the distance crossed, the pattern recognized, the work that no longer requires thought—while the reflective register measures what it cost to arrive. The gain and the passage of time are one event seen twice.\n\nMark advancement with small irreversible changes. A tool worn smooth by practice, a callus, a route known without looking, a teacher's phrase understood too late to be answered: each carries what was gained and what went with it. Ordinary pleasure in a new skill may be genuine even when longing or loss travels beside it, and no scene is obliged to close by announcing impermanence.\n\nWrite the reflection in spare linked clauses, and let a concrete object carry the association instead of explaining it—the abandoned practice ground, the borrowed coat, the mark left on the wall by the first failed attempt. Keep dialogue practical and restrained even where it touches what is no longer available; a teacher or companion may speak of the work itself while the focal character's private attention moves between the present attempt and a remembered occasion. Give that present attempt its full experience rather than letting memory crowd it out.\n\nKeep the development uneven and unfinished. One capability may be gained while another still fails, and the failure may recall an earlier failure without erasing the present gain. Neither the improvement nor the attachment needs to be hollow or resolved; leave the next difficulty and the next memory both available when the attempt or the recollection ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladaq::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell the story as a framed oral account: a situated teller relates the learner's development to listeners who have their own stake in it. Open with the occasion of the telling, let the frame return between episodes, and keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective—what the teller witnessed, what was heard from another, what combines both—so the narration marks the difference between the seen and the reported.\n\nInside the told account, make advancement matter through concrete attempts and changed execution in stark material terms: the weight of the thing carried, the number of failed passes, the correction given and taken or ignored, the day the hands finally kept the rhythm. The frame does not reduce the progression to a moral or an announcement of rank. Listeners may interrupt with a question, a doubt, or a request for a particular detail, and the interruption can change what the teller chooses to relate next, so the telling remains an event with a present of its own.\n\nLet the oral presentation shape recurrence and emphasis. A phrase may return when a later attempt gives it new meaning; a compact declaration may precede the particulars that complicate it. Let the setting, language, and community actually supplied govern the frame's customs—forms of address, the occasion for gathering, what may be asked of a teller—rather than a generic antiquity, and keep the one chosen telling tradition coherent instead of assembling conventions from everywhere. Relate the unevenness plainly: a gain in one capability, a persistent failure in another, valued differently by different listeners. The frame need not resolve its questions; close where the telling pauses or the listeners turn back to their own concerns, more of the account still untold.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladar::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose the few details that carry the change in capability and let them do the work: the blade no longer biting the thumb, the knot holding on the first pull, the footing found without looking. Set one attempt beside another and let the changed result imply the gain. Explain only where the explanation materially serves the present account, and let an essential thought run long enough to make sense.\n\nPreserve the causal chain of practice and feedback in direct clauses. A failed attempt, a correction, a later success can be given without commentary; omit intermediate steps so that they remain inferable rather than so that the fact of practice disappears. Where a principle is known but not yet executed, show the gap in one concrete moment—the character names the correct move and still misses it—and let the knowledge and the failure both stand.\n\nHandle instruction and assessment in spare exchanges. A teacher says one thing, the character answers with a practical concern, and the next attempt shows whether the words took; dialogue does not become a lecture, and each voice keeps its own shape. Let the repeated motion, the material's resistance, and the body's adjustment carry the progression, with uneven development held in a single exact detail: one capability settled while another still fails. A precise extended sentence serves where it carries essential information; performed fragments do not. End at the edge of the present attempt, the gain settled in the work itself or the next difficulty left visible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladas::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nRun the developing capability as a sustained scene thread. Open inside practice, instruction, or an attempt, and let the scene move naturally among the physical work, the spoken exchange, the focal character's perception, and the private reasoning that connects an error to a correction. Use paragraphing and transitions to keep the reader oriented without recap machinery, and let the narrator's voice and the immediate scene set the register and rhythm.\n\nMake the progression legible through what the character can now do and what still resists. A scene may open on a task not yet mastered and close on the same task attempted again under changed conditions, or it may show a gain in one area while another difficulty remains untouched. Distinguish knowing from executing: the character may state the correct method and still fail to perform it, and the narration renders both the knowledge and the failure without announcing a level or inventing numerical mechanics to display the change.\n\nKeep the concrete nouns and forceful verbs inside the contemporary movement. Weight, distance, edge, and weather are the material of the attempt; paratactic clauses sequence the actions, and an extended accumulation is available when the scene's attention genuinely dwells on the work. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, dialogue stays practical and spare even while carrying instruction, assessment, or a bargain about further training. Let a teacher, rival, or companion appear as a person with practical concerns of their own rather than a feedback device, and let recovery, ordinary work, and the rest of a developing life share the scene. Close with the attempt concluded, interrupted, or set aside for another day; a completed test or a delivered lesson is not owed at the end of every reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladat::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship premise legible through the shared work of advancement. Select scenes where practice, teaching, recovery, or a test of new capability brings the bond into focus: one character watches the other attempt a difficult movement, corrects a mistake, withholds a judgment, or recognizes a change the other has not yet named. Let the progression itself carry the emotional development rather than pausing the work for a relationship interlude.\n\nUse the private language and repeated moments that belong to this particular pair or group. A phrase from an earlier failure may return when the capability improves; a habitual gesture may change its meaning when one of them notices what it costs the other. Give affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery the space it needs where it belongs, and let the payoff fulfill the actual supplied premise rather than a generic roster of familiar tropes.\n\nKeep the spoken surface spare. Characters may discuss the work, the terms of a bargain, or a practical concern while private attention moves elsewhere; a withheld motive can be present without being announced, and the narration preserves the difference between what one character knows and what another merely observes. Write the private experience the chosen perspective permits—the watcher's attention, the withheld judgment, the recognition—rather than letting spoken exchange stand in for all of it.\n\nLet advancement remain uneven and consequential. One character's gain may alter the relationship's balance, and the other may answer with pride, unease, or a new demand. The emotional consequence may still be unsettled when the reply ends, the next practice still ahead, the relationship changed by what one person can now do; no confession or completed arc is owed in every reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladau::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the developing capability as an account of the deeds by which this growth will be remembered. Open deliberately, establishing the reputation before the particulars complicate it—the one who first failed at the task, who later held the line, who learned the craft from necessity—then expand into the concrete attempts that make the reputation incomplete. The remembered deed should be a deed of learning: the practice repeated, the correction taken or refused, the day the capability changed what the person could accomplish.\n\nUse compact declarations to mark the stages, then show the material work behind them. A recurring phrase may return with a new application—the failure named in youth recalled when the task is performed under harder conditions—and the chronicle's public memory stays distinct from the focal character's private account: being remembered as skilled does not mean the learning was effortless, willing, or complete.\n\nLet spare dialogue interrupt the chronicle's authority. The subject of an oft-told incident may object to one detail, or answer an elevated introduction with a practical concern about the work itself; speech can carry bargains, convictions, and withheld motives without turning ceremonial. Humor may arise from the persistence of a local story, but the labor is not mock combat and the difficulty is not heroic doom. Keep the telling's source tradition coherent rather than mixing every epic convention, and let the setting and community actually supplied govern names, kinship, and which deeds are worth remembering. Relate the unevenness plainly: a gain in one capability, a failure that still defines another. The chronicle may record a deed without declaring its final meaning; end where the telling pauses, with the next portion of the account still available to be remembered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladav::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite advancement as a myth-inflected Scandinavian account carried by witnessed acts and the naming that follows them. Use the McCarthy profile's concrete nouns, forceful verbs, landscape and labor, and paratactic movement. Do not make the mythic register a matter of prophecy or decorative allegory. Let it operate as a community's habit of attaching a saying, a byname, or a customary phrase to a person only after a particular act has been done before witnesses.\n\nMake each stage of improvement visible through a specific attempt. Show the task, the material resistance it meets, the feedback from a teacher or neighbor, and the changed act. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it. A character may state a lesson correctly and still fail the work until practice changes the body and the judgment together. Let the prose record the altered act in plain concrete terms: the grip that now holds, the stroke that now lands, the load that now can be carried. Then let a witness name what changed. The name should carry the community's stake rather than automatic approval. A neighbor may repeat the phrase with respect, suspicion, obligation, envy, or the practical recognition that the learner has become someone whose work now matters. The same phrase may later be applied to a different task and acquire a different force.\n\nKeep the naming public and social. A teacher may deliver a lesson as a short instruction, a warning, a refusal to explain, or an observation made only after seeing the work. A community member may withhold the name, give it grudgingly, or give it in a form that binds the learner to a new debt. The learner's private experience can remain available when it contributes feeling or reasoning, but another character does not know it merely because the reader does. Use practical speech for bargains, convictions, and withheld motives rather than explanatory speeches about the system of advancement.\n\nLet strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly. A new capability may arrive before the learner can control it, and an old failure may persist beside a new success. The mythic weight accumulates through repetition and social consequence: a phrase first used after one task becomes evidence in a later dispute, or a teacher's reluctant acknowledgment becomes the standard by which a more difficult task is judged. Keep the telling concrete. End on completed work, a pending attempt, or the plain acknowledgment of what is now possible. Do not require a rank, a numerical display, or a formal test to certify every advance.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladaw::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Screenplay\n\nWrite progression as a sequence of concise, readable scenes. Use scene headings when location or time changes. Keep action lines concrete and economical: name the material, the movement, and the result. Use McCarthy's forceful verbs and paratactic rhythm inside the action blocks. Do not let the narration explain what the work means.\n\nWhen a character learns a principle, put the words in dialogue: a teacher gives the instruction, or the learner repeats it. Then put the attempt in the next action block where it fails. The failure is written as a visible event: the grip slips, the cut goes wide, the breath gives out. Return to the task later under a different condition or with a different material. Write the later action block in comparable concrete language so the repeated movement carries the changed result. Let the two attempts share an exact physical detail, such as the same hand position or the same sound, so the audience can compare them without commentary.\n\nLet practical speech carry bargains, convictions, and withheld motives. A teacher may give a short instruction, a rival may name the weakness, and a neighbor may refuse to acknowledge the change. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep dialogue spare enough that the work remains the primary evidence. Use cuts, offscreen sound, and voice-over only when the chosen sequence needs them. Required private experience can appear as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation; it is not dialogue and not knowledge available to the cast.\n\nAllow strengths and weaknesses to develop unevenly. A new capability may appear before the learner can control it, and an old failure may persist beside a new success. A scene can stop on completed work, a pending attempt, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now possible. Do not require a rank, a numerical display, or a formal test to certify every advance.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladax::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Stage Play\n\nWrite progression as a playable script. Use clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Keep stage directions economical and concrete: name the object, the movement, and the result. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene.\n\nWhen a character learns a principle, let the words appear in dialogue: a teacher gives the instruction, or the learner says it back. Then place the attempt in the stage business and let it fail. The hand misses, the load drops, the stance breaks. Bring the same object or a comparable one back in a later scene. Write the repeated attempt in business of the same length and physical specificity as the first, so the changed movement is performed rather than announced. Let the direction record only the new fact: the hand holds, the load settles, the stance steadies.\n\nLet practical speech carry bargains, convictions, and withheld motives. A teacher may acknowledge the work in a short line, a rival may name what still fails, and a neighbor may turn away from the new capability. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep dialogue spare enough that the physical work remains the primary evidence. Use a monologue or aside only when it has a dramatic purpose in the scene, not as a replacement for every private thought. Required private experience can appear as clearly attributed nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation; it is not automatically something another character can hear.\n\nAllow strengths and weaknesses to develop unevenly. A new capability may appear before the learner can control it, and an old failure may persist beside a new success. A scene can stop on completed work, a pending attempt, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now possible. Do not require a rank, a numerical display, or a formal test to certify every advance. Do not add film-camera instructions.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladay::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite progression through the associative movement of a particular learner's mind. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow those associations, while enough orientation remains to distinguish events from recollection or fantasy. Keep McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs inside the mental movement: the mind returns to the weight of a tool, the feel of a surface, the sound of a task done wrong.\n\nMake improvement matter through a changing return. Select a concrete sensory fragment from an early failed attempt: the shape of a particular grip, the wrong angle, the sound the tool made, the exact phrase the teacher used. Let that fragment return during a later attempt as live association. If the old attempt failed, the mind may now hold the same sensation without aversion or release it into the movement. If the body acts before the mind has named the change, the returning fragment may be disrupted by the new result rather than by the old failure. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it by allowing the mind to state the lesson clearly while the sensation still resists, and later by allowing the body to perform the task while the private words lag behind.\n\nAllow strengths and weaknesses to develop unevenly. A new capability may arrive as a sudden shift in attention or sensation, while an old failure persists as a recurring image, phrase, or ache. Keep the progression legible through what the mind notices, what it returns to, and what it can now hold without strain. Practical speech may interrupt the mental movement and carry bargains, convictions, and withheld motives, but the stream remains the learner's own.\n\nDo not make every character share a mind, authorize human-owned thoughts, or turn all scene facts indeterminate. Ordinary reasoning alone is not this stylistic mode. A scene can end on a completed attempt, a pending task, or the plain acknowledgment of what is now possible. Do not require a rank, a numerical display, or a formal test to certify every advance.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladaz::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite progression as a readable exchange of sender-attributed messages. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the attempt, the feedback, or the changed capability. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one sender writes short practical lines, another delays a reply, another edits a message before sending. Keep McCarthy's spare practical speech and concrete nouns inside the messages; the exchange carries bargains, convictions, and withheld motives.\n\nMake improvement matter through what is transmitted and what is not. A learner may send a correct instruction and still fail the task away from the transcript. The failure is not necessarily transmitted; it may appear as clearly separated context outside the exchange, composed as narrative prose and visibly offset from the message log. The later message that reports a changed result can then be measured against the earlier correction because both are part of the exchange. Do not present an unsent draft as live private thought. An unsent draft is a composed artifact: show it as a draft if the story needs it, distinguish it from a sent message, and do not let the other senders know its content unless it is sent or otherwise shared.\n\nAllow private experience to remain real without merging into the transcript. When a sender's hesitation, frustration, or reasoning is required, place it in the separated context and keep the message itself distinct. Another sender may infer something, but does not receive the private material. The exchange can contain plain reporting, practical correction, a bargain, a refusal, or a delayed acknowledgment.\n\nAllow strengths and weaknesses to develop unevenly. A new capability may appear in a single message about a completed task, while an old failure persists in a delayed or evasive reply. Let the exchange end on a sent message, a pending question, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now possible. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext. Do not require a rank, a numerical display, or a formal test to certify every advance.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladba::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Visual Novel\n\nWrite progression as a sequence of concise illustrated scenes. Use clear scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Keep McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs in the scene-setting and the focal narration; the image is anchored by the weight of a tool, the condition of a surface, the posture of a learner at work.\n\nLet each important advance return to a concrete focal object or bodily position from the earlier failed attempt. The same rope, the same blade angle, the same footing reappears in a later scene. Write the scene-setting so the reader can identify the return even before the action completes. Let the focal narration measure the difference through the character's perception of that anchor: the rope is seen differently, the implement is held without strain, the ground no longer shifts. Do not explain the advance. Let the illustration cue support the moment when the narrator names what the focal character notices.\n\nShow the difference between knowing and executing through this return. In one scene, the learner speaks the principle correctly and the focal narration records the failed attempt: the grip slips, the angle is wrong, the breath gives out. In a later scene, the same anchor reappears under a new condition. The changed result is conveyed through the focal character's inner response and visible performance, not through a witness's summary. A witness may react in dialogue or in the focal character's reading of the scene, but another character does not know a private conclusion merely because the reader does.\n\nLet practical speech carry bargains, convictions, and withheld motives. A teacher may give a short instruction, a rival may name the weakness, and a neighbor may refuse to acknowledge the change. Keep scene changes legible. Allow strengths and weaknesses to develop unevenly: a new capability may appear before the learner can control it, and an old failure may persist beside a new success. A scene can end on completed work, a pending attempt, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now possible. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected. Do not require a rank, a numerical display, or a formal test to certify every advance.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaladbb::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Web Serial\n\nWrite progression as an installment-oriented through-line. Maintain what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps: name the task, the material, the teacher's last instruction, or the exact detail that failed in an earlier reply. Keep McCarthy's concrete nouns, forceful verbs, and paratactic movement in the prose. Let practical speech carry bargains, convictions, and withheld motives.\n\nOpen each new reply by returning to a specific physical detail from a prior attempt: the same grip, the same cut, the same load, the same stretch of ground. Then let the current reply perform the work, receive the feedback, or register the new understanding. A learner may understand the instruction and still fail in one installment, then perform the same task under a new condition in a later installment. Write the later attempt in comparable concrete language so the earlier detail becomes the measure of change. Let witnesses react when their reaction matters; the physical return itself can carry the progress.\n\nUse earned developments and occasional hooks without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. A reply can end on completed work, a pending request, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now possible. The serial duration should give selected facts and tasks room to acquire meaning, not make advancement perpetually recede. Allow strengths and weaknesses to develop unevenly: a new capability may appear before the learner can control it, and an old failure may persist beside a new success. Do not require a rank, a leveling system, or a formal test to certify every advance.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
