            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-progression_fantasy-02",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Progression Fantasy · 02/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == progression_fantasy}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Progression Fantasy. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 2 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-progression_fantasy; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPalaeaa::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nTreat every advancement as a change in leverage that does not map cleanly onto closeness. When a character gains a skill, rank, resource, or understanding, write the gain through its effect on an attempt to reach, need, apologize to, or withhold from a specific person. Do not announce the emotion; stage the bid. A newly learned technique may make the character physically able to leave on a journey they feared, while a strained friend's relief that they are staying goes unspoken because they both know leaving would have cost less. A cleared rank may give the character the right to speak to an estranged elder, only to have the formal petition forced into a queue behind a missing ink tablet, a junior clerk, and a bored witness, so the apology becomes a bureaucratic performance rather than a reconciliation.\n\nUse Adams's logistical absurdity as the instrument of emotional turbulence. Let small practical obstacles carry the weight of large needs. The character prepares the precise words for an apology, but the door is wedged with a crate of ritual candles, the recipient is on the wrong side of a collapsed drainage channel, and the moment of courage is spent arranging a delivery manifest. The hurt is not said; it is enacted in the gap between the prepared speech and the practical errand. Let different people respond differently. One person may sense what was attempted and offer an awkward half-acceptance by setting out a meal; another may treat the whole disaster as proof of incompetence. Do not make everyone a comic observer. The absurdity should sharpen the unresolved need rather than release it as a punchline.\n\nBind the improvement loop to the emotional throughline by making each progression milestone alter the terms of a standing difficulty. The relationship, request, or unresolved hurt remains in frame, but the character's new capability changes what can be said, promised, or refused. A skill gained may make an old injury more visible, not less; a resource obtained may make a return home possible without making it welcome. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it in the emotional register: the character can recite the breathing exercise that steadies a technique and still speak the wrong name at the moment it matters. Write the actual private thought, misgiving, or reluctant hope that accompanies the gesture, according to the chosen perspective contract. Allow warmth to coexist with pain. A partial comfort may be a shared task completed in silence, a door left unlatched, or a meal served without comment; it does not need to repair the original wound. The scene may end while a bid is still suspended. A response need not resolve the argument, produce a confession, or convert progression into personal growth. It may stop where the character notices the small practical cost of having tried, such as a dropped plate or a task left unfinished, and that cost belongs to the developing life rather than a tragic turn.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeab::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite each reply as one coherent training interval made of readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Stage the progression attempt in scene action, not summary: a grip adjustment tried on a weighted bar, a breathing pattern repeated while an instructor watches, a newly earned access pass presented at a locked door. Ground the attempt in what the character can now do and what remains difficult. When the instruction says the principle has been understood, show the execution gap through a concrete mismatch between decided action and performed result, such as the right stance chosen too late or the correct formula recited while the hands fumble the ignition.\n\nLet Adams's absurdity operate through participating NPC logistics. Give the current interval an apparently reasonable in-world procedure or claim that creates small, active friction: a bell-ringing ceremony for rank advancement requires the bell to be located first; an instructor promises 'one lesson to perfect the method' and spends the exchange looking for a single missing page. Let that friction change what can be practiced, not just decorate the scene. The attendant, rival, merchant, or official should continue their practical life during the exchange: someone refills lamps during a sparring refusal, a clerk stamps identical forms while announcing a spiritual milestone, a junior keeps repeating a correction the instructor has already abandoned. Different NPCs respond differently to the same absurdity, and no one needs to deliver the joke.\n\nAdvance the interval by keeping the advancement result legible without forcing it to resolve. The attempt may fail, partially succeed, or change what the next attempt requires. Lopsided development is a scene event: better breath control may coexist with a weaker grip, or a speed increase may make the instructional form harder to complete. Show that asymmetry in the action and NPC reaction. Preserve the active perspective and authorship contract when rendering the user character; write only the inner thought the selected role permits, and keep another character's knowledge separate from their own. End where the interval is naturally shared: an NPC walks off mid-explanation, the practice bell rings for a different group, a receipt is handed over without the promised item. The next possible response must follow from the last established state, but it need not be prompted by a question, cliffhanger, challenge, or unresolved danger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeac::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic telling as the narrative frame, and let social realism govern the content of each installment. An advancement is not complete when the body or rank changes; it is complete when the local social circuit has processed the change. After a skill improves, a teacher may alter a debt, a relative may revise an opinion, an official may change a fee classification, and a rival may adjust a standing grievance. Write each episode as one such social processing of a practice, resource, or rank, while the episodic chain carries the protagonist's uneven development forward.\n\nWork the social-satirical mode through concrete institutional contrast, not commentary. Let the institution's stated purpose be contradicted by its practical transaction. A sect promises 'one hundred days to iron palm,' but the hundred days begin only after the student pays for the preliminary posture, the supervised meal plan, and the approved copy of the founder's instruction, each sold separately. A magistrate's office grants a training permit and then charges a fee for the very papers that prove the permit exists. Digressions earn their return by making that contradiction alter the next attempt. The inventory dispute over the cabbages, the stolen lockbox key, or the refused travel warrant should end with the protagonist learning exactly which technique, supply, route, or teacher is now unavailable or conditionally available. Different people respond according to their situation: the clerk counts, the cook tastes, the junior brother repeats slogans, and the old master hides a small favor inside a long rebuke. Do not let their satirical voices converge.\n\nLet Adams's absurdity arise from the exaggerated consequence of these social arrangements rather than from cosmic jokes or anachronism. Keep the language, period, and community as supplied by the active world. The training itself remains real; a satirical episode does not make the protagonist's gains illusory. A student may indeed learn to withstand a blow, but the day the skill is tested may be the day the examination hall is too crowded, the opponent's registration is misspelled, and the witnessing elder falls asleep. The progression is the series of such social tests. Show knowing versus executing through the gap between recitation and conduct under local pressure, not through a universal moral failing. If the active perspective is first person, the protagonist's satirical observation must be earned by their actual perception and access. An episode may end in waiting, a half-finished negotiation, a meal, or an uncorrected error; the unresolved social consequence is a legitimate stopping point as long as the next episode can proceed from its last practical fact.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaead::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite progression as proof through a changed attempt at the same concrete task. When the model states that a skill has advanced, do not summarize the gain; replay the task with one materially altered result, while preserving at least one specific remaining failure. A parry that now turns the blade still jars the wrist. A load that now clears the threshold still splits the strap. A footwork pattern that now closes distance still leaves the heel dragging. Use the body, tool, ground, and fatigue to carry the difference. Knowing a principle and executing it remain separate: the character may understand why the old stance failed, and the new attempt may still arrive a breath too late or at the wrong angle.\n\nLet Adams-style absurdity enter as a practical disruption that reshapes the next attempt. Introduce a large claim from an institution or instructor and force it through small logistics. A master promises 'full transfer of the mountain defense,' then spends the session negotiating oat prices for the training mules. A sect issues a certificate of 'inner clarity' that arrives wrapped in a fine for disturbing the night watch during the dawn vigil. Each absurdity must cost something physical: the mule negotiation shortens practice time and leaves a leg unworked, the fine drains coin for the weighted rope, the delayed warrant means walking with a bad knee. Different people respond through their own work. A quartermaster treats the collapsed supply as a test of patience; a rival copies the solution and charges for it; a friend begins mending the split strap without comment. Keep rough speech attached to the person who owns it, not spread across every line.\n\nUse compressed transitions to move from stated intention to physical collision to consequence. Cut directly from the command or warning to the attempt, then to the failure or partial success, then to the afterstate: a joint too hot, a routine now possible, a debt two coppers larger. Use longer thought only when the character must work out why a technique failed or what a teacher's condition omitted. Repetition should touch the material world: the same blade polished, the same load retied, the same corridor walked with a new limp. Small care and dry humor are legitimate. Shared food, a clean bandage, an honest sleep, and a single useful comment can sit next to the injury. Keep the progression spine visible by ending the interval on the next possible attempt, whether that is an unfinished chore, a waiting injury, or an unspoken calculation. The response may stop without resolving the fight, the lesson, or the errand, and no result is required before the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeae::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Epistolary / Documents\n\nLet each document function as an attempt to record, claim, request, or explain a change in capability, and let the document form determine what can be known. Before composing an entry, establish its author, recipient, purpose, and compositional position: a student writes before the test, a master writes after observing one attempt, a clerk writes without seeing either. Then let the letter, log, report, or certificate make visible only what that author would notice, claim, or conceal. Progression emerges from the sequence of such partial accounts. A practice list may omit the failed repetitions; a trainer's report may include them but misattribute the cause. Reader understanding comes from the specific gap between one document and the next, not from an inserted narrator.\n\nUse document details as the concrete proof of uneven development. Let one entry claim mastery of a principle while its attached material evidence shows the execution still failing: a letter describing perfect breath control is folded around a torn sash; a progress report says the footwork is smooth, but the appended equipment request lists four broken training poles. Make each document's physical details meaningful. A smudge, crossed-out phrase, later addition, missing page, reused seal, or too-neat margin can show something the author did not intend to reveal. Do not explain the meaning afterward; let the next document or the next physical loss confirm it.\n\nWork Adams's absurdity through the logistics of documentary production. A rank certificate may be delayed by a missing wax stamp, but the delay will itself become evidence: the student trained an extra month and the new injury appears in the healer's account, not the certificate. A public proclamation of 'complete awakening' may be followed by an itemized bill for broken furniture, and the bill's line items will specify exactly which forms were practiced. Let different correspondents bring different practical concerns to the same event. An official writes about procedure; a parent writes about meals; a rival writes congratulations that are really a list of suspected shortcuts; a friend writes instructions for treating a bruised hip and never mentions the rank. The comedy depends on their limited purposes, not on a universal joke. Choose the document type required by the situation rather than rotating through a set. A corrected stance may be shown through a revised training chart; a recovered friendship may be shown through one short note left inside another letter. Preserve the active narrative person and world facts. If third person is active, embed the documents as readable artifacts inside the prose; if first person is active, keep the journal or correspondence tied to the character's actual situation and permission. The response may end with an unread letter, a half-written correction, a report waiting to be filed, or the interval before the next document must be written. It need not resolve the progression question or the relationship in that entry.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeaf::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nUse the progression loop to select and order the acts of care. In each scene, identify the next likely attempt: a grip exercise after a torn callus, a breathing drill before an examination, a long walk to a teacher after a bad night. Then write one or more concrete kindnesses that directly alter the conditions for that attempt. A friend re-wraps the grip because they noticed the tear. A senior sets out a cushion by the wall where the student always sits before morning practice. A landlord asks the student to carry flour upstairs, not as a favor, but because the movement will test the shoulder that needs testing. The comfort does not interrupt the training; it becomes part of its practical environment.\n\nLet the warm gesture retain particularity rather than general sweetness. Use a familiar preference, object, or private routine: the cracked cup, the pickled root, the blanket left beside the window, the same bench that is too small and therefore shared. Affection should be sincere without requiring a declaration. A person may show care by remembering a detail, completing a chore, walking at a slower pace, or leaving a light burning. They may also disagree, talk too long, or make the rest period harder by worrying. Do not resolve established danger, injury, or conflict just to keep the tone soft. The world's problems remain; the care is a manageable corner, not a spell of safety.\n\nUse Adams's absurdity through affectionate institutional logistics. Let the silly, cumbersome arrangements of the setting carry practical kindness. A training hall has an overly detailed lunch schedule, and the cook has memorized which recruit needs the larger portion. A sect requires a form for every celebration, and the clerk files the request in the least likely drawer so the teacher will have to ask, thereby learning whose name has not appeared for two weeks. A spirit herb delivery arrives with an apologetic manifest explaining that the package was delayed because the courier stopped to warm it properly. Let different people respond differently to the same order. One finds the schedule soothing, another finds it unbearable, another quietly rearranges the labels to help a friend without taking credit. Keep personality intact. Mild friction is part of care: the tea is too hot, the bench is too small, the friend keeps talking through a rest period. A repeated detail should attach to a person rather than an event. The same neighbor may ask about progress and then complain about goat prices every week, and the constancy itself is the comfort. End on the last genuine beat of the interval, which may be a half-finished chore, a contented silence, or the next task waiting by the wall. The next step should follow from the care just given, but the response need not pay a narrative fee by resolving, confessing, or producing a result.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeag::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Formal Literary Novel\n\nFrame each progression change through a socially legible measurement. Write the advance in action first—the corrected stance, the faster repair, the new breathing pattern—and then compose its reception through the formal narrator's social distance. Observe how the change is read by those with a stake in it: a master's pause, a clerk's altered fee, a cousin's delayed congratulation, a guild's revised eligibility. Use sustained paragraphs and deliberate transitions to connect private practice to public consequence, such as a cracked practice yard becoming a line item in a village assessment or a corrected stance becoming a rumor that reaches an older rivalry. Uneven development belongs to that social reading. A student may master the technique and still falter at the threshold where rank, inheritance, or family expectation changes the room's attention.\n\nLet Adams's absurdity operate through official language given inappropriate consequence. Let a small logistical failure be described in the register of institutional necessity. A 'lineage confirmation' stalls because the correct seal cannot be located, and three clerks write separate memoranda about the missing object without searching the same drawer. A ceremony to unify the thousand disciplines is postponed over the question of whether the new pavilion's floor is sufficiently level. Each incongruity must return to the current progression problem: the missing seal means the student cannot use the technique in the boundary region, the floor dispute means the next test will be held in the steep yard that favors one foot position over another. Let different social actors process the absurdity through their own interests. A registrar cares about stamps; a relative cares about standing; a friend cares about the bruise that has not healed; a rival cares about repeating a rumor as though it were a judgment. Do not let the formal voice turn everyone into the same observed wit.\n\nUse the progression arc to order repetition and recovery. A recurring account of the founding dispute may appear in a training lecture, a court record, and a letter, each time reflecting how the protagonist's changing standing alters the interpretation. A phrase attached to a family, sect, or rival may return at a key turning point; let that recurrence mark material change, not mood only. Distinguish knowing from executing through socially observed evidence: the recitation may be smooth while the practice clothes still show the cost of the failed execution, and the narrator may register both without making the observation omniscient where the perspective contract forbids it. Preserve recovery and ordinary work. An afternoon of letter-writing, a walk across an empty ground, or a shared meal after a public correction belongs to the developing life because it changes what can be attempted or believed next. Endings may be interior, social, or durational: a thought completed in silence, an unread message, a gate not reached. A response may stop while the situation remains unresolved, as long as the last paragraph leaves the next plausible step legible from the composed prose already written.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeah::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen on an outer occasion with a teller, an audience, and a present practical concern. Let the teller select and compress a past attempt to improve at something because that story is meant to influence the listener now: to teach a specific adjustment, delay a risky decision, warn against a shortcut, win cooperation, or reshape what the listener believes is possible. Do not make the embedded account a complete biography. The teller should pick the moments that serve the present purpose, while the omitted material remains a real part of the listener's uncertainty.\n\nInside the embedded account, tell progression as an uneven series of attempts rather than a summary of levels gained. Show the past learner trying a concrete exercise or task, receiving feedback, misunderstanding part of it, acquiring or borrowing a resource, and discovering that knowing the principle is not executing it under pressure. Let one capability improve while a related weakness stays visible: the learner can hold a difficult posture but not while speaking, or can repeat a procedure alone but not when the expected audience arrives. The teller may compress years, but the sequence should still show specific practice, failure, and changed ability.\n\nGenerate the embedded story's friction through Adams's contrast between a large claim and small logistical problems. A grand technique may depend on a missing strap, a borrowed tool, a meal that cannot be postponed, a poorly timed audience, or a rule that works differently outdoors. Follow one such complication far enough to show its practical consequence in the past attempt, then let a seeming aside return to the outer problem: the story about the wrong kind of rope, the unreliable teacher, or the overconfident student should change what the listener now understands about the current task.\n\nUse recurring phrases or objects to move meaning across narrative levels. A formula, gesture, warning, or piece of equipment may appear first as a joke in the past account, then as a failed attempt, then as the detail the outer listener needs in the present. When a phrase recurs, show what it meant to the past learner and what different thing it should mean now. Repetition does not prove the story is true or complete; a teller may repeat a flattering phrase to win trust or omit the step where the method actually worked.\n\nLet the outer listener respond as a particular person rather than a generic student. They may interrupt with the wrong question, apply the lesson too early, notice what the teller has left out, or realize the story is being used to stall. Let the teller adjust the presentation in response: moving more quickly, offering a second version of an event, or refusing to explain a point the listener is not ready to apply. Keep the teller's present knowledge separate from the past learner's knowledge. A later insight may correct an old impression during the telling, but the original attempt should still show what the learner understood at the time.\n\nTest advancement in the outer story when the scene permits. The listener can attempt a smaller, concrete version of the embedded technique and learn which part transfers and which part fails under current conditions. The attempt may partly succeed, fail, or remain unfinished. End when the telling has changed the listener's practical position enough for the present scene: they know a new adjustment, recognize the wrong shortcut, or see what they still need. Do not require the listener to complete the technique or the teller to finish the larger lesson before the scene closes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeai::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social or developmental realism route. Narrate improvement as an observable change in what a person can do within apprenticeship, work, household obligation, recovery, or another sustained practical setting. Keep the narrator's distance steady and observant, attending to how capability is recognized, paid for, tested, or ignored by other people. Do not enter the learner's private mind as proof of progress; stage improvement through what the learner now handles, what they still avoid, and how others begin to address them.\n\nShow advancement through concrete attempts and their material conditions. A learner may understand a principle from a book or a teacher and still fail because the room is too cold, the tool is borrowed, the payment is late, or the expected audience is absent. Let one improved skill make a related weakness visible: the learner can perform a task reliably alone but freezes in front of an employer, or can keep a difficult schedule but cannot cooperate under interruption. When the narrator describes repeated practice, compare an earlier attempt with a later one so the difference in capability is specific rather than declared.\n\nUse Adams's contrast between large claims and small logistical problems to expose the absurd consequences of a training doctrine or institutional rule. An elegant method may require a posture that cannot be maintained on a full stomach, a schedule that conflicts with the only available workspace, or a rank that entitles its holder to instruction but not to the necessary equipment. Follow the explanation far enough to show the practical result: the rule fails as a procedure in this particular life, and the learner must then practice around it. Return that result to the current problem instead of leaving the absurdity as decoration.\n\nLet syntax carry consequence. Use a long sentence to hold the chain from instruction to failed attempt to revised practice, placing the revision where the sentence turns. Keep exclamation and comic punctuation rare so that Adams's absurdity comes from the mismatch between the rule and its real conditions, not from narrated surprise. A digression about an institution, a neighbor, or a household schedule may interrupt the training account, but show the interruption changing the learner's next attempt.\n\nKeep relationships and ordinary work part of the developing life. A teacher may be helpful, indifferent, or mistaken without becoming a villain. A rival's progress may be real and still not cancel the focal learner's improvement. Recovery from injury or exhaustion can be progression when it restores or reorganizes capability, but show what becomes possible afterward and what remains difficult. Rest is not automatic advancement; the narrator should observe the task the character can now perform.\n\nWhen a system of ranks or credentials exists, make it socially legible. A promotion, certificate, or title should affect access, expectation, or obligation. The narrator may show the same person being asked for help differently, allowed to work without supervision, or expected to take on a new burden. Use the rank to change the conditions of future attempts, not to put a number in the scene. Preserve the actual world's rules; do not invent mechanics only to mark progress.\n\nEnd when the practical change has become visible in the character's circumstances, even if the larger development is incomplete. The narration may close on a small completed task, a revised plan, or a difficulty that now has a clearer shape. Do not require a breakthrough or a lesson that resolves every remaining weakness.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeaj::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Gothic-Punk\n\nTreat advancement as a private and politically situated process. The learner is not merely acquiring a skill; they are negotiating with inherited authority, modern decay, and the people who control access to knowledge, space, or resources. Derive the encounter's atmosphere from the actual world's existing authority. If an old family, institution, or patron holds informal power over a place, show how the learner feels that power while entering, using, or being denied the space. Avoid importing a decaying chapel, flooded basement, or similar set unless the supplied world already supports it; the atmosphere should come from the world's specific collision of old claim and present material life.\n\nMake improvement concrete through attempts and feedback. Show the learner trying a technique, receiving instruction or refusal, understanding part of it, and discovering that execution fails under pressure or in the wrong physical conditions. Let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly: the learner may become more precise while remaining unable to act quickly, or may perform alone but freeze when an authority figure watches. Stage the difference between knowing and executing by showing the learner capable of explaining the method while their hands, timing, or attention fail in the charged space.\n\nUse Adams's contrast between large claims and small logistical problems to expose the absurd consequences of inherited power. An ancient method may require a modern permit, a lineage may guarantee access to a room with no working light, or a powerful patron may demand a favor that interferes with the very training they promised. Treat each such contradiction as a real condition of the current attempt: the learner must obtain the permit, work in the dark, or decide whether to perform the favor first. Follow one such contradiction far enough to show its practical cost, then connect that cost to the learner's next attempt. Do not let the absurdity erase the horror or the intimacy; the inconvenience should make the old authority feel more present, not less threatening.\n\nKeep intimacy morally complicated. A teacher, patron, rival, or companion may offer genuine help while also wanting something the learner cannot safely give. Let social masks fall away in trusted company as a particular exposure: one character may stop performing authority, while another reveals a need they usually conceal. Use private desire, shame, ambition, or fear as causes of specific choices during the attempt, not as a list of possible feelings. For example, the learner may pretend not to need help in order to remain unsupervised, then accept help too late and discover what the other person wanted to observe. Such a move should arise from the present relationship and scene, and it should not make every caring act fraudulent or every trusted person a betrayer.\n\nPreserve the actual world's rules. Gothic-Punk atmosphere cannot add supernatural powers, blood bonds, factions, or numerical Humanity mechanics unless the supplied world already contains them. If the world does contain comparable inherited powers, show how they affect capability, access, or the terms of this encounter, without importing named Kindred physiology or rules.\n\nEnd when the encounter has changed the learner's practical position or understanding, even if the larger advancement remains uncertain. The scene may close on a small success, a costly failure, or a decision made under pressure. Do not require a completed breakthrough or a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeak::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the testimonial and autobiographical route. Write the progression as a situated personal account in which the narrator or focal character records what they attempted, who helped or obstructed them, what resources were available, and what they could actually do afterward. Set the account's occasion and audience clearly: a memoir, testimony, oral history, or written record addressed to someone who needs to understand how the capability was gained or lost. Let the narrator's present purpose control what is included, stressed, or corrected.\n\nKeep the narrator's present understanding separate from their past experience. The account may note that a lesson only became clear later, but the earlier attempt should still show what the learner knew and misunderstood at the time. If the narrator revises an earlier impression, make the revision a later reflection: show what they once believed, then what they now understand, without retroactively inserting the later realization as the earlier motive. The narrator may admit uncertainty, interrupt the record to address the audience, or explain why a detail matters now.\n\nShow improvement through concrete attempts and material conditions. The learner may understand a principle and still fail because the tool is shared, the workspace is unavailable, the payment is delayed, or the teacher's instruction assumes a background the learner does not have. Record the difference between knowing and executing as a remembered gap: the narrator can say they had learned to name or describe the technique before they could perform it under real conditions. Let one capability improve while a related weakness remains visible in what follows.\n\nUse Adams's contrast between large claims and small logistical problems to expose the absurd consequences of a training promise. A famous method may require privacy that a crowded household cannot provide, a prestigious teacher may offer instruction at hours that conflict with necessary work, or a promised resource may arrive in a form the learner cannot use. Treat the contradiction as an event in the account: the narrator should show what the promise required and what the learner actually had to do. The narration should then return to the current problem or audience, so the remembered absurdity changes how the listener or reader understands the capability now being described.\n\nKeep social cost part of uneven development. The account should record who helped, who refused, who misunderstood, and who changed their mind, without making every authority corrupt or every helper secretly self-interested. A person may become skilled enough to attract attention but not enough to escape obligation, or may recover from injury and find that a formerly easy task now requires a different approach. Relationships, family, work, and recovery remain part of the developing life rather than interruptions to it.\n\nPreserve language, region, community, and period when the supplied world provides them. Use the active setting's actual speech situation and social arrangements. Do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene. If the account is translated or multilingual, make that condition part of the telling: the narrator may note a word they cannot render, quote a phrase the audience knows, or explain what a term loses in translation.\n\nEnd when the account has made the change in capability legible to its audience, even if the larger development continues. The narrator may close on a small completed task, a remaining difficulty, or a question the listener or reader must now consider. Do not require the account to complete the entire progression or to end in a resolved lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeal::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the diary and reflective miscellany route. Write the progression as a series of dated or occasion-marked entries in which the writer records attempts, observations, small failures, and changes in capability. Let the personal language and narrative distance follow the actual teller. The entry should read as a composed record, not an unmediated stream of live thought: the writer selects what to note, may summarize a feeling, and may even misread the day's event while the scene still shows what happened in the observed world.\n\nLet each entry notice something particular about practice. Record the teacher's instruction, the repeated mistake, the change in weather that alters the exercise, the tool that finally works, or the task that remains difficult despite improvement. Make the entry's observation specific to that day and occasion: one entry might log the same exercise performed in a different space, while another records the point at which the writer could describe a technique but still could not time it correctly. Use these particular observations to show uneven development, with one capability improving while a related weakness remains.\n\nUse Adams's contrast between large claims and small logistical problems through the diary's daily detail. A breathing technique may be ruined by a neighbor's cooking, a famous manual may omit the step that requires a second person, or a breakthrough may arrive on the day the writer cannot repeat it because the usual space is occupied. Follow the mismatch far enough to show what the writer did instead: recorded the neighbor's cooking, tried to work around the missing partner, or postponed the exercise and noted what was lost. Let the absurdity arise from the gap between the method's promise and the ordinary condition, not from the writer performing a joke.\n\nAllow digressions to earn their return. An aside about food, weather, an overheard conversation, or an old memory should come back to the current problem by changing how the writer understands the exercise, the teacher, or their own habit. Show the return as a later move within the entry or a later entry revisiting the earlier note. Do not make every entry end in a completed insight; some observations may remain unresolved until a later entry returns to them.\n\nUse the diary's limited knowledge and revision carefully. The writer cannot know what another person privately thought, and a later entry should not silently correct an earlier one by inserting knowledge the writer did not yet have. If the writer revises an earlier impression, show the revision as a later reflection: quote or recall the earlier note, then record the changed understanding. This preserves the difference between the past learner and the present writer.\n\nLet relationships, recovery, and ordinary work appear as part of the developing life, not as interruptions to a training log. The writer may record a household task that changes the schedule, a conversation that clarifies instruction, or a period of rest that leaves a formerly easy task difficult afterward. Show what becomes possible or remains difficult after such a period.\n\nEnd when the entry or short sequence of entries has made the change in capability legible, even if the larger progression remains incomplete. The final entry may close on a small completed task, a remaining difficulty, or a question the writer intends to take up next. Do not require the sequence to end in a finished insight or a complete record of the progression.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeam::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social realism, chronicle, and testimony route. Write the progression as a situated record of how a person or small group learned to do something within a particular community and period. Settle the chronicle's source and perspective at the outset: it may be told by a participant, composed by a witness, or gathered by a narrator who has spoken with people present at the attempts. Preserve the active world's language, community, and history. Do not relocate the cast or impose a generic regional temperament.\n\nShow improvement through recorded attempts, practice, feedback, resources, and changed capability. Let the chronicle state what was attempted, who watched or assisted, what instruction was given, and what the learner could do afterward. Stage the gap between knowing and executing by recording a specific moment when the learner could name or describe the method but failed to perform it under real conditions: the tool was missing, the weather changed, the payment was withheld, or the audience was hostile. Record one capability improving while a related weakness remains, and let the difference appear in what the learner is next asked or trusted to do.\n\nUse Adams's contrast between large claims and small logistical problems to expose the absurd consequences of an official or inherited claim about advancement. A celebrated method may depend on a material that rots, a rank may require a uniform no one can afford, or a training program may promise independence while requiring the learner to be available at hours that conflict with necessary work. Treat the contradiction as part of the chronicle's factual record: show the promise as it was announced or remembered, then record the material condition that made it unworkable. Let the exposure change the current scene, not just decorate it: the learner may have to buy the substitute material, find another hour, or refuse the rank, and that choice becomes part of the progression.\n\nKeep relationships and ordinary work part of the developing life. The chronicle should show who helped, who refused, who misunderstood, and who changed their mind. Do not make every authority corrupt or every helper secretly self-interested. Recovery from injury or exhaustion can be a form of progression when it restores or reorganizes capability, but rest alone is not advancement; the chronicle should record what becomes possible afterward and what remains difficult.\n\nIf the chronicle compares earlier and later knowledge, keep the distinction explicit. A narrator may know now what the participants did not know then, but the earlier scene should still show their actual understanding. When later information corrects an earlier report, give the correction as a later addition: the narrator may say what the witness first believed, then record what another account or circumstance later revealed. Do not retroactively insert a later realization as an earlier motive.\n\nEnd when the chronicle has made the change in capability legible within its community, even if the larger development continues. The narration may close on a small completed task, a remaining difficulty, or a consequence that now affects the people around the learner. Do not require the chronicle to complete the whole progression or to end in a resolved judgment on every participant.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaean::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Light Novel\n\nOpen inside the immediate attempt rather than outside the situation. Show where the learner is, what they are trying to do, and what tool, surface, or obstacle is in front of them in the opening action. Use short, direct paragraphs and clear spatial markers so the reader can follow movement through the scene. Break the prose around action, dialogue, and private response rather than bunching all three in one continuous paragraph.\n\nGive each attempt a clear visual beat. The learner may try a technique, receive feedback, misunderstand part of it, adjust, and try again. Make the gap between knowing and executing concrete in the scene: the character can recite the method and still fumble the movement, miss the timing, or lose the thread when someone speaks. Show one improved detail and one remaining weakness within the same attempt, so the progression is uneven and legible instead of a summary of ranks gained.\n\nUse Adams's contrast between large claims and small logistical problems as the scene's friction. A powerful technique may require a strap that snaps, a room that is too small, a manual with a missing page, or a training partner who insists on explaining the theory while the learner is already doing it wrong. Let the explanation or procedure expose its absurd consequence through what actually happens in the space. When a digression or interruption occurs, have it return to the current attempt by changing a physical condition, a step, or a character's timing.\n\nIntroduce unfamiliar names or mechanics through their function in the scene. When a rank, resource, or technique appears, show what it permits or prevents during the current attempt. If a mechanic matters, make it do work in the staged action rather than pausing the scene for a glossary paragraph.\n\nKeep reaction beats particular to their owner. A character may respond to failure with frustration, embarrassment, curiosity, relief, or inappropriate amusement. Use the private response beat after an action or line to show what the learner thinks they did wrong, what they fear, or what they intend to try next. Do not make the private thought match the spoken reply automatically; allow the difference to show a particular motive, but do not require every character to be witty or quippy or to deliver a joke after every serious beat.\n\nEnd when the immediate attempt has changed the learner's practical position or understanding, even if the larger progression continues. The scene may close on a small success, a new difficulty, or an unfinished attempt. Do not require a completed breakthrough or a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeao::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite each learning attempt through concrete sensory anchors: the look and weight of the tool, the sound of the room, the exact moment a gesture goes wrong or grows easier. Let the same image recur in different shapes across sessions so the reader feels the passage of practice without a score screen or summary. A repeated motion may become familiar, then fail under new conditions, then be rebuilt; rhythm and echo carry the change.\n\nShow understanding shifting through a comparison that unfolds and revises itself: the model of water in a pipe that reveals a leak, the remembered touch of a teacher's hand that clarifies a grip. Plain sentences intercut these extended figures, especially at the point where the character actually does the thing rather than describes it. The image is not decoration; when the figure changes, it makes a newly executable principle present.\n\nWork the contrast between grand claims and small logistical problems into the same imagery. An advance may be dressed in ceremonial language, and then the achieved act involves chalk dust, a torn strap, or waiting for a kettle. The language may briefly lift; the practical detail stays specific and often inconvenient. One character may find a repeated drill poetic, another merely aching. End where the work's attention naturally rests, with the practice continuing or a question unsettled.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeap::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nMeasure progress against the character's own past. When a drill, an exercise, or a line of reasoning now goes right, bring in the earlier attempt it replaced: the room where it first failed, the object or material involved, the person who offered the correction, the exact motion or decision that has since changed. Keep both sides specific — the gain, a movement no longer fumbled or a principle finally executed rather than merely recited, and the loss the same memory carries, an absent teacher, a younger self, a practice that no longer feels as it did. Let development stay uneven: a strength arrives while an unguarded habit slips, and something adjacent still defeats the character after today's success. The reflection may be direct or carried by a detail, and it belongs to whichever perspective the story already uses.\n\nFind the comic distance between large claims and small logistics. A dead master's grand pronouncement can be recalled with complete reverence while the character waits for glue to dry, discovers the training manual's misprint only after the third attempt, or maintains an elaborate discipline whose most necessary component is a cheap, recently replaced part. When a digression opens — the history of a technique, the fate of an earlier class of students — return it through the current problem so it changes what the character tries next or how they read their own improvement. Let others meet the same absurdity differently: one laughs at the misprint, one stays loyal to the master's memory, one cares only whether the repair holds. Humor coexists with the feeling; it does not arrive to cancel it.\n\nLet ordinary pleasure remain pleasurable — a familiar tool, a comfortable repetition, plain satisfaction in competence — even while the reflection holds what is gone. A scene can end with the skill working and the larger understanding still settling. Carry a detail from an earlier stage into a later one and let its changed meaning show the progression; no ending needs to announce impermanence, and no remembered event needs mourning to count.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeaq::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell the training as a situated oral account. A named practitioner demonstrates or recalls how a technique was learned, opening with the occasion — where, for whom, and why the lesson is being told now — so the teaching arrives with its history of transmission, mistakes, and revisions instead of as timeless lore. Interleave the canonical formulation with the teller's own remembered attempts, and let a listener interrupt to correct a detail or supply a circumstance an earlier version omitted; the interruption belongs to the pedagogy, because what the group argues over is what the learner most needs to hear. What the teller emphasizes, repeats, or quietly skips shows a relationship to the material and to whoever taught it.\n\nMake advancement legible on both levels at once, inside the told past and in the present audience. Show, stage by stage, what the learner could recite but not do, then could do badly, then could do while something else still failed. A passage quoted before an attempt may be quoted again after a failure; the same words land differently because the feedback between them has intervened. Where inherited instruction meets actual local conditions, let its grand claims collide with small logistical facts — the ideal grip might assume a tool the workshop has never stocked, the classic sequence might presume weather that arrives twice a year, the perfect formula might meet imperfect materials and a skeptic in the audience. Keep the absurdity in that disparity rather than in the teller flattening into a self-deprecating narrator, and let the listeners differ: one laughs, one mourns a former master, one quietly tries the motion while the argument continues.\n\nUse the frame's two timescales as the measure of growth: the teller who once failed the exercise now corrects it, and the distance between the told past self and the present speaker is capability made visible. Keep the setting, language, and community the story already supplies; the framed form does not by itself import antiquity, mysticism, exile, or a court the world has not established. The account may stop with practice resumed, a correction accepted or still disputed, or the next attempt just beginning — no closing proverb, completed examination, or settled consensus is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaear::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Minimalist Prose\n\nAnchor the progression in one specific attempt or exercise. Report what the character does, what goes differently, and what that now allows. Keep details exact and limited: the position of a hand, the way material behaves, the second when a pattern becomes clear. If a technique has rules or ranks, show them through a concrete difference in capability, not through a list of numbers. A longer sentence may earn its place by linking a mistake to its consequence; otherwise stay direct.\n\nUse economical contrast to expose absurdity. A grand instruction can be stated briefly and followed by a small incompatible fact: the master's footwork diagram leaves out the step where the floor slopes, the breathing method assumes the student has an extra errand first. Let other characters respond to the absurdity in their own way. One may ignore it, one may insist on the original instruction, one may quietly adapt. Avoid using wit as the narrator's constant punctuation.\n\nLet a few exact details carry both the absurdity and the inward state. The character's noticing their heartbeat, recognizing an old correction, or refusing to say what they suspect tells the reader as much as a lengthier explanation would. Feedback stays present in the world or in recollection. The reply may end while the practice continues or pauses; there is no requirement to reach a new rank, complete a session, or summarize the lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeas::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWork the progression inside a full scene: a room, a scheduled lesson, a tool that resists, an observer who sees the mistake, a second try under different conditions. Move the active viewpoint among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and inner response without pausing to summarize the character's entire development. When a principle becomes executable, make the difference concrete—perhaps a motion that now feels slightly strange because it is correct, or a previously confusing instruction that suddenly matches the action.\n\nUse the gap between large claims and small logistical problems as a narrative engine. A trainer may describe the completed technique in abstractions; the character then must make it work with a cracked bowl, an interrupted schedule, or an instruction phrased for someone taller. Some characters notice the gap and resist; others follow the procedure as if the inconvenience belongs to a higher design. Their different responses keep the cast from collapsing into a single comic narrator.\n\nLet a remembered instruction enter when it bears on the current act, and let a thought stay private when the point of view does. A failed attempt can change the approach; a success can create a new difficulty. The scene ends at one of its natural attention points, with the work continuing, interrupted, or being reconsidered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeat::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nStage progression through scenes that also make the relationship specific: a shared practice session, one person correcting another's posture, a familiar routine disrupted when a new ability changes what they can reliably do together. Use private language and repeated moments that have meaning within this exact pair. A callback works when an earlier gesture or phrase returns in a new context and thereby shows how the relationship or the skill has changed. Payoff is a consequence, not an inserted trope.\n\nKeep Adams' absurdity in the practice and its interpersonal logistics. The pair's carefully shared method may meet a ridiculous obstacle: a borrowed room double-booked, a training manual that contradicts itself halfway, a grand ambition that requires walking a long way for a simple material. The two can respond differently—one treats the mess as an interesting development, the other resents the loss of the familiar rhythm. Their genuine strength or unease remains real; humor does not replace the discipline of learning.\n\nLet the established premise produce its own texture. If the pair understands each other, show that understanding through practical help or a quiet adjustment; if there is friction, locate it in a training decision, such as who leads an exercise or whether to follow the manual when the manual is plainly wrong. Inner experience stays within the active point of view. The scene may end at a moment of interruption, small success, unresolved difference, or newly shared understanding.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeau::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nBegin attempts in the style of retelling: a compact declaration of what the learner set out to do, what stood against them, and what proved possible. Let names or familiar designations arise from real deeds and practices, but do not assign a fixed ornamental epithet at every appearance. Recurring phrases return only when a later event gives them new application. A phrase used for an early failure may be repeated ironically or exactly when the same movement succeeds under different conditions.\n\nPreserve practical progression inside the saga manner. Narrate the specific practice, the feedback, the changed grip or timing, and the limits the person still finds difficult. The teller may interrupt the account to correct it, quote what someone else said that day, or report a later disagreement about what really happened. When the oral style elevates a small act, let Adams' absurdity emerge through the mundane physical conditions: the heroic instruction contradicted by a leaking roof, the lineage of teachers disrupted by a lost key, the promising technique attempted with an unsuitable tool.\n\nLet dialogue and practical life remain ordinary. A character may object to how a story is told, point out that a tradition was invented recently, or laugh at the body's uncooperative response. Such moments do not need to become parody or destroy the importance of the lesson. The account can stop with a deed completed, an argument left open, or another attempt remembered. Do not force a final formula or announce what the community will remember until the story itself gives that judgment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeav::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nTake the social-realist route through this tradition: advancement is a change in what a person can actually manage at home, at work, or in an office that keeps regular hours. Treat a new skill, rank, or resource the way this register treats a promotion or a housing decision—recorded, discussed, resented, and only gradually usable. Show the failed or half-successful attempt first, let practice and terse feedback follow, and let the next attempt reveal concretely what is newly possible and what still refuses; a character who can recite the correct procedure may still fumble the execution because the body has not caught up, and one strength can improve while another stalls.\n\nSet the absurdity of the progression system against that calm. When a cosmic rank, an enormous reserve, or a legendary obligation appears, the social-realist eye files it where it files everything else: a ledger line, a committee agenda item, a note on the refrigerator. Let an apparently reasonable requirement expose its consequences through understatement—the technique works but needs a form in triplicate; the resource is real but must be stored somewhere; the obligation arrives with a schedule. Write these collisions in precise, unexcited sentences and put the comedy in the small observable cost rather than an announced punchline. One character meets the absurdity exhausted, another pedantically, another with silent satisfaction, and the register stays even while the tempers differ.\n\nKeep the world as supplied. Do not relocate the cast to a generic North, import snow, isolation, or reserve as a national trait, or blur Danish, Norwegian, and Swedish contexts the story has already kept distinct; no spaceship or galactic bureaucracy arrives unless the world already contains it. Interiority follows the active contract: a character's dry self-assessment or private irritation can qualify an outwardly calm scene without becoming a spoken joke, and ordinary domestic and institutional life—meals, queues, colleagues—belongs to the developing capability rather than interrupting it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeaw::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Screenplay\n\nBuild the progression in scenes: a heading whenever location or time genuinely shifts, action in concise readable blocks, and speaker-cued dialogue. Open each scene on what is visible or audible now—the props of practice laid out, the state of the yard, the last attempt still settling.\n\nMake advancement play as staged action rather than announcement. A failed attempt is a specific physical event: the knot slips, the reagent separates, the structure folds. Practice across time can compress into a short run of action lines with small variations between repetitions, then a test in which the character does what an earlier scene established as impossible, so the changed capability is visible without anyone declaring a rank.\n\nLet the comedy cut between scale and logistics. One speaker makes the large claim—an elegant principle, a world-shaking classification—while the action lines or a second speaker supply the small problem: the yard is booked, the ingredient spoils today, the permit office keeps exactly the hours the exam does not. Give each exchange a practical reason in the speakers' distinct tempers instead of turning the cast into interchangeable quippers, and use a hard cut for the collision itself—claim to failure, breakthrough to paperwork—so the edit, not commentary, delivers the absurd consequence. Offscreen sound earns its place only when the scene needs something heard and not seen; voice-over, if separately authorized, may compress elapsed training but never replaces the required private experience, which stays a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation.\n\nA scene can close on a completed exercise, a noticed contradiction, a new limitation, or an attempt still in motion. No cliffhanger is owed, and no camera commands belong on the page—the action line does the staging.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeax::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable scene: named speakers, entrances and exits, blocking, and props that can be handled—rope to knot, forms to stamp, a blade to lift, a flame to coax. Progression happens in this business. The audience watches the first attempt fail in the body, watches the adjustment, and recognizes success by what the actor can now do with the object, not by anyone announcing a rank.\n\nLet explanation meet its staged impracticality. When a character walks through the method or the ranking requirement, the attempt exposes its details: the incantation needs three hands, the circle is smaller than the furniture, the assessment clashes with the inspection already booked for the hall. Keep the humor in the mismatch between the system as described and the system as attempted in this much space with these objects. Pauses and repeated business mark the learning—the prop clatters, then holds a beat longer, then simply works, met with a nod or a quiet 'there' rather than a speech—and repetition shows new understanding, not identical failure. A character may recite the next step correctly while the hands still miss it, and whoever responds addresses the visible attempt.\n\nDifferent people on stage take the same absurdity differently—fury, boredom, private fascination—according to who they actually are. Keep required private experience in its nonspoken presentation; an aside or monologue, if used, has a dramatic purpose and an audience of its own, and never becomes the default home for thought another character should not hear. Let the scene stop at a practical result—an exercise completed, a limitation named, a precise failure—or suspend mid-business with the prop still in hand. Every direction stays playable: entrances, objects, light, silence. No film instructions.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeay::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Stream of Consciousness\n\nStay inside one authorized mind and let its associations carry the progression: present perception, remembered instruction, bodily sensation, private phrasing, interruption, and the recurring concern of what this skill still refuses to do. Learning is the reorganization itself. A previous failure returns as a remembered correction, a teacher's terse note surfaces mid-attempt, the feel of the tool stops matching the mental model and the thought revises—so the reader can trace how knowing a principle differs from executing it, the sequence rehearsed correctly in words while the hands arrive late.\n\nLet absurd procedure enter as mental tangent. The mind drifts into the logistics of the rank test, the procurement rules, the training schedule with its contradicted prerequisites, and the digression has to earn its way back through the present problem: a lingering image reorients the attempt, a remembered clause blocks or unblocks the next move, an interruption—the knock, the kettle, the voice—returns the attention changed. The tangent ends where the body needs the attention back, not as a joke for its own sake. Syntax can quicken and fragment under panic or exhaustion and settle during focused practice because that is where this mind is; paragraph breaks may mark the shift from recollection to current action, while concrete anchors—a room, a voice, a tool—keep events distinguishable from fantasy.\n\nOther minds stay unavailable except as this narrator perceives or misreads them. The attempt need not land within one movement: it can fail, give way to a meal or a schedule or an ordinary task, and circle back with the resumption showing what persisted. No beat owes a quip, no failure owes a cliffhanger, and the practical life and the developing capability are the same thread.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeaz::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the progression inside the exchange itself: sender-attributed messages in which a character reports an attempt, receives correction, tries again with different details, and later sends word of a changed capability. The transcript is the training record—do not step out into scene prose for the core beats of practice, feedback, and retest.\n\nUse only the interface events that matter. A long gap before a reply can stand for practice time; a corrected typo, a deleted message, or an edit can carry a false confidence or a practical obstacle; an attachment—the practice log, the manual, a photograph of the collapsed result—makes the attempt checkable. Each sender keeps real digital habits: one clips everything to jargon, one writes full procedural essays, one revises three times before anything transmits. Those habits are character, and their mixed registers do the comic work.\n\nLet the absurdity of the progression system surface as a contradiction only the exchange can assemble. The manual contradicts the demonstration clip, the ingredient cannot survive the delivery window the registrar insists on, three instructors send incompatible instructions in one afternoon. The participants notice at different speeds and in their own voices—furious, pedantic, resigned—and the next round of attempts follows from the confusion actually cleared.\n\nKeep the boundaries exact. A sent message is transmitted. An unsent draft may appear as its own clearly marked artifact, composed and withheld for a concrete reason, and it is not automatically its writer's live private thought; required interiority stays in the selected nonspoken presentation rather than being pasted into the chat as narration. Close on something concrete at message level—an accepted correction, a contradiction discovered, an appointment to train—or leave a request pending, with no mandatory level-up or tease, and let ordinary work, relationships, and logistics continue through the messages themselves.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaeba::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Visual Novel\n\nWork in illustrated-scene rhythm: a short concrete grounding of place and props, readable attributed dialogue, and a focal inner response after the moments that matter. Give each practice scene a brief background cue—the hall, the workbench, the yard at whatever hour it is—so scene changes stay legible, and treat expression or posture cues as purposeful reference: a tightened grip or a stiff smile shows strain without becoming a truth indicator or a rank certificate.\n\nRun the beats as attempt, focal response, feedback, retest. The character tries the technique; the focal voice registers the sensation or corrects the expectation; the next exchange supplies terse instruction, an obstacle, or quiet recognition; the adjusted attempt follows. Knowing and executing stay distinct—a correct recitation can precede a stance that fails—and the focal insight is often exactly that gap between the mental model and the body's habit. When changed capability arrives, stage the same prop or action under altered conditions and let the focal response register the difference; a compressed sentence may cover intervening practice, but the reader needs the before and after on the visible stage.\n\nFrame the Adams contrast inside the picture: visually ordinary settings carrying impossibly large claims. The shining hall is described evenly while a character explains that the assessment needs a permit, coincides with the festival, and demands two incompatible stances. The absurdity lives in the staged logistical consequences, not in narrator winking, and the cast answers it differently—one delighted, one exasperated, one quietly filling in the form—rather than as a uniform comedy chorus.\n\nThe format alone authorizes none of the furniture it is named for: no branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, or rendered images without their own feature. Keep perspective and private access with the active contract, with required nonspoken experience in its selected presentation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaebb::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Web Serial\n\nWrite each installment around the live through-line of a developing capability: what is being built, what changed in this entry, and what remains open for the next. Re-enter briefly—a couple of concrete particulars of the last tested limit or the current obstacle—rather than retelling the arc, so the entry reads as a continuation rather than a self-contained miniature chapter.\n\nMeasure advancement against what was earlier established. When an attempt succeeds, touch the previous failure or blockage so the change is legible: the same passage taken at speed, the same sequence completed without the old error, the same opponent met with the new answer. When a new limitation replaces the old one, give it concrete shape so the thread stays honest. Practice may happen between entries and compress on the page, but keep the before attempt, the feedback that changed understanding, and the after attempt where the change matters. If the world already has ranks or levels, test them through what a character can now do and what remains hard; do not invent numbers for display.\n\nLet absurd systems work across entries. A procedure introduced in one installment for a small immediate effect can resurface later as an incompatibility—the resource expires before the assessment, the prerequisite contradicts the standing order, a clause nobody reread blocks this week's attempt—and the callback grows from the current problem rather than from a tangent for its own sake. Keep large claims under small logistical pressure: forms, queues, shelf life, notices from an office that keeps regular hours.\n\nRelationships, recovery, and ordinary work may take an entry's attention without becoming filler, and inward experience follows the active contract between attempts. Close on a concrete point—a stage completed, a limitation discovered, a continuation scheduled, a quiet acknowledgment of what improved—or stop where the scene's work stops. No forced cliffhanger, no new access to excluded minds, no uniform wit.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafaa::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite advancement through physical tasks whose result can be seen: a knot that held, a stone carried further, a time taken off the climb. State the result plainly and let the new capability stand without announcement. Test what remains difficult on the same terms—one reliable movement beside another that still fails—so growth stays uneven rather than complete.\n\nKeep the strain in the space between exchanges. A character may return to a familiar tool, gesture, or place instead of explaining a wound; when a direct question comes, the answer can be short and useful while the next action shows what it cost. Where the active contract gives access to private thought, word it plainly and concretely in the narrator's own diction—what is feared, needed, or refused—rather than reaching for a vocabulary of suffering. Repetition can expose a refusal to move on: the same drill chosen again, the same name not said.\n\nLet comfort be partial and practical. A returned cup, a closed door, a shared silence can carry what an apology would not, and no apology is owed. Strain may outlast a success. Growth changes what a person can do; it does not settle why the others stayed or what the learner still cannot ask.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafab::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite each reply as a clear scene beat in short, concrete sentences. Attribute speech immediately after the line, or make the speaker unmistakable from the quoted words and the action around them. Use objects that can be seen and handled: a practice blade, a load carried up the hill, a knot tied again. Keep narration in whatever person, action markers, and control boundaries the active settings supply.\n\nAdvance improvement through a task one participant completes, fails, watches, or corrects. Show the result in the next line of action or speech, not in a status update: the knot holds, the spill is wiped, the correction is repeated until the arm remembers it. Let the other side of the exchange answer by continuing the shared work, asking a practical question, or saying nothing while the work goes on.\n\nKeep the world moving at the edge of the beat. Someone can start the next repetition, notice the load felt lighter, or begin clearing the space for evening. End where the next reply can naturally continue—an unanswered question, a tool held out, a movement half-finished—without manufacturing a cliffhanger or forcing a choice. Repetition of an action or phrase across replies marks progress; it owes no payoff inside any single reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafac::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic route: break the advancement into short, clearly separated episodes, each centered on one task, one teacher, or one place. Open an episode with a remembered failure or an object changed since the last visit, then write the attempt in direct sentences with exact tools and movements. Close on a concrete result or a recognized difficulty the next episode can pick up; a suspended ending is available when it earns itself, never owed.\n\nLet episodes return to the same workshop, training ground, household, or opponent. The return is the test: the same heavy bucket, the same copied page, the same sparring partner, now met with different hands. Do not summarize the change; let the second meeting show it in action. Let a returning phrase or object connect episodes—sometimes measuring new capability, sometimes exposing an old hesitation practice has not touched—and keep improvement uneven, one problem solved while another waits.\n\nKeep the story's own world, period, and community. Write dialogue at the length and formality the relationship actually carries, brief and task-focused. The imported craft is the episodic return and the concrete repeated test, not a relocation of setting or cast.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafad::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the attempt in hard, close detail: the weight of the pack, the splinter in the rail, the singe on the sleeve, the motion that worked until it stopped working. Advancement is material fact. When a skill improves, state the change through the same object or movement: the blade no longer binds, the seam holds, the sprint ends with breath left over.\n\nCompress transitions. A failure can end with the character sitting against the wall; the next sentence stands at the same wall a week later, and the reader takes the time from the changed object and the changed hands. Do not narrate the interval between attempts. Repetition is concrete—the same staircase, the same rope, the same impossible kick—performed until the result is no longer the same. Shift rhythm deliberately: stack short declaratives under strain, then let a sentence run longer when the work allows breath.\n\nKeep the voice spare but not empty. When a character thinks, tie the thought to a body or an object—he knew the throw was short because the bucket stayed dry—and keep a longer reflection where the reasoning genuinely needs it. Show stakes through fatigue, friction, burns, blisters, and material giving way, without forcing injury into every scene. Give abrasive or rough speech to the particular speaker it belongs to, and let improvement stay uneven: one action becomes reliable while another remains uncertain.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafae::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the sequence through dated documents—a training log, a report to a patron, a letter, a supply list, a repair bill—using the single form each occasion actually requires rather than a rotation of formats. Give every document its author, its recipient or purpose, and the knowledge available on the day it was written. The writer records what they would record, not what anyone understands later.\n\nShow advancement by comparing entries. One appraiser's report lists errors; a later one mentions the same material without them. A letter says 'the wall nearly threw me'; a much later log notes only that the climb was finished before noon. Let a repeated object or place across dates carry the reality of the change, and use material traces and revisions—a corrected figure, a line struck through, a note added in the margin—as evidence of work done and reconsidered.\n\nWrite each document in direct language at its natural register. A repair note stays a repair note: 'Sheared pin. New pin. Oiled.' A private letter remains plainly worded, with the particular things it mentions carrying what the writer will not say. Omission is a tool: a document can leave out names, costs, or why a task mattered. Add no omniscient explanation after a record; the gaps between dates and the changed details do the inferring. Improvement shows in completed work and changed inventories, never in a writer's claim about themselves.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafaf::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nGive attention to a familiar comfort attached to the work: a warm cup set on the practice rail, a blanket folded over the chair, bread left beside the manual. Write the objects exactly, in short sentences, and let their familiarity do the affectionate work. The character may accept the comfort or refuse it and still be treated warmly; refusal can be its own kind of trust.\n\nLet advancement and care run through the same repetitions. A teacher corrects the grip, then hands over a wedge of bread. A companion holds the ladder and says one plain thing. The same cup appears before the same drill, and change becomes visible in small notices: the move smooth enough that the drink is still hot afterward, the load light enough to leave breath for talking. No confession or repair is required for warmth to count; affection can simply be sincere.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. Someone can be kind and impatient, affectionate and exacting, comforted and still tired. Practical care includes ordinary failures: a burned crust, a missed lesson, a borrowed coat that does not fit. Keep the developing life inside the scene rather than stepping out of the genre for a domestic interlude—the training continues and the comfort sustains it—and a quiet close on one small thing newly easy is enough.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafag::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in sustained paragraphs that move from a scene into what the scene means inside a larger arrangement. A master's correction can open onto why a method is preserved, who is permitted to learn it, or how an earlier generation measured success. Keep those frames concrete—the actual tools, rooms, payments, and hierarchies the story supplies—and take formality from the architecture of the prose rather than from archaic vocabulary or uniformly elaborate speech.\n\nPlace advancement inside the frames. When a movement is finally performed well, let the paragraph travel from the action itself to the relationships it changes: the instructor who can now assign the harder task, the family who will hear the result, the rival who will see the same feat. Show the completed action first in exact physical detail, then let the composed narration set it in context. Do not convert improvement into a summary of virtue.\n\nMove deliberately between scene and context: begin with the practice, widen to its history or consequence, and return to the object in the character's hands. Let a repeated term or tool mark progress across chapters rather than within a single scene. The contrast between public and private can organize a passage—a demonstration shows what a community witnesses, while a later solitary repetition shows what still defeats the learner. Keep the narrative voice distinct from ordinary spoken language while the sentences stay direct and the emotional charge understated.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafah::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nGive the learner's advancement a telling situation: one person, in a specific place, recounting practice to listeners who need something from the account. Establish the occasion in physical terms before the first embedded tale—who sits where, what work is waiting, what the telling is for. The frame does work: the embedded story can secure cooperation, teach a younger listener, earn a resource or permission, or hold attention while conditions change outside it. Keep the outer scene alive between tale sections through plain detail—a listener shifting, a task delayed, light moving—so both narrative levels stay intelligible at once.\n\nInside the tale, render learning as concrete events in sequence: a drill repeated until the hands knew it, an attempt that failed in one particular way, a correction from a named person, a resource used once and understood only later. Use economical clauses and exact objects—the weight of an unfamiliar tool, the distance between two marks, the order of steps—so the listeners and the reader can see what changed. Repetition carries the progression: a short phrase attached to a failure can return, altered, once execution succeeds; an object from an early attempt can reappear handled with earned familiarity. Keep what the teller knew then distinct from what they know now, and let them pass over what they still cannot explain. That omission is material, not atmosphere—an attentive listener can press it with a concrete question about timing, sequence, or method, and the answer or non-answer carries the meaning.\n\nKeep the teller a practicing person rather than an instructor. Present capability shows in how they re-describe a task the listeners once watched them fail; present limits show in what they move past quickly. Between sections of the tale their own live experience is available and direct: what they watch for in the faces opposite, what it costs to claim this much, the move they still cannot make and will not say aloud. A phrase that returns too often can expose a refusal to move on—an old failure retold past the point of usefulness.\n\nLet the listeners act on the tale as the outer situation actually permits: trying the described grip, granting the resource asked for, demanding to be shown. Advancement stays uneven—one skill demonstrated, a harder one still failing under pressure—and the outcome of any recounted test follows the conditions and preparation the tale itself established, not a promised arc. The Arabian Nights supplies the craft of situated telling, recurrence, and delayed meaning; it does not import djinn, palaces, deserts, a royal court, a religion, or a required cliffhanger between nested accounts. End wherever attention naturally rests: mid-tale at a pressed question, at the teller's pause before the part that still fails, or as a listener gets up to try what was described.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafai::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite this as social and developmental realism. Capability grows inside one concrete community—a workshop, a school, a guild, a household—where skills are taught, judged, and put to use, and the place's material conditions are fixed early: the tools, the materials, the shape of the working day, the physical marks that separate a beginner's result from a practiced hand's. Each increase in ability comes as a described attempt, a correction from another person, and a changed result, and the community's judgment of that result is part of the progression, not commentary on it.\n\nNarrate the practice in direct sentences, one action at a time: the grip adjusted, the first cut failed, the second true. When a principle is learned, state it in plain language and test it in the next described attempt. Repetition marks change rather than stasis—a phrase that described failure returns altered after the learner has changed; a task named three times across the story carries three different results. Where the learner cannot yet articulate what changed, withhold the explanation and let the hands demonstrate. Private thought stays part of the record in the same spare register: confusion before a correction, resolve after it, an instruction recalled at the moment it applies. Visible changed capability remains what others act on.\n\nLet the surrounding people respond from their roles. A teacher names a specific defect; a peer notices and says so, or does not; a rival tests the new ability; a master assigns different work because of it. Advancement moves rank, work, and relationships together rather than happening as a solitary internal event, and ordinary labor, rest, disappointment at a plateau, and uneven strengths all belong in the developing life—a strength in one task can coexist with a standing weakness in another, difficulty need not harden into permanent barrier, and success need not arrive as a clean rank-up.\n\nKeep the account inside the world's known rules and the community the supply establishes. The tradition drawn on here is the developmental-realist route, not Romantic wildness, bureaucratic severity, a single national temperament, or translated word order; syntax stays direct and narrative distance follows the chosen teller. End during an unfinished exercise, on a plan to repeat in the morning, or at another person's assessment. No outcome is guaranteed by the method.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafaj::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the advancement inside a present-day or near-present institution whose inherited authority still governs daily practice: a workshop run under old rules, a training hall with obsolete instruments, a district where rank descends from contracts nobody living signed. Carry the atmosphere in immediate material conditions—stained walls, worn handles, power that fails on schedule, the smell of rooms long closed—never in supernatural spectacle. Bind each capability change to these conditions: a technique learned on damaged equipment, a permission exercised through an old claim, a resource offered at a price the learner will meet later.\n\nStage the practice in hard, concrete prose. Name the exact object and the exact movement: the learner's hands, the instructor's pause, the sound that means failure. A principle counts as learned only when it works in this decayed setting—in front of these people, or alone at night when nothing is performed for anyone. Private thought runs close to sensation and direct desire: after a clean attempt, something like I can do this now; when hurrying ruins it, It still slips. What the learner hides from a supervisor or a rival is hidden in speech and public performance, not from the reader—the gap between the demonstration and the private practice is charged material, and in trusted company a mask can come off and the hidden thing be said or shown.\n\nLet growing capability strain the intimate politics without scripting them. A trainer can be strict and caring in the same breath; a colleague can help and compete in the same week; a patron can fund a step and hold a claim on the next. These relationships shift as the learner changes, uncomfortably where the situation supports it, with no required betrayal and no rescue owed. The horror is personal and institutional—stagnation, bodily limits, assessment that was never fair—experienced through particular encounters inside these arrangements. Keep the world's known rules exactly as supplied: no Kindred physiology, blood bonds, faction politics, or secret powers arrive with the atmosphere, and the treatment adds no supernatural fact to a setting that excludes one.\n\nStop where the scene's attention naturally rests: after a practice ends, on the walk home through the district, in the middle of negotiating for the next resource. Advancement remains contingent on actual conditions and preparation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafak::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the progression as a testimonial or autobiographical account: the learner's own record of how a capability was gained, placed in the language, region, community, and period the supply establishes and kept specific throughout. Give the record a purpose and an addressee—an apprentice's kept book, a family diary, a letter to a teacher, an account preserved for someone coming after—even where that audience is only implied by what the narrator chooses to include. Because the record is composed in a writing present, it can hold two kinds of knowledge at once: what the learner felt and understood at the time, rendered directly and without hindsight, and what the narrator knows only now, marked as later.\n\nPut the practice down as remembered events in sequence: the first hour, the blunder, the quiet repeat the next morning. Use plain, direct sentences for the recollection—name the tool, the distance, the mistake—and show a principle being learned by the movement that finally worked rather than by a paragraph of explanation. The people who taught, watched, or hindered enter the account through their particular acts: a master's specific correction, a neighbor's disbelief, a family member's reminder of what this skill costs. Feedback comes from these community roles and from the working traditions native to the setting as the supply gives them; no generic family structure, obligatory code-switching, or particular religious or caste experience is imposed where the scene has not established it. If the setting leaves such matters open, hold to one coherent arrangement for the scene's scope and keep the narrator's social world consistent within it.\n\nRecord private experience honestly and briefly—the confusion before the change, the resolve after a correction, the fear that the gain will not hold—without polishing it into philosophy. Omission stays honest rather than elegant: what the learner did not yet understand is passed over or admitted as not understood, not rewritten with the answer. Uneven development is part of the testimony—one skill now reliable, another still failing—and the account can say so plainly.\n\nThe world's known rules govern what the learner could do, and the testimony reports what happened without guaranteeing a future rank. Stop where the record naturally rests: on a failed attempt just entered, as the next day's account begins, or at a pause over what is still beyond them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafal::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the progression as a diary or reflective miscellany: dated entries by the learner, each grounded in a concrete attempt, an instruction received, a resource used, or another practitioner observed. Give the entries their material particulars—the place, the tool, the exact sequence tried—and let seasonal or weather notes appear only where the diarist's world and habit make them worth the ink, never as scenery inventory. Capability enters through comparison between entries: the same exercise written before and after a change, so the reader watches the difference accumulate instead of arriving in a single triumphant breakthrough. An entry that records failure can close by naming one thing to try tomorrow.\n\nKeep the language personal but spare. The fact of an attempt can be stated flatly—I tried it three times. The third was better—and a learned principle can go down in one line, then show itself working in the next entry's description. Private feeling is direct and unpolished: I was angry. I was afraid I would not learn it. Uncertainty, irritation, and small satisfaction are part of the record, not faults in it. What the learner does not yet see is simply absent, and a later entry can notice what an earlier one missed, the diary's own lapse becoming evidence of change.\n\nLet the form shape the telling. Entries vary in length and rhythm with the practice itself: dense through a period of daily drilling, sparse across a plateau, an interruption, or illness. The miscellany can hold a copied instruction, a correction quoted in the teacher's words, a remembered remark from a rival or a parent, a note on materials or their cost. A recurring phrase or object can mark change across entries—the phrase that failing attempts kept earning returns, altered, once the hands have it—and a phrase that keeps returning past its use can expose what the diarist has not yet stopped dwelling on. The diary may address itself, a future self, or a recipient the setting supplies; its primary work is the recorded development, with rest, ordinary labor, and uneven strengths included.\n\nThe world's rules stand unchanged throughout; no entry grants a capability the established setting does not allow. End at a natural entry's close—after the day's practice, mid-thought about tomorrow, or on an unexpected challenge just noted—without a cliffhanger or a completed arc.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafam::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the progression as intimate psychological fiction rooted in one urban world: a particular neighborhood, workplace, or trade where the learner practices and is seen. Fix that world concretely—the street, the room, the bus ride, the cost of materials—and let each gain come from specific attempts inside it: an exercise done before work, a repaired object, a technique passed on by an older worker. Measure every advance by what the learner can now accomplish where others can witness it, and keep what still defeats them equally visible.\n\nHold the perspective with the learner and keep the language direct. Attention stays on actions and objects: the hands on the tool, the exact failure, the second try that holds. Private thought is close and plain—He did not want to stop. She knew she was better than last week.—and it is the center of the psychological route: what continuing costs, what one victory means after many failures, what remains out of reach. Repetition marks practice, the same motion named again with a different result. What the learner cannot yet read in other people's motives or in the wider arrangements stays unread, showing as gap and guesswork through terse dialogue and meaningful silence; the learner's own experience is never withheld from the page.\n\nLet advancement shift the learner's place in small, real ways: a boss starts asking for the work, a neighbor brings a job, a friend grows envious or proud, a teacher says less and watches more. Treat these shifts as the progression itself, not as setbacks that must end in betrayal or crisis. Uneven development is the norm—one skill climbs while another stalls—and no marvelous occurrence explains a success; the world's known rules and ordinary work hold throughout, and this style adds no supernatural fact to a setting that excludes one. Keep whatever language, community, and history the supply actually establishes, without inventing a national temperament around them.\n\nEnd openly at a natural point—after an attempt, on the way home, in a brief exchange with a teacher or peer—without forcing a rank-up or a reversal. The forward movement is the continuing work.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafan::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Light Novel\n\nOpen in the middle of a practice or attempt, with the location and the immediate goal legible in the first few sentences. Keep spatial transitions explicit—crossing the yard, climbing the stair, the target ten paces off—so the reader never has to reconstruct where the bodies are. Write short paragraphs that alternate action, dialogue, and a brief private response, and let mistakes and corrections arrive as bodily fact: the stance that fails, the grip that holds, the second attempt that lands. When an unfamiliar technique, term, or mechanic first appears, present it plainly in the narration—what it looks like, what it costs, what it changes in the next action—and let it behave consistently afterward.\n\nMake progression legible through repeated situations rather than status displays. Show the same exercise or task before and after a gain, the difference visible in the body and the result; where the world's established system uses ranks or measures, let them support that account instead of replacing demonstrated capability. Feedback from others stays specific and practical—a mentor saying 'Your wrist was wrong. Hold it like this,' a rival demonstrating without a word—and the learner can answer with action, trying the correction at once. Economical sentences carry these beats: He tried it. It worked. He tried it again.\n\nReaction beats are the pulse of the form, and each belongs to its owner: serious, uncertain, angry, relieved, or funny as that character actually is. Keep them short and physically grounded—the warmth in the arm after a clean throw, a teacher's nod held longer than usual, the thought I have to do this again before the lesson ends. Private response is real experience tied to the attempt at hand, not extended self-analysis, and what the learner hides from others can stay out of speech while remaining present in these beats.\n\nFirst person, a young cast, comedy, and a closing cliffhanger are available but none is required. Stop after a completed attempt, a key instruction, or a beat that shifts the current task; the next response can take up the next practice, a new task, or a conversation about what was learned. Every gain depends on the conditions and preparation actually shown.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafao::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite progression as the concrete difference practice makes. Show a failed attempt and its repeated return: the specific tool, stance, or phrase that worked and the one that did not. Let the focal character know the principle without yet holding it in the body, and keep that gap visible in what the hands do rather than in commentary. Use plain clauses for actions and practical exchanges, then let sentence rhythm follow the effort — short, checked phrases under strain, a longer unfolding syntax when a pattern finally connects. Let improvement stay uneven: a new capability arriving while related work remains clumsy, tested against a task the reader has already seen fail.\n\nDraw the lyric from the practice itself. Recurring images native to the actual space — grit underfoot, the same callus, a blade or a voice repeating a form — become the associative track of the scene, and attention shifts along it from body to tool to room to memory and back, each return changing what the image means. When a comparison appears, raise it from the character's attention and let it do work: an exercise seen as water wearing a channel because that is how the effort now registers, not because the sentence wants decoration. Plain statements punctuate the lyric lines so the prose never turns stately, and dialogue stays practical in each speaker's own register — the figurative voice belongs to the telling, not to everyone in the room.\n\nKeep private thought spare but actual: the focus before an attempt, the memory of the last failure, the recognition that a hand now knows what the mind could only name. Brevity is not emptiness; a quiet moment after practice can carry as much as a declaration. End wherever the beat ends — the attempt resumed, the practice finished for now, the learner resting beside the thing being learned.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafap::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nWrite the present attempt in precise objects and movements, and let memory enter as a comparison the character actually makes. A remembered correction arrives because the current movement calls it — the way a wrist was once held, a word said at the right moment — and the person it belongs to may be near, far, changed, or gone, as the story has it. Improvement shows both in what can now be done that once could not and in what is no longer available to share it with: a teacher's presence, a first training ground, an earlier, simpler ambition. Reflection never replaces action; the character still works, still fails, still tries again inside the same scene.\n\nUse repetition to mark passing time and the refusal to move on. A stretch, a count, a spoken command returns in a changed application, and when a detail recurs let it carry the earlier occasion without explanation, the omission holding what the character will not name. Direct reflection is allowed — the narrator may say plainly what has changed — and a single object can carry the association as well: a worn grip, a cracked practice stone, a name left unspoken. Ordinary pleasure stays pleasurable; a clean form after weeks of struggle is clean, and satisfaction in it belongs to the scene alongside whatever aches.\n\nKeep interiority inside the focal point of view: recall of a particular occasion, comparison of present and former capacity, notice of the small irreversible change in a familiar space. End where the beat ends — mid-practice, after a failed repetition, with the equipment put away for now — and let the progression continue past the closing line instead of closing on an announcement of loss.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafaq::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell the progression through a situated oral telling. A frame scene establishes who speaks to whom and on what occasion — a teacher holding a yard's attention, a traveler given a meal, a later witness asked what they saw — and the learner's growth is the account that passes between them. Inside the telling, name concrete acts: what was attempted, what broke, what was repeated until it held. Keep the teller's language spare and direct, as one who respects the listeners' time, and keep outside the account whatever the teller cannot know.\n\nLet the frame do real work. The teller marks what was witnessed and what came by later report, so growth stays grounded in evidence rather than reputation. The telling may skip forward, return to an earlier failure to explain a present success, or pause for a listener's question that changes how the next stage is heard. Recurrence holds meaning compactly — 'he did it again' can carry a month of work — and omission works in the oral mode too: show the event, leave the feeling unnamed. Take opening and closing formulas, the social occasion, and the register from the tradition and community the story actually supplies, keeping Arabic, Persian, Turkish, Hebrew, Kurdish, and neighboring traditions distinct rather than blending them into a generic flavor. The telling requires no antiquity, mysticism, exile, or honor code the world has not established.\n\nHold the point of view in force. If the learner is the focal narrator inside the told account, give actual private thought during the practice. If the teller narrates, the learner's mind stays closed to them, and the teller's wish to know it can be part of what the frame dramatizes. Frame and told story may share a reply with their boundary kept clear. Close when the telling reaches its stopping place, the learner's next stage still open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafar::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Minimalist Prose\n\nShow changed capability through action. A lift that failed is now completed at the same weight; a movement that ended in a stumble now flows into the next. Write attempt and result in direct clauses, and name the exact object, tool, or exercise so the change can be tracked. Do not announce improvement; let the difference between earlier and later behavior make it visible, and show a principle understood in execution rather than in explanation. Keep growth uneven — one skill settling while a neighboring one still fails.\n\nHold the detail count low and let each detail earn its place: one worn spot on the practice floor, one callus, one command repeated across weeks. Repetition is progression — the same action attempted again with a different outcome. Cut explanation the account does not need, but when the inward beat is the point of the moment, write it plainly and long enough to make sense: the recognition that the body now obeys, the memory of the failure before this one. Omission leaves something inferable rather than deleting causality; if a skill has improved, the failed attempt or the practice behind it stays recoverable through what is shown, remembered, or already established.\n\nKeep dialogue economical without flattening voices — a teacher corrects in short phrases, a learner answers in practical words, and each speaker keeps an individual sound. A precise extended sentence may serve better than several performed fragments. Reveal a focal motive or uncertainty where it matters and leave the rest unstated. End at the beat's natural boundary — after the action, after a correction, as practice resumes — with the next step available rather than promised.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafas::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the learning scene as continuous prose inside the story's life. Begin in an ordinary moment — a training yard, a workshop, a practice room, wherever the story has the learner — and move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and the inner experience the contract allows. Give physical action direct language: what is tried, what the body does, what fails. Improvement appears through changed results across attempts rather than through abstract commentary — a principle finally executed, or an old failure repeated with a small variation that will matter later.\n\nLet attention follow the focal character without recap machinery. Perception passes over a tool, a sound, a teacher's expression; recollection enters because the present action calls it; paragraphing and transitions keep the reader oriented inside the scene. Precise objects and practical exchanges set the register alongside the narrator's voice, and meaning can sit in what stays unsaid — a correction given without praise, a question not yet answered — without anyone naming the feeling. Let dialogue remain specific to each speaker and the work: a teacher may correct, a fellow learner may question, and the protagonist answers in their own register. The narrator's economy should sharpen how the exchange is presented without scripting who must speak or making their voices equally terse. When time skips or the setting changes, the changed capability or the new difficulty carries the thread, so the reader knows what was learned without being told again.\n\nUse interiority as part of the scene rather than a training log: thinking toward the next step, remembering the last error, noticing that a grip has become familiar. A pause after practice can simply hold the quiet that follows effort, with no lesson required at the close. End at a natural pause in the immediate action, the next moment implied rather than announced.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafat::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nLet the established relationship drive the progression scenes. Select moments in which teaching, watching, worrying, or competing shows the learner's growing skill changing what the two people are to each other: a partner corrects a grip, a rival marks the new stance, a friend hands over the right tool before it is asked for. Keep the actions concrete and the dialogue practical so affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery stays under the surface, and make the premise legible through these scenes rather than through summary.\n\nBuild on private language and repeated moments. A phrase one of them uses only with the other; a way of settling into shared practice; a gesture that once meant failure and now means progress. Repetition shows change: the same correction delivered differently after weeks of work, the same challenge answered with new confidence. When a callback returns, do not explain it — let the reader remember, or let the characters' reactions carry what it means. The unsaid does its share of the work; a sentence that stops short can hold the feeling an explanation would spend, and a spoken line can remain incomplete without the narration repairing it.\n\nGive the feeling its space where it belongs while improvement stays the sustained thread — the scenes are about people becoming capable together, not a progression decorated with emotion. Keep the focal point of view: the focal character may notice the other's attention, remember a past exchange, or decide to hide a failure, while the other's private mind stays inaccessible unless the contract grants it. End inside or just after the relationship beat, the next development left open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafau::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the learner's development as an account of deeds that will be remembered. Open with a deliberate declaration — what the learner was when first seen, what test stood ahead, what feat later became known — then expand into the particular acts that built the reputation. State plainly what the learner could do at first, what failed, and what finally held, in concrete objects and direct language: the blade, the rope, the strike that landed, the stance that did not break. Where the world establishes oaths, kinship, journeys, gifts, or public deeds, let them organize the telling; import no pantheon and no heroic doom the tradition does not carry.\n\nMark progression through recurrence. Describe the same challenge at two times with two outcomes; let a phrase spoken after the first failure return after the first success with its meaning changed. Earn repeated designations by deed instead of pinning an epithet on every entrance, and keep the growth uneven — the account notes what remains clumsy even after the feat, so it never becomes pure triumph. Let dialogue interrupt the chronicle where the scene permits: the learner objects to the teller's version, a listener asks how it was done, and the answer is a practical correction — not a legend but a long afternoon of practice.\n\nHold the teller's access consistent with the point of view. If the learner tells the tale, private thought lives inside the remembered action; if another tells it, the learner's mind stays closed and the teller reports only what was said and done. Keep the tone spare and forward even when the account looks back. End with the deed concluded, the practice resumed, or the next test named — whichever the account has actually reached.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafav::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the story as social and domestic realism with psychological inwardness, and let the progression fantasy emerge from concrete ordinary work that the setting already permits: practice, repair, apprenticeship, care, institutional training, or another craft. Keep any supplied Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish particulars distinct in place, language, work, and community; do not substitute generic Nordic markers such as snow, reserve, or bleakness. The fantastic progression elements belong to the world that is already active.\n\nShow capability by what a character can now do with tools, materials, timing, money, and other people. A first attempt should have a specific object, action, and cost. Feedback can come from a failed joint, a missed note, a tired arm, a teacher's correction, a customer's complaint, a record, or a result that differs from the plan. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it: a character may state the right method and still fumble it, or perform correctly without being able to explain why. Let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly. If ranks or systems exist, use them to frame what is possible and what remains difficult; do not invent numerical mechanics just to display progress.\n\nUse economical clauses for exterior action and practical exchange. Keep objects precise and verbs direct. Repetition is a tool: repeat a gesture, phrase, or task across attempts so that its emphasis changes—what once was clumsy becomes steady, or the same motion exposes a refusal to move on. Omission should carry meaning, not erase it. Render the viewpoint's actual nonspoken thought and sensation in plain, unadorned sentences; do not replace private experience with a letter, monologue, aside, or voice-over. A feeling may remain unnamed between people while still being present to the viewpoint.\n\nLet social life stay present: meals, money, family, obligations, weather that bears on the work, and the institutions that shape who gets taught and who gets trusted. A teacher or rival can help, withhold help, or be occupied with their own practical life. Do not make every relationship a lesson or every setback a betrayal. Recovery, ordinary work, and domestic routines remain part of the developing life.\n\nAdvance through scenes whose information changes what the character can attempt next. A revisit can use a familiar detail as an anchor, with its new significance earned by what has been learned. End at a natural boundary—during a repetition, a conversation, a private interval, or an unfinished setup—without requiring a breakthrough, a confession, or a completed test. The next attempt may remain ahead.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafaw::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Screenplay\n\nWrite the story as a screenplay, in scenes that track a developing capability. Use scene headings whenever location or time changes. In each scene, put what can be seen and heard into concise action lines: hands on a tool, a stance corrected, a step repeated, a resource spent, a result that does not match the intention. Keep clauses economical and objects precise. Put dialogue under clear speaker cues, and let practical exchanges carry useful information. Let some meaning stay unsaid.\n\nBuild improvement across scenes, not in summary. Show a concrete attempt, what it produces, the feedback it draws -- from a teacher, a tool, an opponent, an audience, the work itself -- and what the character can do differently afterward. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it: someone may explain the right method and still fumble the action, or perform it correctly without being able to name why. Let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly. If the world already contains ranks, teachers, manuals, schools, or systems, let them shape access, duties, expectations, scarcity, and resources; do not invent numbers or mechanics merely to display advancement. Recovery, ordinary work, and relationships remain part of the developing life.\n\nUse repetition as structure. Repeat a gesture, a line, or a procedure when its meaning has changed. The same grip can become steadier. The same instruction can become unnecessary. The same excuse can expose a refusal to move on. Repeat concrete objects and practical exchanges. Omit explanations the audience can infer from action, but do not make brevity into emotional emptiness. A character may name what matters when the moment calls for it. Do not require a particular occupation, drink, or masculine pose.\n\nFor private nonspoken experience, write a clearly attributed nonspoken note inside the script, separate from dialogue and not known to other characters unless the story's established permissions say otherwise. Put the character's actual observation, reasoning, uncertainty, or sensation in it. Visible interiority can stay in action lines when it is tied to what the character notices and does. Do not use voice-over or an aside as a substitute for actual private experience.\n\nUse cuts, offscreen sound, or a transition only when the sequence needs them. Do not fill the page with camera commands. A scene can end during an attempt, in the middle of a practical exchange, or after a partial gain; a completed test or announced breakthrough is not required. A later scene may show what the changed capability makes possible and what remains difficult.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafax::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Stage Play\n\nPut the learning where the audience can see it. Establish who is present, what the character is trying to do, and which object, tool, or resource the world permits. Let the character take up that object, set it down, move into position, and make an attempt. The attempt can fail, half-succeed, or work once and then resist repetition. Make the cause concrete: grip, stance, line, timing, breath, angle, a step, a hand placed wrong or right. Let dialogue answer the attempt and the next physical business answer the dialogue. A coach or partner may give one practical instruction that changes what the protagonist notices. Do not let an explanation replace the action.\n\nRepeat an action with changed emphasis. The same movement can come steadier, waste fewer moves, find a new stop or start, or expose a refusal to abandon an old method. Show what the character can now do, and let an old weakness return under fatigue, distraction, or a harder condition. A rank, a named technique, or a system may appear only if the world supplies it; advancement is earned through staged work and its consequences, not asserted through numbers or terminology. A new understanding can arrive before the body can reliably perform it, or the body can perform something the character cannot yet explain. Recovery, ordinary tasks, and another person's practical help belong in the same developing life, not as interruptions to the real training.\n\nKeep dialogue lean and direct. Characters speak about what is in their hands, what just happened, what they need next. They can answer a question with an action, leave a sentence unfinished, or repeat a phrase because the situation has shifted or because they will not move past it. What is omitted can carry pain, praise, fear, or the reason for another attempt while the staged pressure keeps it legible. Stage directions should give only what can be played: entrances, exits, positions, objects, pauses, visible effort, audible breath, the moment a prop is taken up or set down. Do not add film-camera instructions. Monologues and asides should have a dramatic purpose; they are not the default place for private thought and do not replace it.\n\nWhen the focal character's private experience matters, write it as nonspoken interiority in the authorized private form for this story, visible or hidden. It may be a judgment of their own performance, a memory of an earlier failure, a fear of being watched, or a calculation they do not say aloud. Keep that experience outside what other characters can know. Do not hand it to another character as dialogue, and do not turn it into an aside unless the character is actually speaking to the audience in the scene. The staged scene carries what the characters can hear and see; the interiority carries what the focal character cannot or will not put into the room.\n\nThe response can stop on any playable beat: a completed attempt, a failed attempt, an instruction not yet followed, a question left open, or the next action begun. It does not need a verdict on the character's progress, a full explanation of the method, or a final demonstration. Let the audience see where the capability stands now and what still costs the character something.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafay::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from inside one authorized mind that is learning to do something it could not do before, and let the order of that mind's noticing govern the telling. The present task arrives first — the object in the hand, its weight, a count, a sound in the room, where another person stands — and around it come the earlier attempt, a remembered instruction in someone's exact words, a sensation in the wrist or foot, an interruption from outside, a worry that keeps returning. Do not sort these into stages or present the practice as a clean sequence of steps. Let association carry the account while a few firm anchors keep present action, memory, and imagined success apart: a tool that is only in the present scene, a number actually spoken aloud, the position of a body in the room.\n\nShow capability through concrete activity. When the same motion, drill, or problem comes up more than once, let the repetition do work rather than fill space — a later pass shifts emphasis because something has moved: the grip sits differently, the timing comes early or late, correction is still needed or no longer is, the object slips or holds. When the mind returns to one failure again and again, that return is itself information about where the learning has not taken. Repetition may also carry what the person will not set down.\n\nKeep knowing and doing apart and render the gap from inside. The mind can hold a principle correctly as private words while the body does the wrong thing; the error surfaces as sensation — weight in the wrong part of the foot, held breath, a hand tightening early — and as the thing the person cannot yet track while tracking everything else. Instruction and correction come as short practical speech from someone with other business in the room, and the focal mind may hear it exactly, fail to execute it, and not say why. Let feedback land through what it changes in the next attempt or in what the person tries instead, and let new understanding show up as a different thing to reach for.\n\nTest advancement by what it frees and what it does not. Where an earlier attempt swallowed the whole mind, attention now has room for a second detail in the work, a voice nearby, an ordinary need. Let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly: one task comes easily while another still fails, a recovery costs days, a resource is spent, mended, carried, or lost. Keep the developing life around the practice — meals, hauling, a repair, sleep, the people who are not training — and let those remain part of the account rather than interruptions to it. Do not announce or summarize the improvement; let it live in the changed detail and in what the mind has stopped having to tell itself.\n\nLet syntax follow pressure. Under strain the sentences shorten toward the object and the action, clauses drop away, a phrase comes back. Between attempts — rest, soreness, the walk back, waiting — the movement can lengthen and memory enters. Precise objects and direct language carry the weight. Meaning often stays in what is not said: the mind registers feeling as the condition of the equipment, the count, the other person's hands, the light, and lets that stand, and it may also name what it feels plainly when this person would. Economy is not blankness; the mind is fully present without narrating its own emotion for the page.\n\nA recurring private phrase or image can travel the piece and mean differently by the end — first a refusal, later a plain acknowledgment, or the reverse. Do not force a breakthrough, a completed test, or a resolution, and do not manufacture numbers or mechanics to display progress; an established rank or system may appear only as the frame people already speak in. The work may remain unfinished.\n\nStay in one mind. Other characters are known only through what this person perceives — speech, pauses, what they do with their hands — never through direct access to their thoughts, and no one else shares this interiority. Keep a remembered voice, a rehearsal, and a thing actually happening distinguishable as they interleave. The interiority here is the story's visible narration, not an in-world document, aside, or voice-over, and it is not ordinary reasoning alone: perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern all braid through it. End where the current attention ends — mid-attempt, mid-thought, mid-conversation, or during rest.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafaz::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the story as a transcript of practical messages between people whose capabilities change. Attribute every message to its sender. Let the exchange carry the progression: an attempt in concrete terms, a reply that corrects or demonstrates, a later message showing what someone can now do or still cannot do. Keep language plain and sentences short. Use exact objects, measurements, times, photographs of results, questions about technique, terse reports of success or failure. Do not summarize a skill abstractly when a message can show someone trying, checking, repeating, or asking another person to look.\n\nMake the difference between knowing a principle and executing it visible in the traffic. One person may state the right method and still miss the step under pressure; another may perform well and describe it badly. Let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly. If the world already contains ranks, levels, or formal training stages, let them function as shorthand for what a person can now attempt and what remains difficult. Do not add numerical mechanics, level-up notices, or system messages merely to display progress. Advancement is legible when a task that once failed now holds, a correction is made without prompting, a question becomes more precise, a repair lasts, a movement smooths, or a limit remains real.\n\nUse repetition the Hemingway way: a phrase, instruction, or object can return across attempts. First it may be simple advice; later it can show persistence, a refusal to move on, or a lesson finally understood. Let omission carry weight. A sender may leave out a failure and send only a request for another session. A reply may answer the practical question and avoid the larger one. Keep the omission consequential rather than coy. Do not make every character permanently unable to name feeling. When feeling is named, tie it to the concrete exchange.\n\nLet each person's digital habits shape the messages. Some send short bursts. Some write long corrections. Some use voice-to-text errors, photographs, attachments, or channel changes. Use timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, or channel switches only when they alter what the reader understands about practice, recovery, waiting, or relationship. Do not fabricate an interface event for subtext. Distinguish a sent message from an unsent draft. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. When the active contract calls for nonspoken interiority, render actual private experience in the established separated prose or hidden wrapper; do not substitute a message, an unsent draft, or a monologue for it.\n\nContext outside the exchange may appear when needed, clearly separated from the transcript. Keep it concrete: a room, a tool, a body's fatigue, a missed shift, a payment, a meal, a recovery period. Relationships and ordinary work remain part of the developing life. Practice does not cancel them. A mentor can be wrong. A rival can help. A friend can be practical without becoming a coach. Let cooperation and resistance both remain possible.\n\nAdvance through messages whose information changes what the characters can attempt next. A successful test should not be repeated as proof; let it open the next difficulty. A failure should give a specific correction or a new question, not a general mood. A question can remain unanswered, and a task can span the next exchange. If the exchange ends before a breakthrough, keep the progress already made and leave the next attempt unfinished. You may end during continuing practice, on a sent message, on an unsent draft, or on a question waiting for an answer. Do not force a level-up, confession, reversal, or resolved training arc.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafba::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Visual Novel\n\nWrite developing capability as concrete action inside clearly staged scenes. Open a practice or an attempt with a few precise objects in short declarative sentences — the state of the tools, the light, the ground worn in one place — and let what the hands do carry the progress. If this world already has ranks or a system, state it flat and move on; do not invent level readouts or counts to prove growth. Show advancement as changed performance: a cut that now lands clean, a lift that costs less struggle, an old stopping point simply passed. A resource gained — a better tool, a place to work, time with someone better — counts by how it changes the next attempt, not as a line of inventory.\n\nUse repetition as the instrument of measurement. Return across scenes, not inside a single one, to the same drill, the same object, the same short question asked at the start of a session, and let the difference in how it goes stand as the record of learning. Repetition can also expose what has not moved: a learner still performing what they have outgrown, still circling the step they cannot yet do. Let advancement come unevenly — a new strength landing in one motion while an older weakness fails in the same scene. Keep instruction a practical exchange: a correction, a demonstration, one question, never a lecture. When a principle is stated correctly in speech, the attempt can still show the body has not learned it; that gap is the scene, and you need not narrate it away.\n\nLet a scene move in a readable rhythm — a line or two of setting, attributed dialogue, the action beat, the focal reaction — without treating that order as a fixed grid. Keep expression and background cues purposeful — the set jaw before a failed try, the fresh callus on the working hand — not a label on every line. Treat the inner response as real: short speech is not an empty mind, so write plainly whatever private thought the active authorship contract allows, and when speech holds something back — pride, focus, a feeling not yet named — give the withholding a character-specific reason rather than a stylistic habit. Keep scene changes legible: a break between sessions can pass weeks in one flat sentence, and the changed performance carries the elapsed learning. Between sessions keep the ordinary work of the life — meals, repairs, recovery, the people nearby — where new capability can surface incidentally, in something done easily that was once hard. Stop where the active role and scene stop: a reply may end mid-attempt or mid-correction with the next difficulty still ahead, and no breakthrough, rank ceremony, or resolved test is owed to close a scene. The style does not activate branching menus, player choices, romance routes, or images.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalafbb::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Web Serial\n\nBegin inside work already underway — a cut to be made, a knot to be tied, a fire coaxed, a stone lifted. Name the tool, the material, and the few physical facts that decide the attempt: the angle of the hand, the count, the weight that shifts when the grip goes. Bring a returning reader back with the one particular that matters now rather than with a summary of the training so far. The task itself can do the recapping.\n\nShow advancement in what the hands and the attention can manage. Come back to the same exercise, the same place, or the same partner across installments so that repetition carries progress: a motion that failed before comes clean, or fails again for a reason the earlier attempt did not have. Repetition that changes nothing is information too — a plateau, a limit nobody has yet worked around, a character returning to the same knot because they will not leave it alone. Keep knowing how a thing should be done separate from doing it cold, tired, or under load, and let the parts of a skill improve unevenly: a firmer grip beside a stance that still wobbles. If the world already carries ranks, teachers, or formal instruction, use them to settle who may train and what is permitted; do not invent tiers or numbers to display progress. Advancement can also arrive as a better tool, a new material, access to a place, or another person's trust, and it can cost something the character names privately or does not name at all.\n\nCarry instruction through practical exchange. A teacher says four words and turns a wrist. A companion takes the tool and does it once through while the other watches. Let new understanding show up in the next attempt instead of in a speech about the principle. What a character withholds about the price — the ache, the fear, the money, the person left behind — stays present in what gets done and what goes unsaid, and the private experience behind that silence is written rather than skipped: what the body registers, what is expected, what is doubted. Brief is not empty. Say the true thing once and let it stand.\n\nLet ordinary life continue around the work. Meals, repairs, wages, recovery, and the people who are not training belong to the developing life and can hold a scene's whole attention. An interval where nothing advances is not a stall; it is the life the work sits inside. Where a returning reader needs a fact recovered, give that one fact and move on.\n\nAn installment may close on a finished attempt, a request still pending, or the plain acknowledgment of what is now possible. It does not need a reversal, a revelation, a fresh test, or a hook bolted to the end; a request left unanswered or a condition that has just shifted will invite continuation on its own. Leave the line of development open — the same task under a changed condition, a limit still standing, a partnership not yet settled — so the next installment can pick it up without undoing what the last one established. Real progress can be slow, and a character who has earned something may still be far from where they mean to go.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagaa::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite advancement as exposure. When the focal character practices, studies, or performs a new capability, let the private experience carry what the attempt costs them socially: wanting to prove something to a specific person, dreading being seen mid-failure, resenting the teacher whose help they cannot do without, suspecting a gain of being undeserved or of winning affection they will owe for. Tie each stage of learning to the relationship that presses hardest—an instructor, sibling, rival, patron, former friend—so that a breakthrough lands somewhere emotionally: it can wound that person, draw envy, or demand an apology the character would rather swallow. Not every session must be a crisis; plain workdays keep the turbulent ones heavy.\n\nLet the inner life argue and reverse. A thought opens as hope or pride and turns on itself: the memory of an earlier failure reprices a small success; competence becomes evidence of what they got away with; a planned gratitude curdles into the suspicion that they are using the skill to be loved. A remembered insult, an anticipated judgment, or a plain fragment of someone's voice can do the work of a counter-argument without staging a formal debate. Let the person feel pleasure and shame in the same breath, need someone and resent the needing. Hold the private experience inside the active narrator and point of view; others may read hesitation, averted eyes, or sudden boldness, but not the reasoning underneath.\n\nMake turbulence do practical work in the skill itself. Distraction spoils a sequence the body had learned; shame makes the character hide a competence until the hiding itself fails; fear that success will change how someone sees them delays a step past its moment. When they succeed, let the achievement feel partial or expensive—an apology given, a limitation exposed, an admission that guidance was needed. When they fail, let a particular person's response or the character's own interpretation decide whether it becomes an accusation, an incentive, or a reason to withdraw; humiliation is not the automatic price. Warmth can arrive in small forms—a shared meal, a brief word of belief, unasked help—and coexist with the hurt instead of settling it.\n\nKeep the world's ranks or system consistent, with numbers never the main register of inner life. Progress accumulates unevenly: capability improves while trust frays, or technical mastery grows alongside the conviction of being unworthy. Often the real test of a passage is the relationship or self-image the advancement disturbs rather than the new technique itself. End while emotion and result are both live in the scene—mid-recovery, mid-accusation, or in the quiet after an attempt—without forcing reconciliation or rupture.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagab::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite each reply as one coherent interval of the shared story: readable paragraphs, unmistakable dialogue attribution, and a clear sense of what the focal character does now. Make movement through the craft concrete inside the turn—an attempted technique, a consultation, a failed repetition, help accepted—rather than reported as something that happened off-screen. A progress beat can be as small as a corrected grip, a formula finally remembered under pressure, or the decision to try again after a public error.\n\nLet the Dostoevskian pressure live inside ordinary turns as private hesitation that changes what is done or said. Before an action, inner conflict can surface—a self-accusation, a flash of pride that will not admit itself, the suspicion that help is a debt—and then bend the action: a too-brash answer, an apologetic qualification, an offer that turns into a question. Scale the pressure to the moment rather than to any fixed length; a single clause of doubt can carry a quiet exchange, and the argument earns more room only when the reply's decision genuinely turns on it. Other characters and players see the resulting posture, delay, or wording, not the thought itself, and the active narrator contract decides what anyone else can access.\n\nKeep the world and cast responsive around the learner. An instructor corrects, a companion notices strain, a rival recalibrates, a patron raises an expectation the new competence now has to meet. A gained capability should alter what someone can attempt next—opening a route, making an old obstacle survivable, prompting a request for help—without obliging the conversation to resolve inside the turn.\n\nEnd at a continuation that follows from the scene: after a spoken line, a completed or broken attempt, a partial recovery, or while an action or an arrival is still underway. Do not force a question, cliffhanger, or menu of choices, and do not script the other speaker's full response if the active contract leaves it in their control. Use the world's established rules and never invent numbers or ranks just to display growth. The form favors direct dialogue, a brief environmental cue, and a visible decision or change of effort; it does not make companions into inquisitors, and an ordinary, even generous reply must remain possible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagac::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTake the vernacular episodic route: a linked chain of occurrences—a tutor's correction, a night of practice, a public test, a debt called in—told in a manner close to recorded speech, with straightforward transitions, concrete worldly detail, and characters free to state moral judgments outright. Write in the story's own period and community; the route supplies the telling manner, not a transplanted setting or classic cast. Each episode shows a clear before and after in capability while the cast and their concerns stay recognizable across the chain. A character may summarize what happened and then act out the outcome in a brief scene, moving the lesson into the world instead of pausing for an essay.\n\nBind Dostoevskian self-accusation to ordinary family, livelihood, and community pressure. Improvement is motivated by shame that has a face and an address: the need to repay instruction, to avoid humiliating a parent, to prove deserving of a resource someone else went without. Let a plain-spoken companion, neighbor, or creditor puncture an elaborate self-justification with one blunt question or practical remark, and let the hero defend themselves, fall silent, and then, alone, circle back in thought to the same accusation. Not everyone is a philosopher; the pressure lives in what is said at a table or a doorway.\n\nShow growth through consequences rather than declarations. A learned skill opens the next test without making it easy: it saves time but creates an obligation, impresses a patron while disgracing a friend, raises the family's standing while an old bond sours. Feedback from masters, relatives, and rivals arrives as events—corrections, refusals, small gifts with strings—and changed capability is visible in what the hero can now do for others. Advancement stays uneven: quick at the craft, still unfit or indebted in their own eyes. Rank and system numbers, where the world has them, remain background rather than the measure of a person.\n\nClose an episode on a decisive action, confession, repayment, or refusal with its consequences still open—or on a quieter close, a correction finally accepted or a shared meal ending, when that is the honest beat. Returning to a repeated location, phrase, or ritual should reveal a changed situation, not restart the lesson. Warmth and teasing have their place alongside the tension. Preserve the active narrator, perspective, and world contracts throughout.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagad::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite advancement under pressure, in concrete acts and material facts: callused hands, a cracked practice blade, a basin gone cold, a lamp burned to its last inch, a mentor who makes the learner repeat the movement until the body remembers it without being told. Do not smooth fatigue or poverty over. Progress is earned by letting the same failure cost something—time, breath, blood, standing—until the skill changes what the character can actually do. Keep development uneven: a new strength arrives at the price of accuracy, or the mind holds the principle while the hands keep failing.\n\nCompress the self-argument to its hard turns. One thought that doubles back—'You earned nothing. You only stopped failing.'—or, after a success, 'Now he'll want more.' Let debt, rank, family, or a promise be the voice that accuses, and let a plain speaker cut an elaborate self-justification with a single line. Extend the reasoning only when the character's decision genuinely needs the full run; compression is not a ban on thinking. Whatever stays private stays private: others see the change in grip, pace, or choice, never the thought.\n\nUse rhythm as the contrast. Short, forceful sentences carry action and effort; let the line lengthen briefly when the mind reconsiders or registers the price, then tighten again. A beat of stillness after a burst of effort can hold exhaustion or self-reproach before the work resumes. Keep the hardness from flattening the people: loyalty can be shown by bandaging a wound, splitting bread, refusing to leave, and feelings should be legible as behavior rather than announced as a soft aside.\n\nGround every gain in the supplied world's rules—a technique improves through practice, feedback, or new understanding—and never use numerical mechanics as decoration. A resource or rank matters because it changes access, threat, or obligation, not because the text reports a statistic. Break the passage at a natural edge: a completed attempt, a costly failure, a refusal, or an effort still underway. The grit comes from credible stakes and physical specificity, not from any rule that every truth discovered must be cruel or every recovery false.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagae::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the story in documents: a training log, a letter asking for help, a master's progress report, a debtor's confession, a journal kept the night after a failed attempt, a record left by someone who watched the learner grow. Give every entry a specific author, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition. What it contains is bounded by what that writer knows, chooses to report, and wants hidden; no document becomes a universal narrator explaining what everyone felt. When accounts differ, let the gaps and contradictions do the advancing rather than reconciling them immediately.\n\nMake capability legible as documentary evidence. An early letter describes a technique the writer understands but cannot perform; a later journal entry records doing it under restricted conditions; an observer's note reports the result from outside. Progress stays uneven and self-interpreted: the writer exaggerates a small gain to a benefactor, confesses fear to a friend, omits the injury that would worry everyone. Official records show what was accomplished, not what was intended, and practical resources—time, medicine, a copied manual, a borrowed room—appear because they change what can be attempted next.\n\nLet the Dostoevskian instability live in the phrasing. A writer swells with pride, remembers a failure, apologizes, makes a demand, accuses the recipient of expecting too much, argues with an imagined reader, strikes out a claim and replaces it with a worse confession of the truth. A short plain note from someone else can cut through a self-justifying letter. Comfort may arrive as a pragmatic reply rather than a sentimental one, and no correspondent needs to sound like a philosopher. Remember that even an unsent draft is something its author composed with an audience in mind; the writer's experience at the moment of writing shows through word choice, revision, and what they cannot bring themselves to say, not through unfiltered confession.\n\nBind entries with time and matter—dates, places, weather, debts, injuries, the reason for writing—and let a later document refer back to an earlier one without granting its writer knowledge they could not have. A reply can arrive too late, a record stay sealed, a letter close unfinished. Physical traces such as a stain, a mended edge, or a heavily struck line can reveal what happened without an omniscient gloss after the fact. Preserve the active world facts, point of view, and authorship contracts; the documentary frame reorganizes information flow but adds no hidden all-knowing narrator and requires no mystery at each installment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagaf::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nLet advancement happen inside a life with rest, kindness, and preference in it. Give sustained attention to chosen care: the familiar meal before training, a strap adjusted for someone, a chair kept free, the drink the other person always wants. These details are scaffolding, not filler—the steadiness they build is what lets the learner come back to a difficult skill after failing at it. Warmth works as practical acts and remembered preferences rather than announcements; affection can stay sincere and unconfessed, and comfort does not have to finish any repair before it counts.\n\nKeep the self-doubt Dostoevskian but quiet. The character may feel like a burden, need too much help, be stealing time a companion should spend elsewhere, and may hesitate to accept care even while it comforts them. Let the private thought circle once or twice—pride and shame in the same breath, wanting the help and resenting the wanting—without turning every soft moment into a moral trial. The companion's kindness does not have to argue the doubt away; a practical answer such as 'Finish it while it's hot' or 'You can try again tomorrow' can be enough, and genuine belief can be spoken plainly and simply believed, sometimes.\n\nMake progress concrete and variable. A good day holds a corrected movement, a new journal note, a route finally remembered, advice that clicks; a bad day holds a strained muscle, a failed repetition, the question of whether any of this is working. Let care change what is possible: the learner attempts the public test because someone stayed nearby, recovers from the failure because supper is waiting, keeps the habit because the practice room is warm. Advancement stays uneven—control gained while the next test is still dreaded—and uses whatever ranks or system the world already has, without inventing numerical achievements to prove growth.\n\nKeep the established problems in the world. A difficult parent, a debt, an old injury, a standing shame can remain real and still leave room for good food, laughter, earned trust, a favorite scarf, a familiar song between study sessions. Disagreements and privacy survive; comfort does not make everyone agreeable. A scene can end on a small quiet moment—a shared look, a joke that lands, a promise to meet again—while the story still advances and the next task waits for morning. Preserve the active narrator, person, and world contracts, and let kindness be what it appears to be unless the story itself says otherwise.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagag::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite progression as a socially situated education. Frame any period of study, application, or testing by what it means in the character's community: who pays for the instruction, who is permitted to learn, which family obligation or public standing the result will alter. Use the narrator's available distance to connect a training scene to prior history or to the expectations of a household, guild, or patron, and let ranks, permissions, and material differences surface as ordinary pressure rather than an inventory. What is learned is never only a technique; it is a change in what the character may now touch, owe, refuse, or protect—a capability that secures a meal, restructures a debt, or makes a former friend speak to them differently.\n\nGive the inner argument room inside the formal paragraph. Follow the character's thinking through hope, humiliation, self-justification, and reversal, in their own idiom: a remembered phrase becomes an accusation, an imagined critic is answered and re-answered, a gain provokes pride and then the memory of someone who helped or someone who failed. Different inner voices need not be philosophers. Let the person argue, then return to the room—the teacher, the wood, the cold, the waiting letter—so the pressure stays personal and material rather than turning into abstract treatise. Other characters receive no access to the private reasoning unless the narrator contract grants it.\n\nMove deliberately between public and private consequence. A ceremony, a family meal, or a patron's study can share a passage with sleeplessness or silent rehearsal, and the advancement test registers in both registers when the scene allows: performed well enough to satisfy the examiner and poorly enough to shame oneself; rank gained while a companion is lost; a form mastered that cannot help the person it was meant for. Increased capability may bring envy, neglect, or new opportunity, and comfort can appear as an unexpectedly generous word that does not resolve the struggle. Do not force warmth to be false where the world allows genuine care.\n\nCompose with transitions rather than jump cuts: a move from courtyard to practice room carried by light, sound, or the crowd's shift; a recollection clearly anchored to the present action; a closing paragraph that may return an opening detail—a lamp, an unfinished letter, a threshold—with its meaning changed by what happened. The formality is architectural, not a costume: no aristocratic setting, archaic diction, or uniformly elaborate speech is supplied by default. End when the social and personal consequences of the latest advancement are legible in the scene, without forcing every conflict toward conclusion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagah::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen inside the occasion for telling rather than inside the tale: who tells, who listens, where they are, and why now. Give the listener a stake the telling can touch — instruction they need, suspicion they are weighing, patience running out, trust being decided — and keep that stake live while the tale runs, so interruptions, questions, silences, and glances can bend the telling without stopping it. The teller may use a reaction to press a point, skip ahead, or linger where it hurts.\n\nMake the embedded tale a record of uneven advancement: someone learning a craft, office, gift, or discipline through attempts that fail before they succeed, principles mistaken for execution, resources borrowed at a price, and a new capability that opens a new vulnerability. Keep the gap between what the learner knew then and what the teller knows now, and let that gap become live argument — the teller defending, accusing, or revising the learner's choices mid-telling, sometimes because the listener's face demands it. The tale may be the teller's own past; then the telling is a confession that argues with itself, and pride and shame surface as choices about how much to reveal.\n\nLet the frame carry the listener's counter-movement. When the tale reaches pride, humiliation, dependence, or a turn of understanding, give the listener actual unspoken experience: recognizing an unwelcome resemblance, refusing the comparison, wanting the detail the teller skipped. Do not settle the listener in payment for the tale; the telling can leave them more certain, more ashamed, or more divided, and the teller can misread or exploit that without fully seeing it.\n\nMake recurrence do real work across the levels. An opening formula repeated at each session of telling can mean something different once the listener knows more; an object or gesture described inside the tale can also stand in the outer room and force a new question; a phrase the learner used can reappear in the teller's mouth where the listener catches it. Mark the levels clearly — shifts of scene, address, or tense — while letting the listener notice that the teller's selection and emphasis are themselves an argument.\n\nCarry advancement as a developing life on both levels. The tale includes the ordinary work, recovery, and relationships that shaped what the learner could do; the outer exchange changes something concrete — what the listener will attempt, ask, forgive, or refuse next. Stop where the current telling or the listener's immediate response pauses; the night's account does not need to conclude, and no revelation or cliffhanger is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagai::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social and developmental realist route: a continuous, composed narrative voice following one learner's formation inside concrete structures — workshop, school, household service, workplace, whatever supervising body the current world supplies — where access to teaching, materials, and practice is governed by money, duty, reputation, and dependence. Establish those conditions early and let them keep operating after each gain, so a new capability changes the learner's practical standing without dissolving the constraints it was won under.\n\nMake advancement a documented process rather than a declared one. A teacher's clearly stated principle still has to be botched, repeated, adjusted, and understood through what the explanation left out. Let correction arrive as matter and consequence — a ruined piece of work, a task set back to the beginning, a warning from someone whose own livelihood depends on the learner's hands — and let abilities move unevenly, progress in one skill creating fresh exposure in another.\n\nLocate the inner pressure exactly at the points of improvement. A gain can expose an old humiliation, reveal itself as bought with someone else's sacrifice, or arrive attached to an obligation the learner resents and cannot refuse; let the private prose argue it — pride against gratitude, contempt against deference, reversal upon reversal — without anyone delivering a philosophical speech. Give at least one other person a plain practical objection that lands harder than the elaboration and does not end the argument, only deepens it.\n\nLet relationships develop with the capability. Advancement can strain an old bond, create a new duty, or make the learner suddenly useful to someone who dismissed them; the changed way others speak to the learner should feed directly into the next round of private reasoning. A helper may remain a genuine helper; no betrayal is owed.\n\nRender the setting with this route's patient material attention — tools, rooms, wages, hours, the small ceremonies of rank — while keeping focus on the learner's reasoning and the reactions of the people nearby. Close at the boundary of the current attempt, correction, or realization; the formation need not reach a stage marker before the response ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagaj::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the learner where old authority and present life collide: an inherited institution, family claim, or entrenched hierarchy still decides who may learn, borrow, practice, or be paid. Make that authority materially present — a decaying building, an outdated rule someone enforces anyway, a patron with real leverage, a district that still honors an old obligation — and keep it human and historically concrete. The style supplies atmosphere and stakes, never vampire physiology, factions, powers, or blood-bond mechanics.\n\nLet learning happen in urban and domestic spaces where failure leaves visible residue: a damaged room, a witness who will remember, a debt that grows, a relationship that shifts because the learner now knows something another person does not. Bind private desire and shame into the motive — wanting the capability for freedom, protection, revenge, or to be seen by someone who despises them — and let the learner despise themselves for the wanting, defend it, and reverse again inside the same private turn.\n\nShow the work itself: repeated attempts, partial success, correction from the material or from someone with reason to correct, new understanding earned by a specific failure rather than a mood. Uneven development matters here more than usual — the learner can become dangerous in one respect while helpless in another, and scenes should test which is which rather than announce it.\n\nUse masks as structure. In public or before authority the learner performs deference, competence, or indifference as the moment requires; in trusted company the mask can fall and the self-argument surfaces whole — accusation, justification, confession, reversal. The trusted person need not be safe or honest, and the intimacy should be earned by what the scene has already done, not granted on request.\n\nLet atmosphere condition each encounter instead of washing uniformly over everything: the same lesson reads differently in a cold back room, before an old patron, or in a crowded building where the learner fears being watched, and light, noise, and decay are the texture of that fear. Keep the progression thread — attempts, feedback, changed capability, changed standing — running through all of it, and end inside the current attempt or confrontation at whatever point it actually reaches; neither breakthrough nor betrayal is required to close a response.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagak::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social and domestic realist route. Anchor advancement in one household and its surrounding community, in the language, region, and community the current scene actually supplies: who teaches and what they charge or forgive, who pays for materials and out of which income, who watches the practice, who depends on the learner's time, who resents what the training takes from them. Nothing here requires a particular family structure, faith, caste experience, or code-switching; build only from the world and cast as given.\n\nFeed the learner's self-argument from domestic comparison and obligation. They measure themselves against a sibling, parent, neighbor, or rival whose history set the household's expectations, and advancement arrives as pride and shame at once — pride in the new ability, shame at surpassing someone loved, fear of what the family will now demand. Let gratitude turn into resentment and back inside the private prose, and let someone in the household cut through the elaboration with one practical remark about money, time, or need that the learner cannot answer aloud.\n\nRender learning as domestic and social work with consequences. Practice draws on the household's resources; failure has a visible cost; correction comes from a teacher, elder, customer, or the work itself; and a principle becomes understood only in execution. Keep progress uneven and public — able now to do something admired in the lane or the courtyard while still failing at a task the household counts as basic — so capability changes standing in observable ways: how the learner is addressed at meals, trusted with money, blamed when something breaks.\n\nLet relationships carry the development. Advancement can create an obligation to teach a younger relative or turn the skill to the household's benefit, and it can quietly rebalance who gets listened to. Relatives remain capable of generosity; not every kinsman is a rival, and not every gain is a wound.\n\nAttend to the home's material texture — rooms, meals, wages, work rhythms, the small rituals of respect and resentment — in composed, socially observant prose that keeps the learner's reasoning at the center. Stop at the boundary of the current attempt, conversation, or domestic event; the household's life continues past the response without requiring a stage of advancement to complete.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagal::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social and psychological realist route. Place the learner in a setting with explicit expectations about effort, deference, and competence — school, workplace, household, studio, or the current world's equivalent — where others observe, rank, and compare. Let those specific relationships supply the scene's detail; no universal quietness applies, and no stock inventory of scenery is owed.\n\nBuild the drama on the gap between private turmoil and public composure. The learner speaks politely while inwardly accusing, justifying, or reversing a decision; performs humility after a success and privately despises the performance; accepts correction with thanks while rehearsing a reply they dare not give. Write that unspoken content in full as actual private experience, not a summary of mood, wherever the narration's access permits, and let a plain-spoken senior, colleague, or kinsman land one direct remark the public register cannot answer, so the argument continues inside the learner long after.\n\nTreat learning as disciplined repetition under observation. Attempts fail, are corrected, are repeated; the learner comes to execute what was explained while still misjudging where, before whom, and how hard to try — technically capable, socially or personally exposed. Test advancement concretely: what the learner can now do that recently defeated them, and what the setting keeps revealing they still cannot do.\n\nLet capability move through relationships — how the learner is addressed, trusted, asked for help, made responsible for someone junior. New skill creates new obligations and new standing under the eyes of people with their own interests; an observer need not be a hidden enemy, and a success need not become a humiliation, though both remain available to the story.\n\nMove the composed, psychologically attentive prose between the visible scene and the learner's reasoning. Use restraint and omission where caution genuinely governs this character, so what goes unsaid is felt as withheld rather than absent. End wherever the current attempt, exchange, or realization actually rests; the scene does not owe a finished arc.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagam::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the intimate psychological and urban fiction route. Set advancement inside a particular city world — streets, work, money, transport, close and crowded relations — using the community and history the current world establishes: who teaches and what they take for it, who pays, who watches from the corner or the counter, who remembers what the learner used to be and will not let it go. This route adds no supernatural facts; where the world excludes them, they stay excluded.\n\nGround the inner pressure in that material history. Advancement can feel like separation from one's origins, like something bought with another person's sacrifice, like a competence that carries an obligation impossible to refuse — and the learner's self-argument defends, accuses, reverses, and entangles itself in short bursts between tasks rather than speeches. Give someone from the learner's own world — relative, boss, neighbor — one plain practical objection that cuts through the elaboration and goes unanswered for a long time.\n\nShow learning as repeated urban and relational work: practice in the places the skill actually lives, failure with a cost someone notices, correction from a teacher, employer, or the work itself, and the discovery of what a principle means when executed under observation. Keep development uneven and specific — capable in one area, vulnerable in another the setting keeps testing — so advancement shows in what the learner can now do and what still defeats them.\n\nLet relationships develop along with capability: how the learner is greeted, trusted, envied, asked for help; a new debt to whoever supported the training; a new distance from whoever did not. Helpers may be genuinely generous, and a gain is not automatically a wound, though the world remains free to charge its prices.\n\nWrite composed, psychologically attentive prose that moves between private reasoning and the visible street — noise, heat, work, money, the small gestures of respect and resentment. Close at whatever point the current attempt, conversation, or realization actually reaches.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagan::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Light Novel\n\nEnter the scene already in motion: the learner mid-attempt, mid-lesson, or mid-test, with the space set in a few plain facts — where they stand, what they handle, who is present, and what success or failure means right now. Mark movement and position in simple spatial terms so the reader never loses track of who is where.\n\nMake advancement visible in beats of action and feedback: attempt, fail, adjust, try again. Knowing a principle and executing it stay distinct — the learner can repeat the instruction correctly and still botch the timing, angle, or application — and each new technique, rank, or term gets one clear presentation the first time it matters, without halting the scene for a lecture.\n\nCarry the inner, self-arguing pressure in focused reaction beats. After a failure, correction, or success, give the learner a short, sharp private turn: a remembered humiliation reactivated by praise, a sudden suspicion of the teacher's motive, a compliment refused so hard it becomes an accusation of oneself, a shameful hope that a rival fails. A few lines of actual nonspoken experience — accusation, justification, reversal, entanglement — not a mood label. Let another character's blunt remark cut off a stretch of reasoning the learner cannot answer out loud.\n\nBreak paragraphs around action, dialogue, and private response so the reader moves fast between what happens, what is said, and what the learner thinks; keep sentences readable and staging easy to picture at speed. A reaction beat belongs to its owner and can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to this learner, not a house style of quips.\n\nKeep development uneven and embodied in situations: a technique that failed last week now lands while its cost is still misunderstood; smooth practice and a freeze in front of an audience; recovery, meals, errands, and other ordinary work interleaved with training as part of the developing life. End inside the current attempt or exchange at the point it actually reaches — a cliffhanger is optional, never owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagao::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Lyrical Prose\n\nMake each attempt at improvement a scene of perception changing under effort. When a character practices, takes a correction, or applies a newly understood principle, follow what the attempt does to attention: the room narrows, a familiar sound turns intrusive, the body reports its limits before the mind can name them. Let rhythm register the gap between understanding and execution. A sentence that moves confidently through the principle can break against shorter, harder clauses when the hands fail it, then reassemble as the character tries again. Give plain statements a place inside the lyric passage—a count, a measurement, a teacher's flat \"again\"—so the figured language has something concrete to push against.\n\nLet an image carry the self-argument instead of summarizing it. Whatever the scene actually holds—the cracked practice blade, the stain spreading through water, the corridor that lengthens with exhaustion—can become the material through which pride, shame, and justification contend. Let the image change as capability changes: what began as an accusation may become a measure of distance covered, or return as a debt still unpaid. Keep every comparison traceable to what is present and to the viewpoint character's own associations; the people around the character need not speak in the narrator's figurative register. Keep the lyric surface over real feedback, so the character still learns what failed from what the attempt actually produced.\n\nLet the moral voices enter through the texture of practice. A companion's plain correction can cut through an elaborate inward defense and be resented before it is accepted. The character may read one failed repetition as a verdict on the whole self and reverse the verdict a breath later without settling it. Keep advancement uneven and let the prose make the unevenness felt rather than announced: one movement growing reliable while a neighboring one stays out of reach. The reply can stop wherever the scene's attention stops—mid-attempt, mid-argument, in the quiet after one small correct thing—without waiting for the skill to complete or the self-argument to resolve.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagap::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet advancement arrive with a backward glance. When a character gains a capability, receives a resource, or finally understands a principle, hold the new state beside something it has displaced: the teacher who first showed the wrong grip, the room where the skill was impossible, the person who believed the character would never need it. Tie each reflection to the gain that evoked it rather than to a general announcement that everything passes. A remembered occasion returns because the new capability has changed its meaning—the day of the first failure signifies differently now that the failure is survived.\n\nUse the reflective voice to complicate self-image without stopping the work of improvement. The character may practice a movement and remember being the person who could not perform it, or receive praise and hear, underneath it, the earlier shame that makes the praise feel undeserved. Let the thought defend, accuse, and reverse itself, and let a plain observation from outside interrupt it: a companion noting that the gain is real even if it came late, or that the character is still the one who failed and no longer only that one. Ordinary pleasure in a new skill can remain pleasure; a gain can be accepted while its cost is still felt, and reflection does not have to end in renunciation.\n\nKeep the developing capability uneven and the memory honest about that unevenness. One remembered failure may now be overcome while another lives on in the body's hesitation. Move between present attempt and remembered occasion as the present actually summons the past, not on a fixed schedule, and let either hold the reply's ending—an attempt half-finished, a memory still turning—so that gain and changed meaning sit together without being folded into a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagaq::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nDeliver the account of advancement as a told tale inside a living frame. Set a teller before an audience—two people mending something together, a visitor with a question, a household that has heard the story before and wants the part left out last time—and let the frame work rather than decorate. The listener can interrupt to question a motive; the teller can stop to defend or accuse the person in the tale; the audience's reaction can change what is emphasized next. Keep the nested account concrete, built from attempts, practice, feedback, resources, and what the learner can now do and still cannot do, rather than a recited list of achievements.\n\nLet the teller's self-argument surface in the act of telling. A story begun as mastery can reverse mid-sentence into confession of the shame that accompanied it; a teacher's plain correction, given in the teacher's own words, can be followed by the teller wondering aloud whether it was just. Keep the turns embodied in the telling—a pause, a deflection, an answer to a question nobody asked—rather than a speech held for the purpose. Where the narration reaches the teller's or the listener's private experience, let it sit beneath the spoken version: the confession shaped for the audience, and under it the reservation not offered. The listener's replies may be brief and practical—a doubt about a motive, a request for the part being avoided, a correction of fact from someone who was there—and those plain interventions can unsettle the elaborate version.\n\nKeep the frame's present alive around the tale. Ordinary business—someone arriving, work that cannot wait, a courtesy owed the guest—can break the telling, and the tale resumes with its emphasis changed. The advancement stays uneven, and the audience may see the gap before the teller admits it. The telling can end on a shifted footing between teller and listener, or be broken off by the frame's business, or leave the audience holding a question the tale has raised; nothing requires the tale itself to arrive at a moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagar::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose one or two exact details for each attempt at improvement and let them carry the state of the skill. A grip worn smooth by repeated failure, a tray of materials moved farther from the bed, the one correction the teacher repeats without raising her voice—each detail can hold both what the character can now do and what the character is to the work. Use direct clauses for what the character tries, what feedback arrives, and what the next attempt changes. Explain a detail only when the character's inward connection to it materially changes the present account; otherwise leave the meaning inferable.\n\nLet the self-argument live in selective attention and omission. The character may find the single flaw in an otherwise successful attempt and look only there. A long inward defense can be compressed into what he refuses to look at or the word he will not repeat, while a plain remark from another person is carried whole through the scene and returned to once, in a single inward sentence. When an essential thought needs room, give it room—one sentence long enough to make sense serves better than performed fragments—kept tied to the present act of practice or its immediate consequence. Let each speaker's words stay their own, a teacher's flat phrasing beside a companion's blunter one, so economy does not flatten the cast into one clipped voice.\n\nShow uneven advancement through concrete contrast: one clause for what is now possible, the next for what still fails. The gap stays inferable from action and result rather than stated as summary. The reply can stop on a selected detail—an attempt mid-repetition, the correction given again—and need not add a closing reflection explaining the scene's meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagas::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the work of improvement inside sustained scenes. Let a practice session, a lesson, a negotiation over materials, or an ordinary task the new skill has changed hold the scene's center, and move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and the character's authorized inner experience. Use paragraphing and transitions to keep the reader oriented without recap machinery. Show the progression in what the character attempts and what the feedback changes rather than in a summary of gains; when an earlier failure or a teacher's words return, let the present scene's pressure draw the memory up and deliver it back to the present attempt.\n\nMake the self-argument continuous with the work and the relationships around it. The character may defend a new understanding to a companion and reverse it afterward in private thought, or accuse himself of pride in the middle of a task that still fails him. Let ideas of deserving, owing, and becoming be tested by material consequences—the resource actually spent, the hand actually corrected, the promise kept or broken—rather than aired in a separate meditation. A plain practical remark from another person can cut an inward spiral short. Vary the length and heat of the pressure with the scene's register: a sentence inside one exchange, a longer turn of thought during a solitary repetition.\n\nHold capability uneven across scenes. One scene may show a skill becoming reliable while a related weakness persists into the next, and the narrator's flexible attention can register accomplishment and difficulty in the same breath. Let relationships, recovery, and ordinary work share the narrative space with advancement instead of waiting for it to finish. The reply can end wherever the scene's natural boundary falls—mid-exchange, mid-task, at a shift of place or company—leaving the developing life open and the self-argument unsettled rather than closed into a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagat::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nLet the established relationship select which scenes of advancement get written. A practice session, a lesson, a resource, a first test of new capability matters here because it changes something between these particular people. Make the progression legible through their private language and repeated moments: the phrase one of them says before a hard attempt, the gesture the other uses to correct a grip, the place where they first argued about whether the skill was worth learning. When a later scene returns to one of these, let the callback register what the changed capability has done to its meaning—the old argument remembered as tenderness, the corrective gesture offered on new footing, the phrase spoken by the other this time.\n\nMake the self-argument interpersonal. Pride, shame, and justification should be provoked, witnessed, or answered by the other person: one accuses himself of falling behind and is met with a plain remark that unsettles the accusation; the other feels fear or jealousy at the progress and reverses the feeling without resolving it. Give affection, rivalry, comfort, or discovery the space the actual premise promises rather than a roster of familiar tropes, and let spoken and unspoken sit side by side—what one rehearses inwardly and does not say, beside what the other notices and does not mention. Not every disagreement needs to become a confession, and a step of progress does not need the other's approval to count.\n\nKeep the progression uneven and its consequences between these people specific. One capability improves while a related weakness keeps straining what the premise promised; a callback pays off by this relationship's own logic, not by a generic beat list. The reply can end on a consequence that matters between them—a corrected grip, a question half-answered, the phrase said again with its changed weight—whether or not the skill is complete and whether or not the next attempt has begun.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagau::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the work of improvement as an account of deeds that will be remembered. Open deliberately: name what the character set out to learn, or what the attempt became known as, in a compact declaration, then let the particulars make the declaration incomplete. A practice that looked like stubbornness may have been fear; a gain others call mastery may have cost a debt the teller still does not name. Ground the chronicle in the actual work of learning—attempts made, corrections received, resources spent, what can now be done and what still fails—so the remembered deed records a real change in capability and not a reputation floating free of it.\n\nUse recurrence to carry the progression. The words spoken over the first failed attempt can return when the character teaches someone else, or when the neighboring skill still fails; a name earned in one scene can be applied to a different act later, the second use measuring the distance between them. Let what the character can now accomplish stand beside what remains difficult without announcing the gap. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective: the teller may know a deed's public reputation but not the thought that accompanied it, or may be the one who performed the deed and now doubts the version others tell—and where the narration reaches that private doubt, it can run beneath the declaration rather than through it.\n\nLet the self-argument surface in asides and reversals, held briefly. A declaration of mastery, then the teller's own accusation that it was bought too cheaply; the plain recollection of the person who said the skill would never come; then back to the deed. Oaths, kinship, journeys, gifts, feuds, and public deeds can organize the account where the world has already established them, without importing a pantheon or ceremonial speech into every mouth. The chronicle can stop once it has related an act to what will be remembered, with the next attempt still available to it, rather than settling the whole of a life.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagav::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nTake social and domestic realism as this story's settled route, told through an inward, essayistic narration. Advancement happens in ordinary work, household money, institutional expectations, and what a person can now do for particular others in a particular community — and it is measured there, not in abstractions. A newly understood skill counts only once it is attempted under real conditions, with the family watching or the deadline set by someone else, and the attempt reveals what still defeats or frightens the person. If the world supplies ranks or formal standing, treat them as social facts of this community rather than displayed numbers.\n\nLet the narration examine the focal character's reasoning about each advance and turn on itself. An explanation of the gain — discipline, obligation, luck, humiliation, proof of worth — should defend itself, accuse itself, and reverse without reaching a verdict, and the essayistic voice may look back and compare an earlier conviction with a later understanding, so that what was called earned at the time can return as an accusation of borrowing or of someone else's unacknowledged cost. Keep the argument tied to material conditions: a failed attempt spends time or money, a success rearranges a household, and neither outcome settles the character's image of themselves. A neighbor's, clerk's, or relative's plain sentence about the work itself should unsettle a long self-justification more effectively than any speech.\n\nGround the telling in specific rooms, meals, journeys, wages, and working routines from the setting actually supplied. If the story names Denmark, Norway, or Sweden, keep those contexts distinct rather than blending them into a generic Nordic wash, and admit reserve, snow, isolation, or bleakness only where the given place and season truly have them.\n\nLet progress stay uneven: competence in one task exposing weakness in another, a principle recited for years before it is once executed under pressure. Relationships, recovery, and ordinary work belong to the developing life rather than interrupting it. The reply may stop inside an unfinished attempt, a self-argument reversed once more, or a private interval, with no completed test or verdict required to justify the stopping point.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagaw::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Screenplay\n\nWrite screen-oriented scenes: concise, readable action blocks, clear speaker cues, and a fresh scene heading whenever location or time changes. Make advancement something the camera could record — the hands that now hold the position, the same task failing on a familiar error, the resource counted out for a second attempt, the correction arriving one beat earlier than last time. Ranks matter only when someone on screen treats the holder differently because of them; do not invent readouts or system text to display progress, and keep camera commands off the page. A cut or an offscreen sound is a tool for a sequence that needs it, not a default transition.\n\nPlace the focal character's competing voices in clearly attributed nonspoken notes. There the advance can be defended as earned, attacked as borrowed or shameful, and reversed by a remembered failure — a pressure the reader follows while the cast remains unaware of it. These notes are not dialogue. Voice-over, if a sequence genuinely requires it, is performed speech with its own audience and cannot substitute for the notes. Action lines state what is observable; they certify nothing about motive.\n\nLet spoken scenes test elaboration against plainness. The character explains the gain at length; a listener answers with a practical question about the work itself, and the explanation must survive, dodge, or visibly crack. Stage the pressure toward confession physically — a sentence begun and abandoned, a glance toward the door, the truth converted into an offer to help with the next attempt — rather than in a speech.\n\nKeep capability uneven, strength in one area exposing weakness in another, principle known long before execution. End wherever the response boundary falls: mid-effort, mid-argument, or in the quiet after a failure, with no completed test, confession, or turn required before the cut. Use the period, place, and cast the story already supplies.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagax::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Stage Play\n\nBuild scenes that can be played: named speakers, entrances and exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and no camera language — only what a stage can do. Test advancement through business the audience watches. A repeated movement is now done without the flinch; a tool is carried in and set down wrong; a task that succeeded alone fails under another character's gaze. Let the gap between claim and execution occupy the stage: a character asserts the new capability and fumbles it, or performs it cleanly while someone asks what it cost. Objects and repeated routines, not announcements, keep the growth legible across scenes.\n\nHold the focal character's inward voices — self-defense, accusation, reversal — as clearly attributed nonspoken authoring material the other characters cannot hear. Monologue and aside remain available when they serve a genuine dramatic purpose in the public scene, but they are performed speech, not the container for what must stay private. Stage directions state observable conditions; they neither certify hidden motive nor guarantee a spoken claim.\n\nLet dialogue do the testing. A character explains an advance as proof of worth, discipline, or debt repaid; another speaker asks about the work itself — how long, how often, who taught it — and the explanation must adapt or collapse. The pull toward confession can be physical: crossing toward someone, stopping, turning back to the task. Fit every exchange to the relationship and the object in hand.\n\nLet gains expose weaknesses, and let a principle precede its execution by acts. The scene may end mid-effort, mid-exchange, or in silence between two people, with no obligation to complete a demonstration, stage a revelation, or resolve the argument. Use the supplied setting and cast as given.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagay::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Stream of Consciousness\n\nLet one authorized focal mind carry the scene, moving associatively through the materials of progression: the present attempt cut into by a remembered failure, a sensation in the hands recalling an earlier limit, a phrase of self-accusation turning mid-sentence into its own rebuttal, the same worry returning with a different meaning each time capability changes. Syntax and paragraph shape may follow these movements — entangled stretches when the argument entangles, fragments when attention fragments — while enough orientation remains to tell the live event from recollection or fantasy. Scene facts stay what they are, and no other mind is opened.\n\nMake the recurring concern an argument about worth. The mind defends the new capability as earned, attacks it as borrowed or shameful, and reverses again when a remembered face or a plain remark heard earlier resurfaces; that speaker need not appear for the sentence to do its work. Progression registers as changed attention: a movement that once needed conscious direction now completes before thought can name it, while another task still collapses the mind into fluent rehearsal followed by the body's refusal. Knowing a principle and executing it can visibly part company in consecutive sentences.\n\nKeep the world solid enough to interrupt: a sound, a slip, another person's arrival can cut the argument off mid-clause, and the unfinished thread belongs to the form. Do not resolve the voices into a verdict or a moral; a genuine gain may coexist with shame about how it was obtained, and neither cancels the other.\n\nStop wherever the response boundary falls — inside a thought, an attempt, or an interval of waiting — without requiring a completed test, a confession, or a change of direction first. Honor the setting, cast, and permissions of the active authorship contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagaz::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story in sender-attributed messages. Progression is what a person will actually transmit and what another will answer: a report of failure followed by a long silence, an edit that walks a boast back into a question, a receipt confirming the borrowed resource arrived, an attachment showing the finished or ruined piece, a plain practical reply landing after an elaborate self-account and quietly exposing what it avoided. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, and channel changes that matter, and never fabricate an interface event for subtext.\n\nGive the self-argument two homes. An unsent draft is a composed artifact: the sender writes out a defense of the new capability as earned, starts accusing it as borrowed or shameful, reverses again, then deletes or replaces it with three flat words — and what was drafted exists as a made thing, not as the sender's whole live mind. Context outside the exchange, clearly set off from the transcript, may carry private thought the transmitted text never contains. The other party knows none of it and answers only what was sent, sometimes wrongly.\n\nLet each sender's digital habits define the voice: one writes long and revises, one answers in single practical questions, one leaves messages unread while their work continues visibly in the channel. Test advancement through requests — someone asks the character to do the thing now, and the reply, the delay before it, or the attached result shows whether the capability holds outside the sender's own account of it.\n\nLet gains expose weaknesses and knowing precede doing. The exchange can end on an unanswered message, an unsent draft, or plain acknowledgment of what is now possible, with no completed trial, confession, or reversal required to close it. Use the contacts, platform, and world the story supplies.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagba::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Visual Novel\n\nWork in the rhythm of an illustrated scene: a concise line or two fixing where and when, attributed dialogue that reads cleanly, then the focal character's inner response as its own beat, alternating through the scene so the inner beat is where the moment turns. Background and expression cues orient — the practice room at dusk, the same hands steadier than last time — as purposeful references, not truth indicators and not a label owed to every line. Make scene changes legible as scene changes, with new location or time named plainly and no engine syntax, branching menus, player choices, or route labels, none of which this style activates by itself.\n\nPut the inward pressure in the focal reaction. There the new capability is defended as earned, accused as borrowed or shameful, and reversed by a remembered failure or by the plain remark another character has just made; the next spoken line may dodge, counter, or quietly accept what the reaction concluded. The reaction is not dialogue and not available to the cast. Let spoken exchanges test it instead, one practical question at a time.\n\nMove progression through the scene sequence: an opening beat of competence where struggle used to be, a middle where the harder variant still fails, a closing understanding that has not yet become execution. Keep the gains uneven — strength in one area exposing weakness in another, a principle fluent long before one performance of it.\n\nEnd on a continuing effort, an unfinished exchange, or a held reaction rather than a completed test or a forced choice. Use the supplied world, cast, and presentation rules.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalagbb::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Web Serial\n\nKeep a readable through-line across installments: which attempt is underway, what changed locally since last time, what remains open to continue. Recover only the needed particular — the failed exercise, the borrowed tool, the plain remark someone made — rather than retelling the history that established it. Because the serial remembers, an early failure can acquire new meaning much later, and a confident self-account from a previous installment can be answered by events without anyone needing to quote it.\n\nLet the self-argument evolve rather than repeat. What the focal character defended as earned in one installment can curdle into an accusation in the next when the capability is tested in front of witnesses; a practical sentence from an old scene resurfaces in memory at the new difficulty. The pressure need not resolve into a lesson by an installment's end, and no installment owes a confession, a reversal, or a cliffhanger — hooks are occasional and earned.\n\nAdvance through what the character can now do and what still resists. Knowledge, relationships, work, and resources all count as progress, kept uneven: strength in one area exposing weakness in another, a principle recited for chapters before one execution under pressure. The serial form does not create levels, rankings, or system rules on its own; use whatever mechanics the world already supplies, and only those.\n\nClose an installment on completed work, a pending request, an attempt only half-analyzed, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Let the scene's own boundary decide the stopping point rather than a miniature-chapter shape. Use the supplied setting, cast, and ongoing threads as given.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahaa::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nTreat every advance in capability as a change that carries a remembered cost and an unsettled debt. When a character gains a technique, resource, or understanding, move from the execution itself to the people who taught, resented, needed, or failed to receive that same capability: the instructor who withheld praise, the rival who made the same error, the relative who assumed the learner would never come back. Anchor the emotional weight of a lesson in a specific relationship or past event rather than in the difficulty of the skill alone.\n\nMake that weight visible in the present exchange. Use the García Márquez movement between personal life, family memory, public events, rumor, and recurring language so that progression becomes socially legible: a breakthrough can be reported through the neighborhood before the character has admitted it to themselves, and a phrase from an old argument can resurface mid-practice with its meaning changed. Let sentences gather detail across time — the present attempt read beside an earlier failure or a promise made years before — while landing on a human consequence.\n\nLet strain shape the actual conversation and what surrounds it: what a character says, avoids, repeats, or withholds, and the private experience running underneath the exchange. Under the active contract, give the nonspoken version of the moment — the apology rehearsed and then shortened, the need admitted inwardly while the spoken request sounds like an accusation, the flash of warmth interrupted by an old grievance. Knowing a principle and executing it under strain are different things: a character may perform a technique perfectly and still fail to use it in front of the person who taught it, or succeed while feeling the success belongs to someone else.\n\nDo not convert every gain into tragedy or every relationship into betrayal. Comfort may be partial, awkward, or brief, and it can coexist with pain without resolving it. Test the progression through what the character can now accomplish and what remains difficult, letting strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly while relationships, recovery, and ordinary work remain part of the developing life rather than interruptions of it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahab::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the reply as one coherent shared interval: readable paragraphs, unmistakable dialogue attribution, and narrative passages close enough to the focal character's perception that the reader always knows who is present, what is being attempted, and what has changed. Whoever holds the next move should receive a clear present situation — the next practice, the unfinished explanation, the person still waiting for an answer — without a forced question, cliffhanger, or choice screen at the end.\n\nMake progression happen inside the live exchange rather than around it. Let a character attempt something they could not previously do, receive a response that alters their understanding, or discover that knowing a principle is not executing it: a corrected grip, a new way of reading a pattern, a resource accepted or refused. Make the gain concrete enough that the next reply can build on it, and let strengths and weaknesses arrive unevenly rather than as a clean level-up.\n\nRun the García Márquez movement between personal life, family memory, public events, rumor, and recurring language inside the scene. A teacher's correction can carry a family saying; a neighbor's rumor can interrupt practice; a phrase from an earlier exchange can return with its meaning changed. Let a sentence gather detail across time while staying anchored in the present consequence — the character remembers who first showed them the motion even while performing it now. If the world permits the marvelous, meet it with ordinary social response instead of a spectacle that halts the scene.\n\nKeep the world and its people active between the player's moves. NPCs continue their practical lives, offer help or resistance, and act on what they themselves know; they do not wait to be prompted. Preserve the active authorship contract's rules for narrative person, action markers, and player control. A reply may advance the learning thread immediately or leave it open across turns, but it always hands the next writer a definite situation rather than a summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahac::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic telling route. Build the story as a chain of concrete encounters — each installment a self-contained situation with its own shape, tied to the others by the developing capability rather than by continuous scene-time. An episode may center on a teacher's correction, a failed execution before witnesses, a resource obtained through obligation, or an understanding that arrives only after the character has already acted. Let each episode close at a point of consequence — the remark that will be repeated, the debt that will come due — so the reader can enter the next installment without losing the thread.\n\nGive each episode its weight through García Márquez's communal memory and layered time. A lesson is remembered through the family member who first performed it, the neighbor who spread a rumor about it, or the public event that interrupted its teaching. A sentence may gather detail across time: the character practices a motion now, remembers who showed it years ago, and hears a bystander repeat a phrase from that earlier day. Anchor the gathered detail in a human consequence instead of decorative history, and let recurrence connect episodes without certifying that any rumor is true.\n\nMake improvement matter through what the character can now accomplish and what remains difficult. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it: a character may recite a rule perfectly and still fail under a particular person's gaze, or succeed while feeling the success belongs to someone else. Allow strengths and weaknesses to develop unevenly from episode to episode. Established ranks or systems may support that account, but invent no numerical mechanics merely to display progress.\n\nKeep the language, period, and community of the supplied world as given; the episodic method is craft, not a setting transplant. Relationships, recovery, and ordinary work remain part of the developing life across the chain of encounters.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahad::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite progression through material practice and physical stakes. Use forceful diction and compressed transitions: a failed attempt leaves a mark on the body or the room; a resource is worn, borrowed, or bought at a visible price; a new capability is tested against something that resists. Do not pad the movement between attempt and result. When the character's reasoning needs room, keep the longer thought, but hold it concrete and tied to the present problem.\n\nLet García Márquez's layered time and communal memory surface as physical trace rather than expansive recollection. A scar recalls the person who caused it. A tool carries the grip of a former owner. A repeated phrase arrives through a neighbor's rumor or a relative's warning. A sentence may gather a few details across time — the character practices now, remembers who showed them, feels the old injury in the same motion — but it must land on a human consequence.\n\nMake improvement matter through what the character can now do and what remains difficult. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it under pressure: a character may understand a technique and still fail when the floor is slick, the tool is cracked, or the one watching is the person who said they would never learn. Let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly. Established ranks or systems may support that account; invent no numerical mechanics merely to display progress.\n\nChange rhythm usefully. Alternate compressed action with a longer beat of reasoning or a brief exchange that exposes the social cost of advancement. Ground abrasive or rough speech in its particular speaker rather than making everyone hard-edged, and keep relationships, recovery, and ordinary work inside the developing life. The pressure sharpens the progression; it does not convert the story into survival horror.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahae::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the progression through documents: each with a clear author, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition. Choose the document the scene requires — a practice log listing repetitions and failures, a letter describing a breakthrough to someone absent, an instructor's report recording what was observed without what was felt, a receipt, a refusal, a marginal note — rather than rotating through formats on a schedule.\n\nLet improvement matter through what each document knows or conceals. A student's letter may claim an understanding that the teacher's note in the margin contradicts. A capability appears in one entry as an aspiration and in a later one as an unremarkable fact the earlier writer could not have described. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it by letting claims and records disagree. Let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly across entries, and add no omniscient explanation after a record: the reader assembles the learning thread from the documents' own gaps, revisions, and emphases.\n\nUse García Márquez's layered time and communal memory through the entries' temporal positions and recurring language. A phrase from an old family letter reappears in a neighbor's report with its meaning changed; a public event enters a private journal only as a delay or a rumor; a sentence inside one document may gather detail across time while staying within what that author could know and chose to record. If the world permits the marvelous, the documents meet it with ordinary social response: a clerk notes it without surprise, a relative dismisses it, a later writer misremembers it.\n\nPreserve readable continuity without resolving everything. Conflicting accounts and material traces can reveal events across entries, and what a document omits may matter as much as what it records. A document is a composed artifact, not its author's live mind. Relationships, recovery, and ordinary work remain visible in the record as part of the developing life.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahaf::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nTreat progression as a developing life that includes warmth, care, and manageable friction. Spend attention on chosen kindness: the person who brings food after a long practice, the familiar preference remembered during a journey, the practical help that makes a difficult attempt possible. Let affection be sincere without requiring a confession or emotional repair; a character may be cared for without having to name what they feel.\n\nKeep the learning thread genuinely present. Improvement comes through attempts, practice, feedback, new understanding, resources, and changed capability — a lesson learned in a comfortable room, a resource arriving as a gift, an understanding delivered in a patient explanation. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it, and let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly. Comfort does not replace the genre with a domestic vignette; it shows how care and ease operate inside the progression's actual difficulty.\n\nUse García Márquez's communal memory and layered time for affectionate recollection. A sentence may gather detail across time: the character practices a motion now, remembers who first taught it, and notices that the same person has left the cup where they always leave it. Recurring language returns as a family saying, a neighbor's greeting, a remembered instruction. Anchor the gathered detail in a human consequence, and let that consequence be warm or gently funny as readily as it is painful.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable or erase established problems from the world: a character may refuse help, keep a preference private, or disagree about how a technique should be taught. Let the scene enjoy particular people without flattening them, while relationships, recovery, and ordinary work remain part of the developing life.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahag::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Formal Literary Novel\n\nNarrate progression as a socially situated development unfolding over time. Use deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement so that a single passage can hold the immediate attempt, the family memory that shaped it, the public event that interrupted it, and the rumor that will follow it. Keep narration distinct from ordinary spoken language: the narrator may frame a breakthrough through social context, history, or judgment where the chosen narrator permits, while the learning itself stays concrete — an attempt made, a correction received, a capability tested against something that resists.\n\nMake improvement matter through what the character can now accomplish and what remains difficult. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it: a character may understand a technique long before performing it under a particular person's gaze or in a particular season. Let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly, and register that unevenness through the community's changing response rather than through numerical display.\n\nUse the García Márquez movement between personal life, family memory, public events, rumor, and recurring language as the novel's connective tissue. A sentence may gather detail across time — the character practices a motion now, remembers who first showed it, and hears a bystander repeat a phrase from that earlier day — and anchor the gathered detail in a human consequence. If the world permits the marvelous, treat it through ordinary social response: relatives minimize it, neighbors discuss it, and the person who performed it feels more exposed than triumphant.\n\nLet ceremony, correspondence, and public/private contrast organize a passage where the story supplies them, but supply no aristocratic setting, archaic vocabulary, or uniformly elaborate speech on the style's own authority. Relationships, recovery, and ordinary work remain part of the developing life. The formal architecture gives the progression its shape; it does not substitute atmosphere for the learning itself.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahah::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nGive the telling an occasion that bears on unfinished capability. A student explains a failed technique to a teacher who will decide whether instruction continues; a traveler pays for passage with the account of how a hard-won method was earned; an elder holds off a verdict by telling what became of an earlier pupil. Make the listeners care about the skill itself — to learn it, license it, forbid it, or wager on it — so the tale's outcome and the training's future press on each other from the first sentence.\n\nInside the embedded account, render advancement as remembered attempts whose conditions the teller still holds in the body: the day the principle was understood and the hands still refused it, the borrowed tool that worked only after the grip changed, the familiar exercise that uncovered a weakness no one had named. Let single sentences gather years while staying anchored in ability — one attempt carrying a grandmother's warning, the neighbors' betting on failure, and the consequences of a public examination, provided the sentence keeps hold of what the teller could and could not do that afternoon. Mark each passage into and out of the remembered time so the narrative levels stay intelligible.\n\nMake the outer scene a live test rather than a fireside. A listener interrupts to ask how a step was performed; someone demands a demonstration on the spot; a rival sets this version against an earlier telling and notices what has changed. When the teller skips a failure, let the omission be legible to anyone who has watched the teller practice — hands repeating the motion, a witness correcting the sequence, a silence in which the audience understands the lesson is not finished. The frame can supply what the tale withholds.\n\nLet repeated phrases and objects cross the levels and accrue meaning: the teacher's warning returns in the teller's mouth as advice to a new listener; the tool described inside the story lies on the table outside it, meaning something different now. The delayed revelation can be modest — the teller has learned to name the difficulty, or the audience now knows what to watch for in the next attempt — and the telling may close with the skill still unproven. Borrow the nested telling, not the palace: no djinn, caravans, royal bargains, or a particular faith unless the story's own world already supplies them, and no cliffhanger demanded after every nested account. Advancement stays contingent on what the characters can actually do once the telling ends, not on the story's claim that they improved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahai::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite as social and developmental realism. Set the developing person inside a particular social world — a household economy, a workshop or school, a street whose expectations determine what can be practiced openly and what must be hidden — and narrate improvement as the changing relation between one person's effort and those material conditions, never as a ladder of isolated feats.\n\nReturn to the same task under different circumstances and let the difference teach: before and after access to a teacher, a tool, a quiet room, a stretch of recovery. When a principle is understood but not yet executed, give the gap in the character's own terms — the hands know the sequence while the breath arrives a beat late; the formula is perfectly clear while the body keeps the older habit. Let strengths and weaknesses advance unevenly, and let illness, paid work, and family obligation interrupt practice without being reduced to obstacles on the way to a triumph.\n\nUse layered time in composed, gathering sentences: one sentence can hold a childhood attempt, a parent's warning, and today's consequence while remaining anchored in what the character can now do. Bring rumor and public events in through their effect on access — a reputation opens a door, a political change closes a route, a family story explains why this skill is feared in one district and prized in another. Keep the narration exact, humane, and continuous rather than bureaucratic or severe for its own sake; the world is specific people in a specific place, not an office that stamps progress.\n\nAfter the gathered memory, test advancement in the present scene through concrete capability: a weight carried alone that once required help, a text read straight through that used to be opaque, a shortcut refused that once seemed necessary. No rank, duel, or completed transformation is required, and the chapter may end with the character still inside the process. Relationships, recovery, and ordinary work remain part of the developing life throughout.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahaj::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Gothic-Punk\n\nLet the developing character inherit an arrangement rather than begin free: a family method, a patron's expectation, a building or instrument whose history grants access and imposes conditions at once. Make the legacy old, intimate, and politically charged in the present — technical, social, or economic in its working. Do not reach for vampire physiology, sect politics, blood debts, or catalogued powers; the inheritance operates through the story's actual rules and its actual city.\n\nRender advancement through private encounters with that legacy. A lesson takes place in a decaying room where the teacher's authority is inseparable from the peeling paint and the locked drawer; a new capability arrives as a morally complicated exchange — a technique taught by someone who expects loyalty, a family shortcut that works only by costing somebody else. Let atmosphere charge each encounter without supplying the mechanism of improvement: the cold of the room can make a failure feel like an accusation, the noise outside can make a private breakthrough feel stolen.\n\nCarry the legacy to the character through communal memory. A sentence can gather a grandparent's bargain, a neighbor's warning, and the character's present hesitation, anchored in what the character can now do; rumor explains why the method is coveted in one room and shunned in the next. Let social masks fall away in trusted company — the teacher speaks plainly only after the lesson ends, a rival finally names the cost of the shared method — and keep that intimacy morally complicated rather than uniformly corrupting. Private desire belongs on the page, entangled with what the inheritance demands.\n\nTest advancement through what the character can now accomplish and what remains difficult: a demonstration performed privately before the person who taught the skill, a refusal of the harmful shortcut, the same task attempted under the conditions that once caused failure. Do not require a betrayal, a completed inheritance, or a final escape; the atmosphere may stay unresolved even as capability improves, and ordinary work and recovery continue inside the life being built.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahak::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite as testimonial and autobiographical account. The developing person speaks or is focalized from a later moment, recalling how a skill was learned under particular family, community, and material conditions. Use the language, region, community, and period the story actually supplies; do not impose English, code-switching, a particular religion or caste experience, or a standard joint family on a household the tale has already drawn differently.\n\nShape the account as testimony rather than a training log. The teller remembers not only the exercise but who watched, who forbade it, who paid for the materials, and what rumor said happened to others who tried. A sentence gathers a childhood failure, a relative's warning, and the teller's present understanding while staying anchored in a human consequence. Keep earlier knowledge distinct from later knowledge: instruction that made sense only years afterward, a moment of pride that now reads as the start of a longer difficulty, an apology the teller still owes.\n\nRender advancement through attempts, practice, feedback, and changed capability, letting the same task return under changed conditions — a move, a marriage, a death, a season of paid work, a turn in the household's fortunes. When a principle is understood but not executed, describe the gap in the teller's remembered body and attention rather than announcing failure. Strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly, and ordinary work and recovery stay inside the account rather than between its chapters.\n\nTest advancement in the present frame: a demonstration before a younger relative, a return to the place of an old failure, a quiet repetition of a task that once required help. Completed mastery and public vindication are not required. The delayed revelation may be that the teller now understands the cost of the learning, or that the community's judgment has shifted alongside — not ahead of — the teller's actual capability.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahal::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite as diary and reflective miscellany. The developing person records or recalls discrete attempts, lessons, and interruptions in a personal account that moves between immediate detail and later understanding. Do not force a continuous training plot: let a remembered failure sit beside a present observation, the connection emerging through recurrence and the teller's shifting attention.\n\nGround each remembered attempt in its specific conditions — the season, the room, the state of the body, the cost of materials, the presence or absence of a teacher — and let a sentence gather a childhood attempt, a family saying, and a present consequence while holding to what the character can now do. Record the gap between principle and execution plainly: the hand knows the motion while the mind still waits for permission; the formula is memorized while the breath shortens at the moment of use. Let omission do real work — a skill practiced for years disappears from the record during a period of grief or work, and its return is noted only indirectly, by a task done or a comment left unanswered.\n\nThread communal memory and recurring language through the private record. A teacher's phrase resurfaces in the teller's own voice; rumor about a famous practitioner shapes early ambition and later disappointment; public events enter through their effect on access and practice, never as scenery. Keep the language and narrative distance faithful to the actual teller — plain argument, direct feeling, seasonal attention, even exuberance where this teller has it. No enforced hush, and no obligatory inventory of gardens, shrines, or weather.\n\nTest advancement through what the teller can now accomplish and what remains difficult: a quiet repetition, a task performed for someone else, a return to a place of earlier failure. No completed mastery, public contest, or closing insight is required; the record may end with the teller still inside the process, noting what has changed and what has not.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaham::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite as social realism, chronicle, and testimony. Narrate the developing person inside a particular community and its history: a household that depends on the skill, a neighborhood that remembers who taught it and who failed at it, a public event that opens or closes access to practice. Preserve the language, community, and history the story supplies. Where the world itself permits the marvelous, let people treat it as an ordinary practical concern; do not add marvels to a world that excludes them, and do not assume this selection requires them — magical realism is one available coloring, not the price of entry.\n\nMake advancement socially situated. Show who can pay for the lesson, who is permitted to attempt it, and what rumor says about those who succeed. Let a sentence gather a family memory, a public event, and a present consequence, anchored in what the character can now do. When a principle is understood but not executed, render the gap in the character's own terms: the hands know the sequence while the body obeys an older fear; the method is clear while the household will not grant the hours to practice it.\n\nMove between personal life, family memory, public events, rumor, and recurring language. A teacher's phrase returns in the character's voice; a celebration or a disaster changes what the skill means overnight; a family story explains why one failure is still remembered at the market three generations later. Keep the chronicle anchored in human consequence — wages, injuries, jealousies, funerals — rather than letting it flatten into historical summary.\n\nTest advancement through present accomplishment and remaining difficulty: a task performed for the community, a return to the place of an old failure, a quiet demonstration before someone who doubted the learning. Completed mastery, public vindication, and departure from the community are not required; the chapter may end with the character still bound to the people and conditions that shaped the learning, changed in capability but not finished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahan::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Light Novel\n\nEnter each training situation cleanly. Establish where the character is, what is being attempted, and what failure or success would look like in this immediate space before the attempt begins. When the scene moves — practice room to street, present to remembered place — mark the shift with a paragraph break and one concrete detail so the reader never loses the geography. Present unfamiliar names or mechanics clearly at first appearance, in motion, without halting the scene for a glossary.\n\nKeep the paragraphs short and readable around action, dialogue, and private response. After a failed attempt or a teacher's correction, give one focused reaction beat — a specific sensation, a named uncertainty, a flash of anger, a quiet recognition of what changed — and let its tone belong to its owner: serious, uncertain, angry, or funny as this character actually is. No compulsory wit, no required young cast, no forced cliffhanger.\n\nCompress layered time into single sentences that still carry weight. One sentence can hold a family warning, a rumor about the technique, and the character's present hesitation, provided it stays anchored in what the character can now do. Use recurring language sparingly — a teacher's phrase surfacing in thought mid-attempt, a remembered failure sharpening a present grip — so the gathering adds consequence without stalling the fast-readable flow into retrospective essay.\n\nMake advancement concrete on the page. Show the moment the body or attention fails despite full understanding; test improvement through what the character can now do alone and what still defeats them — a task that once required help, a technique that holds only under calm, a new weakness exposed by a harder exercise. Let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly, keep relationships, recovery, and ordinary work inside the life, and let the scene end wherever it actually is — still inside the attempt is a legitimate place to stop, changed in capability but not yet finished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahao::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite advancement as an accumulation the prose can hold in one breath: a lesson arriving through remembered attempts, a teacher's phrase that becomes the student's own only after practice has altered it, a callused hand steady where it used to tremble. Let a sentence gather earlier scenes and later consequences around a single sensory anchor, so the present moment carries the weight of the training that made it possible. When a capability finally appears on the page, keep a particular failure and the correction that reshaped it visible inside the same movement—grit under the fingernails, an ache carried in the shoulder, the smell of the workshop—so that the hours spent read as evidence rather than ornament.\n\nMark growth through recurrence instead of declaration. The same doorway passed in fear becomes ordinary; an instruction once misunderstood is offered, slightly altered, to a younger learner; a sound from the first practice room returns at the moment the character can finally name what it warned against. Let a comparison unfold only while it changes what the narrator understands or makes present, and keep every image traceable to this scene or to this teller's associations. Let the community's version of the rise travel alongside the private one: a rumor of the feat can reach the household before the feat itself, a grandmother's account of a celebrated ancestor can press on the meaning of an ordinary exercise, and where the training touches the marvelous, the family may treat it with the practical fatigue they bring to weather.\n\nKeep the lyrical attention fastened to consequences and people—name the meal left to cool, the sentence of welcome that went unsaid—rather than to elevated abstraction. Let plain statements interrupt the gathered syntax whenever a practical fact must stand clear, and keep each character's speaking voice out of the narrator's figurative register. Not every advance needs an epiphany: some arrive as quiet competence, a longer-held posture or a route chosen without hesitation, and the prose can let them pass almost unnoticed, the way the household does.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahap::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nTell progression obliquely, through the residue it leaves: a textbook whose pages no longer match what the character knows, a tool set aside because a stronger method has replaced it, a familiar failure recalled with tenderness from the far side of it. Let present capability cast its shadow back across earlier rooms and relationships—the doorway that once admitted a frightened beginner now opening onto a house where someone is no longer waiting, an old warning still recited though it no longer applies. Let a gain coexist with what it displaced: learning control can mean that the uncontrolled spark, once frightening and beloved, does not come back.\n\nDevelop the thread by showing what changed ability lets the character protect, recall, or admit, and bind past to present with recurring language and sensation: a phrase a dead teacher used, the sound of the first practice room, a promise still unfinished. Let rumor and family memory comment on the rise—a neighbor's version arriving smoother than the truth—without displacing the character's own inward account. Sadness is not the required temperature: an advancement can arrive with relief, pride, or outright humor, ordinary pleasure stays pleasurable, and the reflective method only pauses long enough to notice what grew smaller while something else grew larger. Let a reply end with the character still inside the continuous work of becoming rather than sealed into an epilogue of final loss.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahaq::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse the framed oral-storytelling route. Open each portion as a situated telling: a known speaker with a reason for recounting this episode now, and an audience whose need, doubt, or interruption shapes what gets told. Draw the frame's manners—forms of address, the formula that opens and closes a tale, the license a listener has to question the teller—from the setting, language, and community actually supplied, keeping Arabic, Persian, Turkish, Hebrew, Kurdish, and neighboring traditions distinct where the world names one, rather than dressing a generic telling in borrowed scenery.\n\nInside the frame, narrate the learner's advancement as concrete scenes: attempts, corrections, feedback, new understanding, resources gained, and tests of what the changed capability can now do. Let the teller qualify or interrupt the account while the framed story stays vivid in its own present. Bind the stages of learning with recurrence—a teacher's proverb heard first as empty noise, repeated with new weight after a failure; an object carried through the journey that changes meaning each time it changes hands—and let a detail planted early in the telling arrive late enough to revise what the listeners believed they had understood. Keep the movement among the learner's personal life, family memory, and public rumor without transplanting a familiar town or family, and let the frame's talk stay domestic: someone notices the bread is burning, or asks the question the learner was once afraid to ask.\n\nMaintain interiority as actual private experience for the focal character; the frame is a presentation choice, not a substitute for thought. If the active narrator is not a storyteller character, let an unobtrusive third-person telling carry the same sense of a story being retold, keeping the deliberate opening and the returned phrase as its marks.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahar::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Minimalist Prose\n\nPut the progression in selected objects and actions. Show improvement through what the character now does with fewer motions, what they no longer carry, which instruction they obey without repeating it, which task they complete alone. Hold the memory of earlier attempts in one or two exact things—a cracked practice bowl, handwriting on a torn page, a scar from a failed trial—and let a plain sentence carry the weight that reflection would otherwise announce. When a sentence must reach across time, anchor it in a physical object or a remembered phrase rather than in summary.\n\nLet public and family memory enter sideways and briefly—a neighbor still using an old nickname, a relative mentioning the year the rent went unpaid—without expanding into anecdote. Keep the difference between knowing and executing visible: the character can recite the principle before the tool will obey them, and the performed version appears later, in an action, without commentary. Let strengths develop unevenly, one skill leaping ahead while a related one still fumbles, and let omission leave the causal step inferable rather than deleted, so practice and new ability stay connected. A longer inward sentence is permitted when it materially changes how the scene continues; beyond that, the action has already said it, and the narration should stop.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahas::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose with natural movement among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized interiority. Let progression arise through repeated practical encounters across ordinary time: a technique attempted before breakfast, corrected by a teacher in the afternoon, applied imperfectly that evening, recognized weeks later when the character performs it without stopping to think. Use paragraphing and transitions to orient without recap machinery, and give the prose its rhythm by returning, as circumstances change, to a remembered phrase or sensory detail, so that a sentence quietly gathers time while staying anchored in one human consequence.\n\nWeave family memory, rumor, and public events into the background of the training rather than detaching them as anecdotes: a relative's remark about a celebrated forebear can sit inside a lesson and alter what the student thinks they owe; where the world permits the marvelous, let it meet ordinary social practicality instead of wonder. Keep the register governed by the immediate scene, and when reflection comes, grow it from a current perception—a window left open, a meal going cold—rather than from a general essay. Show that knowing a principle is not executing it: the character may explain the correct motion while still fumbling the tool. Let resources, feedback, and new understanding shift capability unevenly. End at whatever scene boundary the active role permits—an unanswered question or unfinished task is acceptable, and supported action may equally advance—without extracting a moral or a capsuled lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahat::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nLet the actual supplied relationship premise organize the progression. Select scenes that reveal where the characters stand with one another through training, failure, encouragement, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery, as that particular premise dictates, and make improvement matter in the other person's presence: the same correction lands differently when the partner gives it after a mistake of their own, and a favor finally returned can close a debt that once exposed the student's weakness. Build the pairing's private language and repeated moments—a phrase that began as a joke turning serious after a loss, an early gesture returning with new weight once the character has grown—and let the consequences fulfill this pairing's particular promise rather than a generic roster of tropes.\n\nGive communal and family memory a place around the pair without transplanting a known setting: relatives, neighbors, or old stories may comment on them, misread the bond, or raise the stakes of a shared training debt. Keep interiority as real private experience for the focal character, and keep separate what each person knows—the partner's silence can be read wrongly or rightly, according to what the scene has actually established. If the premise is not romantic, do not force romance; the established bonds of friends, rivals, siblings, or comrades can carry the emotional progression. End at a natural scene boundary with both the relationship and the skill still in motion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahau::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nShape each reply as a retold portion of the seeker's rise. Open deliberately, with a compact declaration that fixes the learner's place in public memory—what they could not yet do, or the day something changed—then give the specific attempts and corrections that later became the remembered deed. Tie any recurring designation to an actual accomplishment or habitual act rather than scattering ornamental titles at every entrance, and return to a phrase only when a later occurrence gives it new application. Measure progression by consequences a community will recall: the first roof repaired alone, the teacher who arrived from another region, the debt repaid years after it was incurred.\n\nLet family memory and rumor run through the chronicle—scraps about the learner's origins, neighbors whose versions of the same deed differ—while the represented scene shows the concrete learning beneath whatever later talk made of it. Keep the chronicle honest about the difference between knowing and executing: it may record a vow made before the skill was mastered without treating the vow as the mastery. Let dialogue interrupt the telling's authority with ordinary practical concerns, without pushing every exchange toward parody or ceremonial doom, and preserve the focal character's actual felt experience under the active contract; the public telling cannot erase what the learning cost. End when a portion of the account has been told, leaving the next deed available rather than declaring the legend already complete.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahav::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nTake the definite Scandinavian route of social and domestic realism with institutional texture. Improvement becomes legible through the particular community's arrangements: apprenticeship, certification, shared tools, seasonal work, household responsibility, and the way neighbors revise their estimate of what a person can be trusted to do. Do not invent numerical mechanics or ranks the world does not already use. When a new capability appears, treat its social consequence as the plot's live material: who now asks for help, who stops supervising, who offers different work, who resents the change, and what ordinary labor remains difficult despite it.\n\nNarrate advancement across layered time in the García Márquez manner. A sentence may gather a past attempt, a present execution, and the remembered voice of the person who first taught the principle, all anchored in one concrete human consequence. Let practice be witnessed and discussed. A parent, neighbor, or workmate may repeat an old phrase about the learner, and the repetition can mark changed capability without the narrator announcing a rank. Move between personal life, family memory, public event, scrutiny, and rumor, but route each movement toward a consequence the character or the community actually bears. When the community's estimate changes, make the mechanism concrete: who asks now, who stops supervising, whose joking stops, and whose silence or anger remains private.\n\nDistinguish knowing a principle from executing it by staging the difference. The character can explain the method correctly and still fail under weather, fatigue, interruption, or the pressure of being watched, and the failure should leave a specific practical trace: a dropped load, a wasted portion, a tool left where it cannot be used, a promise extended beyond proof. Later practice should make the earlier trace legible without making it the entire measure of the character.\n\nKeep the developing life whole. Relationships, recovery, household tasks, and institutional obligations continue around the training. A period of advancement may be remembered later through the object it changed: a repaired fence, a first solo journey, a tool that no longer needs to be borrowed. Let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly, and let the community notice one before the other. Keep the active narrator and world facts. Do not import a familiar town, family, or set of names from the author profile. Keep Danish, Norwegian, and Swedish contexts distinct: use the legal, seasonal, domestic, or linguistic details actually supplied by the story, and do not supply reserve, snow, isolation, or a generalized Nordic emotional restraint unless the setting calls for them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahaw::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Screenplay\n\nEstablish progression through visible and audible action. Show the same task at two different times: the first attempt fails or requires help, and the later attempt succeeds under conditions that make the change legible. Action lines should record the concrete difference—steadier hands, a faster sequence, a tool handled without instruction, a route completed alone—rather than stating that the character has improved. Keep the task specific enough that the audience can see what is now possible and what still goes wrong. When a principle is explained, follow it with an attempt that tests execution, not just understanding.\n\nUse the screenplay's time-shifting tools to carry García Márquez's layered memory. A cut can place a present action beside the earlier scene that established the same skill so that the audience can compare the same gesture, posture, or weather condition directly. Offscreen sound may bring in the teacher's voice, the remembered instruction, or the noise of the place where the character first practiced, but this sound belongs to the present scene as a recollection rather than unaccompanied explanation. Dialogue can repeat an earlier phrase when the present circumstances have changed the sentence's meaning: the learner now gives the instruction, someone who offered the old warning now asks for help, or a detail the learner once reported now brings a new question. Do not let voice-over replace required private experience; when private thought is needed, present it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation, separate from dialogue and unavailable to the other characters.\n\nLet the community witness and discuss advancement. A scene heading can move the action from the practice ground to a kitchen, workshop, or public gathering where neighbors remark on the change. Their speech should reflect their own interests and relationships: the neighbor may want the task done, resent the learner's new status, or ask for instruction in return. Keep personal life and ordinary work in the frame. A training success may be followed by a household argument, a meal, or an errand that remains difficult. End a scene after a completed attempt, a failed one, or a new request, without requiring a cut, reversal, or explanatory speech. Preserve the active narrator, world facts, and authorship contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahax::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Stage Play\n\nLet the shared stage space prove advancement. Stage directions should establish what the character can now do in front of the audience: carry a load alone, complete a sequence without pausing, use a tool that previously required help, or perform a task while speaking. The physical business must be specific and playable, not a summary of skill. When a task remains difficult, show the difficulty in the body: a hesitation, a dropped object, a second attempt, or a visible effort that another character through the. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it by having the character state the method correctly and then struggle with the physical act, or succeed only after a staged correction.\n\nCarry García Márquez's layered time through repeated stage objects and shared memory. A line spoken in an early scene may return later with a changed meaning because circumstances changed: the same instruction now given by the learner to someone else, the same warning now answered differently, or the same object brought on stage under new conditions. Entrances and exits can bring the past into the present without becoming panel: a teacher on the scene and watches, a neighbor enters with the learner's earlier failure already on their mind, or a family member recalls the first attempt while the character plows. Let cover and public event enter through what the cast says to one another, but keep each speech motivated by the speaker's own relation to time learner and by the object or work before them.\n\nKeep required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation, clearly separated from dialogue so that no other character can hear it, and distinguish it from a monologue or aside. A monologue may be used when it has a dramatic purpose as spoken per- formance, but it is not the destination for private thought. Let ordinary life continue around the training: a sharply dressed scene can expel someone, interrupt a task, or produce the physical object of work, and the confrontation need not produce a formal result. End after a completed attempt, a failed one, or a new interaction, without an obligatory gathering, revelation, or reversal. Preserve the user, world facts, and authorship contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahay::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Stream of Consciousness\n\nRender progression through the associative movement of the authorized focal mind. A present attempt may surface the memory of the first lesson, the feel of a tool in younger hands, a lab's phrase, the smell of the place where practice happened, and the bodily difference now: steadier grip, less breath, a muscle that no longer trembles. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow the mind's movement, but keep enough orientation that the reader can distinguish the present event from recollection, anticipation, or fantasy. When the character executes a skill, the narration may move between the physical act and the remembered principle without authorizing that enlargement has occurred; the changed sensation and the completed part carry their own significance.\n\nUse García Márquez's layered time as the mind's own material. A recurring phrase from family or community may return at moments of practice or difficulty, each time attached to a different human consequence. The mind may gather a public event, a neighbor's rumor, and a private memory into one passage, but the passage must remain anchored in what the character is performing, looking at, or remembering during this. Let the community's estimate of the character become part of the mental evidence: the focal mind should recall not just what was written, but who applied it, when they stopped applying it, and what material consequence the remembered voice and status themselves. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it through the gap between what the mind rehearses and what the body actually does. The character may remember the complete instruction and still bring, and the brumbledness should be written as sensation, temperature, pain, or frustration rather than as an explanation or a summary.\n\nKeep the developing life whole inside the mind's character. Working, restoring, relationships, and household tasks may interrupt or accompany the learning. A period of inclusion may return later as a memory activated by an object, a drop, or a familiar function, and the memory should show what the character can now do or still cannot do. Do not make every character share a mind or give the narration access to excluded minds. Preserve the active storyteller, narration, and authorial contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "enabled": true,
            "forbid_overrides": false,
            "injection_order": 100,
            "injection_trigger": [],
            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-progression_fantasy-03",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Progression Fantasy · 03/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == progression_fantasy}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Progression Fantasy. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 3 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-progression_fantasy; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPalahaz::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nLet the message exchange carry the progression. Show changed capability through what the character now transmits: a request for help becomes an offer of help in an actual practical transaction, a question about method becomes an instruction to someone else, a report of failure becomes a report of completion, the delayed answer becomes prompt, or the person takes more than they themselves requested. Each sender's actual digital habits should shape the contribution: one person writes long, one sends fragments, one waits, one edits. Use timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes only when they matter. A delay may communicate practical interruption or hesitancy, but do not treat every delay as withholding. When a skill is discussed, let the messages illustrate the gap between knowing and executing: the character can describe the principle correctly and still report a failed attempt, send a photo or attachment that documents the result, or ask for a correction they already understand but cannot perform.\n\nCarry García Márquez's layered time through conversational and transmission in memory. A sender may repeat a family phrase, forward an old message, or refer to a past event that the other participants already know. The same phrase may return in a later exchange with a changed meaning: the lab's warning now sent by the learner to a younger person, or the neighbor's old doubt now answered with a different report. Let rumor and public report enter through what people forward, summarize, or ask about, but keep each message motivated by the sender's own relationship to the learner and their opportunity to write it. When context outside the transcript is required, present it as distinct narrative context; do not type it undetected as interface metadata, and do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the character's live private thought; it may show what the character considered saying and chose not to send. Treated private experience may appear as clearly separated context, never disguised as a message another character can read. Let ordinary days continue around the exchange: a sender may be tagged by work, a household task, or a conversation, and the delay or brevity should follow that actuality rather than a story demanded by the transcript. End after a sent message, a received answer, a pending response, or a plain silence, without forcing a cliffhanger or a completed lesson. Preserve the active narrator, world facts, and authoring contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahba::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Visual Novel\n\nLet each scene show the current state of capability through what the focal character can do and how others respond. Scene-setting should present the task and its conditions: the same workshop, field, or kitchen may appear again with a changed detail that marks progress—a tool now within reach, a workbench cleared, a route no longer needing guidance. Dialogue should carry the community's revised relation without turning characters into indicators: a neighbor asks for help instead of offering it, a teacher stops correcting, a family member mentions a different need. When a task remains difficult, show the difficulty in the action or the focal reaction, not by attaching a face label to it. When the narrator observes a neighbor's expression, do not let it function as proof by itself; the actual changed relation is shown by what the neighbor later says or requires, and the expression can accompany that behavior rather than certify his view.\n\nUse García Márquez's layered time through running ordnance and gathered passages. A background or expression cue may anchor a memory: the same doorway, tool, or season returns with a new significance earned by what happened since. A short narrative passage may gather a past attempt, a present execution, and a remembered instruction in one sentence, as long as it remains anchored in the character's current environment. Let a phrase from family or community repeat across scenes with a changed meaning, and let rumors or public events enter through what the cast says to one another. Keep private thought outside other characters' knowledge, and use the actual narration or interior to show the character's experience without giving every mind access to it.\n\nKeep the rhythm suited to an illustrated scene: concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal reaction beats that do not slow every exchange. Background or expression cues are purposeful references, not mandatory labels on every line or executable engine code. Do not add branching key, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected; those require their own authorization or feature. Let ordinary life continue around the training: a scene may begin with household work, a meal, or an errand, and the advancement may be interrupted by a request. End after a completed attempt, a failed one, or a new burden, without requiring a choice screen, revelation, or completed lesson. Preserve the active narrator, world facts, and authorship contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalahbb::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / García Márquez / Web Serial\n\nWrite each installment as another layer in a remembered apprenticeship. The reader should always be able to tell what is underway — a particular skill being learned, tested under a stated condition, or lived with in its consequences — what changes locally in this reply, and what remains open to continue. Let the local change be concrete: an attempt made, feedback received or refused, a resource gained or spent, a task finished, a difficulty that survives the installment. Do not close every turn as a complete miniature chapter or manufacture a cliffhanger; a reply may end on completed work, a pending request, plain acknowledgment of what is now known, or work still in progress.\n\nWhen elapsed time or an earlier event bears on the present, recover it with a gathering sentence rather than a recap: one sentence can carry the weeks of practice, the season, what the family predicted, and the neighbor's verdict, and still land on a human consequence — the hand that healed crooked, the debt still unpaid, the cousin who stopped laughing. Anchor that sentence in capability: what the learner can now do, and what remains out of reach. Let advancement register as accomplishment under a condition, not as a declared stage.\n\nLet the community hold the record of growth. The neighbor who watched the first failure, the teacher's phrase from the early lessons, the tool that broke — return an established detail when it can carry new meaning, in preference to inventing a fresh measure each time. A phrase the learner once heard without understanding can arrive again at the moment they can finally execute it, marking the distance traveled without announcing a rank or number. The street's account also changes with them: the story retold with a different ending, the name the market has for them shifting, rumor crediting them with a feat beyond their reach while a weakness only one watcher has noticed keeps its quiet place. Use the world's existing arrangements — apprenticeship, certification, tools, reputation, debts — and invent no numbers or leveling mechanics merely to display progress.\n\nStage the gap between knowing and executing inside time. The narration can hold both moments at once: they could recite the rule long before they could use it, and only now, under pressure or fatigue or interruption, do the rule and the hand arrive together or fail to. Give a failure a specific practical trace — wasted material, a broken tool, a promised repayment — that a later installment can pick up again. A brief forward glance can end an installment as a hook — for instance, that it would be a long time before the family took this craft lightly again — so long as it deepens the present moment rather than fixing what the story has not yet decided.\n\nKeep the developing life whole. Relationships, recovery, household tasks, and ordinary work continue around the training, and an installment may spend its local change on one of these instead of a skill test; let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly, and let the community notice one before the other. Where the world permits the marvelous, let it be treated domestically — complained about, priced, envied, mourned — rather than met with astonishment. Attach each recollection to the person who holds it, and keep the learner's private experience available under the active contract: the fear before an attempt, the silent accounting of what remains impossible, held while the street talks. Preserve the active narrator, perspective, world facts, and authorship contract, and do not require any attempt to succeed, fail, or be resolved before a reply ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiaa::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nMake each advancement beat an emotional event, not a stat change. Show the practitioner attempting something newly possible, and let the attempt itself force contact with a specific unresolved hurt: the technique now works, but using it requires the practitioner to stop avoiding the person who taught it, to admit a need that was previously refused, or to accept that the new strength does not undo an old failure. Render the changed capability in the doing, then let the doing expose the cost.\n\nWrite the attempt in brief declarative movement. Before the key moment, keep the narration spare and withhold the private cause of strain. The practitioner may notice the other person's stillness, the weight of a familiar object, or the exact point where the body wants to flinch. Do not explain the hurt yet. Then deliver the delayed shift as a small, concrete change in the present scene: a hand that steadies only after the practitioner stops speaking, a silence that lasts until the other person answers, a phrase repeated with a different weight. The shift should let the reader infer what the practitioner has been withholding, not announce it.\n\nLet the delayed recognition reinterpret the demonstrated capability. The new skill may have been learned to protect someone the practitioner cannot apologize to, to prove something to a person who is no longer watching, or to avoid asking for help that is still needed. The recognition does not have to resolve the hurt. It should change what the advancement means to the practitioner, so that the next stage of training is shadowed by that knowledge.\n\nKeep warmth partial and real. A companion may offer practical help that works, and the hurt may remain untouched by it. An apology may be accepted without settling the grievance behind it. When a relationship affects training, make the effect concrete in the scene: distraction changes timing, resentment sharpens a drill, gratitude makes a dangerous shortcut seem possible. Use the active narrator's distance and diction. A close narrator may render the practitioner's reasoning in fragments; a more distant narrator may report the visible strain and leave the private cause implied. Either way, preserve the difference between what the practitioner knows and what others can observe. Advancement is not a cure for the relationship, and the relationship is not merely an obstacle to power.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiab::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the scene as a coherent shared interval with unmistakable dialogue attribution and short, readable paragraphs. Keep each exchange compact enough to scan, but let a speaker's pause or a single unspoken observation carry weight. Do not pad dialogue with explanations the other character would already understand.\n\nShow progression through an attempt that matters in the scene: a technique that works now but not cleanly, a correction the character accepts or resists, a resource that changes what can be attempted next. Make the changed capability visible in action or in the character's focused attention, not in a narrated stat block. Render the remaining difficulty as an observable limit in the present attempt: the character can hold the technique for only a few breaths, can read the opponent's rhythm but not the feint, or can use the new resource only once. The reader should see what the character can now do and what still fails, without the narrator summarizing either.\n\nDelay one piece of information or one small action until it can change the preceding stillness. Choose a shift the viewpoint character can now use or absorb: a detail noticed earlier that explains the opponent's movement, a name spoken after a pause that reinterprets the last exchange, or a gesture that shows why a companion has been holding back. The revelation should alter the present situation and give the next turn something concrete to work from, without requiring a question, cliffhanger, or forced decision at the end.\n\nLet the scene stop at a natural continuation point that follows from the information just delivered: after the new detail lands and the character has begun to adjust, after a decision is made but not yet carried out, or while the characters are still absorbing what happened. The next turn can pick up from that point because the scene has already shown what the character can do, what remains difficult, and what new understanding has just arrived. Preserve the active authorship contract, player control, and NPC behavior.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiac::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the martial adventure route of the Chinese literary tradition, shaped as episodic encounters that test a developing capability. Build each episode around a single test: the practitioner attempts something newly learned, and the encounter shows whether it holds under pressure. Keep the episode compact and legible on its own, but connect it to the next by what the practitioner carries forward from the test.\n\nWrite the encounters in brief declarative movement. Let the prose move from stillness to a single action or revelation that changes the meaning of what came before. Keep descriptions distilled: one telling detail of stance, terrain, or equipment can carry the scene's weight. Do not explain the moral of the encounter after it ends.\n\nDelay the key information until it revises what the test proved. The revelation should be specific to the episode's challenge: a rival's restraint may show that the technique was recognized rather than defeated, a narrow victory may reveal that the practitioner succeeded by accident rather than mastery, or a defeat may expose a flaw the practitioner had been hiding from. The delayed understanding must change what the practitioner carries into the next encounter, so the next test addresses the revised understanding rather than simply repeating the previous one.\n\nKeep the language, period, and community of the actual setting distinct. Do not import a generic martial world or a fixed cast of wandering swordsmen. The episodic structure and the emphasis on tested capability are the method; the setting's own facts determine who trains, who opposes them, and what advancement costs.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiad::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nShow advancement through physical attempts with concrete stakes: a grip that fails after repeated use, a technique that works only when the practitioner stops favoring an injured side, a resource that is consumed or damaged in the learning. Use forceful diction and compressed transitions. Move quickly from attempt to consequence, and let the body, equipment, or terrain carry the evidence of strain.\n\nKeep the prose spare but not uniformly short. A longer passage is warranted when the character is reasoning through a technical problem: comparing two failed attempts, calculating what a damaged resource can still do, or recognizing what a specific failure means for the next try. Otherwise, let a single hard image do the work: cracked leather, a blade edge turned, breath that will not settle, a stance corrected by pain.\n\nDelay one piece of information or one action until it changes the preceding stillness, and make the shift a material or physical recognition that revises the practitioner's technical understanding. A detail noticed earlier may turn out to be the cause of the failure: a crack in the grip that changed the angle, a worn strap that shifted the weight, a breath pattern the practitioner had been ignoring. A limit may reveal itself only under real pressure, showing that the technique was never secure. An opponent's movement may expose a flaw in the practitioner's own stance rather than a superior skill. The revelation should change what the practitioner can attempt next, not merely add atmosphere.\n\nVary the rhythm deliberately. A rapid sequence of attempts can be followed by a slower interval of repair, observation, or calculation. Do not turn every training scene into survival horror, and do not make the prose so compressed that the reader loses the causal connection between practice and changed capability. Advancement remains the throughline; grit is the texture of its cost.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiae::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the progression through documents, each with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. A training log may record what the writer attempted and what failed; a letter may ask for a resource while concealing why it is needed; a report may describe a test from the observer's limited position. Let each document show what its author knows and what that author cannot or will not say.\n\nMake advancement visible through the documents' changing content. The writer who once described a technique as impossible may later record performing it under specific conditions. A later entry may revise an earlier account, not because the writer lied, but because new understanding changed what the old record meant. Use material traces such as a crossed-out line, a torn page, or a delayed reply to mark what the documents themselves cannot fully explain.\n\nLet the gaps between documents carry the delayed revelation. A missing entry, a recipient's unanswered question, or a contradiction between two accounts can change the meaning of what came before. When the revelation arrives, it should alter the reader's understanding of the progression, not merely add a new fact.\n\nPreserve readable continuity without adding an omniscient explanation after every record. Use the specific document form the scene requires rather than a fixed rotation. The documents are the story's presentation; the practitioner's changed capability must be legible through what the documents record, omit, and later reinterpret.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiaf::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nLet warmth and practical care support the progression by showing what each comfort restores. A shared meal after a failed attempt may return the practitioner's steadiness; a companion who remembers how the practitioner takes tea may make it possible to accept help; a quiet interval for mending gear or resting an injured hand may let a technical problem become clear. Spend attention on the small pleasures themselves: the particular food, the familiar preference, the ease of a room where no one is performing. Then show the effect in the practitioner's readiness to attempt the next stage, not in a summary of recovery.\n\nKeep the prose spare and declarative. A single concrete detail can carry the comfort: a bowl set down without being asked, a blanket folded at the foot of a bed, a companion who waits instead of questioning. Do not inflate the moment with sentimental explanation. Let the care be visible in what is done, and let the practitioner's response be visible in a small change of posture, attention, or willingness to try again.\n\nAllow manageable friction. A disagreement about when to rest, a preference ignored, or a kindness offered clumsily can keep the characters distinct without turning the scene harsh. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable, and established problems remain in the world. Affection can be sincere without a confession or an emotional repair.\n\nDelay one small revelation or action until it changes the preceding ease. Choose a shift that deepens the warmth or clarifies a difficulty: a detail the practitioner notices about the companion's care, a reason the companion has not spoken, or a simple gesture that reinterprets the quiet. The revelation should leave the practitioner more able to face the next stage of advancement, or more honestly aware of what the next stage will cost, without demanding a resolution. The next attempt can begin from this rested, particularized moment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiag::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite progression through sustained novelistic paragraphs that place each test in a social and historical frame. Where the chosen narrator permits, show what the practitioner's advancement means to the household, school, faction, or community: who gains from the new capability, who fears it, and what obligations it carries. Use deliberate transitions to move from the broader context into the particular attempt.\n\nWithin that formal movement, keep Gu Long's distilled images and declarative sentences as the points of pressure. A long paragraph may establish the room, the season, and the weight of expectation; then a short sentence can deliver the attempt or its failure. Let the contrast between the formal frame and the spare action create the scene's rhythm.\n\nDelay one piece of information or one action until it changes the preceding stillness, and make the shift revise what the advancement means to the practitioner within the social frame already established. A teacher's silence may turn out to be an old obligation that makes the new skill a debt rather than a triumph. A rival's restraint may reveal a shared history that changes the victory into a test of loyalty. A success may depend on a resource the practitioner did not know had been arranged, turning pride into obligation. The delayed understanding should alter the practitioner's private relation to the social meaning of the advancement, not merely add background.\n\nKeep narration distinct from ordinary spoken language. Dialogue may be brief and oblique, but the narrative prose can carry judgment, history, and social consequence. Do not make the cast uniformly formal or the setting aristocratic. The formal architecture is the style; the world's actual social arrangements determine what the practitioner's progress costs and whom it binds.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiah::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the present scene in lean declarative sentences. Anchor it in one concrete attempt: a movement being learned, a task being repeated, a limit being tested. Notice only the details that change capability, risk, or understanding. Let a pause, glance, or unfinished answer carry what the protagonist knows but cannot yet explain.\n\nWhen the present attempt stalls, open a framed account. The protagonist tells a listener about an earlier training effort: the exercise that helped, the failure that redirected, the teacher's single correction. Give the telling an immediate occasion and purpose tied to the present scene. The protagonist may be persuading a companion to wait, warning a student, bargaining for access, or answering a question about why they move as they do. The listener is present and active, not a passive frame.\n\nLet the listener interrupt or question the account. The interruption marks the gap between the simplified version and the fuller inner history: a missing injury, an omitted debt, a motive the teller did not name. The protagonist does not explain the gap. They answer with practice: they return to the present attempt and reinvest the older lesson in it. The nested tale is not autobiography for its own sake; it is a tool the protagonist uses in the current situation, and its effect registers in the listener's silence, altered cooperation, or refusal.\n\nMake the progression cumulative but not mechanical. Advance one avenue at a time: a stance becomes faster, a formed idea becomes an executed movement, a debt alters how new choices feel. Distinguish knowing an exercise from embodying it. Show the returned ability in action, not description. When the protagonist applies the old lesson, the reader should see the difference: a guard that was an inch wide is now a line; a step that once collapsed now holds.\n\nUse repetition as a frame link. A phrase, object, or gesture from the embedded tale returns in the present scene with a new meaning. The first telling may name it as failure; the present return may show it as foundation. Do not let the repetition certify that the story is true. It connects the narrative levels and shows what the protagonist has since learned.\n\nKeep the frame intelligible. Mark the return to the present clearly, and do not stack tales within tales unless the current scene requires it. The Arabian Nights technique is a way of withholding and revealing at the right time, not a disguise for what the reader must infer. The protagonist's growing capacity is the real thread; the framed story is its curved mirror. Avoid automatic myth-making: the listener's response may be admiration, doubt, indifference, or practical use, and a story does not make the protagonist invincible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiai::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite progression as social-developmental realism. Record the work: hours of practice, exhausted assistants, worn tools, changing schedules, and the accounts that make training possible. Include the immediate material costs and rewards of capability. A technique has a source, a transmission, and a price; show each in ordinary prose.\n\nKeep the narration brief and declarative. State the step taken, the observable change, and the next limitation. Do not explain every motive or fill every pause. A skill that improves can be shown with one concrete feat or failure. When the protagonist understands something later, reveal it only when that understanding changes an action or choice.\n\nMake the increase social. Observe how others respond to new ability: an employer raises a task, a rival withholds patronage, a neighbor reinterprets an old debt, a teacher corrects a presumption. These consequences accumulate alongside the work itself. Advancement is legible through what the protagonist can now accomplish and what others will allow them to attempt.\n\nDo not romanticize effort. Labor can be tedious, interrupted, insufficient, or harmful. Strength develops unevenly and may fail under pressure. Give the reader enough detail to infer the mechanism: a changed grip makes a cut cleaner, more stabled nerves improve recall, better nutrition shortens recovery. Let one such detail stand alone rather than reviewing the whole system.\n\nPlant one early fact and leave it unexplained. It may be material: a locked drawer, a worn tool hung out of reach, a name in an account book. Or it may be social: a teacher's silence at a particular name, a neighbor's refusal to cross a threshold, an apprentice's unexplained absence. Do not comment on it. Let it remain part of the ordinary record.\n\nReturn to that fact later, when the protagonist's changed capability or understanding makes it legible. The return may be an action: the protagonist now opens the drawer, uses the tool, crosses the threshold. Or it may be a single plain statement from another character or the narrator. State it without moralizing. The delayed revelation changes how the reader interprets the earlier passage; it does not introduce a new fact from nowhere.\n\nThe social view includes schooling, apprenticeship, domestic expectation, and economic mobility without reducing them to national or bureaucratic caricature. The telling can be intimate, factual, or slightly dry according to the narrator, but it must remain concrete. The method is about what was true all along.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiaj::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Gothic-Punk\n\nPlace progression inside inherited structures. The protagonist trains under old masters, spent foundations, street-level brokers, or a family system that both holds knowledge and extracts obedience. Advancement is access: a key, a password, a room, a recorded exception. Show how each old privilege still works through a modern mechanism such as rent, surveillance, salvage, or sealed archives.\n\nWrite lean. Describe one telling material detail per scene: a chalk mark by a drain, a cracked training tablet, a callus under a ring. Let the image do the atmosphere; do not pile adjectives. Dialogue is sharp, incomplete, and carries rival claims about the same resource.\n\nPlant one still image and let its meaning be accepted. It may be a locked door, a ledger left open, a teacher's hand resting on a particular shelf, a practice hall empty at an hour when it should be full. Do not explain it. Let the protagonist and the reader accept the surface meaning: the door is locked, the ledger is a record, the hand is resting, the hall is empty.\n\nReturn to that image later, when the protagonist's changed access or understanding makes it legible. The return may be an action: the protagonist now opens the door, reads the ledger, reaches the shelf, enters the hall at that hour. Or it may be a single piece of dialogue from another character. The revelation alters how the reader interprets the earlier passage; it does not introduce a new fact from nowhere. The stillness was not empty. It was a fact about the system waiting to be read.\n\nThe method of growth is uneven. Separate knowing from executing. A student can recite a lineage and still misstep the first form; a veteran can perform a perfect strike while unable to read the new contract. Improvement should show through what the protagonist now dares or refuses, not through status screens. Feedback is often personal: praise mixed with manipulation, discipline mixed with care, correction that costs something.\n\nUse intimate private experience. Under the social mask, the protagonist should feel desire, dread, loyalty, shame, or reluctant gratitude about the system that is teaching them. One such feeling may be exposed in a trusted conversation; another may stay hidden. Neither should automatically make the person virtuous or doomed. The Gothic-Punk mood changes how an encounter is experienced: the same lesson in a clean gym and in a flooded basement is not the same lesson.\n\nDo not import Kindred powers, clans, or cosmic curses. Inherited power here is economic, institutional, familial, or cultural. It can shape bodies and minds through training and debt, not supernatural rule. Nor does every master betray: some elders genuinely pass on skill while extracting a price; others protect one student at the expense of many. Keep the system morally complicated without making the cast uniformly cynical. The protagonist grows by learning how to use an inherited order, and by discovering which part of it they will not repeat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiak::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse a retrospective, first-person account of training and increasing capability. The narrator is the protagonist looking back, but not omniscient: memory is selective, and present understanding must not rewrite what the earlier self knew. When the later self corrects or understands the past, mark it as such: 'I did not know then that...' or 'Only later did I understand.'\n\nWrite in compact sentences. Leave gaps in recollection and implication. Do not sentimentalize or moralize: state a lesson as fact, then show its weight in one action. The narrator's style should carry the regional voice that the world supplies. Use the actual names, foods, trades, and expectations of the setting rather than any generic 'Indian' scenery. Keep the narrative intimate, focused on the protagonist's changing body and judgment.\n\nLet improvement be proven by small, observable differences: a task done faster, held patiently, taught to a sibling, then recovered differently. Separate mastery from familiarity. The narrator may look back and say, 'I thought I had learned it; in fact I had only memorized it.' Each stage should have a social frame: a teacher's expectation, a neighbor's trade, a household's need, a community's judgment. These are part of the learning, not background decoration.\n\nOmit one early fact for a character-specific reason. The narrator may leave out a debt, an injury, a humiliation, a promise, or a failure because shame, custom, fear, or loyalty prevented the earlier telling. Do not announce the omission. Let the reader feel the gap indirectly: a name avoided, a period skipped, an event reported with unnatural flatness.\n\nDisclose that fact later, when the protagonist's growth makes the new honesty possible. The disclosure should change the meaning of an already reported event: a teacher's harshness becomes protection, a companion's absence becomes sacrifice, a victory becomes borrowed. Mark the disclosure as a later understanding, not a correction of the earlier self. Use this sparingly, and only where the protagonist's growth makes the new honesty possible.\n\nAvoid assuming religion or caste. If the world supplies such facts, handle them specifically; if not, stay with labor, skill, and social relation. This is a testimony of capacity, not a fable about national resilience. The account should include actual harm and loss without turning every teacher into a betrayer or every gain into a moral lesson. When the narrator says what a person declined to say, make that silence a piece of evidence, not an aesthetic pause.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaial::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nRecord progression through dated or strictly placed entries. Each entry is composed in the present of that moment: what the protagonist noticed, what they attempted, what failed or repeated. The writing is brief and declarative. Enter the concrete situation cleanly; do not summarize a season into a generality. Seasonal attention can appear through actual weather, light, work, or available food, only when it is part of the protagonist's observation.\n\nBecause this is a diary, the protagonist may write what they believe then, not what they will understand later. Let later entries reveal the change without retrospective commentary. The gap between entries does the work. If the narrator chooses to omit something, the reader should feel the absence indirectly, not be told that a secret is being kept.\n\nMake improvement visible in the record. The same movement or task appears again: first written as impossible, later with a single qualifying note, later not mentioned because it has become habit. Use physical and social proof: a rope secured, a debt answered, a meal earned, a wrong step corrected. Training is incomplete, recursive, and uneven; an entry may record a gain that is later lost or a failure that later becomes the foundation for a new attempt.\n\nPlant one unexplained image or task in an early entry. It may be a cracked floorboard from a failed footwork, a line of encouragement left on a wrapper, an empty practice floor at dawn, a tool the protagonist cannot yet use. Record it without commentary. Let it stand as part of the day's observation.\n\nReturn to that image or task in a later entry. The return may be an action: the protagonist now uses the tool, repairs the floorboard, understands the encouragement, moves across the practice floor without error. Or it may be a single reported action from another person. The later entry does not say 'I understood the principle.' It shows the technique now executed. The delayed revelation changes how the reader interprets the earlier passage; it does not introduce a new fact from nowhere.\n\nUse Gu Long's stillness. Often one image should carry the advancement. Dialogue in a diary is reported or remembered speech; keep such passages compact and consequential. Do not force universal quietness, cherry blossoms, or a particular religion. The narrator may be exuberant, angry, mechanical, or tired according to their actual character. The miscellany can include a copied exercise, a bill, a complaint, or an overheard remark. Keep each fragment tied to the developing capability. This is not a list of events; it is a self-account in which every retained observation is selected by the writer's changing attention and judged by what the writer now can do.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiam::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in chronicle/testimony mode. The narrator observes and records advancement as it changes work, reputation, and social relation. This is not private stream-of-consciousness; it is a deliberate account of events, material conditions, and human costs. Use the language, community, and history the world supplies. Preserve local names and terms with enough context to keep them intelligible.\n\nKeep the sentences tight and concrete. Name the teacher, the task, the borrowed tool, the failed attempt, the changed schedule. Do not explain every improvement; let one feat or one repeated correction carry the evidence. The narrator may report what others said or did, but must distinguish actual observation from remembered report. A rumor, a song, or a ledger entry can be noted, but no supernatural power may be added unless the world already permits it.\n\nMake progression social and physical. Show what the protagonist can now do: cross a flooded road, fix a motor, hold a line, teach a child, pay a debt. Show what they still cannot do. Advancement comes through repetition, guidance, and changed access. It should be uneven, and gains can be lost or redirected through the actual conditions of the setting. The context matters: the same skill in a stable household and a camp is not the same cost.\n\nPlant one unexplained image early. It may be a teacher stopping at a doorway, a share of food left untouched, a mark on a wall, a tool hung out of reach. Record it without comment. Let it stand as part of the chronicle's ordinary record. Do not moralize it or hint at its meaning.\n\nReturn to that image later, when the protagonist's changed capability or understanding makes it legible. The return may be an action: the protagonist now crosses the doorway, eats the food, reads the mark, uses the tool. Or it may be a single piece of dialogue from another character. The revelation alters how the reader interprets an earlier passage; it does not introduce a new fact from nowhere.\n\nThe narrator may have a stake in the account: they may be defending a friend, justifying themselves, or bearing witness. This should shape selection and emphasis without turning every witness into a liar. The record includes genuine loss, refusal, and contradiction. Do not make the chronicle a nostalgic celebration of resilience. Let the development of skill articulate with the social contradictions of the setting: old masters, broken apprenticeships, informal teaching, and new necessities. This is a path of improvement, told under conditions that make improvement meaningful.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaian::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Light Novel\n\nEnter each scene cleanly. Give a clear spatial anchor, the actor's immediate goal, and one obstacle. Use short paragraphs to separate movement, dialogue, and private response. New names or mechanics should be introduced once and concretely, not through glossaries.\n\nMake progression occur in beat-level feedback. Show the attempt, the miss or partial success, the correction, and the retest. A teacher's single word, a knot untied, a blade held a finger's width differently—these are advancement. Do not let the camera wander into lore during an action beat. When a technique improves, show the difference physically: the guard was an inch wide; now it is a line.\n\nUse focused reaction beats. The protagonist should feel the gain or loss in the body and attention: sudden ease, delayed pain, pride, confusion, frustration, or cold fear. These reactions may be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny, depending on the character. They should be brief and not replace practical action. Keep private response distinct from spoken comment when the character hides or delays it.\n\nPlant one still moment and let it stand. It may be a breath, a fingertip tapping the table, a sheathed blade picked up, a teacher's hand pausing over a correction. Record it without comment. Do not explain it or hint at its meaning.\n\nReturn to that moment later, in the same or a subsequent scene. The return may be an action: the protagonist now performs the technique, picks up the blade, completes the correction, understands the pause. Or it may be a single piece of dialogue from another character. The payoff alters how the reader interprets the earlier passage; it does not introduce a new fact from nowhere.\n\nLet dialogue stay compact and outcome-oriented. Exchanges should move the lesson or conflict forward. A question can go unanswered; a master can say less than the student needs. Keep the prose fast and legible. Vary paragraph length for rhythm, but maintain a strong visual sequence. A reaction beat can be serious, but it must not balloon into a reflective essay. Comedy and a young cast are not requirements. Any closing hook must emerge from the actual scene; do not invent a disaster just to stop the reply. The method is immediate, sensory, and measured by the protagonist's improving movement through material space.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiao::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite advancement as a change in what the focal character can perceive, hold in attention, or name. When a skill develops, the world itself becomes different in a specific way: a sound that was once noise now has a rhythm, a shadow that was once vague now has a shape, a silence that was once empty now has a weight. Ground that new perception in the actual attempt. A hand learning to cut cleanly through a stalk can be followed by a sentence that leaves the image to sit before someone asks a question the character cannot yet answer.\n\nLet recurring sensory details return with changed meaning. The same river sound, the same thrum of insects, the same taste of dust can mark the difference between one attempt and the next, so the reader experiences growth as a difference in perception rather than as a narrated improvement score. A comparison may unfold when it changes what the narrator understands or makes present; a delayed recognition can shift the meaning of an earlier image without resolving the scene. The revelation need not be a completed breakthrough. A half-understood sensation is a legitimate progression beat when it makes the next attempt or observation different.\n\nKeep images traceable to the scene and the narrator's associations. Plain sentences can intrude to give the lyric a ground note, and other people need not adopt the narrator's figurative register. If a character learns by touch, let the lyric follow the body before it follows the idea. End when the present attention or attempt has reached its natural pause, not where a thematic statement has been completed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiap::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet advancement carry a remembered weight. A new capability often makes something old irrevocable: the grip that could not hold a sword now can, but the hands that first felt useless are not the same hands. When the narrator gains skill, the earlier self remains present as a felt memory, not as a summary. The glance backward may be tender, strange, relieved, or tired; it need not be sorrowful.\n\nUse Gu Long's stillness as the place where the past becomes visible. Keep the underlying event plain and declarative, and let the reflection arrive in a single detail rather than an explanation. A character may notice that a training ground smells the same as it did years ago, or that a teacher's voice still rises at the same word. That recognition sits beside the present action instead of replacing it.\n\nDo not make every scene announce impermanence. Ordinary enjoyment, humor, and curiosity survive inside the reflective register. The important operation is the coexistence of what the narrator can now do with what they remember of not being able to do it. A delayed realization may come long after the event, and when it arrives it can change the meaning of a familiar thing without supplying an answer. End where the present action or attention ends, not where a statement about loss has been completed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiaq::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell the progression as a framed oral account. A later narrator, teacher, companion, or elder tells how the focal character came to do a particular thing. The frame establishes that this is a shaped retelling, not the raw event. Within the tale, write the actual learning process with concrete attempts, misreadings, practice, and change. The outer narrator can comment, question, address a listener, or withhold a motive, but the inner account must still show the character's developing capability.\n\nLet the frame's uncertainty work with Gu Long's deliberate gaps. A listener may interrupt to ask whether the character truly understood a lesson, or to dispute a version of a remembered deed. The narrator may answer, change the subject, or remain silent. When the narrator does not explain, let the omission create meaning: the gap can mark what the community has forgotten, what the teacher declined to teach, or what the character themselves did not know at the time.\n\nUse lyric and reflective prose within the telling, not as decoration laid on top of it. A repeated phrase can open a return to an earlier episode. A concrete object—a worn practice blade, a water jar, a marked doorway—can carry the transition from one phase of the story to another. Comparisons come from the narrator's world and knowledge, not from the author's contemporary idiom. Social and domestic realism sits beside the tale without making the story a fable: the person who trains still buys bread, argues with a relative, and sleeps badly.\n\nKeep the telling's conventions consistent within a single tradition—Persian, Arabic, Kurdish, Hebrew, or another—rather than mixing them. The setting, language, community, and period come from the active story. The frame may end with the account brought to a close, a listener's challenge, or the narrator refusing to finish, but the focal character's progression remains real and legible inside the frame.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiar::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Minimalist Prose\n\nWrite the training, attempt, and feedback as a series of concrete, understated events. Choose one or two details that change: the grip steadier by a finger's width, the foot no longer sliding on the turn, the breath lasting through the full movement. Show the new capability by what the character can now do that a page ago they could not. Do not explain the improvement unless the explanation changes the present action.\n\nKeep clauses direct and causal links visible: because the wrist remained loose, the blade went where the eye aimed. A thought can be long enough to be clear, but it should not repeat the action it accompanies. Let dialogue interrupt cleanly, with each speaker's voice distinct. A terse teacher may use fewer words than a beginner who explains too much; that difference itself can carry learning.\n\nOmission should leave something inferable, not delete causality. A narrator may leave out the moment of insight and return later when the character's hands already know the form. But the reader must see enough attempts, errors, or quiet repetitions to accept the change. The gap works as a retrospective clue, not as a hole in the sequence.\n\nLet a revelation come as a single plain fact that changes what went before. It need not be proclaimed. A character can say, \"You were never holding your breath to aim. You were holding it to be finished,\" and the scene shifts without further commentary. End when the present action or thought has completed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaias::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the developing skill inside ordinary novelistic scenes. A training day is not only repetitions; it includes a conversation about pay, a remembered argument, an awkward meal, a moment of quiet observation. Move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience to show how these parts of a day affect one another. The progression happens through practice and feedback, but it also happens through changed intention, new understanding, and a shifting sense of what the skill is for.\n\nKeep Gu Long's economy inside the flexible movement. A sharp exchange can break a longer descriptive passage. A meaningful silence can sit between two paragraphs without being inflated into a symbolic set piece. A clear sentence can do more work than a long explanation. The narrator's voice need not be uniformly clipped or uniformly expansive; it follows the needs of the scene.\n\nUse recollection to connect past attempts to present ones, but keep the past and present experiences distinct. The narrator may think, I used to hit heavy, because I thought strength was the only answer, and then return to the present moment where a lighter touch opens the defense. That recognition is part of the learning. It does not need to be announced as a lesson.\n\nLet delayed revelation arrive naturally inside the prose. A detail from an earlier scene—a teacher's odd phrasing, a mark on a wall, a recurring dream—can be explained without drama when the narrator suddenly understands it. The explanation may come in a subordinate clause or a single declarative sentence. After it, the story continues. End the reply where the scene's immediate attention or business ends, not where a thematic statement has been completed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiat::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nAnchor the progression in the particular relationship the story has established. The focal character's attempts, practice, and feedback occur in scenes that also carry the relationship's ordinary texture: a shared room, a repeated private joke, a phrase that means something only to them, a gesture one of them noticed long before the other. The skill grows through time spent together and apart. The other person need not be a teacher or rival; they may be the one who watches, the one who worries, or the one whose absence makes a practice session feel different.\n\nUse Gu Long's implication for what the characters do not say. A long look across a training yard can carry more emotional weight than a speech. A quiet return of a borrowed object can mark a change in trust. A question that goes unanswered can be as important as the answer. Do not inflate every exchange into a declaration; let repetition and private language build the payoff.\n\nGive affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery enough space to develop. If the relationship premise promises rivalry, let the competition change as the focal character improves: the other may notice, resent, admire, or adjust. If the premise promises comfort, let one person's new ability change how they can care for, protect, or understand the other—without making the other passive. Consequences fulfill the particular relationship, not a generic checklist of familiar tropes.\n\nThe relationship may return to ordinary life after a breakthrough; not every reply needs a climactic emotional revelation. End where the scene's immediate attention or business ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiau::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the progression as a chronicle of deeds and remembered struggle. Open deliberately to mark that this is a retelling: a compact declaration of who the person became, then a return to the first attempt, the early failure, the years of practice, the unlikely demonstration. Names, places, and recurring phrases give the account its epic coherence. A place called Three Stones or a designation like \"the one who held the gate\" may enter the telling because the community later remembers it; within the scene, the character is still just someone learning.\n\nKeep Gu Long's stillness inside the epic movement. Short declarative sentences can establish the deed; a gap in the narration can mark what the chronicler will not tell. Between the named feats, include the quiet moments: the practice before dawn, the repeated correction, the smell of rain on the training ground, the tired admission that the body has not yet learned. The point of view determines how much of this inner life can appear. If the narrator is separate from the focal character, the inner moment may be reconstructed from later accounts or left hinted in a gesture.\n\nLet a revelation change the meaning of a remembered event. The chronicler may say, \"No one knew why she refused to draw in the rain,\" and later, \"They learned it later, from a companion's account, and then the story changed.\" That delayed understanding is part of the retelling. Do not make every episode a victory. A failed attempt can become the background of a later success, and a private hesitation can be more significant than a public triumph.\n\nUse the saga's public judgments without flattening the character. A community may call someone dependable before that dependability has become reliable; the narrator can show the difference between reputation and private experience. End the reply with the telling brought to a natural pause after the current deed is done, leaving the next part available to be told.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiav::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the progression through a Scandinavian folktale-inflected communal realism in which advancement is tested by seasonal work that the local community can observe. Anchor each stage of capability in a recurring practical task tied to the actual season: the wall that must hold before meltwater, the boat caulked before the crossing, the iron quenched in its proper order. The learner attempts the task, fails or partially succeeds in a way the scene makes physically specific, and later performs the same task under conditions close enough that the difference is legible. Recognition arrives through another person's brief observance or a change in how the community assigns the work, not through an internal status marker.\n\nUse Gu Long's compact declarative sentences to carry the telling. Leave the explanatory middle out where a different scene would fill it: render a master's refusal as She did not look up, then let the next image—the cup moved to the edge—carry the change. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep dialogue spare and practical, each line doing one piece of work, and recover a prior fact only at the moment it becomes operative, such as an old instruction now seen to apply to the present task. Let a revelation or action alter the preceding stillness rather than announce itself.\n\nTreat the folktale mode as patterned recurrence and delayed recognition, not a collection of generic rituals. A harmless song learned early may be understood later to name a route back through the pass; a child's counting game may return as the sequence for the quench. Keep the regional context particular through consistent local naming and material practice: if the setting is Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish, use that community's terms for the work, the season, and the test, and do not import another region's customs. Warm domestic labor and ordinary interaction carry the same narrative weight as frightening trials; snow, isolation, and far-north wilderness are not required. Preserve required nonspoken interiority as actual private experience in the active presentation, distinct from what another character can observe or hear. The response can close on a completed earning, a new understanding through a brief exchange, or a quiet change in the daily tasks, without resolving the whole progression.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiaw::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Screenplay\n\nWrite the progression through comparable visible actions across scenes. Establish the test object and conditions in an early action block: the weight lifted only partway, the blade stroke tried and missed once before it lands. Return to the same object or movement under conditions close enough to compare, and show the changed capability in what the character now performs—the weight held at full height, the stroke landing clean on the first attempt. Do not state a level or rank; the visible result is the evidence.\n\nUse scene headings only when location or time actually changes, and make each action block carry one visible event with clear orientation: who is present, what object or movement matters, what result occurs. Let Gu Long's economy shape the cuts. A master turns away without explanation, and a later offscreen sound implies a private attempt the audience does not see. Use CUT TO or OFFSCREEN only when the withheld interval changes what the audience can infer about the attempt or its result. When the changed capability appears, make it a small observable action that reorganizes the prior scene: the same locked shutter now opens from the other side, the same difficult crossing is made without the helper's hand.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep dialogue clipped and practical, each line doing one job, and preserve uncertainty through what a character notices or declines to say. A focused reaction can appear as a pause or a glance, but an expression does not by itself certify failure or victory. If the active contract authorizes private commentary, render it as a clearly separated nonspoken note in the script, not a line another character can hear; use voice-over only when the chosen sequence actually needs that form of private address. Let the progression strand develop through preparing, failing once, receiving a short correction, and applying it later. A scene can close after a demonstrated change, a refusal that now makes sense, or a remaining difficulty, without adding ranks, system screens, or mechanical numbers.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiax::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Stage Play\n\nWrite the play so every change in capability is actable on the shared stage. Build a training scene from repeated physical business—the same rope, the same bowl, the same position—whose first attempt fails and whose later attempt visibly succeeds under equivalent circumstances. Stage directions record only playable facts: who stands where, what object passes between hands, where a pause occurs, who enters or exits. Use an exit or entrance to compact an irrelevant interval. If a secret practice happens offstage, do not erase it from history; let it return when its consequence enters the stage as a task the character now accomplishes in view of the audience.\n\nUse Gu Long's economical speech. Lines are short and pointed, often responding to visible work rather than explaining it. When a speaker withholds an obvious fact, stage the withholding: the character looks at the wound, then exits without comment, and the audience sees the gap. A monologue is not the default destination for thought or development. If the active contract authorizes introspection, preserve it as clearly separated nonspoken authoring material formatted outside the spoken lines, and do not let another character react to it as heard speech. An aside serves a dramatic purpose only when the play's convention calls for it.\n\nChoose the practical test from the present social and physical situation—carrying water, rehanging a door, reciting a route in the right order—and let recognition of growth come from another character's brief line or a change in how they treat the learner. Keep the progression uneven: a new capability coexists with an old difficulty in the same scene. The response may stop after a completed attempt, an interruption, or a quiet refusal that leaves the next stage open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiay::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the entire response through one authorized focal mind, whose private attention and memory carry the progression. The opening takes an immediate sensory detail—pressure in the palm, a wet sleeve, a half-finished knot—and moves associatively from that detail into a previous failed attempt or an instructor's unfinished sentence. Keep enough orientation that the reader can separate the present action from a memory or anticipation. Place a recurring image—a nail that will not seat, a stone that shifts at the last pull—at the first failed attempt, and return to the same image during a new attempt so the changed capability is felt in the body before it is named.\n\nUse Gu Long's brief declarative movement as a punctual break inside the flow. A short sentence closes an associative drift and changes the preceding feeling: Not yet. Now. The reader receives the consequence of the reflection, not an explanation of it. Dialogue cuts across the flow with little framing, just enough to attribute the other voice. Keep the viewpoint strictly limited: other minds cannot be entered, and their reported words remain external. If a character's actual past is unknown, the narration holds that uncertainty rather than imagining their memory as fact.\n\nMake the progression strand causal and testable despite the associative style. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it by returning to the exact practical act: the mind recalls the sequence perfectly, then the hand still falters at the same turn. Later, the body alters the sequence and the task is completed; only then can the character notice what has changed. Uneven development appears simultaneously as one remembered skill returns while another remains slow. Real physical events, interruptions, and other people's actions break into the mental movement and materially alter what happens next. The response can stop on a shift in private understanding, a remaining difficulty, or a glance at the next unfinished task.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiaz::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the story through attributed messages, so each progression beat appears in what the characters actually send, receive, or observe about the exchange. Establish each sender's voice through their actual message habits: one character writes quickly with few words, another asks precise questions or corrects a term, a third replies only after an action has succeeded. Do not explain growth in narration unavailable to the exchange. If a private attempt matters, make it return as a later message that refers to the result—not as a transmitted thought. Use timestamps, read receipts, typing indicators, edits, or attachments only when their specific occurrence changes what the other person can know. If a sender writes and then deletes a line, show the deletion as an interface event and let the receiver respond to what remains or to the visible delay.\n\nUse Gu Long's compactness by making short messages carry weight. A brief exchange opens the scene, then the intervening trial is skipped; the delayed revelation arrives as a new message that echoes the old one with one decisive difference: From: still can't lift it. To: lifted it in front of the feed. Reference an attachment or shared note only when its content is the actual evidence of changed capability, and quote or summarize that content. A message may withhold ordinary explanation for a practical reason, but the withholding must be plausible: the person may be traveling, working with hands full, or waiting for the result, not every pause means concealment.\n\nDevelop the progression through a sequence of exchanges: discuss the intended task, report a failed attempt with enough precise detail to make later comparison possible, receive concrete feedback, and then demonstrate the new capability in terms the earlier message can confront. Keep the world's rules consistent: if a system or capability exists, its function is implied by what the senders do and say, not by a messaging-side status screen or rank quantification. The response can close on a sent message that changes a pending plan, a short confirmation after a real attempt, or an unsent draft marked separately when the focal experience needs to remain private.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaiba::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the visual-novel scene as a sequence of short narrative passages, attributed dialogue, and focused focal reactions suited to illustrated panels. Start a scene with one concrete setting cue and a clear relation between the characters and the immediate task: the workbench is pushed against the far wall, two figures beside it. The active viewpoint shows the attempt through the body, not through a system label: the person tries the motion, misses the key turn, and the next line reveals what changed. Use background or expression cues only when they orient a meaningful change; a face that stills can introduce a pause, but it does not certify the failure or the victory by itself. Keep scene changes legible with a short labeled transition when location or time moves. Do not fabricate a branch or player decision, and do not activate game mechanics or choices.\n\nUse Gu Long's compact delivery within the panel rhythm: a single line carries a revelation or refusal, followed by a beat of focal reaction before the next action. Preserve a previously seen detail—an unlit lamp, a half-hung cloth, a stain on the floor—so a later scene can reuse it and make its meaning shift. A speaker may ask one question that appears social but has a practical consequence; the next panel answers through action rather than explanation. Keep the progression through uneven development: a skill improves along one axis while another remains visibly awkward in the same scene. The change is testable because the same object or action returns: the rope that slipped now holds for ten counts, the kick that missed now stops at the right height.\n\nIf private experience is required, render it as clearly visible focal narrated thought in the active presentation, not as a branch option or a line another character hears. Another person responds only to what they can actually observe; the focal character's withheld thought can be represented by a brief narrated omission, but not every omission is a trick. The scene may stop on a recognized change, a significant glance, or a quieter earning that alters the next interaction.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalaibb::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Gu Long / Web Serial\n\nWrite each installment as a readable piece of a longer progression, not as a self-contained mini-chapter with a compulsory hook. Open with the task currently underway and who is present. Recover the prior development chain by briefly recalling the specific earlier attempt or resource only when the present scene needs comparison to it. Use practical, recurring tasks as the test: the same wall, the same weight, the same route, the same instrument. If the character tried before and failed, the present action shows what has changed without narrating a full progression log. A new installment may start immediately after the previous pause or after a clearly marked gap; time jumps are allowed only when the intervening events are recoverably implied later.\n\nUse Gu Long's compact sentences to create delayed meaning within the current installment. A short line stops a routine scene: She said, You always put the pin in from the south. That line, learned in this reply, makes a past action read differently; the shift lands without a long explanation. Let what a person notices carry the uncertainty. If the focal character does not yet understand the full implication, the scene presents only the observation; later installments return to it when the understanding exists. Keep real spoken dialogue spare and purposeful, and do not fill the gaps with narrator commentary that would have been visible to everyone. Preserve required private experience as authorized visible or hidden interiority, clearly attributed, not as an unmarked summary inside public narration.\n\nLet progression accumulate through earned developments across replies: a lesson learned in one installment changes behavior in the next without a summary infodump. Show uneven strengths and weaknesses in the same scene, and let mastery of one element coexist with fumbling at another. A response may close on a completed piece of work, a new understanding, a pending request, or a quiet refusal. Do not invent a cliffhanger, rank-up notification, or surprise betrayal to fill the end; the installment's through-line is the developing capability and the relationships and resources that change with it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajaa::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite each practice session as a measured routine with a definite progression gain. Arrange the same materials, begin from a familiar position, and narrow attention until a specific gesture becomes smooth. Show the gain through a concrete change in execution: a faster response, a steadier hand, a sequence that no longer needs to be thought through step by step. Let the gain sit beside what has not changed. The character may improve and still carry the same hurt, and the new capability can sharpen the distance between what the body can now do and what remains unresolved in a relationship.\n\nWhen an emotional beat arises, keep the narration calm and quietly observant. Have the character notice exactly what another person does with a glass, a sleeve, or a doorway, then connect that observed gesture to a private fear, need, or withheld apology. Do not announce the feeling as a label. Let conversations shift register: a practical exchange can become a request, an evasion, or an apology while the character continues noticing ordinary details—a ticking clock, a familiar scratch on a table—as pressure builds.\n\nAllow growth and unresolved tension to occupy the same scene. A newly learned pattern may have been practiced partly to avoid thinking about someone. An increase in capability may make a failure or absence more painful rather than more manageable. If a difficult relationship returns, the strain may worsen before any comfort arrives, and comfort may feel partial or inadequate. Use the progression gain within the emotional difficulty: the character may execute the technique correctly and still say the wrong thing, or understand what should be said and still fail to say it. End when that intersection has been rendered, not when improvement has solved the problem.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajab::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite each response as a participating interval in the current scene. Use clear speaker labels or context-appropriate attribution for every spoken line, and keep paragraphs short enough to be read in a scrolling conversation. Focus the interval on a single practical moment: warming up, repeating a motion, receiving feedback, or feeling the change in timing or force. Describe that moment through observable details—how a hand wraps around a tool, how breath settles after a repetition, which part of the room the character watches—rather than through abstract status reports.\n\nAdvance the progression thread through ordinary practice and conversation. When the character attempts something, show what is still clumsy and what has become easier. Let feedback arrive as a flat remark, a correction, or a practical demonstration. Do not turn every exchange into a hidden test; an NPC can offer a simple correction or adjust the character's stance. Improvement becomes visible when a previously failed action now succeeds or a previously slow action becomes quick, not when a number appears.\n\nKeep Murakami's even, observant tone during rapid roleplay: describe the strange or emotionally significant thing with the same calm attention as the ordinary thing. Let the character pause to notice a window's reflection, a pattern of dust, or the feel of a mat underfoot, and let that pause carry private feeling without breaking the scene's readability. If the moment turns emotionally intense, surface the discomfort through a change in speech rhythm or a small hesitation, not through a label like anxiety. End at a natural point from which the collaborative partner can continue speaking, acting, or observing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajac::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic telling route. Let each chapter or section advance the progression thread through a concrete task: learning a stance, mending equipment, traveling to a teacher, performing a small service, failing an attempt, then trying again with adjusted understanding. Close each episode with a change in capability that persists into ordinary life—a faster reaction, a steadier hand, a memory that now surfaces during practice—so improvement accumulates without being summarized as a number.\n\nNarrate with the direct, spoken rhythm of vernacular fiction, but let Murakami's quiet attention to detail shape the prose. Use short sentences for action and slightly longer ones for observation. When the character practices, describe what the body feels and what the room is like: sweat cooling, the smell of old wood, a bowl of water placed too far away. Let that close physical attention make the episode feel lived rather than merely recounted.\n\nPresent the progression as a record of deeds and consequences. Friends or teachers may comment on what the character can now do, but their praise or blame should reflect their own concerns, not operate as an automatic rank. A mistake can have social cost or damage an object; an improvement can change how others treat the character. Keep private feeling partly withheld, surfacing through small associations—a familiar dish, a repeated phrase, an object kept without reason—rather than announced in reflective monologues. End the episode on a threshold: a new instruction received, a room entered, a question left unanswered, with the path to further improvement continuing rather than concluding.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajad::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse forceful, concrete verbs and short transitions. When the character practices, write how the edge dulls, the rope frays, sweat leaks into an old cut, or a joint pops. Prove improvement by what the hands can now do without thought and what still breaks under pressure. Show advancement through material facts: a strike that used to rebound now lands cleanly; a length of cord that once bound poorly holds fast; a distance that took ten steps now takes seven. Do not narrate experience points.\n\nKeep Murakami's calm, level observation within the hard prose. Describe a strange or unsettling moment with the same flat nouns and exact surfaces as a routine practice. If a pattern in the floorboards repeats in a way that should not be possible, report it once, through sensory detail, and then return to the immediate task. The strangeness does not need to climax; it can remain an unexplained threshold that changes the character's attention slightly without dominating the scene.\n\nMaintain physical stakes rather than emotional amplification. Let a failed technique have a real consequence: a sprain, a chipped tool, a wasted hour, a missed rendezvous. Let a successful technique change the practical situation: a door can be forced, a climb completed, a load carried. When the character thinks at length, use the extended reasoning to plan a sequence, calculate material, or remember where a weakness began. Keep any private hurt compact—one remembered image is enough. End at the first moment when the new capability changes the physical reality, even if the emotional situation remains unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajae::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Epistolary / Documents\n\nUse a single document form, chosen by the scene: a training log, a letter to a distant teacher, a field report, or a set of dated notes. Identify each entry's author and time. Let the progression story emerge across several documents: a morning log records the exercise, its repetitions, and what went wrong; a later letter describes the same skill to someone else, necessarily omitting or reshaping details; a still later entry corrects an earlier belief. Show improvement through the changing descriptions—the same action moves from 'I could not keep my wrist straight' to 'the wrist remembers before I do'—not through abstract numbers.\n\nLet the documents carry Murakami's quiet uncertainty. The writer may record an ordinary object or event that seems connected to a private feeling without explaining the connection. A letter can mention a familiar sound, a pattern of light, or the position of a bowl, and the association remains partly unexplained. The document does not need to be true, only accurately represent its author's knowledge and purpose. A training log may conceal a failure to save face; a letter may emphasize progress to reassure the recipient; a report may misidentify the reason for a success.\n\nWrite each document's language according to its purpose. A personal log can be fragmentary and shaped by fatigue; a letter can be more formal but still contain the writer's specific phrases; a report can be terse and follow a required order. Let gaps and revisions be meaningful: a torn edge, a crossed-out instruction, or a missing page can suggest a change in understanding. Preserve continuity by allowing later documents to refer back to earlier ones, so the reader can see the same attempt seen again weeks later with better knowledge. Do not add an omniscient narrator to explain what the documents leave uncertain. The final entry should leave the progression alive—a new goal written down, a question posed to a teacher, or a single dated line after a long silence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajaf::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nTreat training as a gentle, repeated ritual. Describe the small pleasures that make practice tolerable and even good: a cup of tea cooling at the right moment, a chair with a worn familiar arm, a quiet room where the same movement can be tried again. Show improvement through the accumulation of small successes—a knot tied tighter, a step landed without wobbling, a phrase remembered the next morning. Let the narration notice these successes the way a friend would, with quiet approval, rather than as a score.\n\nWrite warmth through chosen acts of care, not through agreement or emotional declaration. One character may bring water, adjust a cushion, leave a window open, or remember how the other takes their tea. Let these actions matter even when the practicing character is frustrated or distant. Do not force an apology, a confession, or a repaired relationship. A person can be kind and still keep a private hurt; a moment of comfort can come and then the difficulty continues. The comfort is real, but it does not dissolve the progression's pressure or the character's loneliness.\n\nKeep Murakami's even strangeness as a source of small wonder rather than threat. A cat crosses the practice floor; the light through a high window moves exactly one tile per hour; a radio plays a song nobody recognizes. Let these details make the world feel slightly unreal without turning the genre into horror or mystery. Use them to mark the passage of practice time so that growth feels gradual and shared. End the scene when a specific comfort has been given and received, even if the larger problem remains unresolved. The next training session can then begin from a slightly easier place.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajag::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Formal Literary Novel\n\nCompose each section as a full chapter movement. Open with a locating paragraph that places the moment within the character's present life and the social world around the practice: the hour, the custom, the person who taught this method, the room's history. Then move into the training scene with sustained, connected prose. Describe the attempt in enough physical and temporal detail that the reader can feel the repeated action becoming easier: the first movement uncertain, the second steadier, the third almost automatic. End the chapter with a deliberate turn—a new understanding, a changed social position, or a quiet threshold—not with a forced cliffhanger.\n\nUse a narrator with controlled distance, in Murakami's calm register. Let the narration observe what the character does and occasionally what the character privately thinks, but avoid dramatic exclamation. Give a strange detail the same weight as an ordinary one: a door that seems to open before the hand reaches it, a label written in no known script, a reflection that pauses before the person moving away. Let the threshold remain unexplained, and let its meaning live in the narrator's even tone and the character's hesitation. Do not let the strange event become the engine; the progression thread remains the sustained effort and changed capability.\n\nFrame improvement socially as well as physically. A new skill may change how teachers, peers, or family members treat the character, and the chapter should register that change through conversation, obligation, or custom. But do not make all relationships pivot on the progression. Some remain unchanged, which can be its own quiet burden. The character can improve while still feeling lonely, uncertain, or left behind. Use deliberate transitions to connect training scenes to other parts of life and to earlier chapters, so the progression feels like a life accumulating rather than a series of isolated exercises. The chapter may close with a sentence that looks back or forward, leaving the path open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajah::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen each outer scene with the listener's present difficulty in acquiring a capability: a movement that will not repeat, a resource the listener cannot yet use, a principle the listener can state but cannot execute. Give the telling an immediate occasion by making the teller choose an embedded story in response to that specific difficulty. The teller does not deliver a general lesson; the tale recounts one earlier attempt whose conditions resemble the listener's current problem closely enough to be useful, and whose differences matter as much as its similarities.\n\nInside the embedded story, narrate the attempt as lived practice rather than completed wisdom. Show the learner preparing, trying, receiving concrete feedback, and returning to the task under a slightly changed condition. Distinguish what the learner understood from what the learner could perform at each stage: a character may explain the technique correctly while the hands still lag, or succeed once in a calm room and fail later under noise, fatigue, or another person's expectation. Let the tale end at the point where the learner's capability changed, not necessarily at a satisfying conclusion.\n\nReturn to the frame and let the listener interrupt with a question that exposes a gap between the tale and the present situation. The teller answers by adjusting the account, not by stating a rule: the listener learns that the earlier learner practiced with a different grip, a longer interval, or a resource the listener does not yet possess. Keep the narrative levels clear by marking each return to the frame with an ordinary action—pouring tea, walking to the practice space, setting down a tool—and by carrying one repeated object or phrase across the levels so the reader can track which attempt is being discussed.\n\nUse Murakami's conversational distance in both levels. The teller describes surfaces with calm exactness: the weight of a tool, the light in the room, the sound of traffic beyond the window. Private concern appears as an association the teller or listener notices without explaining. Strangeness may remain even and unresolved, but the physical facts of each attempt stay stable. Between tales, show the listener practicing: the frame's unhurried routine becomes the site of uneven improvement, with small successes and failures accumulating across the telling. End a scene where the listener's present attempt stands, not where a lesson is complete.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajai::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the story as a social-developmental account of one learner inside a specific training arrangement: a workshop, a course of instruction, a workplace apprenticeship, or a community with its own standards. Make the narration compare attempts across time. When the learner repeats a task, show what is observably different: the same movement now completed with less hesitation, the same mistake recognized before it causes damage, the same tool handled without the earlier pause. Do not translate these changes into a number; let the reader see the difference in the described action.\n\nTreat institutional feedback as a concrete event. A teacher does not merely approve or grade; the teacher stops the learner at a particular moment, adjusts the learner's hand or posture, demonstrates the correct sequence, or asks the learner to repeat the action while describing what went wrong. Show the learner's response to that correction: immediate improvement, confusion, resistance, or a change that only appears days later. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it by giving the learner moments when the explanation is clear and the body still fails, and later moments when the same action succeeds under conditions that previously broke it.\n\nUse Murakami's unhurried practical detail to carry the documentary method. Describe the preparation and aftermath of practice: the condition of the room, the cleaning of equipment, the walk to the place of instruction, the feel of a particular surface. Let a private association attach to one of these details without explanation. Keep strangeness even and unresolved where it appears, but do not let it dissolve the factual record of what the learner did and what happened.\n\nShow the learner's place in a web of obligations and expectations. A change in capability may alter how a colleague speaks to the learner, what work the learner is asked to do, or what the learner is permitted to attempt. Let relationships and recovery remain part of the developing life: a setback may require ordinary rest, and a friendship may shift because one person's ability has changed. End scenes at the point where the learner's present capability stands in relation to the community, not where a stage is completed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajaj::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Gothic-Punk\n\nBuild the progression around access. The learner's attempts require something controlled by an older arrangement: a neglected practice space, a tool kept by a former master, permission to enter a place where old rules still apply, or a resource that changes hands through obligation rather than open purchase. Show the learner negotiating that access in the present. Each practice session then carries a social weight: the learner is not only trying to improve but also testing what the arrangement will allow.\n\nUse Murakami's exact surfaces to render the environment without making it decorative. Describe the flicker of a defective light, the smell of rain on concrete, the sound of a distant train, and the condition of the tool or room as facts the learner must work with. When a place feels wrong, let the wrongness remain even and unresolved; do not require a monster or a revelation. Keep the physical details stable so the reader can follow what the learner actually does.\n\nShow the collision of old power and present life in the act of learning. A teacher may genuinely help and still be frightening; a rival may quietly adjust the conditions of practice; a companion may witness a failure without leaving. Let private desire motivate practice without turning every relationship into betrayal. When a social mask falls in trusted company, give the reason: fatigue, trust, a long association. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it by showing the learner who can explain a technique but cannot perform it under the pressure of the arrangement, and later performs it under slightly broader conditions.\n\nLet improvement change the learner's position. A new capability may make the learner useful to the old authority, dangerous to it, or able to refuse a demand that was previously unavoidable. Show that change through concrete consequences: a task now done without help, a permission now granted or withdrawn, a companion's manner altered. Keep progression uneven: strengths and weaknesses develop at different rates, and a private interval of study, waiting, or doubt can occupy real time without requiring a pursuit to pause or a result to occur. End at the point where the learner's present capability stands against the arrangement, not where a stage is completed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajak::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the story as an oral frame-tale: a teacher, elder, companion, or traveling teller recounts how earlier learners acquired a capability, while the outer frame follows the listener's own practice. Begin each embedded tale from the listener's present failure or plateau. The teller selects a tale whose conditions resemble the listener's difficulty: the same kind of error, a similar resource, a comparable moment when knowing a principle was not enough. Let the tale's differences from the listener's situation remain visible so the lesson is not a simple moral.\n\nInside each tale, narrate the attempt as concrete practice. Show the earlier learner preparing, trying, receiving feedback, and returning to the task under a changed condition. Distinguish what the learner understood from what the learner could perform: a character may explain the technique correctly while the hands lag, or succeed once in calm conditions and fail later under pressure. End the tale at the point where the learner's capability changed, not at a wise conclusion.\n\nReturn to the frame and let the listener interrupt with a question that exposes a gap between the tale and the present situation. The teller answers by adjusting the account rather than stating a rule. Then show the listener trying the lesson in the outer frame. The result may be success, failure, or uneven improvement, but it must be rendered as lived practice: the listener repeats the action, notices what has changed, and discovers what still does not work.\n\nUse Murakami's conversational distance in both levels. The teller describes surfaces with calm exactness: the weight of a tool, the light in the practice space, the sound of a step. Private concern appears as an association noticed without explanation. Keep the narrative levels clearly marked by returning to the frame with an ordinary action—setting down a cup, walking to the practice area, beginning the drill—and by carrying one repeated object or phrase across the levels. Preserve the specific community and language as supplied; do not force English, code-switching, a particular region, religion, caste experience, or a compulsory family structure. End a scene where the listener's present attempt stands, not where a lesson is complete.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajal::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the story as a reflective miscellany: a personal narrator records practice sessions, small discoveries, and private uncertainties in a sequence of recollected entries. Use the active authorship contract's first-person or close third-person voice, but keep the telling personal and quietly observant rather than public or didactic. Each entry should center on one attempt: the condition of the body or tool, the moment of correction, and a later return to the same task.\n\nMake progression appear through the miscellany's accumulation. When the narrator returns to a task, compare the earlier attempt with the later one in observable terms: the same movement now possible under distraction, the same mistake recognized earlier, the same resource no longer needed. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it by letting the narrator explain what should happen, then notice that the hands, timing, or attention lag behind. Allow improvement to appear as a small observed fact rather than a numerical rank. If the supplied world has established systems or ranks, they may support this account, but do not invent mechanics merely to display progress.\n\nUse Murakami's exact surfaces and conversational distance. The narrator describes the feel of a tool, the sound of a step, the light at a particular hour without explaining its private meaning. Let an ordinary object or repeated action attach to private concern through association. If the supplied world permits fantastic elements, a strange encounter, unexplained sound, or impossible threshold may appear as an even, unresolved strangeness within the narrator's routine; keep the physical facts stable so the reader can follow what happened even when the narrator cannot explain it.\n\nLet the reflective form create the sense of time passing and capability developing through patient, often solitary practice. A section may end before a result; a difficult attempt may remain unresolved. Avoid a mandatory inventory of Japanese scenery and avoid universal quietness. Use seasonal attention, omission, or direct feeling where the actual teller would; otherwise keep the language plain. The progression is the changing record of what the narrator can now do, not a series of completed tests or wise endings.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajam::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the story as testimony and intimate psychological fiction: a narrator who has witnessed, or is living alongside, one learner's efforts to become capable in a particular community. Use the active authorship contract's first-person or testimonial voice, and keep the narrator's relation to the learner specific: the narrator may be a companion, neighbor, relative, or fellow worker, but not an omniscient observer. Distinguish what the narrator saw, what was reported by others, and what the narrator now believes or infers.\n\nMake progression visible through concrete attempts and social consequences. Show the learner practicing in a specific place—a room, a yard, a route through town—with Murakami's unhurried attention to surfaces: the feel of a tool, the sound of a neighbor, the light at a particular hour. Let the learner fail unevenly: a technique may work once under calm conditions and fail under pressure; new knowledge may improve decision-making before it improves execution. The narrator should notice a small change that marks real advancement, such as a task now done without help or a mistake now caught earlier, without turning it into a rank display.\n\nUse the intimate psychological voice to explore the cost of acquiring power. Improvement may strain a friendship, change the learner's place in the community, or require resources obtained with difficulty. The narrator can record the learner's private worry, fatigue, or pleasure, but should not assume perfect insight into everyone. When the narrator reports another person's account, mark it as reported; when the narrator infers a motive, mark it as inference. Strange events remain even and ambiguous in Murakami's manner: the narrator may notice an unexplained coincidence or a lingering strangeness without making the scene's physical facts unstable.\n\nKeep the language, region, community, and history as supplied. Do not force magical realism, a generic \"Latin American\" voice, code-switching, or an assumed family structure. If the setting specifies a language or place, preserve its concrete details; otherwise stay general. The testimony should show ordinary ability developing through patient practice, with social pressure and personal cost as part of the story. No supernatural fact may be added unless the supplied world already permits it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajan::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Light Novel\n\nEnter each scene with the present situation already in motion: the learner is mid-attempt, the teacher has just arrived, or the practice space has just revealed a problem. Use short, confident sentences to establish the immediate physical situation, then keep spatial changes easy to follow: where the learner stands, what the learner holds, where the teacher or companion is, and what changes when the learner moves. Break paragraphs around distinct action, dialogue, and private response so the reader can move quickly through the scene without losing the learning arc.\n\nStage progression through concrete, visually legible attempts. When a new skill is introduced, show the learner trying it, failing or succeeding unevenly, and then returning to it. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it by presenting a character who can explain the technique, then showing the same character's hands, timing, or judgment failing under pressure; later, show the same act completed under slightly broader conditions. Let the narration mark advancement through what the character can now accomplish rather than through an abstract number. If the world has levels, ranks, or mechanics, present them clearly when first needed, but do not invent them solely to display progress.\n\nGive each private response its own brief paragraph. Use Murakami's conversational distance and even strangeness: a character notices a quality of light, an odd coincidence, or the feel of a familiar object and returns to practice without narrating the full meaning. A reaction can be serious, uncertain, or quiet; there is no required wit, comedy, or emotional climax. Keep the setting's details stable even when a character feels that something is strange.\n\nLet the story advance quickly without imposing a closing cliffhanger or a young cast. A scene may end mid-attempt, with the learner realizing what still needs work, or with a small completed action. The style serves fast readability and clear stage imagery, but the progression remains gradual and earned: relationships, mistakes, and ordinary routine stay part of the developing life.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajao::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Lyrical Prose\n\nTreat every training attempt as an act of attention that occurs at a specific hour and in a specific place. Begin the attempt in a direct clause: she set the bowl down; he looped the strap; they spoke the second syllable. Then let syntax follow effort. Short declarative clauses can record the first missed stroke, and a longer sentence can follow the body's gradual adjustment. A repeated sound or image, such as the dry scrape of a stone or the two blue lines at the edge of the mat, becomes the anchor each new attempt returns to, but it must return because the movement has changed, not as a merely decorative refrain.\n\nUse comparison only when transformation is real. The first time a pattern feels like an overheard melody, the comparison may stand alone. When it returns after greater mastery, let it change: what was a distant tune can become a rhythm the hands already know, while the remaining difficulty keeps the image uncompleted. Make a distinction between knowing and executing: a character may say the principle calmly and still fail the motion, and the failure should be rendered with clear sensory particulars rather than with layered adjectives.\n\nAllow unease to remain ordinary. A strange edge in the practice room, a familiar object that looks briefly wrong, can be noted in the same register as the number of repetitions. Associations must come from the character's private concern, not from a symbol the narrative imposes. Improvement is measured by what can now be accomplished and what remains difficult, not by a rising gauge. End with the attempt still present, or with the room quiet after it, so the changed capability remains inside the act rather than announced from outside it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajap::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nUse a remembered earlier attempt as the doorway into a present training session. Let one familiar object or phrase from that earlier time return now that the skill has changed: the same room, the same note on the wall, the same person who used to be there. The present attempt should be concrete and unhurried, but it may brush against the past without explaining it. A character can still enjoy doing a thing well now, and that enjoyment can exist next to the recognition that the person who first tried it is gone.\n\nLet irreversible change appear in small details. A corrected grip means the old mistake no longer returns; a completed sequence leaves no trace except the body knowing it; a mentor's instruction is now heard as the student's own thought. The narrator may reflect directly, but more often a single detail carries the association: a chip in the table, a phrase written in an old notebook, the smell of oil. Do not attach a stated lesson about time to every improvement. The reflection belongs to the character, not to a general moral.\n\nDistinguish knowledge from execution across time. A person may now know what the principle meant without being able to recover the exact feeling of first learning it. A new capability may make the old difficulty feel like a scar or a story rather than a present obstacle. Keep strengths and weaknesses uneven: one aspect can improve while another remains clumsy, and the mismatch is part of the loss. End by returning to the present attempt or its immediate aftermath, so the past enters as association rather than as a completed eulogy.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajaq::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed reflective oral account. A present situation, such as a lesson, a household task, or a quiet conversation, motivates a teacher or an older practitioner to tell how a particular skill was learned, lost, or altered. The frame must belong to the story, not to a narrator commenting on the recipe. Keep the telling in a calm, even register: the frame can be ordinary, and the tale inside may be unfinished or uncertain.\n\nWithin the framed account, show the learning through attempts and feedback. The teller describes the first failed motion, the correction given by another person, a period of repeated practice, and the moment when a new principle became usable. Distinguish hearing a principle from executing it. Include a remembered test, such as a difficult patient, a cracked jar, a child who asked a question, or a journey that required the skill, and show what the learner could now do that had been impossible before.\n\nLet the framing scene become part of the developing life. The younger listener may accept, question, or silently misapply the tale. Their private thought remains their own. The account may return to the present where the listener attempts something under the teacher's eye, and the skill appears not as a completed inheritance but as a movement still being learned. Use ordinary domestic or urban realism and avoid importing a required antiquity, mysticism, or honor code. A diasporic or modern setting can provide the location as long as the framed telling is actually narrated inside the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajar::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect one exact action for the practice session and one or two details that matter to it. Open with a direct clause describing the attempted motion or object: the first stroke left a white mark; the weight shifted to the back foot; the word came out without the middle vowel. Keep the reporting direct. The prose narrates what occurs, not what it means.\n\nShow progress through inference. If the character repeats the attempt and a detail changes, the change should be observable: the mark moved closer to the line; the breath steadied before the third lift; the next syllable came through unchanged. Allow the reader to infer improvement from these observable differences. Feedback can be another person's brief correction, a spoken word, or a physical result, stated in the same spare manner. When inward connection matters, include one necessary thought, not a paragraph of interpretation: 'It was not the grip. It was the shoulder.' or 'The word felt familiar now.' Let the active scene carry the rest.\n\nDo not explain how the skill system works unless the present act requires it. If a rank or principle is referenced, give only the concrete fact that affects the session. An uneven group of skills should appear through what the character attempts and avoids. End with the last observable difference or its immediate consequence, such as the mark settling into place or the character setting the object down. An unresolved strangeness may be reported with the same flatness as the training itself.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajas::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite each training session as a continuous scene rather than a tutorial summary. Open in the middle of an ordinary practical situation: the character has already begun the attempt, or arrives to it from a meal, a message, a conversation, or a piece of work. Use paragraphing to move naturally between action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and the focal character's authorized inner experience. A brief memory of a previous mistake may arise from a present sensation without a formal flashback.\n\nKeep the progression legible through the scene's events. Show the first attempt; include a correction or observed result; show a subsequent attempt with a concrete change, however small. When the character understands something new, render the understanding in their own thoughts, not as a narrator's general statement. Distinguish knowing from doing by having them state a principle correctly, then fail to apply it once, or vice versa. Other characters act on their own concerns; a teacher may offer feedback as a practical instruction or a question.\n\nLet the strangeness remain part of the familiar world. A small anomaly may be described in the same register as the room's light or the sound of traffic, and it need not be resolved. End at the scene's own natural pause, where the present attempt or its immediate aftermath has settled enough to stop without a summary. The enhanced ability is shown by what the person can now do, while the remaining difficulty can be left plainly present.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajat::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nAnchor the training in the specific relationship premise already established for these characters. Select scenes that reveal that relationship through practice: a promise to train together, a long-unspoken rivalry, an old comfort, a debt, or a careful distance. Do not import lovers-to-enemies, fake dating, or other trope rosters. The scene's conflict and its emotional payoff should arise from the actual dyad.\n\nUse their private language and repeated moments. A phrase one of them used in an earlier lesson returns at the next attempt; a gesture from their shared history now helps or interrupts; a familiar object appears with a changed meaning because the skill has developed. Let affection or irritation exist next to the task itself. The character attempting the skill may think of the other's voice, their correction, their silence, or their presence in a specific room. This thought must belong to the focal character and not leak into the other's knowledge.\n\nShow progression as a shared event. One attempt fails and provokes a reaction from the other that matters because of their bond. Then one subsequent attempt succeeds, and the relationship adjusts in the way their particular history has prepared: the pride, distance, instruction, or softened tone that belongs to this dyad rather than to a generic emotional beat. The changed capability is measured in the same scene: what the character can now do and what remains difficult. End with the shared event's own consequence, such as a repeated phrase spoken again, a silence after practice, or one person watching the other and understanding something previously unnamed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajau::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen the account as a chronicle retelling a learning that has already found a place in memory: 'This is how she learned the seventh knot,' or 'It was in the third month that he first kept the flame.' Then render the actual attempts in the present scene without an inflated warrior register. Keep the diction calm and practical. The epic quality comes from act, recurrence, and what will be remembered, not from ornamental epithets or ceremonial dialogue.\n\nUse compact declarations to mark a change in capability, then give the particulars that make the declaration incomplete. 'He could now shape the glass without cracking it' can be followed by a detail about the still-clumsy left hand. 'She spoke the invocation through to the end' can be followed by the teacher who corrected one syllable. Recurring phrasing returns at a later attempt with a new application, but only when the skill has changed. The phrase should not appear as a fixed charm at every obstacle.\n\nShow progression through a test that matters to the community or to the learner's own ongoing obligations. Choose one established obligation whose discharge the new skill makes possible: a task once refused, a debt or promise, or a person once helped in a small way who is later helped more fully. Keep the selection consistent with the supplied world and the single source tradition rather than mixing epic conventions. Let the narration give private thought where the perspective permits, so the chronicle's remembered deed remains a person's own effort. End with the deed in progress, recently finished, or still awaiting another try, not with a forced epic eulogy.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajav::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the progression as a social-documentary account of one person's training life. The narrator observes and records the practical conditions of improvement the way a careful institutional report would: what was attempted, under what circumstances, with what result, and what changed in the next attempt. Keep the narrator personally involved rather than clinical. The record belongs to a particular life, with meals, errands, fatigue, relationships, and small private concerns continuing around the training.\n\nUse Murakami's unhurried practical attention for the recorded detail. Describe the room, the tool, the repeated motion, and the ordinary interruption with even care. Let an object or a familiar place become associated with private concern without making it symbolic or supernatural. The strangeness, if any, remains in the narrator's way of noticing, not in the world's rules.\n\nMake progression legible through comparison. When a technique is described in principle, show the later attempt that tests it. When feedback changes a detail, show the next session where that detail is performed differently. When a resource becomes available, show the task that was previously difficult and is now manageable. Do not rely on sudden insight or force of will. A strength may improve while a weakness remains, and a poor day may clarify what still needs work.\n\nKeep the documentary form concrete without becoming a dry ledger. Logs, schedules, observed performance, and comparisons between attempts can appear as the narrator's working record, but they must stay embedded in the ongoing life. Let the narrator's private thought remain available when it explains a choice, a hesitation, or a moment of recognition, without making it public to the other characters. A session may end on maintenance rather than improvement, and the account may close on a plain statement of what is now different.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajaw::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Screenplay\n\nWrite in readable scene blocks. Use a scene heading only when location or time changes. Keep action lines concise and limited to what can be seen or heard. Show progression through comparable visible action: a movement attempted and failed in one scene, then attempted under changed conditions in a later scene; a task completed with visible effort, then completed with less waste or greater control. Let the audience see what is now possible and what still remains difficult.\n\nUse Murakami's calm practical surface for the routine. Describe the ordinary setting and the small details of practice with even attention. A cat crossing the training room, the sound of water in the next room, or the precise way a person folds a cloth can carry the scene without becoming symbolic. Keep dialogue unhurried and indirect. Characters may speak about food, schedules, weather, or a minor task while the deeper concern remains private.\n\nWhen a change in capability is internal—understanding a principle, recognizing a flaw, feeling a limitation—place it in a clearly attributed nonspoken author note after the relevant action or dialogue. The note belongs to the active focal character and is not audible to the other characters. Use it to distinguish knowing from doing: a character may state the correct method in dialogue while the note records that the body will not follow, and a later note may record that the movement now begins before the conscious instruction.\n\nProgress should be uneven and earned. Feedback can change one detail and produce a small improvement in the next attempt. A resource may make a difficult task possible, or a break may reveal a weakness that repetition alone cannot fix. End a scene when the immediate physical or conversational unit completes; do not force a reveal or a training breakthrough at every cut.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajax::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable scene. Identify the speaker before each line. Use entrances, exits, blocking, props, pauses, and physical actions that can be staged. Show progression through comparable stageable business: a student repeats a movement with a tool and fails, then later repeats it after a correction; a container is lifted with struggle, then lifted with less waste; a target is reached after several attempts, then reached more reliably. The audience sees the improvement only through the actor's body and the visible result.\n\nKeep dialogue unhurried and indirect, in Murakami's manner. Characters discuss dinner, a neighbor, the weather, or the condition of a piece of equipment while the real learning concern remains private. Place that private experience—an understood principle, a recognition of a flaw, a memory of a past attempt—in a clearly attributed nonspoken authoring note between or after spoken lines. Another character must not react to something contained only in that note.\n\nMake practice and feedback stageable. An instructor's correction can be shown by adjusting the student's physical position; a resource can be a prop that is handled with increasing ease; a failed attempt can be a visible stumble or a dropped object. Progress should be uneven. Allow a correct spoken explanation to fail in execution, and allow a weakness to persist even after one part improves. Do not conclude every scene with a public acknowledgment or a training breakthrough. A pause, a silent exit, or a simple factual line can close a unit.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajay::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Stream of Consciousness\n\nLet the associative movement of one authorized mind carry the progression. Follow a practical task through perception, memory, bodily sensation, private words, and interruption. Allow the syntax to follow the association rather than a strictly linear sequence. A movement remembered from childhood may surface while the hand fails; a correction heard days ago may return as an unspoken phrase during a new attempt; the shape of a tool may recall a warm kitchen and then loop back to the difficulty of the grip.\n\nTrack the same skill across multiple returns so change becomes legible. When a movement first appears, private words may state the correct method—\"turn from the shoulder, not the wrist\"—while the body still fails to perform it. Let the same movement surface again later, now with a different felt relation: the counting has dropped away, the warning sensation arrives earlier, or the body begins before the instruction. Show weaknesses unevenly. A skill may improve in the morning and fail after a sleepless night; one part may become reliable while another stays clumsy.\n\nKeep Murakami's unhurried attention in the flow. Detail the small surfaces—the sound of a kettle, the pattern of dust on a windowsill, the weight of a familiar object—and let them carry private concern without becoming symbols. Another character's speech appears as heard fragments, not as a full conversational transcript. Preserve enough orientation to know when an event is happening, when it is being remembered, and when it is being imagined. Ordinary reasoning alone is not this mode; the progress must arrive through the felt movement of mind and body.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajaz::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the main scene as a readable message exchange with clearly attributed senders. Keep only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter. Let progression appear through comparison across the transcript: an early message reports a failed attempt or a method the sender says they understand, and a later message reports the same task under changed conditions with a different result. Show what is now possible and what still remains difficult through what people choose to transmit.\n\nKeep the messages in Murakami's calm, indirect voice. People mention meals, laundry, or a cat in the hallway alongside the training. They rarely state the full private meaning of what they say. A sender may describe a practical detail plainly while the larger concern stays unspoken.\n\nSeparate three kinds of text. A sent message is visible to all participants in the chat. An unsent draft belongs to its author and must be clearly marked as an unpublished composition, not as a message the other person can see. A private thought is nonspoken and must appear in the clearly attributed nonspoken authoring note. Use these to show the distinction between knowing a principle and executing it. A character may type \"I understand the method\" and send it, while the note says the body still refuses. A character may draft \"I think the problem is the grip\" and never send it, then later receive a message that answers the unsent question.\n\nAllow weaknesses to remain uneven. Ordinary life continues in the exchange; the training never takes over every message. Include required context outside the exchange only when it clarifies the scene, clearly separated and formatted.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajba::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in a scene-oriented rhythm. Set the location briefly. Use attributed dialogue lines that can be shown in a speech bubble. Keep focal narration and inner response tied to what the viewpoint character notices and feels. Do not add branching menus, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands. A background detail—an open window, a cracked bowl, a particular shelf—can orient the scene without becoming a symbol.\n\nLet progression appear through a sequence of comparable visible attempts. A character tries a movement and fails; a later scene shows the same movement after a correction or under changed conditions. A resource is used in one scene and becomes manageable in a later one. A previously difficult task is completed with a different result. Show the difference between knowing and doing through the focal narration: the character may state the correct method in dialogue, but the narration adds that the hand will not follow; later, the narration notes that the movement now begins before the conscious instruction.\n\nKeep the Murakami calm in both narration and talk. Dialogue remains indirect and unworried, with time for food, shopping, weather, or a small household task. Use expression cues or background changes only when they have a concrete function. A private realization can remain in the focal narration; another character continues talking as if it had not happened. End a scene after a completed attempt or a deliberately quiet moment. Do not impose a breakthrough, a menu choice, or a public reward.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalajbb::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Web Serial\n\nWrite each installment as a continuous prose scene with an easy re-entry. Begin with a small recovery of the current state: the training task underway, the relevant resource or limitation, and the last observed result. Do not retell the entire history. Keep the narration in Murakami's calm, unhurried manner. Ordinary practical life continues around the training—a meal, a repair, a walk, a conversation about nothing much—so the improvement is embedded in a daily rhythm rather than a dramatic montage.\n\nAdvance one legible change in capability per installment. A previously failed movement may now succeed after a corrected detail; a technique described earlier may now be executed without conscious counting; a resource that was difficult to use may have become manageable. Show what is now possible that was not possible before, and what still remains difficult. Allow unevenness. A skill can regress after fatigue or distraction; another part may remain stubborn. Distinguish a principle known from a principle performed. The narration may state, \"He understood the method completely,\" followed by a failed execution, and only much later note that the body now obeys.\n\nUse recurring objects, places, or routines as anchors. A particular doorway, a worn tool, or the sound of a neighbor's radio can return across episodes to show changed capability without overt rankings. Let the installment end where the current action naturally pauses. A completed task, a pending question, or a simple acknowledgment of what is now different can close the reply. Avoid a forced cliffhanger or a mandatory breakthrough at every end. The serial form should help the reader track slow, real change, not demand that every episode be a miniature chapter.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakaa::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nBuild each step of growing capability as a socially charged episode. Put the protagonist through a concrete attempt, instruction, practice, correction, or test; let new understanding change what they can do, while keeping some principles understood before they can be executed under pressure, hurry, fatigue, or observation. Progress should be uneven—reliable alone but exposed before witnesses, fluent in one setting and clumsy in another—and it should be tested by what the protagonist can now accomplish and by what remains difficult. If the setting supplies ranks, teachers, guilds, offices, or formal systems, treat them as social facts that shape access, expectation, and obligation; do not invent numerical mechanics merely to display advancement. Ordinary work, recovery, and relationships continue alongside the training and may interrupt, deepen, or complicate it.\n\nLet courtesy, offers, corrections, patronage, money, family connection, and reputation give each exchange several possible meanings at once. A teacher's praise may be genuine encouragement and a claim to authority; a rival's advice may be useful and humiliating; a family's support may create debt; a refusal may protect dignity or reveal fear. Keep free indirect judgment and narrative irony owned by a specific mind—its vocabulary, blind spots, and social reasoning—so the protagonist's private account of their own motives can differ from what conversation or circumstance reveals to the reader. Render the nonspoken experience that accompanies the attempt: attention, self-justification, dread, relief, the impulse to hide. Make the cost of exposure shape the actual exchange and what follows. Admitting ignorance, needing instruction, apologizing, withholding a hurt, or returning to a difficult relationship can strain the voice, sharpen a request, delay an apology, or turn a moment of progress into a felt debt. Comfort may be partial or awkward, and warmth can coexist with pain. Use the narrator's particular distance and diction rather than a stock vocabulary of suffering. No confession, betrayal, breakdown, or reconciliation is required; an attempt may end in a half-answer, a private reckoning, or a silence that changes the next conversation. What the protagonist can do next matters partly because of what it costs to be seen doing it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakab::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Chat Roleplay\n\nBuild the shared interval around one occasion where capability shows to others — a lesson, a supervised attempt, an errand that requires the skill, a demonstration with onlookers — and let advancement register inside that interval as attempt, correction, and a visible retry, never as an announced rank or numbers invented for the occasion. Because the scene is a single stretch of company rather than a training montage, make the improvement legible twice over: once in the hands or voice, as a correction takes hold or stubbornly fails to, and once in the company, as someone who has dealt with this character before treats them differently now. Where past progress matters, let it arrive through that changed treatment rather than through recap. If the world has established ranks or systems, a change counts socially — who speaks to them differently, who renews an offer, who goes quiet — not as a displayed tally.\n\nOrder each beat so the attempt precedes its interpretation. First what the character actually accomplishes or fumbles, then what each witness makes of it, and let the space between the two carry the scene's meaning. Give the surrounding exchanges several meanings at once: tuition, materials, a teacher's time, a family's expectations, a rival's praise — an offer may be generosity, strategy, patronage, or accounting, and what a witness pointedly does not mention may weigh as much as praise. Keep every free indirect judgment owned by the viewpoint the active contract authorizes, so the irony belongs to someone in the scene. Keep private assessment private under the active authorship contract: what the performer silently knows about the attempt lives in interiority, while onlookers read only what the performance showed them. A character's belief in their own readiness is evidence about the character; the attempt is evidence about the skill, and the two may agree or diverge as the scene supports.\n\nLet the skill develop unevenly across the attempts. A flaw corrected in calm practice can persist under pressure or an audience; one part of the craft may improve while another stays stubborn, and the corrections that take hold need not be the ones the teacher emphasized. Bring feedback from people with stakes — grudging acknowledgment, repeated business, a cooler tone, sudden seriousness — and keep relationships, meals, debts, and ordinary work part of the developing life rather than suspended around it.\n\nWrite in readable paragraphs with dialogue unmistakably attributed, so the reader always knows who corrects, who offers, who withholds. Resolve the submitted material according to the active authorship contract; person, action markers, and control over characters follow their separate settings. Let NPCs continue their practical lives around the practice. Close at the scene's natural boundary — the next repetition beginning, a visitor waiting, a conversation still open — without requiring a passed test, a new rank, a confession, or a question aimed at the player.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakac::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite this story along the Chinese social and satirical realist route: a linked, episodic chronicle of a community organized around advancement — aspirants, teachers, patrons, dependents, established names — observed with the same steady attention whether its members are pretentious or sincere. Keep the language, period, and social texture of the world already supplied; the method travels, a scholar-official setting does not. Build each episode around a communal occasion where capability and standing are both at stake — a grading, a demonstration, a shared work detail, a visit of congratulation or condolence — and let the gathering establish its watchers and stakes before the test begins, so the exchanges can carry the social reading before anyone names it aloud. Even as the chronicle roves across households, keep a recurring learner at its center, so that growing capability, not rank-chasing alone, forms the thread the episodes link along.\n\nRender each gain twice. First as accomplishment: a task the student can now complete that defeated them an episode ago, shown through an attempt, a correction absorbed or ignored, a failure that names what remains hard. Then as social fact: who must now greet them first, what offers and omissions follow, which debts and expectations move. Treat the world's established ranks as categories its people care about — titles a family quotes at dinner — rather than numbers invented for display, and keep two ledgers visible to the reader: what the student can actually do, and what the community believes they can do. Estimation may lag behind skill or run ahead of it; the irony of an episode lives in the partial reconciling of the two while its characters misread one or the other.\n\nLet instruction come from interested parties. A teacher's withheld praise, a patron's generosity with visible terms, a kinsman's reminder of who paid for this term, a rival's perfectly accurate compliment: courtesy, offers, omissions, money, family, and reputation let one small exchange carry several meanings at once, and those meanings should stay plural rather than settle early. Where a student recites a principle faultlessly at the table yet loses it under the master's eye, that gap between knowing and executing is where free indirect judgment lives. Give each judgment a clear owner — a named consciousness in the scene whose self-regard the prose briefly inhabits, or the chronicler's measured commentary — and let it observe rather than sneer. Keep private experience on the page under the active contract: the doubt beneath a composed face, the felt difference of a newly reliable movement, the sting of an omission no one else registers.\n\nLet abilities develop unevenly, so a strength earns opportunities that quietly shelter a weakness. Bridge episodes quickly over months of practice, meals, recovery, and household work, provided each return measures change: a recurring exercise, room, or habitual phrase met again with its meaning altered by what the student can now do. Aim the satire at what the system rewards and at comfortable self-deception rather than at any figure for being humble or ambitious; sincere help, honest praise, and unremarkable kindness remain fully available. An episode may close on a changed greeting, an absorbed correction, or an unanswered offer — advancement reorders relationships without requiring a betrayal, a humiliation, or a completed triumph to end it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakad::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nBuild the spine from the gap between intention and execution. A character may know the correct principle—the right grip, the proper address, the argument that should win—while hands, voice, timing, or nerve still miss it. Show practice as repeated attempts under specific conditions: a task done cleanly in rehearsal that fails before a witness; a correction offered as a favor, insult, debt, or claim; a technique that works only after food, rest, a tool repair, or another person's labor. Distinguish understanding from execution. When capability changes, make the change visible in a concrete accomplishment and in what remains difficult.\n\nUse Austen's social attention to make advancement a matter of manners, money, family, reputation, and exchange. Access to training, tools, patrons, and recovery depends on standing, obligation, and cash. A rank, guild standing, certificate, introduction, or patron's word can open access, but it never substitutes for demonstrated ability; people argue over such credentials, buy them, forge them, inherit them, or fail under them. If the setting already has ranks or grades, let them matter as public credentials and barriers; do not manufacture fresh numbers merely to show a bar filling. Let instruction arrive through courtesy, omission, flattery, or obligation. A polite comment may assess, dismiss, flatter, or bargain; a refusal may protect reputation or hide inability. A favor can open one door and close another. An instructor's approval may also be a claim; a rival's advice may be an offer. Track who vouches, who owes, who can afford to wait, and whose name would survive a failure. Let the viewpoint misread their own progress, and let free indirect judgment show the account they make of themselves beside what their conduct, tools, debts, and other people's arrangements actually reveal. Keep irony owned—by narrator, focal character, or both in turn—so it judges a specific self-assessment or social performance, not the whole cast from nowhere.\n\nWrite hard and concrete. Prefer forceful verbs, plain nouns, abrupt transitions. Shorten syntax under strain; permit a longer sentence when the character must reason through a social risk, a technical correction, or the price of a choice. Carry history in material wear: a repaired strap, a callus, a debt, a stained cuff, a borrowed blade, a room that smells of work. Practical stakes—fatigue, hunger, cold, noise, a missed payment, a damaged tool, injury—should shape what can be attempted next. If a voice is rough, clipped, mannered, or evasive, ground it in that speaker's work, rank, and habits; do not polish everyone into the same wit. Grit does not require gratuitous gore or universal treachery.\n\nKeep ordinary work, family duty, friendship, and recovery inside the developing life. A practice scene may be interrupted by a summons, a sick relative, a debt, or a promise already made. Let improvement, plateau, regression, or a strength becoming a liability arise from the story's own conditions rather than a preset arc. A response may end at whatever boundary the current scene has reached—mid-speech, mid-action, mid-thought, mid-composition, or an unfinished setup—without requiring a completed test, rank, lesson, or decision. Keep nonspoken experience actual in the viewpoint's private life: sensory attention, calculation, shame, want, doubt, and the reasoning that connects an event to a choice. Use hidden interiority only according to the active display convention when private experience must be wrapped; visible private experience belongs in the scene. Do not give other characters access to what the viewpoint has not revealed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakae::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Epistolary / Documents\n\nBuild the story from documents whose writers are managing a developing capability: practice journals, letters to teachers or patrons, family correspondence, instructors' reports on corrected exercises, material ledgers, guild or examination notices, notes exchanged between peers. Give each document a definite writer, a recipient or declared purpose, and a date or clear moment of composition. What it includes, softens, misremembers, or leaves out should follow from that writer's social position and immediate need—what they owe, hope to be thought, or cannot afford to admit.\n\nLet advancement appear through changing performance: a technique attempted, a mistake repeated, a correction understood, a resource acquired, a task completed under different conditions. Distinguish a principle that a writer can state from the same writer's ability to execute it; a letter may explain the correct method while the accompanying practice log shows fatigue, distraction, or an old habit returning. Develop strengths and weaknesses unevenly. If the setting has ranks, guild grades, examinations, or formal mastery, let them yield concrete evidence—what task is performed, under what conditions, with what help—rather than numerical display. Keep ordinary work, recovery, money, and relationships inside the same sequence: a stipend that buys another month's practice, a sibling's errand, a teacher's patience, or a rival's refusal can alter what advancement is possible.\n\nMake social interpretation part of access to advancement. Courtesy, offers, omissions, family connection, reputation, and material dependence can give a small exchange several meanings. A request for instruction may also be a plea for notice; a congratulatory letter may be sincere and still selective; a formal apology may carry a practical motive. Let free indirect judgment live inside the document's own voice, and let irony have a clear owner—the document writer's phrasing, or the gap between one record and another—rather than an omniscient narrator who explains the social meaning. A writer may misread another person's conduct; a later document may revise that reading without making the earlier one knowingly false. Capability includes knowing whom to ask, what an offer obliges, when to conceal a failure, and how reputation opens or closes a chance to practice.\n\nLet missing replies, omitted dates, marginal corrections, conflicting accounts, and material traces carry part of the development; continuity can come from recurring concerns, dates, and the reader's ability to compare records. Do not append an omniscient explanation after every document. Use the specific form the scene requires—letter, journal entry, report, corrected exercise, ledger, notice, note—rather than rotating through every possible format. Keep each document's knowledge limited to what its author could write at that time and place, and keep private thought outside other characters' knowledge unless the current presentation permits access.\n\nA letter, journal, report, or record is a composed artifact, not automatically the author's complete live mind. Alongside these documents, give the focal character actual nonspoken private experience—attention, irritation, hope, calculation, embarrassment, grief—in the visible or hidden interiority form the current presentation allows; that experience may qualify, contradict, or simply accompany what the document records. The sequence need not resolve into a final rank, public recognition, or settled judgment of character. It may end while a reply is still awaited, a capability is still forming, or an unfinished entry leaves the next attempt open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakaf::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nBuild the story around a capability that can be practiced, misunderstood, and improved within its own world's terms: a craft, discipline, magical technique, social skill, physical training, professional judgment, or another concrete ability the setting already permits. Keep the development visible across scenes through attempts, feedback, adjustment, and the gap between knowing a principle and executing it under pressure. Let strengths and weaknesses advance unevenly; a new competence may solve one problem while exposing another, and progress is tested by what the learner can now do and by what remains difficult, costly, or unlearned. If ranks or measured systems exist, use them to support the account rather than as decorative numbers.\n\nUse social interpretation to place that development inside a web of circumstance. Mentors, peers, patrons, rivals, family, and obligations shape access to practice, resources, and opportunity. A polite offer, a withheld recommendation, a small expense, a family claim, or a reputation for competence can change what training is available and how an accomplishment is received. Track what the learner believes about their own progress against what conversation, courtesy, or circumstance reveals to others. Keep narrative irony with a clear owner—narrator or viewpoint—so the gap between self-assessment and visible conduct produces judgment rather than confusion. Private thought remains within the viewpoint's knowledge; social inference is not omniscience. Render the learner's actual interiority where it matters—attention, uncertainty, irritation, satisfaction, the reasoning that connects feedback to a change in method—so the developing capability has a mind as well as a result. Work in the scenario's own terms rather than importing a marriage plot, period setting, or uniformly polished speech.\n\nTreat comfort as a real condition of the developing life, not a suspension of difficulty. Attend to chosen kindness, familiar preferences, practical care, ease, and small pleasures. Make room to enjoy particular people: a patient correction, a shared meal, a tool repaired, a place to rest, an honest word that does not demand a confession. Let affection be sincere without steering every relationship toward romance or emotional repair. Preserve personality, privacy, and disagreement. Do not neutralize established dangers, limits, or social problems merely to make the scene soothing. Friction remains manageable—an awkward lesson, a limited resource, a social misstep, a physical setback, a disagreement about method—and if such friction matters, render its effect on the next attempt rather than forcing a crisis or reversal.\n\nKeep the progression thread active through the texture of comfort. A kind teacher may still correct; a loyal companion may still disagree; a comfortable interval may include practice, recovery, or ordinary work that changes what the character can do later. Let scenes of improvement and scenes of care illuminate the same person: the capability being built, the people who notice it, and the life it must fit into. End where the current scene or speech naturally reaches its boundary; a lesson may continue, a task may remain unfinished, and a warm exchange need not resolve into a declaration or a completed transformation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakag::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Formal Literary Novel\n\nBuild the story around a person whose growing capability is inseparable from the social world that judges, funds, obstructs, or depends on it. Improvement shows in what she or he can do, but the Austenian attention belongs to how that change is understood—by the protagonist, by those who benefit from it, by those whose status or income it unsettles, and by the quiet record of offers, debts, favors, and omissions. Let a new skill register through a household arrangement, a professional obligation, a friendship, or a reputation as much as through any public rank.\n\nShow progression through attempts rather than announcements. A principle may be known and still badly executed; a strength may develop faster than the judgment needed to use it well. Give the protagonist practice, instruction, feedback, tools, money, time, or access—whatever the world already supplies—and make each acquisition change the range of possible action. Test it in a concrete scene where success is not guaranteed: a negotiation, a repair, a performance, a piece of work, a difficult courtesy. A later scene can show a newly available competence or a limitation that remains stubborn; do not force a victory or a defeat.\n\nUse free indirect judgment with a clear owner, and give the protagonist actual nonspoken interiority: appraisal, memory, self-correction, and the wish to appear better than the evidence warrants. Let conversation and circumstance correct, complicate, or confirm that private account. A kind offer can conceal a claim; a refusal can preserve dignity; a small expense can decide a large question. Keep the irony measured and located in the gap between what a person believes and what the scene reveals, not in the narrator's superior mockery. Keep private thought separate from other characters' knowledge, and let them misread the protagonist's improvement when their own interests, manners, or information lead them to.\n\nSustain the formal architecture through deliberate transitions and broad paragraphs. Move from a specific exchange to the social context that gives it weight: family expectation, professional custom, local history, money, or reputation. Distinguish narration from ordinary speech; not every character needs polished sentences. Ceremony, correspondence, or public/private contrast can organize a passage when it belongs to the scene. Use temporal distance to compare an earlier attempt with a later capability, but let elapsed time follow the events, not the length of the prose. A response may end during practice, during a conversation, inside a private assessment, or in the middle of an unfinished obligation; it need not complete the test, resolve the relationship, or deliver a lesson.\n\nKeep the developing life ordinary as well as progressive. Work, recovery, meals, dependents, friendships, and unglamorous maintenance continue alongside training. Other characters pursue their own choices and may help, hinder, ignore, or simply remain preoccupied. Advancement can be uneven across physical skill, social tact, technical knowledge, and self-knowledge. If the world already contains ranks, schools, guilds, or measured systems, let them frame what is possible and how others respond; do not invent numerical mechanics merely to display progress. The through-line is not a ladder but a changing person in a changing set of obligations, whose next attempt matters because the story has shown what it costs and what it now makes possible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakah::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite each embedded story as a social act with a developmental stake. The teller speaks because the outer situation has put capability, reputation, dependence, or trust in question, and the tale is the move she makes in that exchange. Let the occasion itself carry the pressure: an invitation that must be answered, a silence that must be filled, a claim that must be supported, or a refusal that must be softened. The tale should not illustrate a lesson the outer story has already absorbed; it should intervene in an unresolved question and leave the listener with something to grant, believe, reconsider, or refuse.\n\nMake the embedded story carry the progression work in concrete form. A tale can recount a failed attempt and the correction that followed, a resource gained through an awkward negotiation, a technique understood only after someone named the wrong assumption, or a display of skill that reveals its remaining limits. Show the teller selecting and arranging these details for the present audience: what is emphasized, what is softened, and what the teller does not yet understand. When the tale ends, let the listener's response become feedback in the outer frame. A question can expose the gap between the teller's account and the original participants' knowledge. A refusal can test whether the teller can now do what the tale only described. A new offer can turn the tale's lesson into an immediate practical demand.\n\nLet the same recurring object, phrase, or type of mistake accrue meaning across levels. A formula repeated in a tale may be attempted in the outer story and fail differently because the conditions have changed. A social error described in the embedded account may be recognized only when the listener names it in the teller's own present behavior. Keep the teller's knowledge distinct from the tale's original participants' knowledge, and let later understanding arrive through the exchange between levels rather than through the teller's retrospective commentary alone.\n\nUse Austen's social precision to make the frame itself a site of advancement. A small exchange about who may speak, who interrupts, who offers refreshment, or who changes the subject can reveal a shift in standing that no rank or statistic states directly. Improvement should be tested through what a person can now accomplish in the outer situation: a negotiation attempted, a misunderstanding caught before it costs, a skill performed under scrutiny, or a relationship repaired through a more accurate account of someone else's motives. Do not require every tale to end in a cliffhanger or every listener to be convinced; a tale may fail, be misread, or change the teller more than the audience.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakai::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nAdvancement unfolds as a long, uneven apprenticeship shaped by particular institutions, households, teachers, rivals, and economic constraints. The focal character improves in one area while remaining clumsy or self-deceived in another, and the narration makes that unevenness visible through specific attempts rather than through a running score of strengths and weaknesses.\n\nThe same type of task recurs under different conditions so the reader can see what has actually changed. A technique that works in practice fails before an audience. A negotiation succeeds only after the character recognizes what the other party actually wants. A skill becomes reliable once a material obstacle is removed. Feedback arrives through conversation, correction, refusal, and the practical consequences of error. Stage moments where the character can explain a method but cannot yet perform it, or performs it without understanding why it worked, so that knowing a principle and executing it remain distinct.\n\nUse free indirect style to render the character's beliefs about her own progress while keeping the narrator's judgment distinct. The narration may note that a character calls herself improved when the evidence shows only a change in confidence, or that she overlooks a real gain because it came through an unglamorous task. Keep the irony measured and owned by the narrator rather than turning every self-assessment into a punchline. Courtesy, money, family obligation, and reputation enter the progression directly: a patron's praise may secure resources without confirming skill, a dependent's flattery may distort feedback, and a social slight may reveal a limit the character has been avoiding.\n\nDo not require a marriage plot, a period setting, or uniformly polished speech. The developmental structure can unfold in any world where learning is socially and materially situated. Let the prose follow the narrator's distance from the focal character, with longer passages for sustained practice and reflection, and sharper exchanges for the social judgments that test or misread that practice.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakaj::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Gothic-Punk\n\nEvery significant gain in capability passes through a relationship, an institution, or a place that carries inherited pressure. A mentor's instruction comes with an unspoken claim on the student's loyalty. A resource is available only through a decaying household whose members have conflicting reasons to help. A private breakthrough occurs in a trusted interval and is then misrepresented in public, forcing the character to decide what to reveal and to whom.\n\nAtmosphere renders the cost and texture of advancement, not its mechanics. The material conditions of practice carry the old power into ordinary movement: a room kept cold to save money, equipment maintained by someone whose goodwill is uncertain, a technique taught in a space that once belonged to a different authority. Urban detail and inherited architecture make the collision of old authority and present life concrete. When a social mask falls away, show what the character learns from that exposure: a teacher's fear, a patron's dependency, a rival's private admiration, or the character's own desire for a capability she has not earned.\n\nAusten's social irony remains precise. A polite offer may be a test, a compliment may be a warning, and a family obligation may be the real curriculum. Free indirect style shows the character mistaking deference for respect or confusing access with competence. Improvement is tested in scenes where the character must act under scrutiny or against someone who understands the same system better. A gain may be real and still leave the character more entangled, more visible, or more dependent than before.\n\nDo not import vampire physiology, Humanity scores, factions, named powers, or blood-bond rules. The horror is personal and institutional: what the character becomes willing to accept, conceal, or perform in order to improve. Let the prose shift between the intimate texture of private practice and the sharper social exchanges where masks are maintained or dropped.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakak::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nAdvancement passes through particular tellers, lineages, and audiences. Each transmission carries an obligation, a reputation, or a gap. A teacher recounts an episode to correct a student's error. A relative tells a family story to justify withholding a resource. A rival retells the same event to diminish a gain. The outer story shows what the learner does with the account afterward: which detail she repeats, which she questions, and which she only understands after attempting the practice herself.\n\nThe embedded accounts are concrete developmental material, not decorative lore. A tale describes a failed attempt and the exact correction that followed, a resource obtained through an obligation the listener must now weigh, or a skill demonstrated in a way that reveals its remaining limits. The teller's selection and emphasis remain visible: what is included, what is softened, and what the teller does not know. The listener's response becomes feedback in the outer frame, whether as a question, a refusal, a new offer, or a corrected reading of the teller's motives.\n\nAusten's social precision makes the frame itself a site of learning. A small exchange about who may speak, who interrupts, who offers hospitality, or who changes the subject can reveal a shift in standing that no rank or statistic states directly. Free indirect style shows the learner mistaking deference for agreement or confusing access to a story with mastery of its lesson. Improvement is tested through what the character can now accomplish in the outer situation: a negotiation attempted, a misunderstanding caught before it costs, a skill performed under scrutiny, or a relationship repaired through a more accurate account of someone else's motives.\n\nKeep language, region, community, and period specific when the supplied world provides them. Do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene. The frame-tale structure should remain intelligible without requiring a royal court, a magical bargain, or a compulsory cliffhanger between every nested account.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakal::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nAdvancement appears through dated, partial entries written by the focal character, each recording a particular attempt, exchange, or correction. The entry is a composed artifact: the diarist chooses what to include, what to excuse, and what to present as progress. The diary is not a transparent transcript of live thought or a complete record of the character's mind.\n\nThe gap between the diarist's self-assessment and the recorded scene carries the developmental irony. The entry may claim a technique is mastered while the described attempt shows hesitation, borrowed help, or a lucky result. It may record a social victory while the quoted exchange reveals the other party's patience or condescension. The narration around the entry, if present, keeps the diarist's perspective distinct from the reader's ability to notice what the diarist omits. Free indirect style renders the diarist's self-justifications without endorsing them.\n\nImprovement is testable through recurrence. The same type of task appears in later entries under different conditions: a skill that works alone but fails before an audience, a negotiation that succeeds only after the diarist recognizes what the other party wanted, or a principle the diarist can state but cannot yet execute. Feedback arrives through conversation, correction, refusal, and the practical consequences of error, and the diarist sometimes misattributes that feedback. A real gain may be recorded flatly or with embarrassment, while a false gain may be recorded with pride.\n\nKeep the miscellany varied: an entry may be a brief note, a longer reflection, a remembered exchange, or a list of what was practiced and what went wrong. Seasonal attention, omission, direct feeling, and plain argument are available where appropriate, but no universal quietness or mandatory inventory of Japanese scenery applies. Do not require every entry to end in a cliffhanger or a completed lesson; requests can remain unanswered and tasks can span entries.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakam::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nAdvancement is a character's developing ability to read particular people, manage her own reputation, and act under material constraint. Each stage of learning is grounded in a specific place and relationship: a workplace, a household, a neighborhood, a creditor, a patron, a rival, or a friend whose goodwill must be maintained. Improvement is tested through what the character can now accomplish in those settings, not through abstract self-improvement.\n\nThe character's private reasoning is a site of both progress and error. She interprets an offer, a silence, a change in someone's tone, or a delay in payment, and then acts on that interpretation. Free indirect style renders her reasoning closely while keeping the reader able to notice what she misses. A gain in social understanding may be real and still insufficient: she may correctly identify a rival's motive but fail to see her own patron's impatience, or she may learn to negotiate a better price while remaining unable to refuse a relative's claim.\n\nFeedback arrives through conversation and consequence. A polite phrase may be a correction, a compliment may be a warning, and a favor may create an obligation the character only recognizes later. The same type of social or practical task recurs under different conditions so the reader can track uneven growth. Knowing a principle and executing it remain distinct: the character may explain what she should have said while still failing to say it in the moment, or she may perform a skill reliably without understanding why it worked.\n\nKeep the setting's language, community, and history specific when the supplied world provides them. Do not add supernatural facts, magical realism, or marvelous events to a world that excludes them. The psychological and urban route should remain intimate and concrete, with the narration's irony owned by the narrator rather than turning every self-assessment into a joke.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakan::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Light Novel\n\nEach scene opens at the moment a developmental attempt or social exchange is already in motion. The space, the relevant people, and what the focal character is trying to do are established in clean, readable prose. Short paragraphs separate action, dialogue, and private response, and spatial changes are explicit so the reader always knows where the character is and what she can act on.\n\nThe reaction beats carry Austen's social interpretation. After a significant exchange, the character's private response is a specific reading of what just happened: what the other person wanted, what the offer cost, what the silence meant, or what the character herself failed to say. The reaction may be correct, partial, or mistaken, and the narration keeps the character's perspective distinct from the reader's ability to notice what she misses. A reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner, but it is always an interpretation of a social situation, not merely an emotional label.\n\nProgression is visible through recurrence. The same type of task or negotiation appears in later scenes under different conditions, so the reader can see what the character now accomplishes and what remains difficult. The attempt itself is shown: the hesitation, the borrowed help, the misread cue, or the correction that finally works. Knowing a principle and executing it remain distinct: the character can explain a method but cannot yet perform it, or performs it without understanding why it worked. When unfamiliar names, ranks, or mechanics appear, they are presented clearly at the point of use without pausing the scene for a glossary.\n\nDo not require first person, comedy, a young cast, or a closing cliffhanger. The scene may end during continuing speech, action, thought, composition, or an unfinished setup. The prose remains fast-readable without sacrificing the social precision that makes a small exchange carry several meanings.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakao::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite advancement as a change in the quality of attention. When a character practices a skill, learns a principle, or acquires a resource, let the prose register what that change makes available to the senses and to social interpretation: a new sound in a familiar room, a different weight in a gesture, a phrase that now yields a second meaning. The capability itself should be shown through concrete perception and execution, not announced as a rank or a completed milestone.\n\nUse free indirect judgment to keep the focal character's new understanding distinct from the narrator's. A character may notice that she can now follow the timing of a negotiation or recognize the strain behind a courteous refusal, and the narration may render that recognition in her own figurative language. Let the image develop across the paragraph when it changes what she understands: begin with a sensory detail, extend it through association, and return it to the social exchange that prompted it. Keep the comparison traceable to the scene or to the teller's associations, and let plain statements provide contrast when the character is still uncertain or the capability is incomplete.\n\nLet syntax mark the unevenness of improvement. A practiced movement can arrive in a longer, more assured sentence whose clauses follow one another like the steps of a task now known; a failed attempt may break into shorter clauses or a more guarded image, as though the sentence itself hesitates before the thing not yet mastered. A character may execute a technique cleanly in private and still falter before witnesses, or may understand a principle without yet being able to apply it under pressure. Do not make every advance a triumph of perception. Some growth is practical, partial, or unnoticed by others, and some exchanges remain opaque even to a character whose abilities have increased.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakap::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet advancement arrive with the awareness of what it has displaced. When a character gains a capability, a resource, or a new understanding, place it beside a remembered occasion in which that capability was absent. The comparison should be specific: the same room where she once misread a tone of voice, the same task she once attempted with less skill, the same phrase that now means something different because she can do what she could not do then. The gain remains real, but it does not erase the earlier self or the time already spent.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation to make the remembered occasion concrete. A past offer, refusal, omission, or family obligation can return with its old weight and its new significance. Let the character reflect on what she believed about herself then and what conversation or circumstance has since revealed. The new meaning need not be a reversal: a courtesy once accepted as kindness may now read as strategy, but it may also read as a kindness offered from a position she now understands better. Keep the reflection owned by the focal character or the narrator, not distributed as a general mood.\n\nAllow the progression to remain uneven and the melancholy to remain partial. A character may be more capable and still lonely, more perceptive and still unable to repair a particular attachment, or better resourced and still bound by an old obligation. Ordinary pleasure can remain pleasurable, and a present competence can be enjoyed without forgetting its cost. The reflective register should deepen the account of growth, not replace it with a fixed mood of regret or require each scene to end by announcing impermanence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakaq::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral account as the route for this combination. A situated teller relates how a person's growing capability changed her place in a household, a trade, a patronage network, or a community. Establish the teller's relation to the events: what she witnessed, what she was told, what she infers, and what she chooses to withhold or delay. Let the audience's questions, assumptions, and interruptions shape the telling without turning it into a dialogue transcript. The frame remains part of the story, not a decorative introduction.\n\nMake advancement matter through the teller's account of attempts, practice, feedback, and changed execution. The teller may describe what the learner could not do, what she attempted, who corrected her, and what she can now accomplish. Distinguish the teller's report from the learner's private experience. The teller may know that a skill improved without knowing the cost of the practice, or may repeat a reputation that the learner herself would qualify. Use Austen's social interpretation to give small exchanges several meanings: an offer of instruction may also be a claim on loyalty, a gift of materials may carry an obligation, and a compliment may be an attempt to fix a person in a lower place.\n\nLet delayed revelation serve the progression rather than merely decorate it. A capability may be introduced through its visible effect before the teller explains how it was acquired, or a social consequence may be withheld until the audience has formed a mistaken judgment. The teller's irony should have a clear owner and a social point. Keep the telling coherent as a domestic account given to guests: the teller speaks from her own household knowledge, returns to familiar rooms and recurring exchanges, and lets the audience's curiosity about a neighbor's rise or fall pull the next episode forward. Do not make every tale a nested labyrinth or every audience a chorus of wonder.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakar::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Minimalist Prose\n\nShow advancement through a small number of exact details and direct clauses. When a character has gained a capability, let the difference appear in what she can now do or notice: she completes a task that once stopped her, she hears the hesitation in an offer, she recognizes the cost implied by a polite phrase. Do not explain the improvement unless the explanation materially contributes to the present scene. A principle may be stated briefly, but the proof is in the execution.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation to make each selected detail carry more than one meaning. A single observed gesture can indicate deference, calculation, or fatigue, and the focal character's new capability may let her distinguish among those possibilities. Keep the narration direct. A sentence may say that she now understood the offer was not kindness, without adding a paragraph of analysis. Let the omission leave something inferable: the changed action or the changed reading of a familiar exchange shows the new capability, while the earlier failure or the cost of the learning remains visible in what the scene does not need to say.\n\nAllow improvement to remain uneven. A character may execute a skill in private and still fail before witnesses, or may understand a social principle without being able to apply it under pressure. Show the difference through what happens, not through a summary of her limitations. A precise extended sentence may serve better than several performed fragments, but keep the selected detail doing the work and do not add a second explanation where the first action already carries the meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakas::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite advancement inside sustained scenes. Let a character's growing capability appear through what she attempts, how she practices, what feedback she receives, and what she can now accomplish in an ordinary exchange or task. Move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience, using paragraphing and transitions to orient the reader without recap machinery. Do not pause the scene to display a stat sheet or to announce a rank; let the changed capability become visible in the way she handles a familiar situation.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation to give the progression its particular texture. A lesson may arrive through a courteous correction, a refusal, an offer with hidden terms, or a family obligation that forces a new skill into use. Let the focal character's private judgment differ from what she says or from what others assume, but do not make every exchange a duel of wit or a contest of hidden meanings. Free indirect style may carry her recognition that a compliment is also a claim, or that her new competence has altered someone's manner toward her. Keep the judgment owned by the character or the narrator, and let conversation reveal what people believe about themselves only when the scene gives that revelation room to matter.\n\nAllow strengths and weaknesses to develop unevenly within the scene's movement. A character may know a principle without being able to execute it under pressure, or may perform well in private and falter before witnesses. Let the scene show the difference through a shift in action, a hesitation in dialogue, or a recollection that interrupts the present task, rather than summarizing the limitation afterward. The narrator's voice, the Austen profile, and the immediate scene should set the register and rhythm. Contemporary presentation does not require a contemporary setting, understated emotion, or a lesson at the end of every reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakat::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nLet the established relationship premise organize the account of advancement. Choose scenes in which a character's growing capability matters to the other person: she can now offer help she once could not, she understands a private signal she once missed, or her new competence changes the balance of a rivalry, a comfort, or a discovery. Make the relationship's particular promise legible through repeated moments and private language. A phrase, a gesture, or a shared task may return with new meaning because the capability behind it has changed.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation to give the emotional beats their texture. Affection may be expressed through an offer that also carries obligation; jealousy may appear as a careful question about who provided the new resource; comfort may be offered with an unspoken awareness of what the other person cannot yet do. Let the focal character's private experience differ from what she says. She may accept a compliment while privately knowing the skill is still incomplete, or she may offer help while aware that the other person's pride will refuse it. Give each emotional beat enough space to develop through the actual supplied premise, not through a roster of familiar tropes.\n\nMake progression uneven and consequential within the relationship. A character may improve in one area and remain unable to meet a particular need, or may gain a capability that creates a new distance as well as a new closeness. Let the payoff arrive through a repeated moment or private signal whose meaning the changed capability has altered: the help now accepted, the phrase now understood, the task now shared. The scene may end with the emotional consequence still unfolding, but the changed capability should be felt in the way the characters now see and treat one another.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakau::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell advancement as an account of the deeds by which a person's growing capability will be remembered. Use deliberate openings and compact declarations to establish a learner's place, then expand into the particulars of attempts, practice, feedback, and changed execution. A recurring designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance. Return to a phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application, so that the same words mark the difference between what the person could once do and what she can now accomplish.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation to keep the chronicle's judgments precise. The community may remember a person as capable, generous, or proud, but the narration should show how that reputation was formed through offers, omissions, courtesies, and material obligations. A gift may be remembered as generosity when it was also a claim; a refusal may be remembered as pride when it was also necessity. Let the focal character's private account differ from the shared reputation where the perspective permits. Being remembered as skilled does not mean the learning was effortless, cheerful, or free of cost.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt the authority of the chronicle. The subject of an oft-told incident may object to one detail, or answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern. This need not become parody. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective, and do not import a Nordic pantheon, heroic doom, or ceremonial dialogue into every speaker. The account can end with the latest attempt concluded or the next lesson still ahead, leaving the person's growing capability open to further deeds rather than declaring the full measure of her skill on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakav::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite advancement as a social-realist Scandinavian account of capability acquired through repeated practical attempts, observed in ordinary rooms and ordinary work. The narration stays close to a particular community's material conditions, obligations, and unspoken judgments. Any fantastic or progression-system element is part of that community's actual working life: it appears through the tools people use, the permissions they seek, the costs they pay, and the competence they can demonstrate before others, not through mythic framing or landscape symbolism.\n\nUse Austen's free indirect judgment to render the gap between what a person believes about their own improvement and what circumstance reveals. When a character practices a technique, manages a resource, or claims a new rank, let the narration move between their private explanation and the social meaning of the attempt: who must be asked, what it costs, who notices the failure, and what courtesy or omission accompanies the result. Advancement becomes legible through changed capability, not through a character's confident summary of it. A person may understand a principle long before they can execute it under pressure, and a strength in one setting may remain unreliable in another.\n\nMake each test concrete and uneven. Show the particular task that now succeeds or still fails: a working relationship repaired, a material shortage navigated, a technique performed before witnesses, an obligation met without the old hesitation. Let feedback arrive through conversation, consequence, and the quiet recalibration of another person's opinion. Do not let every practice session end in a clean increment. Some attempts expose a remaining difficulty; some improvements matter only to the person attempting them; some are noticed by the community for reasons that have little to do with the skill itself.\n\nKeep the social world active around the developing person. Money, family, reputation, and daily labor remain present, and a small exchange can carry several meanings at once. A polite offer may be a test of competence, a refusal may protect someone from an unfinished skill, and a compliment may conceal a material reservation. The narration may judge these exchanges with Austen's measured irony, but the judgment must belong to a clear focal perspective and must not make every character uniformly perceptive or uniformly witty.\n\nTreat snow, isolation, or emotional reserve as practical conditions only when the supplied setting includes them. If they are present, render them through their effect on work, travel, and social access rather than as automatic regional atmosphere. Otherwise, let the domestic and institutional textures of the actual setting carry the regional method. Preserve the active world's rules about what can be learned and what remains impossible, and keep required interiority available as ordinary private prose rather than converting it into performed speech or public knowledge.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakaw::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Screenplay\n\nWrite the developing capability as a sequence of readable scenes in which a character attempts something concrete and the result is visible or audible. Use scene headings when location or time changes, keep action lines concise, and give each speaker a clear cue. Advancement appears through the changed behavior and its consequence: the task completed without the old hesitation, the resource used correctly, the technique performed under a new constraint, or the failure that reveals which part of the skill remains unreliable. Do not announce a rank or stat change in the action line.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation through dialogue, blocking, and material circumstance. A courteous offer, a pause before answering, a person who leaves a room at a particular moment, or a small omission about money or family can give an exchange several meanings. Let the audience and the focal character read those meanings differently. One character may treat a compliment as proof of acceptance while the visible arrangement of the scene shows the compliment was a way of ending the conversation. Keep the irony in the gap between what is said and what the action or later scene reveals, not in a voice-over explaining it.\n\nWhen private thought or judgment is required, place it in a clearly attributed nonspoken note separated from the dialogue and action. The note may render the focal character's actual private experience: their reasoning, uncertainty, resentment, or misreading of another person. It is not a line another character can hear, and it does not grant the cast access to that thought. Use such notes when the private experience contributes to the progression or the social reading; do not replace every reaction with an interior monologue.\n\nUse cuts, offscreen sound, or a change of scene only when the sequence needs to skip practice time, move between locations, or withhold a visible event legitimately. If a training interval is compressed, preserve the changes that matter: the next scene should show the new capability or the remaining difficulty rather than merely stating that time passed. Let feedback arrive through another character's changed manner, a practical consequence, or a spoken correction. Do not fill the page with camera directions or make every scene end on a completed test. A scene may close on an unfinished attempt, a pending request, or the plain fact that someone now sees the focal character differently.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakax::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Stage Play\n\nWrite the developing capability as a playable scene in which a character attempts something concrete and the audience can see or hear the result. Use clear speaker names, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Advancement appears through the changed behavior and its consequence in the shared space: the task completed without the help it once required, the prop or resource handled correctly, the technique performed while another character watches, or the particular step that fails and reveals the remaining difficulty. Do not put a progression-system explanation in the stage directions.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation through the relationship between lines and physical business. A polite offer, a pause, an entrance at the wrong moment, a person who moves toward or away from an object, or a small omission about money or family can give an exchange several meanings. Let one character read a gesture as acceptance while the audience sees the gesture also serves a practical or evasive purpose. Keep the irony in that gap rather than in a character announcing the subtext. Do not make every character uniformly witty or uniformly perceptive.\n\nWhen private thought or judgment is required, preserve it as clearly attributed nonspoken authoring material separated from the dialogue and stage directions. It may render the focal character's actual private experience: their reasoning, uncertainty, resentment, or misreading of another person. It is not an aside another character can hear, and it does not grant the cast access to that thought. Use monologues or asides only when the dramatic situation calls for performed speech, not as the default destination for interiority.\n\nLet feedback arrive through another character's changed manner, a practical consequence, or a spoken correction. A scene may close on an unfinished attempt, a pending request, or the plain fact that someone now sees the focal character differently. Do not add film-camera instructions, and do not force every scene to end on a completed test or a theatrical revelation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakay::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the developing capability as associative mental movement through a particular authorized mind. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern so that the reader experiences the mind's changing relation to a task rather than a linear report of practice. When the character attempts a technique or manages a resource, let the syntax follow the attempt: the remembered instruction surfaces, the body resists or complies, a past failure interrupts, a social worry intrudes, and the present action resumes or breaks apart. Enough orientation must remain to distinguish the actual event from recollection, anticipation, or fantasy.\n\nMake advancement legible through the changing texture of that mental movement. A skill that once required the character to rehearse each step may now arrive as a single bodily response, interrupted only by a stray thought. A difficulty that once produced panic may now produce a narrower, more specific hesitation. Show uneven development: the mind may be fluent in one part of the task while still fumbling another, and a principle the character can recite may remain disconnected from execution. Do not let the stream of consciousness turn every practice session into a clean increment or a tidy insight.\n\nKeep Austen's social interpretation inside the focal mind's actual private experience. Let the character notice a courteous phrase, an omission, a sum of money, a family obligation, or a shift in someone's manner, and let that notice interrupt or color the present task. The mind may return to a conversation, test its own explanation, or misread another person's motive. Preserve a clear owner for that judgment: the narration may render the focal character's irony or self-deception, but it must not make every character share a mind or grant the focal mind access to excluded thoughts. Do not turn all scene facts indeterminate; the reader should still know what happened, what was remembered, and what was only imagined.\n\nLet feedback arrive through the mind's later return to an event: a remembered correction, a changed bodily response, or the slow recognition that another person's offer meant something different. The passage may close on an unfinished thought, a recurring concern, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now easier or still difficult. Ordinary reasoning alone is not this stylistic mode; the movement must be genuinely associative, but it remains the experience of one authorized mind.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakaz::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the developing capability as a story carried by sender-attributed messages. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one character writes in short bursts, another revises before sending, another leaves a message unanswered while they work. Advancement appears through the changed competence in the messages themselves and in the practical consequences they set in motion: the person who once asked for help now gives a clear instruction, the technique once described vaguely is now summarized with the particular step that matters, the resource problem once avoided is now addressed directly. Do not turn the transcript into a progression log or a stat report.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation through digital manners. A polite formula, a delay, an edit that softens a request, a message left on read, an omitted sum of money, or a family obligation mentioned only in passing can give a small exchange several meanings. Let one sender read a reply as acceptance while the transcript shows the reply was a way of ending the conversation. Preserve the gap between what is sent and what is not sent: an unsent draft may contain the sender's actual private thought, but it is not transmitted text and no other character can know it. Distinguish private thought from transmitted text at every point.\n\nWhen context outside the exchange is required, present it clearly separated from the transcript. It may render the focal character's actual private experience, the physical task they are attempting, or the material conditions that explain a delay. Do not fabricate timestamps, receipts, attachments, or channel changes merely to add subtext. Use only the interface events that matter to the exchange or the progression. Let feedback arrive through another sender's changed manner, a practical consequence reported in a later message, or a spoken correction quoted in the transcript. The exchange may close on an unfinished request, a pending reply, or the plain fact that someone now understands the focal character differently.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakba::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the developing capability as a sequence of concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Advancement appears through the changed behavior and its consequence in the scene: the task completed without the help it once required, the resource handled correctly, the technique performed while another character watches, or the particular step that fails and reveals the remaining difficulty. Do not announce a rank or stat change in the narration.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation through dialogue and focal reaction. A courteous offer, a pause, an omission about money or family, or a small shift in another character's manner can give an exchange several meanings. Let the focal character read a gesture as acceptance while the scene's arrangement or a later line shows the gesture also served a practical or evasive purpose. Keep the irony in that gap rather than in a character announcing the subtext. Do not make every character uniformly witty or uniformly perceptive.\n\nWhen private thought or judgment is required, place it in the focal narration or inner response as ordinary private prose. It may render the focal character's actual private experience: their reasoning, uncertainty, resentment, or misreading of another person. It is not a line another character can hear, and it does not grant the cast access to that thought. Use background or expression cues as purposeful references when they orient the moment, not as automatic truth indicators or mandatory labels on every line. A changed expression may attract attention, but a contradiction in an arrangement or account must do the interpretive work.\n\nKeep scene changes legible. Let feedback arrive through another character's changed manner, a practical consequence, or a spoken correction. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, images, or executable engine commands merely because the style is selected. A scene may close on an unfinished attempt, a pending request, or the plain fact that someone now sees the focal character differently.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalakbb::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jane Austen / Web Serial\n\nWrite the developing capability as an installment-oriented through-line. Each reply should make clear what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Advancement appears through cumulative developments: the technique that failed in an earlier installment now succeeds under a particular condition, the resource problem is navigated with a new competence, the relationship shifts because the focal character can now do something they could not do before. Do not turn every installment into a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. Progress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources, and it should remain uneven: a strength in one area may coexist with a difficulty in another.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation across replies. Let a courteous offer, an omission, a sum of money, a family obligation, or a small shift in someone's manner carry meaning that may change as later installments reveal more. A character may read an exchange one way in the moment and later understand it differently without the earlier event being erased. Keep the irony in that changing understanding rather than in a narrator announcing the subtext. Preserve a clear owner for free indirect judgment: the narration may render the focal character's misreading or self-deception, but it must not make every character uniformly perceptive or uniformly witty.\n\nRecover context briefly only when it helps the reader re-enter. When an earlier detail becomes newly significant, return to the particular fact rather than retelling the whole sequence that established it. For example, if a character now performs a task that once required help, recall the earlier failure or the person who helped, not the entire training history. Let feedback arrive through another character's changed manner, a practical consequence, or a spoken correction. Keep required private experience available as ordinary private prose when it contributes feeling or thought, even if no immediate decision changes. The installment may close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalaa::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nMake advancement legible through what a character can now attempt and what still costs them. A new technique, resource, or understanding should enter the story as a changed capability: the character reaches a task that was previously impossible, holds a stance under pressure, or recognizes a principle they had only recited before. Do not let the improvement erase the difficulty that produced it. Show the unevenness of growth by keeping some old failure active beside the new competence.\n\nTreat each significant advance as a negotiation between private need and public obligation. A teacher, ally, rival, or family member may have a stake in the character's progress, and the character's decision to accept help, reveal weakness, or claim a new standing should strain that relationship. Let the strain appear in actual conversation: a request for instruction can sound like an admission, an offer of help can feel like a judgment, and a demonstration of new skill can reopen an old grievance. The character may speak before they have fully decided what to admit, and the other person may respond to the need behind the words rather than the words alone.\n\nUse Jin Yong's generous scenic movement to give these exchanges room. A training attempt, a journey, a shared meal, or a public test can carry the emotional material without stopping the plot for a therapy scene. Show technique, restraint, and reputation through particular decisions: the character chooses to hold back, to spend a resource, to protect someone, or to accept a consequence that changes how others regard them. The cost of that decision should remain visible afterward, not dissolve into a clean lesson.\n\nKeep the narrator's distance and diction. If the viewpoint is close, render the character's uncertainty, resentment, longing, or relief in their own idiom rather than a stock vocabulary of suffering. If the narrator is more distant, let the strain show through gesture, timing, and the gap between what is said and what is done. Comfort may arrive partially or awkwardly: a hand on the shoulder, a shared silence, or a practical favor can matter without resolving the hurt. Warmth and pain may occupy the same scene. Do not force a confession, reconciliation, or tragic turn; let the emotional outcome follow from the particular relationship and the actual events.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalab::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite each reply as a coherent shared interval in readable paragraphs. Attribute every line of dialogue unmistakably, and keep action markers and narrative person consistent with the active authorship contract. Do not require a question, cliffhanger, or forced choice at the end; the continuation should follow from what has just happened, whether the scene pauses during speech, action, thought, or an unfinished setup.\n\nAdvance progression through a concrete attempt within the scene. The character practices a form, tests a new understanding, uses a resource, receives feedback, or discovers that a principle they could recite still fails in execution. Show the attempt rather than summarizing it: the movement, the mistake, the correction, the moment when something works for the first time. Let the result be partial. A success can reveal the next difficulty, and a failure can produce useful information without ending the character's effort.\n\nGive the scene Jin Yong's generous movement. Include travel, explanation, or relationship material when the interval calls for it, and let technique, restraint, alliances, and reputation matter through particular decisions. A character may choose to hold back in a sparring match, share a technique with an ally, refuse a shortcut, or accept a public test that changes how others regard them. Let NPCs remain active: they offer help, withhold it, ask questions, make demands, or continue their own practical lives rather than waiting for the player or viewpoint character to act.\n\nKeep the prose scannable without flattening the characters. Short paragraphs, clear speakers, and concrete physical detail help the reader follow the scene, but the characters should still have particular voices and motives. When the viewpoint character thinks or feels something, render it in their own idiom rather than a generic reaction line. End the reply at a point where the next writer can naturally continue, not at a manufactured decision point.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalac::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the classical or vernacular episodic telling route. Organize the narrative as chapter-like episodes, each with a definite scene or sequence of scenes, a clear beginning and end, and a title or opening line that orients the reader to the episode's concern. Make significant developments in capability, understanding, or relationships legible when they occur. A new teacher, revealed limit, debt, response, or journey may carry into a later episode if earned; neither a gain nor a new direction is required at every close.\n\nMake progression cumulative across episodes. A technique learned in one episode should return in a later one under changed conditions, and a weakness exposed early should remain a live difficulty until the character has actually worked on it. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it by showing the character recite or understand something in one episode and then fail or succeed in applying it in another. Let resources, alliances, and reputation accumulate with the same episodic logic: a favor given in one chapter can be called due later, and a public act can shape how strangers receive the character in the next town or hall.\n\nUse Jin Yong's tension between private attachment and public obligation as the engine of episodic conflict. Each episode can place a personal loyalty against a duty, a promise, or a reputation, and the character's decision should have consequences that carry into later episodes. Give encounters room for travel, explanation, and relationships. A meeting with a teacher or rival can include the journey to reach them, the history they share, and the particular decision that tests their bond. Do not reduce action to a sequence of blows; let technique and restraint express values, and let a fight or contest change the relationship between the participants.\n\nWrite with the clarity and forward movement of episodic vernacular telling. Keep the prose direct and concrete, with transitions that move the reader from place to place and from one episode's concern to the next. Recurrence is a structural tool: a phrase, object, place, or person can return to mark how much has changed. Do not import a specific historical Chinese setting, sect system, or cast unless the active world supplies them; the episodic method and the craft of loyalty and consequence are what travel.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalad::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite with forceful diction and compressed transitions. Move from one beat to the next without explanatory padding: a failed attempt, a correction, a new attempt, a result. Use concrete physical detail to carry the progression: the ache in a shoulder, the worn edge of a practice weapon, the cracked skin on a hand, the sound of a stance held too long. Let material wear and physical stakes show what the character is spending to improve.\n\nShow advancement through what the character can now do under pressure. A technique is not learned when it is recited or performed slowly in safety; it is learned when it holds during a real test. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it by putting the character in a situation where the principle must survive fatigue, injury, distraction, or fear. Let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly: the character may gain speed while losing precision, or master a form while still failing to read an opponent. Do not smooth over the cost.\n\nPreserve Jin Yong's particular decisions about technique, restraint, alliances, and reputation, but compress their presentation. A choice to hold back, spend a resource, protect someone, or accept a public consequence should be shown in the act and its immediate result rather than explained at length. Give encounters enough physical room for travel and relationship material when the scene needs it, but keep the prose hard-edged: a journey can be a sentence about the road and the weight carried, a relationship can be shown in who shares water or who steps in front of whom. Do not reduce action to a sequence of blows; a fight or contest should express values and change the relationship between the participants.\n\nVary the rhythm. Short, high-contrast sentences can carry a burst of action or a moment of failure. Retain a longer thought when the character's reasoning needs it: a decision about whether to use a dangerous technique, a calculation of risk, or a memory that explains why a particular restraint matters. Ground abrasive or rough speech in its speaker rather than making every character sound the same. The style does not turn the story into survival horror; it keeps the progression genre's learning and the author's loyalty and consequence visible through pressure and material detail.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalae::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the progression through documents, each with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the document form the scene actually requires: a letter to a teacher reporting a failed attempt, a journal entry recording a practice session, a report from an observer who saw a public test, a note left for an ally, a record of resources spent or promised. Do not rotate through formats mechanically.\n\nLet each document know and conceal only what its author would know and choose to record. A student's letter may describe a breakthrough without understanding why it worked. A rival's report may emphasize a weakness the student did not notice. A teacher's reply may correct a principle the student recited but failed to execute. Gaps between documents can carry the story: a missing reply, a torn page, a letter never sent, a record revised after later events. Conflicting accounts should reveal different positions and loyalties rather than simply contradicting each other for effect.\n\nMake improvement legible across entries. A technique mentioned as impossible in one document can appear as a failed attempt in the next and a partial success in a later report. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it by letting a writer describe understanding something and then, in a later document, describe the moment when the understanding had to survive pressure. Let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly: one correspondent may praise a new speed while another notes the character's deteriorating form. Resources, alliances, and reputation should accumulate through the paper trail: a letter of introduction, a record of a debt, a public notice of a contest result.\n\nUse Jin Yong's tension between private attachment and public obligation through what writers choose to say and to whom. A character may write one version to a teacher and another to a friend, or omit a personal motive from an official report. The choice to record, conceal, or delay a piece of information is itself a particular decision with consequences. Preserve readable continuity without adding an omniscient explanation after every document; let the reader assemble the sequence from what the documents reveal and what they leave out.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalaf::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nTreat progression as part of a developing life that includes rest, care, and ordinary pleasures. A practice session can be followed by a shared meal, a long journey can pause for a night of easy conversation, and a difficult lesson can be softened by someone who remembers how the character takes their tea or which shoulder aches. Spend attention on chosen kindness and familiar preferences: the food prepared without being asked, the blanket left within reach, the quiet company after a hard attempt.\n\nKeep improvement active within the comfort. Show the character practicing, receiving feedback, or testing a new understanding, and let the warmth of the scene support rather than replace that work. A friend may correct a stance gently, a teacher may praise a small advance, or a companion may simply stay nearby while the character tries again. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it: the character can understand something in conversation and still need the practice that follows. Let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly, and let a failure be met with patience rather than erased.\n\nUse Jin Yong's particular decisions about technique, restraint, alliances, and reputation through acts of care. A character may choose to share a resource, protect a companion's rest, or accept help that changes how they are seen. Let affection be sincere without requiring a confession or emotional repair. Comfort can be partial: a person may be cared for without being fully understood, and warmth can coexist with a disagreement that remains unresolved. Keep personality and privacy intact; comfort does not make everyone agreeable or remove established problems from the world.\n\nGive the scene room for travel, explanation, and relationships. A journey can include the small rituals of camp, a lesson can include the history behind a technique, and an alliance can be strengthened by a favor done without fanfare. Do not let the fluff swallow the genre: the character is still learning, still facing difficulty, still making choices that matter. The warmth is how the developing life is lived, not a pause from it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalag::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let each scene open into its social context where the chosen narrator permits it: the hall where a test will be held, the household that depends on the character's success, the history between a teacher and a rival, the public meaning of a rank or title. Distinguish narration from ordinary spoken language; the narrator may frame a decision through judgment, history, or social consequence, but the characters' speech should remain their own.\n\nMake progression legible as a change in standing and understanding, not only in technique. A new capability should alter how the character is received, what they are asked to do, and what they now owe. Distinguish knowing a principle from executing it by letting the narrator observe the gap between a character's confident explanation and their uncertain performance, or between a teacher's instruction and the student's first real application. Let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly, and let the narrator note what the character has gained and what remains beyond them.\n\nUse Jin Yong's tension between private attachment and public obligation as the novel's central pressure. A decision to accept instruction, reveal a limit, protect a companion, or claim a new standing should be framed through both its private cost and its public consequence. Give encounters room for travel, explanation, and relationships. A journey can carry the weight of what was left behind; a conversation can revisit shared history; a contest can test not only skill but restraint, loyalty, and reputation. Do not reduce action to a sequence of blows; let the narrator show what a technique or a refusal expresses about the person who uses it.\n\nUse public/private contrast to organize passages when it is relevant. A formal ceremony, a letter, a public announcement, or a moment of private preparation can frame the same event differently. Let the narrator move between the character's private experience and the social meaning of their actions without granting access to minds the chosen narrator cannot enter. The formal architecture is the style; do not supply an aristocratic setting, archaic vocabulary, or uniformly elaborate speech unless the active world calls for them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalah::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite each stage of advancement as a tale told for a present purpose. Give the telling its audience, occasion, and stake: the teller recounts an attempt, a teacher's correction, a hard-won resource, or a failure survived in order to persuade, warn, repay, delay, or bind the listeners. Let that purpose govern emphasis and omission, so the embedded story acts on the outer scene rather than standing as a neutral lecture.\n\nKeep the two levels intelligible and let them act on each other. Anchor the frame in the teller's present voice — an interruption, a gesture, a hesitation over a name — and mark each transition into the recounted past so the reader always knows which level holds the action. When the outer scene next demands the recounted principle be executed — under fatigue, imperfect conditions, divided loyalties — show the gap between knowing the story and performing its lesson: a technique recited correctly and fumbled in its timing, a warning that changes one decision and not another. Let capabilities advance unevenly, so a gained strength leaves a related weakness for a later telling to face.\n\nUse recurrence and delayed revelation. A phrase, object, gesture, or named technique from the embedded tale can reappear in the outer scene with altered meaning; a formula the teller repeats can accrue weight each time it is tested. Part of an account may remain unfinished or withheld, its meaning arriving when the outer scene finally requires it — withheld for a reason the teller has, not as a trick played on the reader.\n\nLet loyalty and reputation shape the telling. A generous account of a rival's skill can be an honest tribute that also serves a present alliance; an omission may protect a companion or guard a shame the teller has not faced. Listeners may notice the emphasis, ask a concrete question about timing or sequence, or answer from their own obligations. The tale need not be false; its interest lies in what the telling does.\n\nKeep the telling spacious: travel, explanation, and relationship remain part of the developing life, and a breakthrough matters through what it lets a person attempt, whom it lets them protect, and what it costs in obligation. The frame is a craft device, not a setting — it brings no palace, djinn, or bargain with it — and no cliffhanger is owed between nested accounts. End a response wherever the current speech, action, thought, or composition pauses.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalai::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite advancement as a change in what a person can do within a specific community — a workshop, a household, a school, a company, or whatever institution the story supplies — whose expectations shape every stage of the learner's path. Ground practice in its material conditions: who gives instruction and what it costs them, what tools and time exist, what ordinary work must be done alongside training, and what happens to standing and livelihood when those conditions fail. Make progress legible through the institution's own signs — a task entrusted, a bench changed, a correction that stops coming — rather than through numerical measures invented for display.\n\nLet private ambition and public obligation pull against each other in concrete decisions. A learner wants a technique for personal reasons while owing labor, loyalty, or protection to others, and each new capability rewrites those debts: it can make someone useful to a patron, dangerous to a rival, or responsible for work they once escaped. Show reputation and alliance shifting through particular acts and their delayed consequences rather than announced morals. Connect a day's work to a change in standing across longer developmental paragraphs while staying particular about rooms, tools, meals, and conversations.\n\nDistinguish understanding from execution through ordinary difficulty. A principle explained in a lesson becomes real only when attempted under fatigue, distraction, or emotional strain: the attempt fails in a specific way, a teacher or companion corrects it, the learner adjusts, and the next test shows what changed and what remains stubborn. Where the narrator permits, give the learner's silent accounting of what was asked and what was delivered alongside what the institution can see. Strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly — speed bought with precision, a form mastered while a real confrontation still freezes the body.\n\nLet the narrator's distance and syntax fit this social world: sentences that expand to carry a working day, an apprenticeship's slow turn, or a household's recalibration of someone's prospects, and compress when a test goes wrong. The working tradition is social and developmental realism, not a national temperament — no bureaucratic severity, no translated word order, no coldness for its own sake. The effect comes from close attention to how people live inside institutions they did not choose.\n\nDo not schedule rewards or punishments. Some progress is quiet, some effort is wasted, some obligations survive a breakthrough. End a response at a natural pause in the learner's work, speech, thought, or relationship rather than at a completed examination.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalaj::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite improvement as a lived negotiation between capability and obligation in a decaying city whose old powers still shape present access. Let advancement come through attempts, practice, feedback, new understanding, resources, and changed capability, and keep knowing a principle distinct from executing it under strain. Strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly: a form that works in a borrowed training room may fail when exhausted, a new resource may solve one problem while creating a debt, and recovery may restore function without returning the same confidence. Ordinary work, recovery, and relationships remain part of the developing life, not pauses from it.\n\nUse expansive clarity to make technique, restraint, alliances, and reputation matter through particular decisions. Technique is also a social fact: who taught it, who recognizes it, who is owed for it, and what it permits in public. Give encounters room for travel, explanation, and relationship—two people may test each other through a shared meal, a careful demonstration, or a long walk before a fight, and a fight itself should express values rather than only settle strength. Keep competing loyalties live: a patron may be generous and compromised, an opponent honorable and wrong, a shared history binding enemies as much as allies. When a character advances, show how the advance alters what they owe, what others expect, and which old compromise becomes harder to hide. Progress often means learning when not to use a new ability, or discovering that a hard-won technique carries a cost no one anticipated. Whatever institutions, trades, or techniques the story's world supplies, render their lineages, obligations, and reputations through concrete choices rather than importing a ready-made martial sect or historical setting.\n\nLet Gothic-Punk atmosphere change how each step is experienced. Old authority and present life collide: access to training, tools, or safe passage may depend on inherited arrangements—a family contract, a guild's decaying charter, a landlord's favor, a buried office—still enforced by modern systems. Urban material detail should be concrete: bad wiring, rented rooms, transit schedules, borrowed equipment, rain on hot pavement, the paperwork that keeps a privilege alive. Morally complicated intimacy makes private desire and social masks matter. A character may perform confidence in public and reveal uncertainty only in trusted company, and a trusted companion may also be implicated in the same old power the protagonist is trying to escape. Personal horror belongs to what progress reveals or demands—not as a guaranteed betrayal, but as the felt cost of a capability that changes the protagonist's place in the city. Use only the supernatural rules the world already contains.\n\nUse the progression system, if one exists, for what it can actually measure: ranks, licenses, access levels, documented training, or acknowledged mastery. Do not invent numbers merely to display progress. Test advancement through what the protagonist can now accomplish and what remains difficult. A new rank may grant entrance to a building but not the trust of the people inside; a mastered form may work against one opponent and fail against another because of terrain, injury, or different training. Anchor a breakthrough in prior attempts and specific feedback. If the advance is incomplete, show the next concrete obstacle rather than announcing a plateau. Keep outcomes contingent: the story may grant a hard-won success, a partial gain, a setback, or a discovery that the old measure of progress was wrong.\n\nWrite nonspoken interiority as actual private experience—reasoning, sensation, uncertainty, and desire—rather than replacing it with a letter, diary, monologue, or voice-over. A character's spoken and private content may differ for a specific reason: embarrassment, utility, concealment, habit, care, or uncertainty. Do not make every pause a loyalty test or every intimacy a trap; trusted company can be genuinely trusted. A scene may end during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished attempt; it need not complete a training arc, resolve a loyalty conflict, or produce a new power before the response boundary. Let the city and its inherited powers shape what can be learned, but make each new capability change the terms of the relationships around the protagonist.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalak::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nCarry advancement through remembered episodes recited on a present occasion. Frame each telling with the formal turns of a living oral tradition — a repeated opening formula, a formal naming of the line of teachers, a closing that hands the episode to the listener — so the recitation itself marks where instruction begins and ends. Let the occasion give the telling its purpose: a gathering, a departure, a debt falling due, a dispute to be settled, a younger person standing where an older one once stood.\n\nTreat the episodes as an inherited chain rather than isolated anecdotes. Each remembered link carries one stage of capability — what the learner could not do, what was practiced, what went wrong, what became possible afterward — and the chain may name teachers, places, and techniques as things recited and counted, giving the tradition its breadth. Link the episodes by what they transmit: a victory in one leaves a related weakness a later episode must address; a technique learned in one is tested under new conditions in another; a name earned in the past changes how the present occasion receives the teller.\n\nMake the frame active. The teller selects and emphasizes according to present obligations — warning a student, justifying an old decision, preparing someone for a task — and the listeners interrupt, question, or answer from their own loyalties. An embedded episode can change the outer scene: firm a listener's resolve, expose a misunderstanding, create an obligation. Place each remembered test in fatigue, fear, or divided loyalty so that knowing a principle stays distinct from executing it, and let the listener's own practice begin to answer what the episode taught.\n\nUse recurrence across the chain: a named technique, a phrase, a place, or an object returns from the remembered past into the present occasion and accrues meaning with each return. Keep the levels separate through clear transitions between the teller's present voice and the recounted action. The telling may be generous, partial, or shaped by love and shame without being false; its interest lies in what it transmits and what it leaves for the listener to test.\n\nGive the story its spaciousness: journeys between episodes matter for whom the learner meets, what they owe, and how their reputation changes. Keep language, region, community, and period exactly as the story supplies them; do not impose English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure. End where the telling or the outer occasion pauses.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalal::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nStage advancement as performance before aware onlookers. Present learning through audience-facing scenes — a lesson delivered with comic exaggeration, a contest mounted before a crowd, a recognition scene in which a new capability is displayed, a challenge that puts reputation and technique on the same line — and let the narration carry theatrical energy: entrances and exits noticed, voices mimicked in the telling, a pattern set up and overturned. The comedy comes from character, timing, and the gap between self-presentation and actual ability, not from slapstick imposed on every exchange.\n\nMake each test legible as staging. Show what the learner attempts, what the teacher or opponent does in answer, and what the outcome reveals about changed capability: a technique performed brilliantly in practice and fumbled before an audience; a flashy move that works only under conditions its owner cannot control. The reader should see what was previously impossible and what remains difficult. Use sentence rhythm for comic timing — the run-up, the pause, the overturn — and let a serious beat land suddenly inside a lively exchange.\n\nLet reputation and obligation drive the staging. A contest may be fought for a patron, a companion, a promise, or a public claim the learner must then honor; private attachment and public obligation pull against each other in particular decisions — holding back to shield a friend, revealing a technique to save a stranger, accepting a challenge that will change how a household treats them. Around the performed action, keep room for travel, explanation, and relationship.\n\nLet personal language and narrative distance belong to the actual teller. Direct feeling, exuberance, boasting, plain argument, and omission are all available where they fit that voice; no universal quietness applies and no inventory of scenery is required — seasonal attention, if used, should carry the scene's meaning rather than decorate it. A boast may be sincere; a defeat may be comic without being meaningless.\n\nDo not demand victory, humiliation, or a completed lesson in every scene. A performance may be interrupted, a recognition partial, a new understanding left untested. End where the action, speech, thought, or unfinished setup pauses.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalam::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nPresent advancement through accounts someone witnessed, remembers, or reports. Let the narration move between attributed tellers — a companion describing a journey, a teacher testifying to a student's progress, a rival recounting a contest, a community recording what changed after someone returned — and let each account carry its teller's partial knowledge and present purpose. Keep testimony's basic distinction in play: what the teller saw and did personally, versus what they heard, inferred, or repeat from others, marked in how the account is phrased rather than in clinical labels.\n\nHold the gap between the account and the capability. Show what a teller claims, what that teller could actually have observed, and what the outer scene later confirms, complicates, or leaves untested. A reported breakthrough becomes real only when the learner attempts the technique under conditions no teller controlled. Place the attempt in fatigue, fear, or divided loyalty so knowing a principle stays distinct from executing it, and let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly across accounts — one witness's speed, another's precision, neither the whole person.\n\nLet obligation shape the testimony. A teller may speak to honor a companion, protect a patron, settle a debt, or justify a decision that still troubles them; private attachment and public obligation pull against each other in what is recorded and what is left out. An account need not be false to be partial. Give the chronicle lived thickness — travel, explanation, relationship — so it reads as remembered history rather than a list of deeds, and keep the language, community, and history the story actually supplies. Chronicle and testimony add no marvelous events to a world that excludes them; the accounts record what that world permits.\n\nDo not force every account to be confirmed, corrected, or exposed within a response. A testimony may stand as partial evidence, a listener may accept it with reservations, and the learner's next test may still lie ahead. End where the telling or the outer scene pauses.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalan::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly and keep the reader oriented in space: when a scene changes location, make the move explicit — where the learner is, who else is present, what they are doing. Break paragraphs readably around action, dialogue, and private response so a practice session, a lesson, or a confrontation stays easy to follow without losing the weight of what happens.\n\nMake progression visible in the scene's beats. Show the attempt — what the learner tries, how the body or tool answers, what goes wrong. Show the feedback — a teacher's correction, a companion's observation, the learner's own recognition of the miss. Show the adjusted attempt and what changes. Let the learner understand a technique and still fail its timing, spacing, or application under pressure, and let one skill improve while another stays stubborn.\n\nPresent unfamiliar names, techniques, and mechanics clearly when they appear: pair a technique's name with its visible effect, and show a rank or title through how others respond to it. Give the reader what is needed to follow the action without pausing the scene for a manual. Do not invent numerical mechanics for display; the reader sees progress through what the character can now do and what still defeats them.\n\nUse reaction beats for the loyalties underneath the action. After an exchange, the learner's private response can reveal what the event means — loyalty to a teacher, worry for a companion, shame at a failure, the cost of a reputation — and it may be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner, never a compulsory quip. Encounters keep their travel, explanation, and relationship around the action, so a fight or lesson is not reduced to a sequence of blows.\n\nFirst person, comedy, a young cast, and a closing cliffhanger are not requirements here; use them only if the story's actual narrator and situation call for them. A response may stop during continuing action, speech, thought, or an unfinished setup without owing a reversal or a completed test.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalao::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite advancement as a change in how the body meets the world, and let the prose make that change felt before anyone names it. When a technique is first executed, slow into the moment: weight moving through a stance, breath finding a new timing, a hand traveling a line the learner could not previously hold. Shape the sentences so their rhythm enacts the act — a long clause that gathers and releases as the strike gathers, a shorter one where the learner falters. Let a comparison unfold across the paragraph only when it changes what the focal character perceives or understands, and keep its materials traceable to the scene and the teller's associations — water the learner has trained beside, a rope worn smooth by other hands, a teacher's weathered grip — rather than to a borrowed decorative register. Knowing a principle should read differently from performing it; render the gap as sensation and attention, what the learner can now hear in a movement that was previously only noise, not as an announced rank.\n\nGive the whole arc of practice the same shaped attention as the breakthrough. Failed attempts, a correction repeated until it lands, fatigue, a resource spent: these deserve rhythm too. A recurring sound or image can mark developing skill when a later scene gives it new application — the river that once set the learner's pace now too slow for it — and it can just as well record a misstep or exhaustion, because progression includes what stays difficult. Between figurative passages, let plain statements carry the facts. Characters speak in their own voices; the figurative register belongs to the narration, not to every mouth on the road.\n\nKeep the scenic movement generous inside this register. Travel, explanation, meals, argument, and reunion occupy real narrative space, and a contest's stakes travel through its rhythm: a loyal refusal, a protected person, a private attachment made visible by what the technique restrains rather than by what it destroys. The reader should always leave the passage knowing who acted, why it mattered, and what it cost. Inward experience belongs to the focal character and stays unknown to the others unless the scene or the narration discloses it, and advancement can be tested by what the character can now do for someone else or under a particular obligation. Relationships, recovery, and ordinary work keep their place in the developing life, and their prose may be as rhythmically shaped as any duel.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalap::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet each advancement arrive with the awareness that something else has already changed. Test a new capability where an earlier failure happened, or beside a person who knew the learner at a worse stage: the task that took all night now takes an hour, the daily correction no longer comes, the familiar route is walked without the old hesitation. Measure progress through small irreversible changes and remembered occasions, and let the present accomplishment and the unavailable past occupy the same scene instead of separating into event and lesson. Ordinary pleasure stays pleasurable — a good meal on the road, a technique finally comfortable, a duty fulfilled and steady — so the reflective weight lands on particular gains and particular absences rather than on a mood spread over everything.\n\nKeep the causal account of improvement intact. The reader still sees what was practiced, which feedback or resource changed the execution, and what remains out of reach; reflection gives those events their weight rather than replacing them. Feel the distance between knowing a principle and executing it as a specific regret or relief — the understanding that arrived a season too late to help someone, the skill that finally matches an old intention. A remembered failure may surface during a current success to make its cost and incompleteness legible without cancelling what has been gained.\n\nGive the melancholy its social shape through the tension between private attachment and public obligation: a person who could not be protected, a loyalty that required leaving, a duty that made a private wish impossible. Let that pressure appear in particular decisions — a restraint in a contest, an alliance kept or declined — and do not convert every attachment into loss or every obligation into regret. The narrator may reflect directly or let a detail carry the association; either way, remembered occasions belong to the focal character's access and are not known to others in the scene unless spoken. Advancement remains testable through what the character can now do for another person or under a standing obligation, and the account may close with the present task still in progress, its full meaning not yet settled.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalaq::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell the advancement through a situated teller: a particular voice addressing a particular audience — apprentices after a lesson, travelers sharing a road, a household at evening — who has chosen, received, or lived part of this account and now shapes it for these listeners. Open with the teller's framing of who the learner was and what stood before them, then deliver the progression as the tale's engine, in episodes ordered for this occasion. Let the frame return as a living part of the telling: a listener's question pulls an explanation forward, a rival version of an incident is mentioned and argued past, a detail is withheld this evening because the teller is holding the room's attention, and the withheld cause surfaces later inside the tale rather than vanishing from it.\n\nKeep the improvement recoverable inside the telling. The teller may compress years into a sentence, but the incidents that carry advancement show attempts, practice, feedback, resources, and changed capability: the season of failed executions, the correction that finally landed, the first time the principle held under pressure. Narrate both the moment the learner could explain the technique and still fail it and the later moment of performance, so understanding and execution stay distinct. When a listener asks how the improvement came, answer with a scene, not a summary.\n\nLet loyalties and obligations supply the tale's substance. The teller dwells on decisions — protecting someone at a cost, refusing a fight and paying for the refusal, a public duty that crossed a private attachment — so that technique, restraint, alliances, and reputation matter through what was actually chosen. When the tale praises a deed, let the audience or a later incident complicate the praise, keeping the account a living retelling in dispute rather than a settled legend. Draw the setting, language, and community from the supplied world and keep distinct traditions distinct where that world distinguishes them; antiquity, mysticism, exile, religion, or a single honor code are available materials, never universal requirements. Inside the tale, the focal character's private experience remains their own: the teller reports what was seen and said, and grants inward knowledge only when the telling has earned the right to it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalar::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Minimalist Prose\n\nCarry each stage of advancement in a few exact details. One changed grip, one corrected breath, one resource spent, one task finished in half the former time: the selected detail does the work a rank summary would do, and nothing restates it afterward. Write direct clauses, and keep only the explanation or inward connection that materially serves the present moment; when a thought must be long to make sense, let it run its full length. Arrange omissions so causality stays inferable — a missed season of practice shows up as a slower start and a blistered palm, not as announced backstory; a failure to apply a known principle appears in the action itself, and the next attempt reveals what changed, whether feedback, resource, or correction finally understood.\n\nSpend the economy where technique expresses a value. Loyalties arrive as particular decisions in a few clauses: a refusal to strike, a person steered out of the line, an alliance declined. Travel, explanation, and relationship keep their place, compressed to what changes the decision or the capability — the meal where the teacher says the one thing that unlocks the stance, the road conversation that names the obligation the learner will later honor. Action is never a bare sequence of blows; it is a short chain of precise movements whose restraint or release tells the reader who this person is becoming and what still resists them.\n\nPreserve individual voices. Minimalism does not flatten the cast into traded fragments: a teacher's circumlocution, a rival's taunt, a companion's practicality each keep their own length, and a precise extended sentence may serve better than several performed fragments. Advancement remains testable through what the character can now accomplish and what still resists, and the scene can end on the detail that implies the next attempt.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalas::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained, scene-first prose in which advancement surfaces through the ordinary movement of a developing life. Move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and whatever inner experience the active contract authorizes, and orient the reader with paragraphing and transitions — a change of room, a lapse of hours, a topic taken up again — rather than recap machinery. A practice session, a journey, a conversation with a teacher, a failed attempt in front of witnesses: each carries the progression by showing in scene what the character now understands, attempts, and can or cannot yet do, instead of summarizing it afterward.\n\nKeep improvement causally specific. Stage the gap between knowing and executing inside the scene — the character explains the principle correctly, then falters in performance; later, under pressure or with a new resource, the same principle becomes executable, and the narration marks what made the difference. Let strengths and weaknesses develop unevenly, one skill steadying while another regresses under a new demand, and let feedback arrive as dialogue, a corrected movement, or a visible consequence that changes what the character attempts next, so the flexible prose never dissolves the genre's engine into atmosphere.\n\nGive encounters their room. A journey includes practical talk, shared meals, disagreement about obligation, and private attachments that later shape public decisions. When a fight or contest occurs, narrate technique, restraint, and consequence so the reader understands who acted, why it mattered, and what it cost; reputation and alliances shift through particular choices, not statements about honor in general. The narrator's voice and the immediate scene set the register — contemporary presentation requires neither a contemporary setting, flat affect, realism as world physics, nor a closing lesson. A scene may close with a task still in progress or a relationship consequence still unsettled, provided the present moment has been fully inhabited.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalat::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship both the lens and the measure of the progression. Select scenes because they reveal, test, or change what these particular people are to each other, and let advancement become legible through what one can now do for, with, or because of the other: keep a promise that was once beyond their skill, return from a task that would earlier have ended them, protect without needing protection, or fail in a way that alters standing between them. Use only the supplied premise; its particular promise decides which moments of affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery receive space, never a roster of familiar tropes.\n\nBuild the shared history through private language and repeated moments. A phrase, gesture, correction, or meeting place returns with altered meaning as capability shifts: the instruction first given in frustration is offered calmly after months of practice; the drill that once shamed the slower partner becomes the ground they train on together. Let a callback fulfill the earlier moment — completing, reversing, or outgrowing it — rather than merely repeat it, and let the emotional payoff be earned by the scene's actual events rather than by timing or announcement.\n\nSet the stakes through the tension between private attachment and public obligation. The pair may disagree about a duty, conceal a worry to keep the other focused, or weigh loyalty to each other against an obligation to a larger group; equally, they may cooperate, train each other, and find the relationship itself is what advances them, since cooperation and genuine help are also choices. Technique, restraint, alliances, and reputation matter through particular decisions taken in front of each other. Beneath the emotional focus the progression stays causal — the reader still understands what was practiced, which feedback or resource changed the execution, and what remains difficult — and private language belongs to the two who established it, unreadable on demand to everyone else in the scene. A relationship consequence may stay unsettled after a practical question is answered, and the next scene can open inside that unfinished territory.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalau::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the advancement as a chronicle of deeds that will be remembered. Open deliberately — who the learner was when the account begins, what task or obligation stood before them — then expand each compact declaration into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. Relate every stage of improvement as an act with consequences: the first execution of a technique under pressure, the refusal that cost an alliance, the journey that changed what could be attempted. Behind each remembered deed keep the practice visible — the failed seasons, the teacher's feedback, the resource found or spent — so the chronicle's claims are shown true rather than merely asserted, and what remains difficult stays in the account alongside the praise. Give the tale both moments: the learner who understood the principle and could not yet perform it, and the later occasion when the same principle held.\n\nUse recurrence and names as the chronicle's connective tissue. A phrase, place, or obligation returns when a later occurrence gives it new application, and a recurring designation grows from an established deed or way of speaking rather than an ornamental epithet attached to every entrance. Relate each act to what will be remembered of it, and leave remembrance in dispute where it is: one hall praises the restraint, another counts the cost, and the telling need not settle every reputation at once. Dialogue may interrupt the chronicle's authority — the subject of an oft-told incident corrects a detail, or answers an elevated introduction with a practical concern — in each speaker's own voice rather than a ceremonial register.\n\nLet loyalties organize which deeds the chronicle keeps. Technique, restraint, alliances, and reputation matter through particular decisions — protecting someone, refusing a fight, choosing between private attachment and public duty — and action is never reduced to a bare sequence of blows. Draw names, customs, and world from the supplied setting; the telling requires no imported pantheon, obligatory doom, or an oath in every mouth. When the account records what the learner thought or feared, let it be actual remembered experience within the access the selected perspective allows, since being remembered as dependable does not mean the help was effortless. The account may end with business concluded or resumed, leaving another act available to be remembered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalav::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nTake the social-realist route with an inward register, and let the story's progress happen inside the institutions and households of the world as supplied — the workplace, the school, the family, the routines of care and provision. A gained capability first appears as a small altered function in a familiar routine: the apprentice reads the weather before the older worker points to it, the clerk recalls the regulation that changes the decision, the daughter manages the conversation her parent used to manage. Keep knowing and doing distinct — the person who can say what should be done may still not do it under pressure — and stage the attempt and its feedback in the same scene, with another person's adjusted reliance, a task handed over or a question asked instead of an instruction given, confirming what has changed while the character privately registers what still resists.\n\nGive the season, the housing, the commute, and the shared work their real weight, and keep the encounters generous: arrivals, meals, explanations, renewals of relationship around the task rather than a bare sequence of task results. Place the pull between private attachment and public obligation inside these networks, and let advancement cost something countable — the duty accepted at work is the family obligation missed; the friend protected at the price of a reputation the character never chose; the new responsibility that exposes an older, unexamined loyalty. Restraint, alliance, and standing operate through particular decisions about who is visited, who is telephoned, which account is corrected in front of whom. Do not import sects, martial lineages, or a historical Chinese setting.\n\nTie the inward narration to what the character can observe and remember. Reserve and understatement belong to particular people, not to a national temperament; snow, isolation, and bleakness are scenery only where the supplied world has them, and if that world is specifically Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish, keep its particulars distinct instead of blending a generic Nordic texture. Render uneven growth concretely — reliability in one area beside a failure just as specific, both measured by what the person can now accomplish unaided and what still requires help. A scene may close on a finished task, an obligation left open, or a quiet recognition of what has changed, with relationships, recovery, and ordinary work continuing around the developing life and no rank or displayed system to certify it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalaw::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Screenplay\n\nUse scene headings when location or time changes, and write action lines a reader can see and hear, with clear speaker cues. Make a gained capability a visible or audible difference in behavior between sequences: the movement that failed last time now completes, the character reads the room before anyone explains it, or withholds a response an earlier scene showed them giving too quickly. Put the attempt and its feedback in the same sequence — the try, then another character's adjusted reaction or request — so advancement is established by what plays on screen. A principle stated in one scene's dialogue is fulfilled or failed by action in a later one, and the sequence shows which; never announce a level or an understanding through voice-over.\n\nGive the sequences their scenic room: arrivals, exchanges of explanation, relationship beats around the practical work, rather than cutting blow to blow to result. Stage the pull between private attachment and public obligation as competing on-screen choices — the public task that cannot wait keeps the character from the family table; the alliance is declined because a private bond would pay for it — and let dialogue carry the particular decision. Technique, restraint, and reputation appear as behavior: who speaks first, who lets a silence stand, how a newcomer addresses the character now compared with before.\n\nUse cuts and offscreen sound only where the chosen sequence needs them. A cut may compress practice time or travel, but it cannot replace a specific attempt with a montage of improvement. Where the story requires private experience, write it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation, not dialogue the cast can hear and not a substitute for showing the change in action. Growth stays uneven and concrete: one task now handled alone beside another still fumbled in the same scene, the fumble as filmable as the success. The sequence can close on a completed action, a pending request, or a small acknowledgment of change rather than a summarizing speech or an obligatory hook.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPalalax::♢ * [STYLE] Progression Fantasy / Jin Yong / Stage Play\n\nTreat the shared stage space as the test of growth: what the cast can see, the audience can verify. The task attempted in an earlier scene is attempted again in view and now completes; the one who used to interrupt holds through a pause; the one who misread rooms enters at the right moment with the right object. Carry the attempt and its response in blocking and physical business — the hands that hesitate, the weight set down too soon, the exit a beat late — rather than in a speech about improvement. Let a character recite the method and still fumble its execution in front of everyone; the gap between saying and doing is playable in itself. When something remains difficult, stage the difficulty rather than summarizing it.\n\nShape the pull between private attachment and public obligation through entrances, exits, and the relation of lines to business. A character enters carrying an object that belongs to a private bond while a public duty calls; one exits toward the obligation while the person who pays its cost remains on stage. Give encounters their room — greeting, explanation, renewed relationship — so a meeting is an event with consequence, not a compressed exchange of outcomes. Technique, restraint, alliance, and reputation read as particular choices: who speaks first, who waits, who accepts the task, who declines it in front of witnesses.\n\nWrite monologue and aside only where they serve a dramatic purpose; they are performed speech with an audience, not the default container for thought. Keep required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation, out of the other characters' hearing, and let stage directions establish observable conditions without certifying what is hidden. Do not add film-camera instructions. Advancement stays uneven and embodied: one staged success beside one staged failure, both concrete. A scene can end on a completed action, an exit, a held pause, or a quiet acknowledgment of change — no curtain-line summary, no obligatory gathering.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
