            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-romance-02",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Romance · 02/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == romance}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Romance. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 2 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-romance; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPamadat::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Cormac McCarthy / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship premise legible through the scenes you choose. If the premise is established love, show the habits and accommodations that love has built. If it is slow development, show the particular moments that move the two people closer or hold them apart. If it is repair, show the work of return and the weight of what still needs mending. Do not announce the premise. Let the scene selection do the work: a shared task, a familiar argument, a private joke, a moment of care that only these two would recognize.\n\nUse private language and repeated moments as the relationship's callbacks. A phrase one of them said once and the other now returns to, a gesture that has become a signal, a place or object that belongs to their history—these recurrences carry the emotional payoff. Each return should add something new: a different context, a changed meaning, a deeper understanding. The payoff is the consequence of the premise, not a reward inserted to please the reader.\n\nKeep McCarthy's physical attention as the scene's texture. The relationship lives in concrete particulars: the way one person hands the other a cup, the space they leave between them on a bench, the work they do in the same room without speaking. Paratactic movement lets the scene accumulate without over-explaining. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, speech remains spare and practical, carrying what the characters can say to each other and what they cannot. The emotional content lives in the gap between the practical surface and the private meaning the scene has established.\n\nGive the relationship's particular emotions enough space where they belong. Affection may be shown through care rather than declaration. Jealousy may surface as a practical objection or a sharp remark. Comfort may be a hand on a shoulder, a blanket fetched, a silence kept. Rivalry may be a contest over work or attention. Discovery may be a new fact about the other person that changes the premise's terms. Use only the actual supplied premise and the particular people it involves. The scene may end with the relationship's promise fulfilled in a small way, or with its tension still present, or with a new consequence beginning to unfold. The reader should recognize that this moment belongs to these two people and could not belong to anyone else.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamadau::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Cormac McCarthy / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the relationship as an account of the deeds by which these two people will be remembered. Open with a deliberate declaration that establishes the telling's stance: who these two were to each other, what passed between them, what the account will relate. The declaration need not be grand. It may be as plain as saying they met in a bad year, or that she kept his promise longer than he did. Let the opening set the chronicle's terms, then move into the particulars that complicate and fill them.\n\nUse compact declarations to mark the relationship's turns. A journey undertaken, a gift given, a wrong done, a debt repaid, a word kept or broken—these are the acts the chronicle relates. After a declaration, expand into the concrete particulars: the weather on the road, the weight of the thing carried, what was said at the door, what work was left undone. Keep the scale human. The deeds are the ordinary acts by which people bind themselves to one another or fail to.\n\nLet recurring phrasing carry the sense of retelling. A phrase may return when a later occurrence gives it new application: the same road traveled again, the same promise spoken under different circumstances, the same object passed from hand to hand. The recurrence should deepen the account rather than decorate it. The chronicle may note what will be remembered of an act. Some acts are remembered only by the two people involved, and that is enough.\n\nKeep the chronicle's access consistent with the selected perspective. The teller may relate what was seen, heard, or later learned, but not the hidden minds of the lovers except through inference or later disclosure. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, speech remains spare and practical, carrying bargains, refusals, and withheld feeling. The account may end with a deed concluded or still in motion, leaving the relationship's final shape to be remembered rather than resolved. What matters is that the telling has made the particular history of these two people present and has shown what of it would be worth telling again.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamadav::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Cormac McCarthy / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the relationship through the material and institutional conditions that surround the characters: housing, work schedules, wages, weather, shared meals, maintenance, transport, and the small public obligations of family, neighbors, or employers. Choose a definite Scandinavian social-realist route: focus on daily domestic and work rhythms, the concrete costs and repairs of daily life, and the public expectations that shape private choices. If the story supplies a Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish context, use its actual institutions, names, domestic details, and seasonal routines. Otherwise keep the method social and concrete without inventing a national backdrop.\n\nLet attraction appear in what a person notices, repairs, carries, waits for, or remembers rather than in stated emotional summaries. Use McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs, paratactic movement, and spare practical speech. A question about a bus schedule, a rent payment, a broken window, or a shift change can also be a question about whether someone will stay, return, or accept help. Do not force every line into double meaning; let some exchanges remain plainly about work or weather so the charged ones stand out. Another character does not know a private conclusion merely because the reader has followed its formation.\n\nLet social and domestic realism mean that the relationship is tested or confirmed through ordinary obligations rather than isolated dramatic confessions. Show a disagreement about money, a missed appointment, a family demand, or a housing decision as it arises from a specific obligation. Then show the aftermath: a repair made or postponed, a debt acknowledged or ignored, a routine changed or restored. These material outcomes should carry relationship change without neat reconciliation or permanent rupture. Let the response end at an ordinary threshold—a task completed, a question left unanswered, a routine resumed—rather than a confession or a climax.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamadaw::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Cormac McCarthy / Screenplay\n\nWrite the relationship through what the camera can see and the microphone can hear: movement through rooms and landscapes, hands on tools and objects, food prepared or left uneaten, clothes worn or changed, distances kept or closed. Use concise action lines with concrete nouns and forceful verbs. Let a character's attention show in what they notice, reach for, repair, or avoid.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep dialogue spare and practical. A line about weather, work, money, a vehicle, or a schedule can carry a bargain, a warning, an offer, or a withheld motive. Do not force every exchange into subtext; let some lines remain plainly practical so the charged ones land. Use scene headings only when location or time changes, and use cuts, offscreen sound, or voice-over only when the chosen sequence needs them. Do not fill the page with camera commands.\n\nWhen required private experience belongs in the scene, render it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation, not as dialogue another character can hear and not as knowledge available to the cast. Distinguish what a character knows from what they say. A character can notice a gesture and draw no conclusion, draw a wrong conclusion, or understand something the other person has not admitted. The reader may know a motive while another character does not.\n\nLet the relationship develop through repeated visible actions and their interruptions. A person returns to a task, waits in a particular place, leaves a door open, or fails to appear. Show the aftermath of such moments in a later scene rather than explaining it in dialogue. A disagreement can remain unresolved; a request can go unanswered; a decision can be postponed. Stop at the natural scene boundary, not a completed beat or reversal. Preserve the active world and authorship contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamadax::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Cormac McCarthy / Stage Play\n\nWrite the relationship through playable physical business and speaker-led dialogue. Use clear entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Let a character's attention show in what they pick up, set down, hand over, or leave behind. A shared task can become a scene about trust or reluctance without either person announcing it.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep dialogue spare and practical. A line about a schedule, a payment, a repair, a meal, or a journey can carry a bargain, a conviction, or a withheld motive. Let some exchanges remain plainly about the task at hand so the charged ones stand out. Do not make every character witty or ready with polished banter. Let each person's speech fit their particular habits and history.\n\nUse stage directions economically to establish observable conditions: who can see an entrance, reach an object, hear a line, or move between two places. These conditions do not certify hidden feeling. A character can stand close without intimacy, speak softly without tenderness, or perform a helpful act without commitment. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene.\n\nWhen required private experience belongs in the scene, preserve it as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation. An aside or monologue is performed speech when separately authorized and must serve a dramatic purpose in the performance, such as an address to the audience that changes the public scene; it does not replace required private thought. Distinguish what a character knows from what they say or show.\n\nLet the relationship develop through repeated staged actions and their interruptions. A person returns to a room, waits, leaves a door open, or fails to appear. Show the aftermath in later scenes rather than explaining it in a speech. A disagreement can remain unresolved after the scene ends; a request can go unanswered; a decision can be postponed. Do not require every scene to end on a completed beat or a theatrical revelation. Preserve the active world and authorship contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaday::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Cormac McCarthy / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the relationship through one authorized mind's associative movement. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern so that the reader experiences the character's attention as it moves among present objects, past scenes, and imagined or feared futures. Anchor each drift in McCarthy's concrete physical register: a hand on a cup, a smell of rain, a door that needs oil, a worn tool, a task left unfinished, a phrase someone used in a particular room.\n\nLet syntax and paragraph shape follow the mind's movement, but keep enough orientation to distinguish what is happening now from what is remembered or imagined. A present event can trigger a memory that returns to the present with a changed perception. A recurring concern can surface during labor, travel, or conversation and then subside without being resolved. Do not let association blur scene facts; the reader should still know what occurred, what was said, and what the character only imagined.\n\nKeep the stream particular to the authorized viewpoint. Another character does not share the same mind. When the viewpoint character recalls something another person said, mark it as recollection rather than present speech. When the viewpoint character imagines a response, keep it distinct from what the other person actually says or does.\n\nLet the relationship develop through what the mind returns to and what it avoids. A person may notice a detail about the other and then move away from it, returning later with a different feeling. A disagreement may surface as a remembered phrase that interrupts an unrelated task. Show how attention, memory, and bodily sensation register care, uncertainty, or longing without requiring the character to announce a conclusion. The movement must be associative and particular, not ordinary reasoning laid out as a chain of explanations.\n\nDo not require every difficulty to produce a permanent rupture or a neat reconciliation. The response can end while the mind is still moving, with a perception, a memory, or an unfinished thought. Preserve the active world and authorship contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamadaz::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Cormac McCarthy / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the relationship through what each person chooses to send, delay, edit, or leave unsent. Use readable sender-attributed messages with only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one may write short practical lines, another may type and delete, another may leave a message unanswered for hours without meaning anything by it.\n\nKeep the transmitted text spare and practical, in McCarthy's economical register. A message about a schedule, a payment, a repair, a meal, or a journey can carry a bargain, a conviction, or a withheld motive. Do not force every message into double meaning; let some remain plainly about logistics so the charged ones stand out. A sent message is what the sender chose to transmit. A typed-then-deleted draft is an unsent artifact, not a live private thought, and must never appear inside the transmitted stream or be treated as if the other person received it.\n\nWhen required private experience belongs in the scene, render it as clearly separated context outside the transcript, not as transmitted text and not as knowledge available to the other person. Distinguish what a character knows or feels from what they type. A character can notice an implication, miss it, interpret it incorrectly, or respond at more than one conversational level. The reader may know a motive while the other person does not.\n\nLet the relationship develop through the exchange's actual rhythm: a question that goes unanswered, a reply that arrives late, a message sent and then followed by silence, a practical request that becomes a test of attention or care. Show the aftermath in later messages or in separated context. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext. A read receipt or typing indicator should appear only when it matters to what a character notices or decides.\n\nPreserve the active world and authorship contract. The transcript form does not make the cast universally witty or ready with polished banter. Let each person's messages fit their particular habits, obligations, and history. The response can end with a sent message, an unsent draft preserved as separated context, a pending reply, or a separated reflection, without requiring a completed beat or a reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamadba::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Cormac McCarthy / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the relationship through a rhythm of concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response suited to an illustrated scene. Use McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs in the scene-setting so the reader sees what the viewpoint character sees and handles: a room, a road, a tool, a meal, a piece of clothing, a change in light or weather. Let attention show in what the narration selects and lingers on.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep dialogue spare and practical. A line about work, money, weather, a schedule, or an object can carry a bargain, a conviction, or a withheld motive. Do not force every exchange into subtext; let some lines remain plainly practical so the charged ones stand out. Use background or expression cues as purposeful references that orient the illustrated moment. A changed expression may make the narration linger, but what a person says and does must carry the relationship work; a face alone never certifies that someone is lying, hiding feeling, or returning affection.\n\nLet focal narration or inner response carry the viewpoint character's actual private experience: what they notice, remember, want, fear, or decide. Distinguish what the viewpoint character knows from what the other person has said or shown. Another character does not know a private conclusion merely because the reader has followed its formation. Keep the inner response particular to the authorized mind.\n\nLet the relationship develop through repeated scenes whose familiar details acquire new significance. A person returns to a place, notices a change, or remembers an earlier moment. Repetition should deepen understanding rather than restart the same suspicion or unresolved feeling. Show the aftermath of a disagreement or a moment of care in later scenes rather than explaining it in dialogue.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands merely because the style is selected. Preserve the active world and authorship contract. The response can end after a scene, a line, or a focal reaction without requiring a completed beat or a reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamadbb::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Cormac McCarthy / Web Serial\n\nWrite the relationship as a readable through-line across installments. Keep what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue clear enough that a reader can re-enter without a full recap. Recover context briefly only when it helps. Use recurrence as the serial engine: an earlier object, place, phrase, or obligation can return with new significance because conditions around it have changed, not because the narrator announces a shift.\n\nUse McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs so those recurring elements stay materially legible. A person uses the tool previously left behind, returns to the place where an earlier conversation stopped, or answers a familiar question under a different condition. Let the local change be earned by what the installment actually shows. A repair made or postponed, a debt acknowledged or ignored, a routine changed or restored can carry relationship progress without a confession.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep dialogue spare and practical. A line about work, money, weather, a schedule, or an object can carry a bargain, a conviction, or a withheld motive. Let some exchanges remain plainly practical so the charged ones stand out. Do not make every character witty or ready with polished banter. Let each person's speech fit their particular habits and history.\n\nLet the relationship develop through attention, memory, proximity, disclosure, disagreement, care, and decisions about belonging together. Progress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources. A character can learn something about the other without immediately acting on it; a disagreement can remain unresolved across installments; a request can go unanswered. Do not require every installment to end on a completed beat or an artificial cliffhanger. An occasional hook is enough when the development supports it.\n\nPreserve the active world and authorship contract. The web serial form does not automatically create leveling systems, rankings, or progression-fantasy world rules. It does not make the cast universally witty or ready with polished banter. Let each installment stop according to the existing role and scene boundary, not a generic writing-form quota.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeaa::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nTreat the relationship's emotional stakes as a large claim that the world keeps failing to honor at the scale where the characters actually live. A confession of need can be interrupted by a billing dispute, a missed train, or an appliance that chooses this moment to stop working. Do not let the interruption cancel the feeling; let it sharpen the cost of trying to say something true while the practical world refuses to cooperate.\n\nWhen a character tries to explain what they want, let the explanation expose its own absurd consequence. A person who says they need space may discover that the only available space is a shared kitchen with a broken extractor fan. A person who says they want to be closer may be standing in a queue that will not move. The absurdity belongs to the situation, not to a detached comic narrator mocking the characters. Each person responds differently: one may push through the inconvenience, another may use it as an excuse to retreat, and a third may not notice it at all because they are preoccupied with a different hurt.\n\nDigressions are permitted, but they must return to the relationship problem with new pressure. A long account of why the landlord has not fixed the heating can end with the realization that one character has been sleeping in the cold room for weeks rather than ask the other for help. A digression about a misdelivered parcel can reveal that someone has been ordering things for a future they have not admitted they want. The return is the point; the digression is not a break from feeling but a route through it.\n\nLet strain affect the actual conversation. A character may say something generous while sounding exhausted, or something cruel while trying to be practical. Comfort may arrive as a cup of tea placed beside a pile of unopened mail, and it may not be acknowledged. Warmth can coexist with the knowledge that the same argument will happen again. Do not require the scene to end in repair, confession, or a clean resolution. The emotional turbulence is in the attempt to connect across the gap between what the characters mean and what their circumstances allow them to do.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeab::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the romantic scene as a shared interval in which the characters are trying to accomplish something ordinary while their attention keeps catching on each other. Use clear paragraphs and unmistakable dialogue attribution so the reader always knows who is speaking. The activity can be as small as assembling furniture, returning a faulty purchase, or trying to follow instructions that were clearly written for a different product. The activity is not a metaphor; it is the actual situation they are in.\n\nLet the procedure expose its absurd consequences. The instructions say to insert part A into slot B, but part A is the wrong shape, and the argument about whether to force it becomes an argument about which of them always assumes the other one is wrong. The customer service hold music plays while one character says something they have been meaning to say, and the other character has to decide whether to respond or to take the call when the music finally stops. Different people respond differently: one may find the absurdity funny, another may find it exhausting, and a third may be too focused on the task to notice that anything else is happening.\n\nKeep the romance in the attention and proximity rather than in declarations. A character may notice that the other person always reads the instructions twice before admitting they do not understand them. A character may stand close enough that the other person can smell the paint on their sleeve. A disagreement about how to hold a shelf level can become a disagreement about who trusts whose judgment, and the aftermath can be a quiet moment of one person handing the other the correct tool without being asked.\n\nEnd at a natural continuation point that follows from the scene. The shelf may still be half-assembled, the call may still be on hold, or the characters may have just realized that they have been standing in the same room for an hour without finishing the task. The continuation is the unresolved practical situation and the emotional attention still moving between them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeac::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and satirical realism route. Situate the relationship within a specific community whose expectations, obligations, and practical arrangements are treated as the apparently reasonable premise. The absurd consequences arise when the couple tries to follow those arrangements and discovers that the arrangements do not actually fit the people involved.\n\nLet a social procedure expose its own contradictions. A family expects the couple to observe a particular sequence of formal steps before their relationship is recognized, but each step requires a document that the previous step was supposed to produce. A well-meaning relative offers advice that is perfectly logical and completely inapplicable to the actual situation. The satire is directed at the procedure, not at the community as a whole, and not at the characters for failing to manage it. Different people respond differently: one character may try to satisfy every requirement, another may quietly refuse to participate, and a third may not understand why the requirements exist at all.\n\nKeep the romance in the particular attention the characters pay to each other while the social machinery grinds on. One character may remember exactly how the other takes their tea and prepare it without being asked during a long family negotiation. One character may defend the other against a relative's criticism and then be unable to say why they did it when they are alone. A disagreement about whether to comply with a demand can become a disagreement about what they are willing to sacrifice for each other, and the aftermath can be a shared meal eaten in silence because neither of them knows what to say next.\n\nLet digressions into family history, local custom, or the logistics of a gathering return to the current relationship problem. A story about an aunt's disastrous engagement can turn out to be a warning that one character takes seriously and the other dismisses. A long account of why the banquet hall is double-booked can end with the couple sitting on the kitchen floor eating the food that was meant for the guests, and the conversation that happens there is the real scene. The meaningful aftermath is the change in how the characters understand what they are willing to do for each other.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaead::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the relationship through what the characters handle, repair, break, and carry. Use forceful diction and compressed transitions. A scene can move from a door that will not latch to a conversation about who is leaving without a paragraph of setup. The physical world is not decoration; it is where the relationship happens.\n\nLet practical procedures expose their absurd consequences in material terms. The radiator that was supposed to be fixed last month is still cold, and the character who said they would fix it is standing in front of it with a wrench they do not know how to use. The other character says nothing and goes to boil water. The absurdity is in the gap between the claim and the object, and the emotional stakes are in who has to live with the cold. Different people respond differently: one may attack the problem with tools, another may ignore it, and a third may use it as proof that nothing in the relationship ever gets finished.\n\nKeep attention and care concrete. A character may notice that the other person always leaves the good mug for them. A character may remember the exact sound of the other person's key in the lock. A disagreement may be carried out through the way one person stacks dishes or the way another person folds a receipt. When a longer thought is needed, let the character's reasoning run: they may work through why a broken hinge matters more than it should, or why they cannot bring themselves to ask for help with something they could easily do alone.\n\nVary the rhythm. Short sentences can land a realization. A longer passage can follow a character through the steps of a task while their attention keeps returning to the other person. The warmth in this style is a cup held out without comment, a coat hung where the other person can reach it, a hand on a shoulder that does not say anything. The relationship is in the wear.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeae::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the relationship through documents, each with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. The documents are not neutral records; they are attempts by the characters to explain, manage, request, or avoid something in the relationship. A message apologizing for missing dinner may be written while the author is still standing in the place where dinner was supposed to happen. A list of reasons to stay may be drafted and then never sent.\n\nLet the document form expose the absurd consequences of trying to manage feeling through procedure. A character writes a carefully numbered list of issues to discuss, and the list itself becomes the problem because the other person reads it as an accusation. A shared calendar meant to coordinate schedules fills with entries that contradict each other. A formal complaint about a neighbor's noise turns out to be the only place where one character admits they cannot sleep because the other person is not there. The absurdity is in the gap between the document's stated purpose and what it actually reveals.\n\nWhat each document knows or conceals follows its author's situation. A letter may omit the one fact that matters because the author does not know it, or because they know it and cannot bring themselves to write it down. A journal entry may record an event accurately and still misunderstand what the other person meant. A report may be perfectly factual and completely useless for understanding why the relationship is changing. Gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts are part of the story. Let the next document complicate or correct the previous one, and let the reader's knowledge outstrip any single author's.\n\nUse the specific document form the scene requires rather than rotating through formats. A text message can carry an argument in fragments. A receipt can mark a shared purchase that one character kept. A note left on a kitchen counter can say more than a long letter. The romance is in the attention the documents show: what the author remembers to include, what they choose to leave out, and what they cannot stop themselves from adding at the end.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeaf::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend attention on the particular ways these characters are kind to each other. The warmth is in chosen details: one person knows how the other takes their tea, remembers which chair they prefer, or saves the last of something without being asked. The characters can disagree about how to load the dishwasher, whether a map is upside down, or which route is actually faster, and the disagreement can be sincere without becoming a crisis.\n\nLet small practical absurdities provide the friction. The bakery is out of the one thing they came for, and the detour to another bakery turns into an hour-long walk that neither of them minds. The instructions for the new coffee machine are printed in a language neither of them reads, and they figure it out together by pressing buttons and laughing at the results. The absurdity is gentle and shared; it does not mock the characters or punish their affection. Different people respond differently: one may find the detour delightful, another may be mildly annoyed, and a third may be too busy noticing the other person's expression to care about the delay.\n\nLet affection be sincere without requiring a confession or emotional repair. A character may show love by fixing a wobbly table leg before the other person notices it. A character may say something kind in an offhand way and then change the subject because they are not ready to make a speech about it. Established problems from the world remain part of the setting, but this scene does not have to resolve them. The comfort is in the room they make for each other right now.\n\nKeep privacy and personality intact. One character may need quiet after a long day, and the other may know to sit nearby without talking. A familiar preference can be honored without being announced. The scene can end in ease: a shared meal, a finished puzzle, a blanket draped over someone who has fallen asleep on the couch. The romance is in the accumulated small attentions that show these people know each other.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeag::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Formal Literary Novel\n\nFrame the relationship through the social context in which it is trying to exist. Use deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. A scene may open with the history of the house they are standing in, the expectations of the people who invited them, or the public version of the relationship that both characters have learned to perform. The narration is composed, not conversational, and it may judge or contextualize where the chosen narrator permits it.\n\nLet the social forms expose their absurd consequences when applied to the actual people involved. The couple is expected to host a dinner according to a set of rules that everyone pretends to understand, and the evening becomes a study in the gap between the form and the reality: the seating arrangement separates the two people who most need to speak to each other, the toast celebrates a future that neither of them has agreed to, and the polite conversation keeps circling a question that no one is allowed to ask. The absurdity is not a joke inserted into the scene; it is the structure of the occasion itself.\n\nGive the private feeling room to develop alongside the social observation. A long paragraph may follow a character through the reception rooms while their attention returns again and again to the other person's face across the crowd. The narrator may observe that the character has been in love for longer than they have admitted, or that the other person's composure is a recent and fragile achievement. The romance is in the particular attention the narration grants to memory, proximity, and the decisions the characters are avoiding or approaching.\n\nUse public and private contrast where it matters. A conversation held in a hallway between courses can carry more weight than the entire dinner. A character may say something perfectly appropriate in public and then stand alone by a window, unable to explain why the appropriate thing felt like a betrayal. The meaningful aftermath may be a silence in the car afterward, a letter that is begun and abandoned, or a decision that one character makes without telling the other. The formal architecture holds the scene; the feeling remains unsettled.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeah::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the relationship as a live telling between two people who both have a stake in the outcome. Open inside the present scene: one of them is trying to persuade, explain, delay, apologize, or test how much patience remains, and the other is listening under actual time pressure. The telling must be an action in this relationship, not an illustration of a past that has already been settled.\n\nInside the nested story, use concrete scenes with dialogue and observed action rather than summary. The teller selects and shapes events to produce an effect—a changed memory, a softened blame, a request the listener is not yet ready to hear—and that shaping is part of the evidence. When the narrator of an embedded scene begins with a large claim, such as a perfect plan or an uncomplicated intention, bring in the mundane detail that undoes it: the missed bus, the unread message, the person who arrives too early. Let the absurdity land through accumulated specifics, not through sarcastic commentary.\n\nMake the frame respond to the tale at intervals. The listener can interrupt with a question, correct a detail, laugh at the wrong point, or lapse into silence. Use those interruptions to change the teller's pacing, emphasis, or choice of what to reveal next. Recurrence should accrue meaning: a particular object, phrase, or excuse can return inside the narrative and then appear in the live frame, so the reader sees the listener learning to interpret it. A repeated phrase is not a guarantee of truth; it shows what the teller is handling with care.\n\nKeep the two levels distinct. The embedded story may delay a decision or confession, but it must feel urgent in the present. Do not let the telling drift into general reflection or world-building. Whatever is narrated must return to the unresolved question between the two people: whether one will stay, whether the other can believe them, what was actually said on a difficult night. The relationship develops through the listener's reaction and subsequent choice, not through the past story's emotional weight alone. End when the live exchange has moved—a new question, an abrupt silence, a decision postponed for a different reason—not with a completed moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeai::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the social-realism route and stay there throughout the scene. Write a calm, unobtrusive third-person narrator with precise subordinate clauses that set emotion inside practical circumstance: a delayed train changes what can be said, a landlord's notice changes who is brave enough to ask a question. Do not decorate the setting with national or regional clichés; use the actual names, jobs, housing, family arrangements, and language rhythms present in the supplied world.\n\nLet the pair read each other through behavior under obligation. Attraction appears not as a sudden insight but as accumulated recognition: one person notices that the other remembered a deadline, waited without complaint, argued honestly about money, or refused to turn a favor into leverage. Trust is not a single confession but a gradual correction of mistaken inferences, each tied to a specific event. Different people handle pressure differently; one becomes practical, one becomes evasive, one asks for help too late. Preserve that difference rather than making them into quotable personalities.\n\nUse Adams-style collisions as domestic and bureaucratic precision. A character says the matter will be simple, then the bank's online portal closes at an unadvertised hour, the shared calendar has two different addresses, or the promised lift depends on a car that has already been borrowed. When one character tries to reassure the other with a confident summary, let the follow-up expose what the summary left out. Keep the narrator matter-of-fact; let the accumulating details produce the comedy, not a jokey aside. Allow the absurdity to be uncomfortable as often as it is funny, since it is happening to people with limited time and money.\n\nReturn every digression to the present relational problem. A memory of a failed flat-share can surface when the couple must decide whether to sign a lease together; a long explanation about an old family debt can be the reason one person resists borrowing. Recurring images should carry changed meaning: the same unread document, the same unanswered question about a shift swap, the same phrase said lightly and then heard seriously. Show the characters' private reasoning where the narrator's access and the contract permit it, including the gap between what they say and what they actually fear, want, or understand. End at a realized present choice, an unfinished disagreement, or a small decision that shifts what they can ask of each other.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeaj::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Gothic-Punk\n\nCreate the romance inside a collision between inherited claims and present material life. The scene should be physically exact: a building with outdated locks and laminated instructions, a shared office above a shop that no longer matches its sign, a family arrangement that survives only through unread paperwork and old keys. Let the setting enter the characters' perception, not as decoration but as something they must work around—cold rooms, unreliable heating, a door that sticks, an address that appears in two different records.\n\nMake intimacy morally and practically complicated. Both characters should have reasons to want closeness and reasons to fear what closeness will cost. One may protect the other in private while remaining formal where they are watched; one may resent being needed while continuing to show up. Show these contradictions through small, concrete choices: who pays, who waits outside, who reads the notice twice, who volunteers to phone the office that never answers. Do not make family or institutional authority a uniform enemy; some characters cooperate, some misunderstand, some genuinely help, and the couple's decisions about them reveal what the relationship means.\n\nUse Douglas Adams's absurdity as the slow reveal inside ordinary systems. A rule that everyone treats as important may depend on a form no one has seen, a department that was merged, or a deadline based on a calendar error. An explanation offered with confidence leads to a small practical consequence—an appointment that overlaps another, an account with the wrong surname, a key that does not fit the lock it was labeled for. Present these moments objectively; let one character find them comic, another frightening, another merely inconvenient. The comedy should come from the institutional and material precision, not from one-liners.\n\nMake disclosure recursive and grounded in objects. The same coat, phone number, photograph, or door can return as the couple learns more about each other. A private confidence should change how one of them reads a previously ordinary detail. The narrator may show a character noticing what they previously ignored or realizing that a familiar gesture has a history. Keep actual nonspoken interiority available where the contract permits, including thoughts that differ from speech. End at a present, emotionally legible moment: a decision made, an argument stopped short, a small act of care accepted after being refused many times. Do not force a reunion, a break-up, or a tidy restoration.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeak::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nSelect the modern urban Indian fiction route. Keep the story in a particular city, neighborhood, workplace, or community supplied by the world, and narrate in the third person with direct sentences that occasionally extend to follow a character through a crowded street, a long phone call, or a delayed decision. Preserve the actual language, food, transport, housing, and family arrangements of that setting without importing generic markers. Use English or another supplied narrative language according to the story, not as a decorative translation of an imagined \"Indian speech.\"\n\nBuild the romance out of contested time and attention. Both characters have obligations that are not excuses but real limits: a relative's arrival, a workplace deadline, a shared lease, a festival, a bus route that stops early. Show what each chooses to rearrange and what they refuse to rearrange. A missed meeting is not automatically a rejection; its handling—whether someone calls, waits, apologizes, or pretends the plan was never firm—reveals how much room the other person actually holds. Attraction surfaces as precise recognition: one notices that the other remembered a food allergy, asked about a sick relative, or argued honestly about a deadline instead of making a vague promise.\n\nUse Adams-style absurdity through infrastructure and procedure. When a character says something will take twenty minutes, follow the actual sequence: the app shows one route, the driver will not go beyond the flyover, the office window closes before the stated time, the documents are at another branch. Let one confident explanation create a further complication: a shared address that is not really shared, an old phone number that now belongs to someone else, a family obligation that turns out to involve two different expectations. Narrate the accumulating detail with cool precision rather than mockery. Different characters may respond with irritation, patience, laughter, or quiet calculation; do not give everyone the same wit.\n\nLet digressions earn their return through the current relationship. A long story about a cousin's wedding may end in the detail that decides whether the couple can attend together. A side trip to exchange a faulty charger may reveal how often one person has been repairing the other's oversights. Recurrence should be material and meaningful: the same train platform, the same borrowed umbrella, the same food container returned late, the same phrase said in jest and later heard as a boundary. Keep the narrator focused on what the couple is learning about each other. End at a point where something has shifted in the present: a date is finally fixed, an apology is accepted or refused, a long-held assumption is corrected, or a new complication becomes visible. Do not close with a moral summary or a forced reunion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeal::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nSelect the social-psychological realism route. Use a composed third-person narrator limited to the focal character or, where the contract permits, two focal characters, and let feeling emerge through exact perception: the position of a seat, the temperature of a cup, the lag before a reply, the change in a train announcement. Keep sentences largely clause-bound: a main action, then a subordinate clause specifying perception or social consequence. Do not introduce seasonal imagery unless the scene's actual date and place support it, and do not force quietness onto characters who are angry, embarrassed, or in a hurry.\n\nDevelop the relationship through specific misreadings. Each wrong inference should have a discoverable social cause: a workplace rule about phones, a family obligation that cannot be explained over text, a previous let-down that makes one person interpret silence as refusal. Show the misreading in a scene, not merely state it. One character hears a practical question as reluctance; another accepts a polite phrase as commitment. Let the focal character's memory, hunger, fatigue, or distraction shape what they notice and what they miss. Attraction accumulates through small observances—what is remembered, waited for, noticed about an allergy or a dislike—rather than through a single dramatic realization.\n\nUse Adams-style absurdity in procedural and social form. A simple plan fails because the reservation is at the wrong branch, the form requires a stamp from an office that closes early, or the polite phrase had two meanings that the speakers did not share. Follow one failure into its practical and emotional consequences before starting another. Let different characters respond differently: one becomes embarrassed, one pedantic, one quietly fixes the problem, one laughs only after the tension passes. Keep the narration cool enough that the comedy comes from the gap between intention and execution, not from a narrator's jokes.\n\nMake digressions return through a particular person. A memory of a childhood neighborhood may explain why the character now avoids being introduced. A long workplace anecdote may conclude with the exact assumption that must be corrected before the next meeting. Recurrence should be quiet and cumulative: the same convenience-store alarm, the same unfinished sentence, the same unread notification, the same question that neither asks again. Use recurring details to show learning, so that a later return carries a changed interpretation. End at a shift in the present encounter: one character says the thing they had been rehearsing, recognizes that the other has stopped waiting, or lets a false assumption become visible. Do not resolve all affection into a single outcome or closing confession.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeam::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nSelect the intimate urban psychological route and keep it consistent. Use a tightly focalized third-person narrator inside one character's perceptions, with sentences that gather sensory material before admitting the emotional point: the glint on a wet kerb, the voice from a neighboring window, the smell of cooking, the cracked tile, and only then the memory or desire the character has been postponing. Make the style circular, not ornamental; every sensory sentence should belong to this character's actual history. If the story's community has a specific language, music, or family idiom, use those as supplied. Do not create generic \"Latin American\" color or add supernatural events.\n\nBuild the relationship through the focal character's memory colliding with present pressure. The other person carries a practical burden the narrator gradually perceives—a job, a child, an eviction, a visa appointment, a family errand—while the focal character wrestles with a private promise, an old failure, or a half-remembered warning. Attraction appears as exact physical and social memory: how a hand hesitates near a cup, the smell of a particular café on someone's clothes, the sound of a name said carelessly. Disclosure is earned by shared events and interruptions, not by a sudden need to confess. Let the focal character sometimes misinterpret and then correct the interpretation later when the other person acts.\n\nUse Adams-style absurdity as urban and administrative machinery. A passionate decision to meet at a famous corner runs into the closed metro line, the changed street name, the bus that only takes exact change, the friend who promised to keep a key and has gone to a funeral. When a character explains a feeling as if it were a simple procedure, follow the procedure until it breaks: the plan to stay together may depend on a lease neither has read, the promise to call may depend on a contact saved under two different names. Keep the narrator steady while the facts compound. The comic effect comes from precision, not from mockery of the lovers; one may laugh, one may become furious, one may treat the failure as fate.\n\nLet digressions weave back through association. A paragraph about a street may end in the reason the character no longer walks there. A remembered argument may explain why the current silence feels familiar. Repetition should be musical and revisable: the same phrase returns when the character is about to leave, but its meaning changes as the couple learns each other's practical lives. The narrator may show the focal character noticing when a repeated object or gesture no longer belongs only to the past. End at a moment of genuine movement in the present: an invitation accepted, a mistaken assumption corrected, a small promise recognized as serious, or a line the character finally says without first explaining it away. Do not force a melodramatic reunion or a perfect insight.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaean::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Light Novel\n\nWrite in short, visually continuous paragraphs. Open each scene with a concrete location the reader can hold—a station exit, a rooftop stairwell, a convenience-store queue, a shared kitchen—and move from action to dialogue to private response with clean paragraph breaks. When a new place or person appears, give one identifying detail before dialogue starts: the color of a bag, the sound of an automatic door, a name badge. Do not use a separate narrator to explain what the reader can see.\n\nAfter a significant line or touch, cut to a brief private interval in the focal character's mind. Do not merely restate the line. Show the character noticing a specific sensation, replaying a fragment of wording, realizing what they did not say, or noticing that the room has changed. Vary the shape of these intervals by character: one person becomes sharply practical, another embarrassed, another angry, another amused only after the moment passes. The beat can be as short as one sentence, but it must render actual nonspoken experience, not a summary like \"she felt nervous.\"\n\nUse Adams-style absurdity through environmental and logistical precision. The confession happens beside a vending machine that rejects the only coin the character has. A perfectly planned meeting depends on a phone battery, a bus that arrives early, and a wrong contact saved under a similar name. The affectionate note is written on a receipt no one keeps. Follow one such detail into its consequence—the character misses the train, waits at the wrong exit, discovers the message too late—and then return to the relationship question. The comedy should come from the facts, not from wit. Different characters may find the same event funny, annoying, or unimportant.\n\nLet dialogue carry misapprehension cleanly. A line intended as comfort can sound like dismissal; a practical question can be heard as reluctance; an apology can be mistaken for sarcasm. Let the listener respond to the heard meaning, and let the misunderstanding continue until a concrete event corrects it: a repeated phrase, a changed address, an object returned, a message finally seen. Use callbacks sparingly, returning to the same bench, umbrella, or unfinished meal only when the earlier context adds new significance. End at a readable continuation point—someone has just asked a question, produced a key, stepped closer, or noticed the other's expression change—but do not force a cliffhanger if the scene has reached a natural pause. Keep the relationship's future contingent on the characters' actual choices, not on a required confession or break-up.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeao::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the relationship's turning points as if each ordinary act carries an unexpected gravity, then undercut that gravity with the practical inconvenience of the act itself. Build a paragraph's rhythm so a repeated sound, phrase, or image returns at the moment attention shifts, linking a glance, a remembered sentence, or a delayed admission to something in the room. Let a comparison change what the viewpoint understands: love compared to a long-distance message can become less about the message and more about the unread notification on a phone held in the wrong pocket.\n\nAnchor imagery in observable domestic or urban details rather than free-floating sentiment. A sustained metaphor about tides is fine only if the scene contains water, a map, a calendar, or some traceable source. When a metaphor risks aggrandizing a small event, let the plain register of someone asking for the salt interrupt the rhythm, then permit the lyrical movement to recover with a changed emphasis. Characters need not speak figuratively; the narrator's style is not a universal voice.\n\nPreserve romance beats through concrete relationship material: what someone remembers about the other, what they choose to disclose, what they notice before they speak, how they negotiate proximity and uncertainty. Douglas Adams's absurdity emerges from procedure: a lovingly planned evening collapses because the oven clock is wrong, or a declaration of devotion is postponed by an argument about whose responsibility it was to book the table. The absurd consequence matters, but different participants react differently, and a comic interruption does not erase the emotional stake. The scene may end mid-recovery, with the real question still open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeap::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet a small change in a familiar routine carry the awareness of passing time: a café stops serving the tea she once ordered, or a route home is rerouted and one remembered conversation loses its usual landmark. Pair that change with a present, practical inconvenience so the reflection does not become a single tone—he may stand at the new bus stop, remembering his ex, while his phone's autocorrect changes her name to something absurd and he has to decide whether to fix it.\n\nUse memory and return selectively rather than declaring every object a relic. A single recurring image can gather new meaning when a later occasion revises its earlier interpretation. Show what the viewpoint now understands differently without announcing that everything is loss; a remembered break-up can coexist with present laughter about a mislabeled delivery.\n\nRomance material remains concrete: the particular way someone used to wait, the unreturned key, the voicemail left at the wrong number, the decision not to cancel a subscription because it feels like contact. Douglas Adams's method appears in the procedural failure of a gesture meant to be meaningful—a carefully composed message sent to the wrong person, or a reunion delayed by an airport's new luggage system. The absurd consequence is real and emotionally specific. Keep the narrator's reflective access consistent: he may compare past and present, but he does not know what the other person privately felt then. End where the present attention naturally pauses, whether the feeling sharpens, eases, or remains unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeaq::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell the romance as a framed story: a present scene establishes a narrator and a listener, and the narrator relays how a relationship began, failed, or persisted. The present frame should require the telling for a concrete reason—a long drive, a power outage, a wedding speech being drafted, or some other occasion where the story is wanted or needed. The listener may interrupt with practical objections, but the interruptions must matter to the account, not merely decorate it.\n\nInside the frame, use Douglas Adams's digressive method. The narrator begins a romantic episode with a grand claim about trust or destiny, then attaches it to the ordinary system that complicated it: the visa application that expired before the declaration, the family dinner where the seating chart placed the couple at the same table as a very vocal cousin, or the voice message that auto-corrected an endearment into a bank notification. Digressions return through the current listener's question, not through cosmic coincidence. If the narrator explains neighborhood history or local bureaucracy, that segment must change what the listener understands about the romance.\n\nKeep the cultural setting and language supplied by the actual story; do not invent a generic bazaar, honor code, or mystical atmosphere. The telling belongs to a particular narrator, and the relationship remains in the listening present: as the narrator recounts a missed connection, he may notice the listener reacting in a way that revises the present relationship. The story can end with the frame's small obligation—coffee to be made, a call arriving, a nephew asking for the next part—leaving the couple's meaning still under negotiation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaear::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Minimalist Prose\n\nMove from one exact detail to the next. When the narrator feels something large, compress the explanation to the shortest sentence that retains causality. Example: \"He had practiced the sentence twice. The train announcer changed the platform, and he said the wrong name.\" The emotional moment is present, the absurdity is present, and no commentary tells the reader how to feel.\n\nLet one observational detail do the work of affection or doubt. The way a lover hands over a charger, the pause before a yes, the person texting from the wrong side of the bed. Prefer an action or utterance that changes the relationship's available meanings over a list of private impressions. If a broad Adams-style claim appears (\"love, he told himself, was a very efficient system of transportation\"), follow it with one concrete logistical failure or one unexpected success. Let different characters respond differently: one can ignore a mishap, another can treat it as final proof.\n\nDialogue is precise. Keep exchanges short enough to show disagreement or care without debate transcripts. A break-up need not be narrated as a storm; a single object returned by the door can carry the consequence. If the scene requires an internal explanation, keep it to an essential inference: the narrator realizes she wasn't looking at him, she was looking past him for the bus. No summary of the whole relationship at every beat. End after one further small act or observation, leaving what comes next inferable rather than spelled out.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeas::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the relationship through continuous scenes. Begin in a physical and social here: a flat, a workplace, a queue, a delayed train, a family call in another room. Move between the focal character's observation of the lover, the exact exchange, a brief remembered moment, and a practical task. Use paragraph breaks to change the plane of attention without announcing the shift.\n\nLet Douglas Adams's large-versus-small structure live inside the scene's systems. A character promises, over breakfast, that tonight they will finally say everything they should have said. Then the evening's systems intervene: the restaurant reservation was made under the name of a former partner, the coat check ticket dissolves in the rain, the motorway signs point to the wrong town. The narrator does not explain the cosmic irony. The prose reports where they stand, what they say, what the other person does with the inconvenience, and what inner reasoning accompanies the pause. Different characters respond differently: one may see the delay as a sign, the other as simply bad planning, and the disagreement returns to their specific affection rather than a universal comedic script.\n\nUse recollections only when the present scene needs them: a phrase said earlier explains a hesitation now; a previous argument clarifies a current silence. Authorized interiority is normal private novel prose. It can be extensive while both characters are in the room, or brief while one waits for a reply. Do not impose understated emotion as a style requirement. The scene may end on an unfinished exchange, a practical interruption, or a sentence left hanging in the middle of a thought. What is legible is the specific relationship state created by the day's procedures.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeat::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nUse the established relationship promise as the scene's engine. If the premise is former rivals now sharing a flat, every choice—affection, jealousy, comfort, discovery, care—should be made concrete in a small exchange that draws on their particular history: the phrase one used to mock, the shared habit of leaving notes, the argument about whose turn it is to buy milk. The payoff must arise from that specific history, not from an imported list of tropes such as only one bed, arranged marriage, or seasonal festival.\n\nMake one repeated moment or private language carry emotional weight. It may be a phrase, a gesture, or the way one person texts. Use that repetition only when the scene genuinely needs it: when trust is tested, when a memory revises an old hurt, when someone surprises the other by remembering. The callbacks should deepen what the relationship has already built rather than restart the same misunderstanding.\n\nDouglas Adams's absurdity enters through the procedure surrounding a romantic payoff. The couple finally decide to discuss whether they are together. They plan a calm conversation. The landlord's maintenance visit, a misdelivered package containing a wedding album meant for a different address, and an internet outage with a help desk call that lasts forty minutes complicate the plan. The relationship feeling remains present in how they handle each interruption: one partner starts making tea during the hold music, the other sharpens a pencil with unnecessary concentration. Affection and anxiety are shown in small actions. The scene may finish while the emotional promise is still unspoken but legibly advanced—perhaps one says the wrong thing, or the right thing in a low voice as the doorbell rings again. Do not force a conclusion where every pay-off resolves in this scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeau::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account a sense of retelling. Open with a compact declaration that establishes the relationship as something the teller will relate, such as a line about how they met or what remained after a particular argument. Use recurring phrasing for a small set of acts or qualities attached to one person, but let the recurrence change application each time. One lover might be remembered as the one who always left early; that phrase is first a complaint, then a retort during a reconciliation, then a detail no longer true.\n\nTreat a romantic event as a deed with practical texture. The night someone crossed the city to apologize is not only the journey's intention but the bus breakdown, the wrong station, the receipt for a coffee bought while waiting, and the fact that the apology arrived after the other person had already explained the problem to a customer service representative. Douglas Adams's procedural absurdity belongs to the teller's account of what actually happened, not to sarcastic narration over the characters. Where the community later retells the event, vary the report: one listener remembers the broken bus, another insists the apology was sent before the rain started.\n\nLet the public telling and the focal character's private account diverge. A grand deed of devotion may be remembered by others as an awkward moment; the private version includes the fear or the reason for the delay. Show the couple themselves correcting the story only when a detail matters to their present feelings—one may ask, \"Was that before or after you lost the keys?\" The answer revises how they both remember what happened. Keep every speaker's diction consistent with the selected saga source tradition; do not force ceremonial oaths into ordinary dialogue. End with a compact statement of where the couple or the teller now stands, leaving the next act still available to be remembered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeav::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the relationship as an ongoing social and domestic reality. Each scene should be grounded in a particular household, workplace, or communal routine—a shared meal, a bus ride, a laundry schedule, a municipal form, a family visit—so that the characters' attraction and care become visible through small observable behaviors and everyday decisions. Keep local conventions distinct and concrete: if the setting is Danish, show the specific address forms, food arrangements, transport habits, or institutional details that shape the scene; if Norwegian or Swedish, change those details accordingly, but never let the setting become a personality or a list of regional traits.\n\nUse the Adams contrast between large claims and small logistical problems as a structural principle. When one character voices a grand feeling, a plan, or a general principle—\"I could live anywhere with you,\" \"Let's leave together in the spring,\" \"People should always be honest\"—let the next composed beat test that claim against a concrete domestic obstacle such as a missed bus, a burnt dish, a form that must be filed in triplicate, a door that will not close, or a neighbour who arrives at the wrong moment. The obstacle should not merely interrupt; it should expose a real difference in temperament, priority, or obligation. Let one partner respond seriously and the other find the mismatch absurd, or let both be caught in the same inconvenience with distinct practical styles. The comedy should arise from the situation's logistical friction, not from a uniform witty narrator; characters may be quiet, angry, resigned, or amused, and their responses should feel their own.\n\nBuild romantic attention through precise memory and return. The focal character may notice how the other eats, closes a cupboard, speaks to a cashier, folds a coat, or reacts to a two-minute delay. Later, when the relationship has shifted, bring back one of those observed domestic details with new weight: a habit that irritated is now a sign of care, a remembered joke is replayed during a difficult morning, a cupboard already fixed becomes a silent promise. Disagreement should develop from an actual difference in duty or preference—one wants to stay late, the other must pick up a child; one saves for a deposit, the other spends on train tickets—and its aftermath should be specific: a repaired meal, a period of cooler speech, a reconsidered schedule. Let reconciliation stay conditional; a couple may agree on the practical fix but not yet on the feeling behind it.\n\nKeep inward experience as plain private narrative whenever it matters. The narrator may be irritated by a partner's habit and still recognise a reason to stay; may imagine walking out and then choose to set the table; may notice a refusal without announcing it. Other characters do not know that thought unless it becomes speech or action. Use the narrative's ordinary domestic compression to move between days and weeks, but stop within a scene when the current practical or emotional turn has been registered. Do not force a romantic climax or a comic beat at every pause. Let the relationship's logic determine the rhythm: some conversations resolve a household problem, some merely reveal it, and some leave the two people standing in a kitchen with the kettle just beginning to boil.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeaw::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Screenplay\n\nWrite the relationship in screenplay format. Use scene headings whenever the location or time changes, keeping them simple and informative. Write action lines in concise, present-tense blocks that show visible and audible behavior: a glance at a timetable repeated twice, a wrapped sandwich offered after a quarrel, a door held, a phone left unanswered, a coat hung on the wrong hook. Keep attraction, memory, and decision legible through what an audience can see or hear; when a character notices something privately, show it through a small observable action—a pause, a shift, a carefully chosen object—unless the scene genuinely requires knowledge that no one else can have.\n\nLet the Adams contrast operate through practical, location-based complications. Introduce a plan or procedure early: a lift arranged, a flat to view, an item to return, a form to sign, a route planned. Then let its consequences unfold one or two scenes later through an ordinary physical obstacle: a mislabeled key, a wrong bus, a locked receiving office, a hold on an account, a lift that stops between floors. The obstacle should delay or reshape the relationship's next step in a way that reveals a character's priority or temperament. One partner may argue with the institution, another may find the paperwork quietly funny, a third may try a workaround; the reactions should be specific to the people, not a universal comic response. Make sure every failed plan affects the couple's ability to meet, talk, or decide, so the absurdity is not an isolated joke but a pressure on the romance.\n\nUse dialogue as the primary medium for disclosure and disagreement. Let characters speak in natural patterns shaped by the current situation, not in long monologues. They may interrupt, misunderstand, revisit a practical point, or circle back to a larger feeling. A conversation can announce a plan and then be cut off by a phone call, a delivery, or a sudden need to leave; the next scene can resume it with changed terms. Keep the audience aware of what has been said and what remains unspoken, but do not let every line carry a punchline. Show the romantic development through small exchanges—a question answered the next day, an apology accepted but not smoothed over, a shared joke that becomes a private reference.\n\nWhen private thought is required, place it in a clearly attributed nonspoken note within the active presentation, not as a voice-over by default and not as dialogue. For example: a character may notice that the other has started packing before either says anything, or may recognize that a joke landed badly and choose to say nothing. The note should be brief and tied to the visible moment; the reader knows it, but the other character does not. Do not let that private knowledge leak into subsequent dialogue unless the character chooses to reveal it through an act or words. Keep scene transitions clean, using cuts only when the selected sequence needs them, and avoid camera commands or editing terminology beyond the necessary scene structure. End a scene where the current practical or emotional turn has registered, whether that is a failed plan, a small adjustment, or the beginning of another attempt.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeax::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Stage Play\n\nWrite the relationship as a stage play. Use clear speaker cues, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and physical business that can be performed on a real stage. Dialogue lines should read naturally when spoken aloud, with each character's speech pattern distinct to their temperament and current situation. Give every important object a practical purpose: a scarf left behind, a drawer that sticks, a plant that needs watering, an intercom that announces visitors, a bag that opens at the wrong moment. Let the couple's attention and memory show through ordinary stage behavior—one person remembers where the other left the scarf; a shared task requires passing an object, holding a door, clearing a table. Proximity should create meaning: stage directions can indicate who stands near whom, who sits first, who moves away, and who stays just a moment longer after the other has left.\n\nIntroduce a practical stage inconvenience through a prop or the set itself, and let its absurd consequence arise from a character's decision or stated plan. When a character announces a plan or a general principle—\"I'll be back by eight,\" \"We can manage if we stay organised,\" \"No one needs to know\"—the immediate staged action can expose its small logistical failure: the drawer sticks, the visitor arrives early, the form must be filled in duplicate, the intercom repeats the wrong name, the bag opens and scatters the contents. Keep the failure consequential for the relationship: after the mishap, one partner must decide whether to help, correct, laugh, or leave. Let different characters respond differently: one argues with the object, another tries to fix it quietly, a third sees the humor and then feels guilty. Do not let every complication be resolved into comedy or comfort; some failures produce a shift in the couple's understanding without a punchline.\n\nUse pauses to carry private reaction that is not spoken. A pause after a line may signal the speaker's realization, embarrassment, or hesitation, but it must be supported by the preceding dialogue and visible business. Do not rely on asides or monologues to replace a character's live private thought. When the active contract requires hidden interiority, use the supplied nonspoken wrapper in the raw source, clearly attributed, so the reader can follow a motive while the other character remains unaware. Place that wrapped material outside the performed stage text—before the scene, after the line, or in a clearly marked block—never as spoken dialogue or a visible stage direction. The audience does not hear it; the reader may know it without that knowledge entering another character's lines.\n\nLet the play's time pass in stage terms. A scene can cover a single evening of practice, a shared meal, or a difficult conversation; a later scene can return to the same room with objects rearranged, a drawer fixed, a plant moved, or a coat missing. Show the romantic arc through practical work and disagreement: a disagreement over how to fix the drawer may reveal different styles of care, and that difference can resurface in later decisions. The audience knows what it can see and hear; the reader may also know a private thought without requiring a theatrical confession. Never add film-camera instructions or cinematographic directions. End a scene where the performed action reaches a natural pause: a character exits, a task is left unfinished, a line is answered by silence, or the two people simply look at the object between them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeay::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite through one authorized mind, letting association rather than linear report organize the telling. Begin with whatever the viewpoint notices first—a tone of voice, a physical detail, the exact wording of a promise, a sound from another room—then let that detail pull up a memory, a comparison, or an unfinished sentence. Carry the relationship through these crossings: what the person wants to say to the other and what they actually do, which obligations keep intruding, what they remember about proximity and past disagreements, where care shows up in small practical acts. Keep present action, recollection, speculation, and fantasy distinguishable even when syntax follows the mind's jumps; a reader should be able to tell which moment is happening now and which is being replayed. The romance is this specific attention, not generic attraction.\n\nUse large claims and small inconveniences as reciprocal pressures. Let an explanation, plan, or reasonable-sounding premise reveal its absurd consequences—not as a detachable joke, but as a change in what the viewpoint can notice, admit, or bear to do next. A declaration about love, duty, or the future can collide with a missed call, a form, a broken appliance, a schedule that will not align, or the sheer administrative cost of being close to someone. Let the digression earn its return by altering the emotional problem it interrupted. Different people respond differently to the absurdity: the other person may answer with earnestness, practicality, irritation, or a humor of their own, and their response should complicate the viewpoint's interpretation rather than confirm it.\n\nDevelop attraction, trust, vulnerability, and chosen connection through disclosure, disagreement, care, and decisions about belonging together. Let timing, competing wants, and uncertainty produce opportunities and complications with real aftermath; slow development, immediate attraction, established intimacy, and repair are all possible, but none is required by the mode. The stream may circle a question without resolving it. No confession, apology, kiss, rupture, or practical decision must complete the moment. Keep private thought within the viewpoint's knowledge; where the active contract hides interiority from display, write the actual nonspoken experience inside the designated interiority element rather than replacing it with summary. Do not let the style make scene facts indeterminate, grant access to other minds, or turn the romance into detached comedy. End during continuing thought, speech, action, or an unfinished setup if that is where the moment rests.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeaz::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nBuild this story from sender-attributed messages: every line belongs to a named sender, and the transcript's order is the order of transmission. Place the connection wherever it actually is—new, familiar, strained, repairing, or not yet named—and let the current thread expose its history through digital habits rather than summary. Show who starts, who answers at once, who drafts and rewrites, who sends in fragments, who waits, who double-texts, who moves to another channel, and who leaves a question alone. Use timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes only when they alter what someone understands, risks, or decides; a receipt is significant because of the hope or notice attached to it, not because the interface carries a fixed meaning.\n\nKeep the romance inside the exchange and its immediate pressures. Attraction, memory, proximity, disagreement, care, and decisions about closeness should shape what is sent, unsent, deleted, deferred, or answered twice. Let ordinary logistics—meeting, arriving, charging a phone, sharing a document, managing a group chat, filling out a form—carry the same emotional weight as a declaration. A grand claim about the universe may sit beside a dead battery, but the return to the person waiting must matter. No one is owed a confession, kiss, repair, or resolved plan; the thread may end with a message unanswered, a draft still open, or a practical problem still unsolved.\n\nUse Douglas Adams's exact, deadpan absurdity as pressure on contact. Introduce one apparently reasonable premise—a delivery window, a scheduling rule, a notification policy, a form's required field, a shared document's permissions—and follow its consequences precisely until the inconvenience becomes disproportionate. Let the explanation stay calm and worded exactly, and let the absurdity emerge from scale mismatch: a cosmic-sounding principle applied to a bus route, a bureaucratic procedure that treats a relationship as a category error, a group chat whose rules have acquired constitutional force. The digression should leave from the current relationship problem and return to it with a sharper question: what does this person risk by telling the truth, waiting, staying silent, or trying again?\n\nDifferent participants respond differently to the absurdity. One may solve it as a puzzle, one may triage it practically, one may exploit it, one may be simply tired. Do not turn everyone into the same comic narrator, and do not convert every vulnerable beat into a joke. The absurdity must be the one already permitted by the situation; do not add space travel, magic, or a universal punch line.\n\nWhen a participant's unsaid experience matters—an interpretation they do not type, a fear they route around, a memory that changes what they send—write it as actual private experience in a clearly separated narrative passage. If the active source requires hidden interiority, use its supplied wrapper. Keep such passages outside the transcript grammar so they cannot be mistaken for sent messages, unsent drafts, or channel events. An unsent draft is a composed artifact with its own wording and omissions; it does not replace live private thought. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nThe response may end during continuing messages, while someone waits, while a draft is being composed, or after a message that has not arrived. Fictional elapsed time follows the actual attention, waiting, and events; a delay can be long even in few lines, or brief amid many messages. Preserve the active narrator, knowledge limits, and any established facts about who can see which channel.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaeba::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the relationship in a scene-oriented rhythm suited to an illustrated story. Begin each segment with a concise scene-setting line that establishes where the couple is and what practical object or condition surrounds them: a kitchen counter with a coffee maker, a bus stop with a timetable, a flat with a window that will not close, a library table with a returned book. Follow with attributed dialogue and a short focal reaction. The reaction may be observation, memory, recognition, or a private response the other person does not hear. The reader should be able to picture the two people, the room or street, and the small object or task between them; background and expression cues can orient the moment, but they are not truth indicators.\n\nApply the Adams contrast through practical visual details. When a character announces a plan, a feeling, or a general rule, let the next beat show a small failure or absurd consequence attached to an object or location: a coffee lid that will not seal, a map that folds the wrong way, a parking ticket at the exact moment of a declaration, a key that snaps in the lock, a tram that closes its doors a second too early. The focal character may notice the mismatch and respond with humor, irritation, or a sudden private tenderness; do not force a witty comeback. The other person can respond in character, whether with laughter, reason, silence, or a practical attempt to fix the thing. Keep the failure tied to the relationship's immediate need—they were about to leave, to sign, to meet someone, to say something important—so the absurdity is not a random interruption but a pressure on their shared situation.\n\nUse background or expression cues as orientation, not certification. A delayed glance can invite interest; a repeated object—an umbrella, a library book, a broken watch—can become an anchor on a later revisit when its meaning has changed. But a contradiction in what someone says or does must carry the interpretive weight, not a painted smile or a stage direction. When the scene revisits a place, use one familiar visual detail to anchor the change rather than restating the entire setting: the umbrella is gone, the library book is due, the broken watch has been replaced. Let the focal character notice that change and register what it means for the relationship.\n\nKeep focal private experience separate from the other person's knowledge. The reader may know the focal character recognized a lie or felt a surge of care, but the partner can remain unaware until that knowledge becomes speech or an observable act. Write the focal reaction as a short narrative beat after the dialogue, not as a line the other character hears and not as a branching menu option. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands. The style is a presentation rhythm, not an interactive feature set. End the segment where the current scene's practical or emotional turn has registered, whether that is a failed plan, a small adjustment, or the two people looking at the same broken object.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaebb::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Douglas Adams / Web Serial\n\nWrite the relationship as an ongoing line across installments. Each reply should carry forward a specific practical continuity: what task, visit, arrangement, or unresolved conversation is already underway; what small change occurs now; and what remains available for the next passage. Recover only the necessary earlier fact—a missed call, a borrowed jacket, a shared recipe, a leftover argument—not a full recap. Keep the through-line visible to a reader who may return after a gap, but do not repeat the whole history; a brief concrete reminder is enough to make the current scene legible.\n\nUse the Adams absurdity through delayed consequences and the return of small logistical details. A procedure set up in one installment can pay off in a later one: the form filed incorrectly, the door left unlocked, the invitation sent to the wrong address, the bus route changed, the item that was supposed to arrive still missing. When the consequence lands, let it alter the couple's immediate plan or expose a character's priority. For example, a partner who said they would handle the paperwork discovers it was never posted; the other must decide whether to laugh, take over, or confront them. Do not make every installment build to a cliffhanger; a simple result, a shift in understanding, or an unresolved quiet can close the reply. The absurdity should arise from the situation's practical friction, not from a uniform comic response; characters may react with irritation, resignation, amusement, or silence.\n\nLet the romantic development accumulate from attention and memory across time. An object one person noticed early may reappear when the other gives it meaning; a habit that once irritated may become a sign of care; a disagreement repeated twice may reveal a deeper need. Keep each example particular to the people: the way one checks the door twice, the way the other always saves the last biscuit, the way a borrowed item is returned a week late with a note. When the focal character has a private thought, the other person does not know it unless it appears in speech or action. Use plain narrative prose for that private beat, not a cliffhanger or a forced revelation.\n\nAllow the serial shape to hold longer arcs without making every reply a miniature chapter. A relationship decision may wait while practical life continues; a plan may be discussed in one installment and tested in the next. End where the scene naturally pauses—after a completed task, an unanswered question, an arrival, a final looked-back detail, or the moment someone reaches for the wrong key. The next installment can resume directly from that pause, carrying forward the same practical and emotional state. Do not introduce a new problem in the final sentence simply to create suspense; if a turn is genuinely underway, it can remain unfinished without being artificial.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafaa::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the relationship's trouble through concrete action and plain speech rather than a vocabulary of suffering. Make each difficult move cost something visible: an apology arrives after a hesitation the reader can track, a return is framed as a practical errand, and the sentence that admits need comes out flatter than the feeling behind it. Strain bends the actual conversation—answers a beat late, a question answered with a task, a name avoided and replaced with 'you.'\n\nAnchor the hurt in particulars this couple shares. Let the viewpoint character remember one exact object, gesture, or place—the cup she turned by its handle, the platform where he did not wave—and let that memory interrupt the present exchange: a hand pauses, a sentence changes direction. Repetition measures change: an endearment, a shared habit, or an agreed phrase returns, and its second occurrence lands differently because one of them has moved closer or further away.\n\nKeep private thought on the same plane as the remembered pain, in the narrator's own diction—she watched the door the way you watch a train you have missed—instead of a label such as abandonment or fear. Emotion becomes behavior: holding a cup without drinking, folding a letter twice, speaking to a window. The viewpoint may notice a truth about the other person without being able to use it, and no other mind is directly known. Comfort can arrive and still be partial or awkward; warmth and hurt occupy the same room. A scene may stop at the point of sharpest exposure—an admission half-finished, a refusal just answered—without forcing either reconciliation or a break.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafab::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Chat Roleplay\n\nShape each reply as one coherent shared interval: scannable paragraphs, unmistakable attribution, and enough concrete staging that a returning reader re-enters instantly. Attribute speech directly—'Come back,' she said. He set the glass down.—so no line's owner is in doubt. A turn may hold a brief action, a line of dialogue, and one precise observation; let a paragraph run longer when the moment needs it instead of flattening everything to a single line.\n\nWork with what the other participant has given: let their line or action land and be answered in kind rather than restated or steered past. Carry the Hemingway profile through chosen objects and small physical work that register the relationship—an untouched plate, a coat left on its hook, a door held a second too long—and when a character cannot say the thing, show the substitute act: straightening the sugar bowl, looking at the window, finishing the other's sentence. Keep dialogue plain and practical, and let a phrase that returns in a later turn sound different because something between them has shifted.\n\nKeep to the active narrator, person, and action-marker conventions rather than imposing a format of your own, and leave closed whatever minds the authorship contract closes; motives are inferred from what is done and said. Let the world and any other characters keep behaving—the radio plays, a neighbor knocks, work continues—so the scene has life beyond the couple. Take the reply to whatever boundary the scene reaches: continuing speech, an unfinished gesture, a silence that holds. The next move should follow from what has just happened; do not manufacture a question, cliffhanger, or forced choice to bait it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafac::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic route. Tell the romance as a chain of short, consecutive episodes bound by a small recurring cast and one shared ground—a household, a shop, a courtyard, a shift—whatever obligation the supplied world actually provides. Move in story order: an episode opens on a concrete exchange—a meal carried upstairs, a mended tool, a seat held, a message passed through a neighbor—and closes when the private meaning of that exchange has changed for at least one of them, whether or not anyone speaks it. The next episode takes up the consequence, re-grounding place and people in a plain sentence or two.\n\nKeep the telling vernacular: plain, direct speech in the community's own idiom and period as the story supplies it, carried by a settled storyteller's forward motion—what happened, who said what, what was seen. No ornamental proverbs and no honorifics bolted on for flavor; texture comes from practical talk in which feeling travels under errands. A line about the heat or the price of fish can answer a question nobody asked aloud.\n\nRun Hemingway's economy alongside the episodic shape: a name, a question, or a gesture returns only when the second occurrence carries different weight—a refusal to answer this week is not the same refusal three days later. Material detail supplies the pressure—food cooling, a lamp trimmed, a gate latched—and the viewpoint stays inside one character's knowledge, with family, neighborly, and workplace claims shown as duties that person actually owes rather than as atmosphere. An episode can end on acknowledgment, refusal, or a half-understood reunion, in whatever register it has earned; no confession or tragic turn is owed at the close.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafad::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite hard, economical paragraphs in which affection and damage show through physical work and objects. A character checks a strap, wipes down the bar, folds the blanket, bolts the door—done things instead of explained feelings. Material wear does the confessing: a chair repaired twice, a lamp that cuts out, a coat mended badly at the shoulder. Compress the transitions: cut from one handled object or one exchange to the next and let the reader bridge the gap; do not summarize the emotional shift between beats.\n\nTreat dialogue as practical negotiation between two people with histories. Offering to carry, to split, to wait outside is a move in the relationship; answer moves with moves and keep the adjectives out. Clipped or rough speech belongs to a particular speaker's history and present need, not to a house voice—someone else in the scene may be gentle, or blunt in another key. Let the hard true thing come out short: 'You should have come.' 'I kept the key.' The answer can be an action rather than a word.\n\nChange rhythm when the relationship asks for it: a fast run of practical lines, then a stiller paragraph in which someone turns an object over or stands in the doorway working through whether to stay, apologize, or accept help. That reasoning may run longer, but keep it concrete—steps, costs, what was promised, what is owed within this pair's actual situation. You can end on a sharp physical edge, mid-motion or just after a refusal, without folding the scene into resolution; if the moment instead settles, let it settle without undercutting it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafae::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the romance through authored documents in sequence—letters, notes, a returned enclosure, a telegram, a list, whatever the scene's actual business requires—each with a writer, an intended reader or purpose, and a time of writing. Keep letters short and practical: confirming a meeting, returning a borrowed key, asking whether someone arrived. Feeling moves through what the writer records and omits—the errands listed, the name not used, the sentence lifted from an earlier note and sent back. An object-document can speak where prose cannot: a receipt kept, an address forwarded, a postcard mailed blank.\n\nHonor each document's limits; it records what its writer chose to write, not the whole of what the writer knew or felt. A letter written on Tuesday cannot reassure about Thursday. A note meant for a third party hides feeling behind necessary fact; a note never sent shows its status by its own condition—crossed-out lines, a fold, no signature. When two documents disagree, the gap is the story: an apology posted before the event it answers, a message returned unopened, an invitation answered too late. Material traces mean something specific—a torn edge, a stain, handwriting loosening—only where they indicate the writer's state or the document's passage, never as decoration.\n\nTrust sequence and recurrence to assemble the relationship without an omniscient voice explaining between items: a phrase used twice, an address corrected, a signature gaining or losing a first name. A final document may shift what the pair can do next—an offer on the table, a door closed by its own wording—while the feeling between them stays unresolved on the page.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafaf::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nBuild warmth out of remembered particulars and done things. One character knows that the other takes tea left to cool, sleeps with the window open a hand's width, sits in the chair that catches the late sun—and acts on the knowledge without announcing it. Put the love in the carrying: a cup brought without being asked, a blanket folded over the sofa arm, the joke retold because of what it once did to her face. Keep sentences direct and spare, and slow the pace around the small pleasure itself—the first sip, the radio finding the station, a shoe set by the door where a shoe goes.\n\nProtect personality. Disagreement and privacy stay in the scene: the coffee can be called too strong, the room can be left when the talk goes dull, help can be refused without anyone being wounded. Sincerity does not require a confession; 'Stay' can mean the next hour and only the next hour. Belonging gets chosen in small practical decisions—walking the long way together, keeping the second cup—rather than in declarations. Repetition works as affectionate ritual: the same greeting at the door, the same corner table, the same question each evening, reliable rather than ominous.\n\nStay in the viewpoint the story uses, and read the other person's pleasure from evidence—the emptied cup, the plate saved back—instead of asserting it. Attend to what these two actually do and like, not to a stock inventory of cozy props: a fire is warm because someone built it and someone else moved the screen. Comfort does not erase whatever else the wider story holds; it makes this shared interval genuinely livable. The scene can close inside the comfort—a shared silence, a hand crossing the table, a light put out at the end of the evening—without a stake being raised or a repair being owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafag::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Formal Literary Novel\n\nCompose in deliberate, sustained paragraphs that carry a scene across a room, a street, a season, a social occasion. Frame the private pair inside the public world: a dinner where they sit too far apart, a letter delivered during a family visit, a walk that begins on the promenade and ends behind a closed door. Where the chosen narrator permits, the narration may hold social context, family history, or a measured judgment in composed sentences distinct from ordinary speech, while the center of each paragraph stays concrete—what was on the table, who stood first, how the room emptied.\n\nUse dialogue as partial disclosure. Use economical presentation and let speech follow each character's established voice and purpose. A short practical exchange can surface only part of the conflict when restraint fits the occasion; an expansive speaker may disclose, explain, or evade at greater length in their own register. An object or action—a glove picked up, a chair moved nearer, a window opened against the stated cold—can carry a turn. Authorized direct feeling can accompany it when that is part of the character's actual experience. Repetition can mark the seam between public and private selves: the greeting used at the threshold, the name permitted in company that cannot be used when they are alone.\n\nDo not costume the style: social framing imports no aristocracy, archaic diction, or uniformly elaborate speech—take the setting and its manners as supplied. Keep access to knowledge and private experience within the selected narrator and viewpoint contract. A limited passage can stay close to one character; an authorized broader narrator may also connect minds, family history, or the town's social life without turning those private facts into shared character knowledge. A passage can close where the occasion or the exchange gives out—after the significant turn, as the guests move on, while the unanswered question is still held—leaving the pair's next choice open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafah::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite each scene as an occasion for telling: someone offers a story because waiting, debt, travel, apology, jealousy, or a refused question has made the immediate conversation too dangerous to complete. Name the listener and the place, and let the tale's practical purpose shape its selection. The tale functions in the present even if the teller never returns to it directly.\n\nKeep the told narrative plain. Use short declarative sentences for its physical events, concrete objects, and completed speech. Do not let the tale explain how its narrator felt. Instead, choose the incident he or she repeats, the detail changed on a later mention, and the action stopped before description. Return to the surrounding scene through a visible particular: the listener's face, a hand motion, a glass or cup left untouched, the pause before someone asks for the rest.\n\nLet the embedded story encode the risk the teller will not yet state. A remembered betrayal can warn without accusing; an act of loyalty can invite without pleading; a name omitted from the tale can reveal who is now too close to mention. A listener may answer with silence, a small correction, an offer of a match or blanket, or a question about a practical detail. Neither a correct interpretation nor a perfect response is required. The romance advances because one person has agreed to hear and the other has agreed to be heard again.\n\nClose when the outer story has moved one concrete step: the listener asks for another tale, the teller reaches the scene he or she had been avoiding, or an ordinary task is understood by both through a new shared reference. Do not force a completed confession, a happy reconciliation, a magical bargain, or a cliffhanger. Use recurrence of a phrase, object, or person from the told story to mark what the lovers now recognize without saying it. If the scene's natural stopping point arrives during the listener's reaction or the teller's unfinished speech, end there without requiring the present conversation to finish.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafai::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nSelect the social-realist route. Use a third-person narrator who watches two people with calm precision, but do not forbid the focal character's nonspoken attention from entering the prose. Show the relationship through its settings: a rented room, a workplace corridor, a family table, a train station, a public office where a form must be signed or refused. Make obligations and economic constraints concrete—rent, working hours, an elderly relative's expectations, a job in another city.\n\nWrite short, unadorned scenes in Hemingway's manner. Each scene includes an ordinary practical action with an emotional consequence: preparing a meal while one person waits for a decision, walking a child to school after an argument, choosing which coat to pack. Let the characters speak directly and sparingly. Do not explain their feelings. Instead, return to an object or gesture the narrator or focal character can perceive: a chair not used at supper, a key returned to its hook too early, a light left on in a room no one enters.\n\nLet repetition carry meaning. A recurring phrase or task can show what a person refuses to abandon or discuss. The same request made again after an interval can mean pressure, hope, or the exhausted end of patience. When one character repeats a refusal, the other may understand it less as new information than as a known condition of staying together—or may be newly wounded by the repetition. The perception of sameness is itself an emotional fact.\n\nDevelopment proceeds through observable change over time, but keep the characters' interior access according to the chosen narrator. A practical arrangement shifts; one person accepts a worse schedule or gives up a small private custom without being asked; the other notices and does not yet thank him or her. A quarrel may happen through smaller failures to arrive, contribute, or answer over several days instead of one scene of raised voices. Do not determine that all conflict must resolve through resumed tasks or that a declaration can never occur. Let the particular people and scene supply the outcome.\n\nEnd a scene when a social or material fact has altered slightly and the narrative can register it without commentary. Do not force a declaration, a broken relationship, or a tidy resolution. Keep the language plain, the judgment withheld, and the love visible in observed habits and in those moments when private feeling becomes a small sustained act.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafaj::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the romance in a present-day world where an older authority persists: a family name, a property deed, a recorded surname, an unpaid debt, a building's history, or the expectation that a particular kind of person should remain in the shadows. Do not import vampire physiology, clans, or blood rules. Let the inherited pressure be ordinary and material: someone can be fired, evicted, remembered, claimed by relatives, or made unwelcome in a neighborhood.\n\nUse Hemingway's concrete prose for intimacy and threat alike. Describe the staircase light, the door that sticks, the broken lock, the cold tile, the precise place where a hand is placed and not moved. Social masks are observable habits: a smile used at work, a window not opened, a phone turned face down, an address never given. Love appears when these habits loosen in one other person's company, but the loosening does not have to be total or permanent within a scene.\n\nBuild the relationship through small exchanges made riskier by inherited authority. A shared meal in a part of town where one of them can be recognized matters. A borrowed coat carries a name. A voicemail left from a payphone protects and asks at once. One person may explain only a practical rule—never use the front entrance, never be seen together on Thursdays—and the other must decide whether to obey, ask why, or refuse to live under the rule. The atmosphere is not a backdrop; it changes what trust costs. The decision may remain unresolved at the reply's end.\n\nLet a recurring phrase or gesture accrue meaning instead of following a fixed arc of refusal-then-acceptance. A hand withdrawn in fear might later remain, or might still pull back in a new situation. A question refused once might be answered after trust grows, or might stay impossible while the relationship strengthens in some other way. The same door closed quietly at the beginning can become the door one person finally opens, but the story decides that through its people and events, not through a mandated pattern.\n\nEnd when a pattern of access, visibility, or inherited obligation shifts enough for the reader to notice, without requiring the couple to defeat the system, reconcile with the family, or escape the city. The strength is in the characters' awareness and their small mutual adjustments: one knows where the other can be found; the other knows which name will not be answered in daylight. The ending may be a moment of realization, a continuation of a trusted ritual, or an interruption that leaves the next step contingent.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafak::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the oral and frame-tale route. Let the romance be told as a set of remembered accounts passed among people with different degrees of knowledge and authority. Do not set the story inside a generic Indian family. Use the language, region, community, and period the scene already supplies, including its specific terms for relatives, festivals, foods, and places.\n\nBegin with an occasion for telling: a visitor asks how the couple met, a younger cousin wants the story again, a mother corrects a version at a wedding, or one partner spends a journey recounting the other to a friend. Keep Hemingway's sentences short and concrete inside the reported speech. Let the teller supply the exact weather, the bus route, the puja day, the name of the cousin who introduced them, the rent paid for the first room, the food refused after a quarrel.\n\nUse the difference between tellers to create the romance's complications. An aunt tells the story as a caution about duty. A friend remembers only the laughter. One partner tells the journey as a sequence of practical failures; the other tells the same journey as the moment of decision. The outer narration should remain direct and restrained, but the focal romantic partner's private perception may appear plainly when the chosen narrator permits it: a woman touches her own wrist when a relative praises the match too soon; the man notices that a particular year makes him leave the room.\n\nLet repetition function like a refrain. A phrase from a grandmother, a repeated line of a song, or a small object handed down through the family becomes what one partner quotes and the other refuses to quote. The refusal to tell some parts is as revealing as the telling. A listener may ask a direct question and receive only a silence, a poured glass of water, or the start of a different story.\n\nThe romance develops when one partner tells a version of the shared past that the other has not heard or does not contradict, or when a private memory is offered to a chosen listener. Trust grows not through endless confession but through the recognition that some memories belong to the couple and are now being shared deliberately. A public story may be simplified, corrected, or finally spoken by the person usually silent.\n\nEnd when a telling has moved the relationship one recognizable step: a listener stops asking, a partner repeats wording for the first time, or a story withheld from strangers is given to someone new. Do not require a full reconciliation, a magical event, a marriage arranged by the narrative, or a fixed guarantee that every scene must end with a changed present relationship. The power is in who is permitted to tell and who chooses to listen.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafal::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the diary and reflective miscellany route, but do not force first person. If the selected story requires another narrator or person, keep the same dated, occasion-marked presentation: short chapters or sections set apart by a day, a season, an errand, or a piece of news. Within each section, follow one person's ordinary activity in Hemingway's plain present-tense or past-tense prose.\n\nName the concrete things that carry feeling without being explained: the persimmon on the kitchen counter, the umbrella left behind, the train timetable circled, the scratched thermos, the sound of rain on the metal roof, the particular way a shirt is folded. Describe domestic work as evidence of care or neglect: who washes the dishes, who buys the rice, who remembers to bring up the winter quilt, who does not look at the calendar.\n\nLet attention itself be the romance. One person notices that the other has stopped eating a certain food, has begun wearing a particular jacket, or always turns a certain street corner too quickly. A small repeated object—a coin, a pencil, a park bench, a phone charm—becomes the emblem of a promise, a quarrel, or an apology without the narrative saying so. Repetition carries feeling: the same errand repeated on different days can mean boredom, hope, or a refusal to let go.\n\nKeep dialogue sparse and practical, but do not restrict conflict to one pattern. A conversation may be only: \"I went to the market.\" \"The fish was not good.\" \"I'll go tomorrow.\" The pause after each line is the emotional content. Disagreement may appear through daily refusals—not eating together, not mentioning a phone call, closing a door with slightly more force—or through a direct argument that the scene's people actually choose. Affection appears through resumed table settings, an extra portion prepared, a blanket placed over sleeping legs, a question asked at the right time.\n\nDo not explain a character's loneliness or love. Let the diary-like occasion stand beside another occasion and mark change by small difference. A section might end with the sentence: \"The other side of the bed was still warm.\" Another, weeks later: \"She set two cups on the table without thinking.\"\n\nEnd a section when a recurring detail shifts meaning, when one person recognizes something he or she had previously missed, or when an ordinary moment of attention has made the present attachment available to the reader. Do not require a confession, a dramatic reunion, a seasonal metaphor, or a complete emotional resolution. The method is patience: plain objects, named days, and the slow accumulation of shared habits.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafam::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the intimate psychological or urban fiction route. Keep the narrative close to one character's immediate perception, but write in Hemingway's direct, unadorned prose: short clauses, concrete nouns, few adjectives, and meaning carried by what the character does and notices rather than by long analysis.\n\nAnchor the romance in a specific city, neighborhood, job, and time. Name the bus line, the corner store, the factory, the clinic, the street market, the borrowed apartment. Let money, work schedules, family obligations, and political or social noise enter through practical details: the cost of a taxi, the queue for water, the news on a neighbor's radio, a route avoided because of a demonstration. The love is not abstract; it is two people deciding to meet despite the city's constraints.\n\nShow the focal character's private experience through concrete perception and restrained thought. We may read: \"She watched his hands on the knife. He cut the bread badly. It made her want to laugh, and she did not laugh.\" Do not translate every feeling into introspection. A woman notices that he always sits with his back to the door. A man remembers the exact color of her dress from the day she said she would leave her husband. Memory works by sensory detail, not by summary.\n\nLet attraction and doubt appear in small physical exchanges: the pressure of two fingers on a wrist, a cigarette shared, a glass set down too hard, the smell of rain and dust on someone's coat. Trust grows through practical acts: he waits outside her father's apartment; she brings food when he is ill; one of them tells the truth about a failed plan and expects rejection but receives only a practical question.\n\nUse recurrence with changing meaning. A song heard in a taxi at the beginning of the relationship returns later when the two can no longer listen to it together. A phrase from a family member becomes the code for a secret. A street corner remembered in pleasure becomes the place of a final disagreement, or vice versa.\n\nWrite dialogue plainly. People speak about what they need: money, time, a key, a promise, a name. What is left unsaid should be felt through the pause and through what the focal character does next. He does not say he loves her; he walks across the city to return a book she left. She does not say she is afraid; she asks if he has eaten.\n\nEnd when the focal character takes in a consequential detail that changes his or her position toward the relationship. The detail may be a phone call answered, a ring removed, a door left unlocked, or any other concrete perception the scene supports. Do not force a declaration, a suicide, a political resolution, or a magical occurrence. If the natural stop occurs before the character can process the detail, end with that unprocessed perception rather than inventing a completed realization. The power is in the exact weight of ordinary life made intimate.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafan::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Light Novel\n\nOpen each scene with a clear location and immediate action: a bookshelf being straightened, a classroom door opened too quickly, a convenience store bag set on a counter, a phone checked at the station. Keep spatial movement easy to follow. Use short paragraphs and simple stage directions, but do not write a script. The narration should be lean, direct, and concrete.\n\nUse Hemingway's plain vocabulary and sentence rhythm for narration and thought. Avoid exclamation marks and melodramatic similes. A romantic feeling is communicated by what a character notices and what he or she fails to say: the corner of a textbook cover, a cup left on the windowsill, the second button undone on a school shirt, the pause before \"see you tomorrow.\"\n\nMake the romance advance through small, repeated encounters whose meaning shifts. The two might meet on the same train, wait at the same crosswalk, or share a part-time job. At first the repeated detail is neutral: a borrowed eraser, a wrong umbrella, a seat by the window. Later the same detail becomes loaded: one of them saves the seat without asking; the umbrella is returned washed; the eraser is carried in a different pocket. Repetition marks growing attention without requiring internal monologue.\n\nInclude private reaction beats after significant dialogue or contact. A beat can be one or two sentences of the focal character's immediate perception: \"Her hand was warm. He did not move his own.\" \"He remembered the heat of the drink through the paper cup.\" The thought does not need to explain the romantic meaning. It is enough that the character remembers the physical detail and that the reader sees the shift.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, dialogue should be practical but able to carry subtext. Two people discuss homework, shift times, food, or a broken phone; what matters is who offers to help, who waits, who lies about having no plans, who does not ask the obvious question. Avoid rapid witty banter for every pair. Let silence or a short refusal create the strongest romantic pressure.\n\nFor miscommunication, do not rely on coincidence alone. Use the character's actual knowledge and fear. One person may see the other laughing with someone else and enter the room late; the other may see a saved contact name and misunderstand; an existing belief about being ignored or mocked may make an invitation seem insincere. These obstacles should arise from what each person already believes about being liked, not from external comic contrivance inserted solely to delay the scene.\n\nEnd the scene when the situation changes enough to make the next step natural: one of them agrees to stay longer, makes a small correction, or notices something that will matter in a later encounter. A light-novel cliffhanger is not required. Closing with a simple visual can be stronger—two cups in the sink the next morning, a saved train seat, a returned object placed where it had not been placed before—but choose the detail the actual scene has made relevant. Keep the prose fast, the feeling understated, and the attraction a matter of accumulated particularities rather than abstract adoration.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafao::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Lyrical Prose\n\nChoose the particular objects, gestures, and places that show what each person does with their attention when the other is or is not present. Anchor each lyrical passage in something the viewpoint can see or remember seeing: a hand on a glass, the edge of light, the late sound from the street. Let the rhythm develop from the movement among these particulars rather than from abstract states of love or longing. When a sentence turns associative, return it to something in the scene or the teller's usable memory so the lyric line does not float away from the world these people inhabit.\n\nUse deliberate repetition the way Hemingway uses recurrence: insist on the thing itself, then change its meaning by what has happened around it. A repeated image can acquire new weight when its context shifts—the same chair now empty, the same road now wet, the same name said differently. Do not use repetition as a noise effect; let each return carry a small change in knowledge or feeling. A single exact comparison can unfold across a paragraph if it changes what the narrator understands, but keep the comparison's parts tied to sensory evidence already present or remembered.\n\nLet silences and concrete practical exchanges do romantic work. Gesture, placement of objects, going and returning, weather, domestic details—these can carry attraction, hesitation, care, or regret without the characters naming their feelings. Plain statements should interrupt lyricism and thereby give it force. The prose may drift into shaped attention, but it must always come back to the physical present enough for desire and uncertainty to remain grounded in a particular person and a particular time.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafap::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet reflection arise from a present detail that cannot be made to mean the same thing as it once did. Choose a changed object or routine that belongs to the relationship: the coffee still made for two, the bench now worn at one side, the shelf where something used to stand. Let that observation lead quietly back to a remembered occasion, using only the sensory particulars of what happened then—who said what, which way the light fell, what was left unsaid. Do not announce that time has passed. Show the small irreversible difference in the physical world and let the past coexist with the present in the same paragraph, without forcing every recollection to end in sorrow.\n\nUse repetition to mark continuity and change together. A phrase or action that returns can now mean something different because the person or place has changed around it. A greeting that once came easily can hesitate. A thing done daily can go on being done after its original reason is gone. Keep the repeated element concrete and ordinary; let the reader feel the change through the altered weight of the same action. The refusal to move on can be visible in what the character does and does not say, not in italic introspection.\n\nKeep ordinary pleasure available. A good meal, a warm room, a familiar voice can still be what they are. Melancholic reflection does not require every pleasant moment to be undercut by an announcement of impermanence. Let happiness and loss sit in the same scene without forcing them into tidy opposition. The reflective turn may come with no resolution, because some change is unfinished and some attachment continues beyond explanation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafaq::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the romance inside social and domestic realism. Let the relationship become legible through what people do in rooms, streets, workplaces, and family settings: who brings tea, who waits, who repairs what, whose voice changes when a name is mentioned. Keep the language plain and the objects exact. Do not make the cast into emblems of a region or an honor code; use the particular place and season supplied by the story. Practical obligations—work, visitors, arrangements, meals—can create proximity and delay without becoming symbolic of fate.\n\nUse a situated teller without turning the whole into nested tales. A short frame may locate who is telling and to whom, but keep the account close to present action and memory. Let the teller report what she saw or was told and mark the difference. Inner life remains with the focal character under the active authorship contract; the teller can notice outward signs but cannot report thoughts she did not hear. Repetition can come from social practice—a fixed phrase, a customary question, a repeated chore—and its meaning can shift when circumstances change.\n\nLet brief reflective or lyric passages rise from concrete moments: the light at a particular window, the sound of a gate, a hand around a glass. These passages stay tied to the room and the people; they are not ornamental. Plain statements should do most of the emotional work, including silence and refusal. The account may end with a practical matter resumed or unresolved rather than with a moral or a sealing declaration.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafar::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose the few physical details that do the emotional work. The relationship should be visible in what is done, not in a stream of feelings: a glass set down, a chair moved, a door held, a look that lasts one beat longer. If a thought is included, it must materially change how the reader understands the action or choice that follows. Do not cut essential causality to make the prose shorter; an exact extended sentence is better than several performed fragments. Let silence and omission leave something inferable: the reader can know why a person pauses without being told three times.\n\nUse repetition sparingly and precisely. Repeat only what changes meaning or exposes a refusal to move on. The same phrase from one morning can return at a later meeting with different weight, marking how the relationship has changed. Give dialogue the plainness of practical speech, but do not flatten every character into the same terse voice. People can talk around a subject, offer help, ask a direct question, or leave a sentence unfinished. Keep the scene's scale credible; a romance can turn on a small act, a decision to stay, or a withheld word.\n\nLet obligation, timing, and competing wants appear as concrete choices rather than explanations. Show the choice through what a person does next—takes the phone, stays at the table, goes outside, says yes or says nothing. The narrative does not need to announce the relationship's status. It can leave the reader watching one person decide something, with the consequence still to come.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafas::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite scene by scene, letting each moment carry enough of its own action to keep the reader oriented without recap machinery. Begin with what the viewpoint can see or do: a room, a task, a meeting, a person entering. Move naturally among speech, gesture, perception, memory, and authorized thought; when a memory or thought arises, use a concrete trigger in the scene—a sound, an object, a repeated gesture—and return to the present action. Use transitions that place the next scene through physical continuity, a clear temporal marker, or a character's next action, not sentences that announce a theme.\n\nKeep Hemingway's practicality in the speech and the objects. Characters can discuss money, travel, work, weather, food, arrangements, or a plan without the novel explaining their feelings. The romance appears in what they choose to attend to and what they omit. When inner experience is available under the active contract, give it the same plainness as the external action; it may be one clear thought, a felt hesitation, or a sustained uncertainty. Do not turn everyone perfectly perceptive. A character can miss an implication or interpret it incorrectly without the surrounding narrative doing the thinking for the reader.\n\nLet pacing come from scene length and the consequence of what happens next rather than from a required mini-resolution. A scene may end with a phone unanswered, a meeting arranged, a memory surfacing, or the pair simply walking. The relationship develops through particular exchanges and decisions: who calls, who waits, who says what, who changes a plan. Ordinary causality remains. If a story event permits an immediate response, do not stall; if it does not, the scene can pause at the natural boundary without forcing a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafat::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nUse the supplied relationship premise as the scene's organizing law. First, identify the central promise or tension the story has established—what draws these two together, what threatens or changes their understanding, what private history they share. Then choose scenes, objects, and exchanges that make that premise legible: if the story promises slow discovery, show the small steps; if it promises an established attachment, show the habits and private shorthand that carry it. Do not import a roster of familiar tropes the premise has not established. Affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery should ground themselves in particular actions—a hand held a moment too long, a name said differently, a door left open, a silence that used to be easy.\n\nBuild callbacks out of concrete repeated details rather than emotional announcements. A private language can be one phrase, one gesture, one shared reference; the second occurrence deepens the relationship only when circumstances have changed around it. Repetition can mark continuity or expose a breach. Keep the objects and dialogue plain, with Hemingway's directness: characters may avoid naming what they feel, but the scene should let the reader infer it from what they do or don't do. Do not make every pause a concealed confession.\n\nLet consequences fulfill the premise without forcing a lesson or a neat resolution. A scene can end on a held glance, a changed plan, a remembered phrase, or the moment one person realizes something the other may not. The emotional payoff can be quiet. Give the relationship room to be specific: competing wants, practical obligations, and ordinary disagreement can occur without turning the couple into a generic reward or a permanent injury.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafau::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nLet the telling sound as if it is being remembered and repeated. Open with a compact declaration that establishes who these people were to each other in the account—not by announcing their hidden feelings, but by naming an act or way of being in plain terms: she was the one who waited; he was the one who came back slowly. Then expand into the particular scene that complicates that reputation, showing the ordinary details the declaration cannot contain. Keep the chronicle's present scale believable. An errand remains an errand; a night spent talking does not become a legendary battle merely because the style is a saga.\n\nUse recurrence the way an oral account holds meaning: repeat a phrase, a name, a place, or a small action when a later occurrence gives it new weight. The repeated element should come from the people themselves—what they say to one another, how one of them fixes a door, which road they take—rather than from ornamental epithets. Shared reputation must remain distinct from private experience. Being remembered as faithful does not mean the person found waiting easy or certain. Keep the active perspective's access consistent: the teller may report what was seen and said but not a private thought she did not know.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt the chronicle's authority. The subject of an often-remembered moment may object to a detail or answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern. This is not mock combat, and the humor, if any, comes from the persistence of a shared story. Romance appears through chosen connection: a return, a shared task, a promise kept or broken, a decision to belong in someone's account. The telling may end with business concluded or resumed, not with a sealing epitaph on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafav::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nPlace the couple inside a concrete Scandinavian social world: a particular workplace, shared residence, board, school, club, parish, or seasonal obligation. Use that setting's ordinary rhythms—meals, shifts, repairs, transport, holidays—to bring the two into repeated, practical contact. Let the community's expectations and practical needs create closeness, delay, or accepted separation; do not treat the setting as mere weather or scenic background.\n\nWrite in Hemingway's exact, economical manner. Name the real object and the real action: the repaired bicycle, the borrowed oven dish, the coat hung by the wrong door. Keep sentences short, dialogue concrete, and emotional interpretation mostly unsaid. When attraction, resentment, comfort, or uncertainty matters, show it through a change in a familiar task or phrase rather than through commentary. A person may be unable to say what has happened, but may return a tool cleaned, take the long road, stand too long at the sink, or sit down at the table without being asked.\n\nLet repetition carry the relationship. A recurring errand, question, seat, window, piece of equipment, or greeting can mark ease, avoidance, or a silent decision. When the same detail returns, alter one concrete element—one word added, one pause, one person now doing the task alone, or one object left behind—and let that alteration bear the emotional weight. If disagreement appears, keep it specific: one wants to leave, the other to stay; one expects more help, the other offers less. The dispute is resolved, postponed, or endured through practical actions, not through a mutual confession.\n\nUse private feeling according to the active interiority contract, visible or hidden as authorized. Render the nonspoken experience concretely and briefly: a remembered preference, an avoided question, a sensation that joins two scenes. Other characters do not know this private content unless the scene shows them learning it. The response may end at a natural pause in the action or exchange—a task finished, an invitation declined, a door closed—without requiring a declaration, kiss, or new obstacle.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafaw::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Screenplay\n\nWrite in short scenes with clear location and time markers, speaker-attributed dialogue, and concise action lines. Use the action line for exact physical behavior rather than emotional commentary: 'She puts the cup on the shelf,' not 'They feel close.' Show affection, distance, or dispute through touch, timing, object handling, and silence. A line of dialogue may say 'I'll stay' while the action line shows the speaker still holding a coat or not crossing the threshold.\n\nUse cuts and scene transitions when location or time changes. Do not write camera movements or editing instructions. If the active contract supplies a voice-over for a purpose, use it as a distinct nonspoken track; otherwise allow the visible scene and audible words to carry the relationship. Required private experience goes into the approved hidden/attributed nonspoken material, clearly separated from spoken dialogue and never placed in a character's mouth as knowledge another character can hear.\n\nDevelop the romance through ordinary repeated situations: waiting for the same bus, sharing a narrow kitchen, folding sheets, walking the same route. Let repetition mark change—the same phrase said with less hesitation, the same task done together instead of separately, or the same object placed in a new location. When disagreement occurs, make it practical and audible: one proposes leaving, the other stays; one asks for help, the other gives less. The scene can end when the specific action or exchange completes or is cut by a real interruption. Do not add a confession, kiss, rupture, or reversal at the end of every response.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafax::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable scene with speaker-led dialogue, entrances, exits, props, pauses, and minimal blocking notes. Use each physical choice as relationship information: a coat handed over, a glass set down, a door left open, someone sitting in the other's usual chair. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, make dialogue practical and spare, with short declarative sentences and actions that can be performed. When feeling would be obvious to the audience, let the character's physical business carry it instead of an explanatory speech.\n\nLet repetition become stage evidence. A question asked again, a gesture first accepted and then ignored, a shared task performed together and later alone, a prop moved from one side of the room to the other. In disagreement, keep the lines specific: 'You always leave,' followed by silence or by the other person taking a coat from the hook. People can be hurt, careful, or forgiving through action; no one needs to announce an emotion.\n\nPlace required private interiority in the approved nonspoken authoring material, clearly separated from dialogue. Do not disguise it as an aside or monologue that another character would hear. A monologue or aside may occur only if the active contracts give it a dramatic function, and even then it does not replace the hidden interiority requirement. The scene may end when a physical action completes—a door closes, a bag is lifted, a person sits down—without a resolution or final emotional line.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafay::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from one authorized point-of-view mind, letting a concrete stimulus start the mental movement: the smell of coffee, the weight of a wet coat, a chair still pulled out, the sound of a door latch. Follow the association into memory, anticipation, physical sensation, or a repeated phrase, then let the thought loop back to the present object. Keep enough orientation that the reader can tell what is happening now, what is remembered, and what is imagined or feared. The syntax may stretch, fragment, or repeat; the triggers and returns are materially grounded in Hemingway's world.\n\nDevelop the romance through what the mind selects and revisits. The character recalls the exact angle of a head, words not said, the particular way someone held a bag or waited at a corner. A recurring image may signal comfort, obsession, resentment, or an attempt to move on. Do not announce love or uncertainty; let the chosen detail and its associated feeling emerge from the movement. If a fact is uncertain—what another person meant, whether a call will come—show the mind attempting an inference without granting knowledge of that person's private state.\n\nKeep all interiority within this authorized mind. Other characters may speak and act, but the reader sees them only through this focal perception; their speech is sparse and arrives inside the thought-flow context. Do not let the technique grant access to human-owned thoughts outside the active contract or turn all scene facts indeterminate. The response may end when the associative movement reaches a natural settling—a return to the present object, a shift from memory to a next action, or a pause that leaves the thought incomplete. No external event is needed to justify an interior close.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafaz::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the story as a legible message exchange: each line attributed to its sender, with timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes included only when they alter the exchange. Use Hemingway's economical message style—short declaratives, concrete news, ordinary requests. A character writes 'The store closed' or 'Outside now,' not a paragraph about feelings. Tension and care appear in the choices each person makes: what to send, how to phrase it, when to wait, what to revise, and what never to send.\n\nLet repetition work through the medium. A question asked again days later, the same word spelled differently, an old message quoted back, a notification left unanswered. If one person writes in fragments and another in complete sentences, keep those habits consistent and meaningful. Do not make every message equally terse or equally expressive; the difference is part of the relationship.\n\nDistinguish sharply among sent message, unsent draft, and private thought. A composed-but-unsent text is a deliberate artifact; show its unsent state through the interface (a draft line, an edit, a note) or through separated authoring material if the active contract allows it. Do not treat an unsent draft as the character's live inner monologue or as something the recipient can see. Other characters know only what has been transmitted or their own private thoughts. The response may stop in the middle of a real exchange, after a delay, or after an unsent message; no confession or resolution is needed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafba::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Visual Novel\n\nWrite each scene with a concise setting line, attributed dialogue, and focal narration that records what this viewpoint character notices. Use Hemingway's concrete detail: the color of the table, the weight of a mug, the position of a chair, whether a coat is on the hook. Let attention itself show feeling—what the focal character looks at, avoids, remembers, or returns to. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, dialogue is direct and practical, with silence and short exchanges carrying emotion.\n\nIf a background or expression cue appears, use it to orient the current scene, not to reveal a hidden truth. A smile may mean ease, politeness, or distraction. Meaning is established by the focal character's observation and later action or contradiction, not by labeling the expression. Repetition can mark change: the same cafe, but now the adjacent chair is empty; the same words, but spoken with a different pause or after a different task.\n\nKeep scene changes legible through location or time markers, and do not add branching menus, player choices, romance routes, or images unless the active contract explicitly authorizes them. When the contract asks for private interiority, render it as focal narration or as the approved separated nonspoken material; never give another character knowledge of it unless the scene shows them learning it. The response may stop at a scene break, after a line, or after a brief reaction. No confession or relationship checkpoint is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamafbb::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Ernest Hemingway / Web Serial\n\nWrite each installment as a present scene with a clear local arc and an open through-line. Begin by touching one or two exact earlier details—the same chair, train timetable, task, phrase, or object—instead of a general recap. Then move into the current interaction. Use Hemingway's compressed prose for the immediate scene: short paragraphs, concrete objects, practical dialogue. The episode should feel like a real step in an ongoing relationship, not a standalone mini-chapter.\n\nBuild the romance through repeated situations and small changes. A place revisited, a task shared differently, a phrase now spoken with new weight, a familiar object absent or returned. Keep exact recall across installments; when a detail comes back, alter one element to show ease, withdrawal, or a decision. Do not invent a contradiction with prior events to create tension. Progress can mean new knowledge, a shift in trust, a small choice to call or not—no game-like relationship levels.\n\nEnd each installment when the scene's immediate action or exchange reaches a natural stop: a task finished, a question left unanswered, someone staying or leaving. Use a hook only when it arises from the scene, such as an unfinished sentence or a delayed reply; do not force a cliffhanger, kiss, confession, or argument every time. Required private interiority remains available under the active contract, concrete in its attention to what a character notices or avoids. Give developed thought or feeling its needed space while keeping the installment readable. Other characters do not know this private content.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagaa::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nTreat every romantic turn as an occasion for the viewpoint character to put his or her own motive on trial. When a caress, apology, request, silence, or withdrawal occurs, follow it with a spiraling private response that accuses, excuses, reverses, or condemns the feeling behind it. Let this argument arise from the history of the particular couple: a remembered kindness becomes evidence of manipulation, a remembered wound becomes proof of the other's refusal, and then memory turns again. Do not let the reversal cancel the feeling; let both tenderness and suspicion remain true at once.\n\nKeep the other character unaware of this inward trial. The spoken line or performed gesture may be stiff, abrupt, too soft, or unfinished, but the full accusation remains private. Use that mismatch to drive the next exchange: the partner responds to what was actually shown, and the viewpoint must answer from inside a self-argument the partner cannot hear. Dialogue may strike, soften, evade, or inadvertently confirm a fear. A plain statement from the other person can unsettle an elaborate self-justification more effectively than a counter-argument.\n\nDo not convert every beat into philosophical speech or a named psychological condition. The method is to make emotional exposure costly and specific rather than generic. A hand accepted can be immediately experienced as a debt; a confession can feel like self-betrayal the moment it is spoken; an apology can carry a hidden reservation that the speaker despises. When comfort arrives, let it be partial or awkward. The point is not to force separation or collapse, but to keep the couple's connection lived inside that unresolved pressure. End on the current moment of that struggle: a question left open, a held breath, a response that neither fully repairs nor fully destroys. Let the next movement begin from what the character now believes he or she may say.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagab::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite in compact paragraphs with every spoken line clearly attributed. After each important speech, give one or two sentences of the viewpoint character's private argument: what the speaker desired, resented, retracted, or could not believe about his or her own words. Keep the thought tied directly to the line spoken, not to an abstract philosophy. The other character cannot hear it, but the player can see the pressure that shapes the next reply.\n\nLet the dialogue carry the romance. When one offers care, the other may accept and immediately add a small condition or accusation. Let memory intrude without exposition: a remembered promise can make an invitation feel like a demand, or a past silence can make a present apology seem rehearsed. These background pieces should be quick and specific, never long flashback blocks.\n\nKeep the immediate world alive. NPCs pour tea, look away, adjust a coat, or wait by a door. Use those physical movements to create an opening for the next speaker, not to force a choice. The scene may rest on a refusal, a half-gesture, or an unfinished sentence and still be ready for the next player.\n\nEnd at a continuation the next player can enter naturally: a hand not yet taken, a door ajar, a proposal just made, a return mentioned and not yet accepted. No cliffhanger or mandatory question is required. Preserve the selected narrator, action marker, and player control. Leave the pressure in the room so the partner must choose whether to pursue, deflect, or expose that private argument.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagac::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic route. Build the romance from a sequence of concrete, everyday scenes: a shared meal, an errand in the lane, a doorway meeting, a borrowed object returned late. Each episode should contain one small exchange that opens the couple's larger difficulty. A promise nearly made, a debt remembered, or a kindness that sounds like an accusation can start the trouble without expanding the episode into a set piece.\n\nGround the emotional pressure in physical particulars of the immediate world: a wet threshold, a half-finished basket, a lamp lit early, a coat offered and refused. These objects are not symbols; they are the surface across which the characters' hesitation becomes visible. Let the selected period and community supply their own manners of address, visiting, and obligation. Do not import a fixed cast or dialect, and do not add a supernatural or experimental frame.\n\nInside each episode, give the focal character a Dostoevskian interior argument. After a refusal or a tender act, let thought reverse: the character pleads for a return, then accuses himself of asking for what he has no right to demand; the other decides to speak plainly, then hears her first sentence go cold with pride. Keep this reversal brief and grounded in the episode's actual exchange. It is scene-specific, not a philosophy speech.\n\nLink episodes by consequence. A refusal at the end of one becomes the unspoken topic at the start of the next. A confession may offer relief and then make both parties more guarded. Familial or neighborly opinion can press on the couple, but it must remain a particular pressure from particular people, not the only obstacle. End each episode at a natural pause: a gate closed, a lamp blown out, a reply withheld. The whole should feel like a break in an ongoing record rather than a completed moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagad::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite in short, concrete sentences with hard edges. Name the worn collar, the chipped cup, the loose hinge, the cold hand, the coin counted twice. Let physical facts carry the feeling. A cup set down hard can show anger without the paragraph saying the person is angry. A coat left hanging can become the whole argument about not leaving.\n\nTurn Dostoevskian self-argument into quick material reversals. A character decides to go, sees the other's hand on the latch, and accuses herself of using that hand as an excuse to stay. Another starts to apologize, then notices the torn sleeve and turns the apology bitter. The thought can stretch a little when the reasoning needs it, but do not let every pause become a speech. Show desire and distrust as practical hesitations: a lifted hand, a turned key, a bowl pushed across the table.\n\nTie intimacy to effort and cost. Missed shifts, a long walk in the cold, tired feet, a borrowed fare, a door that catches on a loose nail: these are romantic facts. A kindness may require giving up a meal or standing in a queue. An apology may be cut by a cough or an arriving bus. Do not make the world a survival horror, but keep the relationship tied to material pressure.\n\nChange rhythm deliberately. A fast exchange of half-sentences can stop on a longer paragraph in which one character understands what the other is actually asking. Let rough speech belong to a specific speaker. One may curse because his hand hurts; another may go silent because pride catches in her throat. Nobody is universally witty or combative. End close to the physical moment: a movement begun, a door not quite closed, a line spoken but not yet answered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagae::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCompose the story through letters, notes, journal pages, lists, or records. Give each document a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of writing. The document's situation controls what is said and what is hidden. A love letter may begin as a request, cross out the request, then finish with a colder question. A journal entry may remember a kindness and then accuse itself of inventing that kindness. Let the paper itself show the struggle: a torn corner, a repeated draft, a line crossed so hard the ink bled.\n\nUse gaps and retractions as romantic events. A reply may ignore the confession, answer an earlier quarrel, or apologize for a sentence the original writer did not think mattered. A note may be folded, sealed, reopened, and never sent. A shared list or account book may become a record of two people's different days. These material traces reveal moments of doubt without an omniscient narrator explaining them.\n\nKeep the Dostoevskian argu­ment inside each document. The writer may put forward a reason for staying away, then immediately undermine it with a memory. The next sentence may restore the original reason, now with more shame. The prose is the character's own, not a uniform philosophical voice. The recipient's document answers what was actually written, not what the first writer privately intended. Misunderstanding, partial confession, and deliberate silence therefore change the next exchange.\n\nMaintain readable continuity. Choose the document form that fits the immediate scene: a short note under a door, a formal letter, a shared account list, a diary page. Do not move through every possible format just to vary the presentation. The reader should be able to follow who knows what and when each document was created.\n\nEnd with a document that leaves the relationship moving: a message delivered but not yet answered, a page begun and set aside, a record whose next entry has not been written. The reply, if any, belongs to the following document, not to an editorial summary after the record.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagaf::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nLet the couple enjoy particular, familiar things: the drink he always forgets to order, the joke she repeats too often, the errand that has become a shared excuse to walk together. Keep these pleasures concrete and personal. The comfort should not erase their personalities or make every problem vanish, but the emphasis belongs on genuine care rather than crisis.\n\nAllow small Dostoevskian doubts to pass through the warmth without exploding into accusation. When one remembers a tender gesture, let the next thought wonder whether it was owed or manipulative. When the other accepts help, let an impulse to refuse flicker and then pass. The thought may be ironic, tender, or faintly ashamed. Do not let it become a long self-trial; let it settle into a decision to stay, to laugh, or to do one more kind thing.\n\nUse mild friction to keep the scene alive. A difference in taste, a forgotten errand, an unwelcome comparison can be acknowledged and then softened. One may say something kind and the other may find it unexpectedly hard to hear; that hardness can be met with a second, simpler kindness. Comfort may be partial and awkward without becoming painful.\n\nAttend to practical care: food set aside, a blanket fetched, a drawer fixed, a shoe dried by the stove. A small gesture may be offered and accepted with a touch of embarrassment. Show the effort and thought behind the kindness rather than turning it into a grand confession. The romance advances through receiving and returning these ordinary attentions.\n\nEnd on a settled, pleasant note or a promise of more time together. No compulsory confession or reward is required. Leave the pair in a moment they can both inhabit, even if one of them is still quietly arguing with the fact that it felt good.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagag::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Formal Literary Novel\n\nFrame each romantic scene through its social situation. The meeting may occur in a family parlor, a workplace, a dining hall, or a visit whose timing is governed by obligation and reputation. Use sustained paragraphs to move from that surrounding context into the personal exchange, and then back out to what the context allows or forbids. The narrator may notice the position of chairs, the distance between guests, or the particular phrase custom requires; these details should open the scene, not decorate it.\n\nWhere the chosen narrator permits, hold a slight distance and make quiet judgments. The prose may observe that one character sits too straight or that another avoids the window when a certain name is spoken. But preserve the perspective's limits: the other characters' private thoughts remain hidden. Let a third person's arrival change the room, and let that interruption redirect the couple's unfinished sentence. Do not turn the narrator into an omniscient moralist.\n\nInside this formal movement, keep the Dostoevskian argument tightly bound to the couple. One character may spend a paragraph recalling a promise and a second paragraph accusing herself of using that promise as a weapon. The other may prepare a polite sentence and hear it become a confession before he can stop it. The narration can slow to follow this spiral, but it must return to the concrete social moment: a letter read at table, a visitor announced, a carriage ordered. Use ceremony and correspondence when the scene calls for them; these formal pressures make the romantic decision heavier without becoming melodrama.\n\nDo not assume the style supplies aristocratic setting or archaic speech. Use the actual world's manners, rooms, and forms of address. The contrast between public composure and private reversal is the method, not a period costume. End at a deliberate stopping point in the larger movement: an exit from the room, a door closed behind a guest, a letter placed in a pocket. The relationship may remain unsettled while the social situation continues. Let the next chapter begin from the judgment the scene has made possible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagah::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nGive the telling a specific audience, occasion, and purpose inside the current relationship. One partner tells an embedded story because it is easier, safer, or more persuasive than direct speech: delaying a confession, testing whether the listener can accept a version of them, warning about a past failure, or trying to hold the listener's attention a little longer. Show what the telling costs in the outer scene — the teller's hands, pauses, revisions, and choices about what to include are part of the romantic exchange, not stage dressing around it.\n\nRun the Dostoevskian pressure inside the tale itself. The teller can defend a decision, reverse it, accuse themselves, or entangle motive with self-image while narrating, and the listener can hear where the teller identifies with one figure and distances themselves from another. Keep the embedded story from becoming transparent allegory: give it a genuine disagreement, an unresolved shame, or a competing conviction the teller cannot settle. The listener may challenge the selection or the emphasis — 'You left out what you did afterward,' or 'You make yourself sound worse than she did' — and that challenge is a relationship event, not a riddle with a solution. Give the listener private reactions of their own at whatever level the active narrator permits, and keep the tale they are hearing a composed artifact, distinct from either person's live inner experience.\n\nKeep the levels intelligible: mark the opening of the embedded story clearly, and when the outer scene returns, let the listener's face, posture, or question show how the tale has changed the room. A repeated phrase or object may recur across the telling and accrue meaning, but it belongs to these characters' shared history, not to any magical verification. The telling may remain unfinished, be interrupted, or end with the listener refusing the meaning the teller wanted; the romance develops through what the telling reveals and what each person then chooses, not through a guaranteed reconciliation or a compulsory confession. The delay itself must have a character-specific reason — fear, pride, care, uncertainty, or the hope of being understood without saying it outright.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagai::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and developmental realism route. Place the relationship in a specific material and institutional world — work, training, housing, money, family expectation, association or congregation, the class markers that matter to these people — as the story supplies it, and let that world shape what each person can offer, risk, or postpone. Treat the romance as development: the two people should be measurably different by the end of a scene sequence because of what they have admitted, refused, or learned about each other, and let the narration register that change with a settled, judging distance appropriate to the narrator's social position. Follow the social world closely enough that a change in work, rent, health, or duty has visible consequences for the relationship, without turning the prose into a survey; two particular people stay at the center.\n\nCarry the Dostoevskian pressure through narrated interiority rather than speeches. One character can spend a long passage defending a decision, then reverse it, accuse themselves, or fold desire into self-image — believing that accepting help would prove weakness, that leaving would prove selfishness, that staying would prove they have no principles. The other need not share this style of mind; a plain speaker may unsettle the elaborate argument with a direct question or a practical observation, and the gap between the spun-out inner case and the simple spoken sentence can itself be the romantic event. Let conviction become personal inside the material conditions: a belief about dignity, work, or worth should press on concrete choices such as whether to take the room, attend the dinner, or sign the paper.\n\nLet timing and obligation create complications with aftermath. A postponed conversation, a missed visit, or a promise made under pressure changes what the characters believe is possible, and later scenes should remember it. Disagreements need not end in confession or reconciliation; the relationship may advance through one character recognizing that self-justification has become a wall between them, or through the other's refusal of a flattering version of events. Keep syntax and distance consistent with the narrator's position and the realist route; do not import national scenery, bureaucracy, or severity — use the setting, language, period, and community the story actually supplies.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagaj::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Gothic-Punk\n\nLet the relationship exist inside a collision between old authority and present life. The characters may be held by an inherited obligation, a family claim, an institution's expectation, or a decaying arrangement that still has power; at the same time they live in a modern urban world of rent, transit, work, noise, and practical need. Make the collision felt in particular encounters — a private conversation in a cramped flat, a meeting under institutional light, a walk through a district where old wealth and present disrepair sit side by side. The old authority here is social and material, with its own history and texture; do not import vampire physiology, factions, powers, or bond mechanics to supply it.\n\nMake intimacy morally complicated. In trusted company a character may let a social mask fall, and that exposure should carry risk: the person who has seen behind it cannot unsee it. Put the Dostoevskian pressure inside that exposure — wanting the other person while accusing oneself of using them, betraying someone, or escaping a duty, with desire and accusation in the same beat. The other may answer with a plain sentence that cuts through the argument, or bring a competing conviction of their own. The romance develops through what the characters choose to reveal, protect, or refuse, not through a guaranteed safe haven.\n\nGround the relationship in urban material detail and give atmosphere real work to do on each encounter. A locked door, a shared meal, a phone that keeps ringing, a coat wrong for the weather, a room that belongs to someone else — such objects carry emotional weight through what they cost or promise. Choose one or two sensory registers per scene, such as light, sound, or state of repair, and let the same location read differently as the relationship shifts: a refuge in one scene, a trap in the next, because of what the characters bring into it. Do not make every caring act fraudulent or every private moment a trap; genuine care is available, but it has to be earned against the pressures the world and the characters themselves create.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagak::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and domestic realism route. Place the relationship inside a specific household, neighborhood, community, or workplace with concrete obligations — a parent's expectation, a sibling's need, an arranged introduction, a financial duty, a regional or religious practice, a reputation that must be managed — as the story supplies them. Render the household as a working system of daily practice: meals, chores, visits, ceremonies, and errands through which the characters encounter each other and the people with claims on them. Give the surrounding voices their own stakes rather than using them as obstacles; a relative, neighbor, or colleague who pressures one character also wants something understandable. The romance develops through particular people negotiating these conditions, not through a universal conflict of love against tradition.\n\nCarry the Dostoevskian pressure through private self-argument. One character may want to stay with the other and simultaneously believe that staying betrays a parent, breaks a promise, or proves selfishness; let the thought defend, accuse, reverse, and entangle itself across scenes. The other character need not think this way and may answer with a plain question or a practical observation, and that contrast shows what each person is willing to risk. Adapt the pressure to the person — not everyone delivers the elaborate case, and a quiet refusal can carry as much conviction as a spiraling monologue.\n\nLet timing and obligation create complications with meaningful aftermath. A postponed conversation, a family event, a financial setback, or a promise made under pressure should change what the characters believe is possible, and later scenes should remember the cost. The relationship may advance through a character recognizing that self-justification has become a way of avoiding a decision, or through the other's refusal to accept a flattering version of events. Keep the narration composed and continuous, attentive to the social world and the characters' places in it; keep language, region, community, and period specific only as supplied, without forcing English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene. The ending remains contingent on the actual choices and conditions.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagal::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and psychological realism route. Place the relationship inside a specific social world — workplace, school, family, neighborhood, or another institution with its own rhythms and expectations — and build the romance primarily out of perception: one character noticing a small change in the other's manner, remembering an earlier conversation, rereading a brief message, or choosing not to say something yet. Treat omission as a character-specific act with a reason — embarrassment, care, uncertainty, or the hope of being understood without being told — and let the narration mark what is being withheld and from whom. Do not impose universal quietness; direct feeling, plain argument, and even bluntness belong to whichever character actually talks that way.\n\nCarry the Dostoevskian pressure beneath the surface exchanges. A character may want the other and simultaneously accuse themselves of being unworthy, selfish, or dishonest; the thought defends, accuses, reverses, and entangles. Let the gap between the measured surface and the spiraling interior generate the scene's energy: a polite exchange over tea or a shared task can carry an entire unsaid argument. The other character may answer an elaborate inner case with one plain spoken sentence, and that contrast can be the romantic event. Give each person's inner life its own shape rather than distributing the same style of self-argument to everyone.\n\nLet attention and memory accumulate. A character may remember a past moment differently after a new disclosure, or discover that the other has kept something they themselves forgot; these asymmetries of memory should accrue meaning across scenes without becoming a puzzle with a solution. The relationship develops through what the characters reveal, protect, and refuse, and through the gradual discovery of what the other has noticed all along. Keep the prose composed and continuous, with seasonal attention available where it fits the world rather than as mandatory scenery. The ending remains contingent: the relationship may survive, change, or fail according to the actual choices and conditions in the story.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagam::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate psychological and urban fiction route. Place the relationship in a specific city, neighborhood, or community where material conditions — work, housing, transit, money, family, local history — press on private life, and let the setting work as a layer of memory: a street, a bus route, a market, a family gathering, or a particular building can carry emotional weight because of what happened there and to whom. Preserve the language, community, and history the story supplies. This route is psychological and urban, not fantastic; do not add supernatural facts to a world that excludes them.\n\nCarry the Dostoevskian pressure through private self-argument tied to the character's maintained self-version. A character may want the other and simultaneously believe that wanting them betrays a family loyalty, a political commitment, or an idea of who they have tried to be; let the thought defend, accuse, reverse, and entangle itself. Set the inner case against ordinary urban speech: the other character may answer with a plain sentence or a practical observation that cuts through, and that contrast can be the romantic event. Adapt the pressure to the particular person rather than giving everyone the same monologue.\n\nLet memory and proximity develop the relationship across scenes. A character may reread a past event after a new disclosure, notice that the other has remembered something they forgot, or find that a familiar corner of the city now means something different because of who they walked it with. These details accrue meaning without becoming a puzzle; the relationship advances through what the characters choose to reveal, protect, or refuse. Keep the narration close to the inner lives while the city and its history stay present without overwhelming the particular people at the center. The ending remains contingent: the relationship may survive, change, or fail according to the actual choices and conditions in the story.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagan::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Open on a concrete scene — a room, a street, a workplace — where the two characters are present or about to meet, and establish who is where before the first line of dialogue. When the scene changes location, mark the shift with a clear line or paragraph break so spatial movement stays easy to follow. Break paragraphs around action, dialogue, and private response so the reader can track what happens and what each character feels about it in the same readable flow.\n\nCompress the Dostoevskian self-argument into short, legible reaction beats instead of philosophical paragraphs. Give the character a sharp burst of private thought — one sentence that defends a choice, one that accuses, one that reverses. For example: 'I should go before I say it.' 'No — leaving now would prove I was afraid.' 'And saying it would prove I was selfish.' Keep the bursts short and clearly the character's own, and let the other character's plain spoken answer land right after them; the contrast between the compressed inner spiral and the direct line is the romantic beat. A reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner — not every beat is torment, and not every character spirals.\n\nDevelop the relationship through attention, memory, and disclosure in scene-sized increments: a noticed change in manner, a remembered moment that returns with new meaning, a small reveal of something hidden. When an unfamiliar name, term, or mechanic appears, give it a clear one-line presentation at first mention rather than a lecture. End each response at the scene's natural boundary; do not manufacture a closing cliffhanger, and do not assume a young cast or first person unless the story supplies them. The outcome stays contingent on what the characters actually choose.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagao::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the relationship as sustained attention rather than summarized feeling. Let a crowded room, an uneaten meal, a coat left on a chair, or a sentence withheld become the visible shape of a decision still being made. Move the prose the way attention moves: one clause extending into another while hope and suspicion turn together, then a short plain statement when a character becomes honest without warning. The rhythm carries the pressure; do not convert each moment into calm analysis.\n\nGive each lover a moral vocabulary and an unspoken need the other cannot fully satisfy. When attraction, jealousy, pride, or caretaking presses toward a choice, render the competing voices as a live argument rather than a tidy psychological case: a generous explanation of someone's absence, offered aloud or in private thought, followed at once by the smaller resentful reading, both earned. Keep images traceable to the scene or to the speaker's own associations; a doorway, a street at a fixed hour, an object given or borrowed can return with changed meaning, making the relationship's past present without explanatory flashback. The language may confess more than the speaker intends while remaining inside that person's actual awareness. Where the contract permits more than one viewpoint, keep each person's stated reasoning distinct from what the imagery reveals about them; where only one perspective is available, do not enter the other's private mind. A plain request or refusal can cut through a dense passage precisely because the surrounding prose has been dense.\n\nDevelop the romance through ordinary obligations that become tests of belonging: money, living arrangements, work that must be finished, an invitation accepted or refused, a promise kept at a cost. Register a choice as a moment the story can later recall with changed meaning, not as a genre milestone. When the narration lingers on an object or scene, that thing must genuinely occupy the character's attention. Lyrical prose is not permission to make every event tender or every room portentous; let a dry observation or a practical complaint supply the contrast.\n\nEnd a reply wherever a credible action, exchange, or inward reckoning has occurred: mid-conversation, mid-journey, at the moment a window is opened, a letter picked up, or a sentence changes the next exchange. No declaration, decision, or completed emotional summary is required at the boundary. Each section should read as the continuation of an ongoing relationship, not a self-contained vignette that must resolve its own mood.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagap::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the developing relationship be seen through what has already altered between the two. A present exchange can be shaped by a previous conversation, an earlier promise, or a version of one of them the other can no longer reach. Recall these changes directly, or let an object carry them—a cup, a gate, a particular phrase—without turning every memory into a wound. A remembered joke or an ordinary afternoon can still be recalled with pleasure, and that pleasure may make the present distance more complicated rather than less.\n\nGive the reflection a Dostoevskyan edge by letting self-accusation, pride, or shame attach to the past without collapsing into pure self-laceration. A character remembering a scene may ask whether he acted from love or from the need to see himself as generous; the question is hesitating moral attention that belongs to the relationship, not a verdict. Where the other person has directly challenged his account, let the present encounter supply the competing version. Memories can conflict without a final referee: a remembered look means one thing to the person who saw it and another to the person who gave it.\n\nUse small irreversible changes as the plot's evidence of progress or strain: a habit abandoned, a door left unlocked, a seating arrangement changed and then restored, a phrase no longer used. The narration may notice such a change without announcing its meaning; when a decision approaches, a character can reflect on how long it has been present. Keep the past actually past—later knowledge may add interpretation, but it must not be silently inserted as earlier awareness.\n\nTreat ordinary pleasure as legitimate. A shared meal, a walk in mild weather, an unexpectedly easy evening may be described warmly and need not be undercut by anxiety. Sadness, when it comes, should be tied to a specific loss or uncertainty rather than diffused over every scene. A reply can end before the reflective mood resolves: the character observes a change, turns toward a memory, and a present interruption—a knock, a question, the other person entering—postpones any conclusion about permanent loss or permanent reunion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagaq::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell the romance through a frame: a situated storyteller addressing a particular listener or small gathering under identifiable conditions—an evening visit, a long journey, a period of waiting, an occasion of hospitality, a need to delay a difficult matter. Keep the language, place, and social world specific to the community the story actually supplies, and keep the source tradition coherent; if the frame belongs to a Persian practice, do not attach a non-contiguous Arabic convention as though the two were one. Do not assemble a general exotic backdrop.\n\nTreat the telling as an event in the romance rather than a neutral illustration. The teller shapes the embedded account to accuse, warn, excuse, or test the audience, and the listeners' reactions affect what can be said next. Let the Dostoevskyan pressure appear as the teller's visible struggle: a line delivered with certainty, then corrected; a pause while the speaker weighs whether the lovers acted from virtue or from the need to be seen as virtuous; an admission that a flattering detail was reshaped. A plain objection from a listener can unsettle an elaborate passage without anyone delivering a philosophical speech.\n\nInside the tale, use reflective passages to slow the advance toward decision. A remembered room, a repeated phrase, or an object passed between the lovers can recur as a motif whose meaning changes across tellings; the return should build the sense that some fact is being withheld or that the teller cannot yet name what happened—not merely decorate the suspense. Keep the obligations concrete—money, family expectations, work, travel, reputation—and let them carry consequences in both the frame and the tale. Ordinary social, domestic, or urban realism can carry the whole account; mysticism, exile, and a single honor code are not universal requirements.\n\nA reply may leave the story unfinished: the teller pauses because morning arrives, a guest departs, or the central question becomes too painful to complete. The romance stays present in the exchange itself—a question asked, a silence kept, an objection that reveals what the tale could not—so the frame relationship carries the charge while the tale's ending remains to be earned.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagar::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Minimalist Prose\n\nBuild each scene from a small set of exact details and direct clauses. Select the few objects, movements, and lines that make the lovers' choices legible; do not describe a room merely because the characters are in it. If a cup is mentioned, its position or use should register an action or an absence. If the narration says it rained, the rain is a practical fact of the scene, not a mood ornament.\n\nLet the profile's inner pressure operate through restraint. A character may carry on a long private self-argument, but expose the moment when the argument breaks its pattern: a full justification for leaving, then one noticed object, then a single-sentence revision. Keep only the interior sequence that materially contributes to the present choice. When a competing voice speaks aloud, its plainness should unsettle the elaborate reasoning—\"You always say that\" can be enough. Voices remain individual: one person may still speak at length from conviction or discomfort, since the style governs narrative selection, not a uniform clipped register, and an exchange need not collapse into deadpan banter.\n\nPreserve causality while omitting. If a gift is refused, keep the refusal's motivation inferable from an earlier detail or phrase rather than erasing the connection between event and response. Avoid explaining a gesture's meaning immediately after showing it. Where a meaning is central and would not otherwise be recoverable, write one longer precise sentence instead of multiplying performed fragments.\n\nStop a reply at a decisive detail or after a terse exchange that changes the relationship's stakes. No emotional summary is owed at the boundary. The unsaid remains available for the next turn, while the present answer shows enough concrete action or speech for the reader to know where the scene has moved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagas::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the romance in sustained scene-first prose, giving each scene enough physical and social context that the lovers' decisions can be followed rather than summarized. Move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and whatever inner experience the active viewpoint authorizes. Use paragraphing and transitions to signal shifts of focus without head-hopping or recap machinery, and keep to the perspective, period, and world the story contract supplies rather than assuming a contemporary setting.\n\nLet the moral pressure arise from the practical materials of the relationship. A character believes one thing and fails to act on it; defends a choice while half-aware of the competing motive; walks out of a conversation, argues with the other person's remembered words, and then notices he is still performing the argument for an imaginary audience. Include such inner argument as a component of the scene when the present action makes it relevant, without switching into essay form. The narrator's voice can be intimate without turning thought into a voice-over addressed to the reader.\n\nLet modest revelations create consequences that shape later scenes. A disclosure about money, jealousy, illness, or a previous relationship need not resolve the relationship or produce a clean moral; it can alter what the characters are willing to risk, what they avoid saying, or what a certain gesture now means to them. A scene can escalate, reverse, or simply continue.\n\nClose a reply at a coherent pause in the ongoing relationship—after a gift, an argument, a meal, a journey, or a night that leaves a question unanswered—while speech, business, or thought may still be in motion. The boundary marks where the scene stands, not a completed arc, and no lesson is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagat::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the pairing's premise legible through scene selection. Open with a moment that shows what these two particular people share or lack: an inside joke, a shared task, a boundary neither has crossed, an argument repeated with familiar rhythms. Do not list traits, and do not add popular tropes the supplied premise does not support. If the pair are rivals, show the competition in practice; if they are established lovers, the affection must affect their choices, not merely appear in a line of dialogue.\n\nGive the emotional beats enough space. A bout of jealousy does not resolve in a single paragraph unless the story's own logic makes the sudden resolution meaningful; comfort can be tactile and long, its physical and emotional details specific to these characters. Where a competing inner voice arises—\"You deserve this,\" \"You always ruin it,\" \"She stayed out of pity\"—let it run long enough to be felt, then meet it with a concrete fact or a spoken objection from the other person. Self-accusation can matter as much as external conflict.\n\nUse callbacks and private language deliberately. A phrase from an earlier scene returns with changed meaning; a repeated gesture signals comfort in one context and warning in another. Not every difficulty resolves into softness: rivalry and hurt may persist past a tender moment, and the next scene may test what was gained. The emotional payoff should fulfill the premise's particular promise without flattening the characters into guaranteed happiness, and neither partner is a reward the genre owes the other.\n\nKeep scenes complete enough to satisfy as an installment while leaving a natural continuation point—mid-conversation, mid-gesture, before a larger decision—and let the pairing's history weight the stop: what was said in the past may be exactly what a character remembers next. No author's note, tag list, or summary belongs in the body.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagau::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nCompose the romance as an account shaped by retelling. Open deliberately with the reputation or remembered occasion: who was there, what was said afterward, what people called the two before they themselves agreed to the name. Then move into particulars. A relationship deed should be concrete and repeatable—a gift brought back, a task done against expectation, a promise made in public, a quarrel others now misremember—and each deed should be related to what it will come to mean among the community or in the pair's own later retelling.\n\nUse recurring phrasing with change of application. A formula such as \"he said once\" or \"they did not speak of it\" can return when the situation has altered, so that the same words read loyal, then stubborn, then ashamed. Let a designation grow from an established deed or habit of speech rather than attaching an ornamental epithet to every entrance. Compact declarations establish a fact or reputation; concrete scene work then complicates it.\n\nLet spoken objection break the chronicle's certainty. The partner or a listener may answer an elevated line with a practical concern—\"That is not how it happened\"—and the teller pauses, defends, accuses himself, or admits he has left out the matter that would make the deed less honorable. That lapse of narrative confidence is where the self-argument lives: it can reveal love, guilt, or pride without a psychological essay. Keep the teller's access consistent with the chosen perspective; a narrator who is not omniscient does not report the unexpressed thought of a character the chronicle withholds.\n\nDo not import a Nordic pantheon, heroic doom, or ceremonial dialogue unless the story establishes those elements; the epic register is a matter of form and remembered consequence, not costume. Household work is not mock combat, and a quarrel over a borrowed object need not become a feud. The momentum comes from people keeping the story of their bond alive and revising it. End a reply after a remembered deed has been related and its present consequence touched, leaving the next retelling open—without declaring the lovers legendary unless the story has genuinely earned that judgment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagav::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nTake the social and domestic realist route: the relationship lives inside arrangements people can name—work, housing, debts, family duty, public institutions—and a change of feeling changes what those arrangements cost. Locate each scene in a specific, functioning place: a workplace, a shared kitchen, a family gathering, a welfare office, a train compartment, a borrowed summer cabin. Make the practical facts load-bearing. Who pays for what, who is expected to care for a parent, who can afford to leave, whose name is on the lease, what was promised to someone else—these conditions, not a stated mood, give feeling its visible consequence in the room.\n\nRun the focal character's self-arguing thought through these circumstances rather than as a set speech. The mind defends a decision, accuses itself of the opposite motive, reverses—because the rent notice is on the table, because a sibling said something at dinner, because the other person has just said one plain sentence that undoes an elaborate justification. Let the beloved's ordinary words or actions break the spiral; the interruption need not win the argument, only change what the focal character can go on believing. Keep the other person partly opaque: the focal mind reads a silence, a habit, a tone of voice, and may be wrong or incomplete about it.\n\nLet disclosure come out of relationship pressure rather than a scheduled moment of truth: something small admitted over dishes, an omission held through a phone call, a resentment confessed only after the other has already acted on incomplete knowledge. Make the setting the one actually supplied. If it is Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish, let institutions, housing, work customs, family expectations, and forms of address belong to that country and language rather than to a generic Nordic atmosphere; do not import snow, isolation, or reticence the actual place and season do not have, and do not treat every Nordic reference as interchangeable Scandinavian. Neither person is a guaranteed reward for the other's suffering; belonging together is a decision reached—or deferred—through what these two particular people do with attention, memory, and obligation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagaw::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Screenplay\n\nBuild the romance in screen form: a scene heading whenever location or time changes, action lines a viewer could verify with eyes and ears, dialogue blocks with clear speaker cues. Let the relationship advance through visible behavior—who arrives early, who keeps to the far side of the room, who picks up the other person's cup without being asked, who answers a question by starting a task. Keep action lines concise and readable; no shot lists, no camera commands. Use a cut, an offscreen sound, or voice-over only when this particular sequence needs it, and let voice-over carry nothing the scene has not earned.\n\nHold the Dostoevsky profile's self-arguing thought as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the screenplay's presentation—the focal character's private defense, accusation, reversal—while the filmed surface keeps moving. He sits down to the meal, says something ordinary about the day, and privately argues that eating it proves weakness, then accuses the cook of staging kindness, then remembers the debt that makes the kindness unbearable. On screen there is only the pause, the fork lowered, the glance toward the door. The other character knows nothing of the note and reacts to the visible pause alone.\n\nGive the plain spoken line its power. A short, unornamented sentence can cut under the private spiral without resolving it, and the focal character answers with something that hides the interruption or something that lets it show. Develop the relationship across scenes whose information changes what the two can do next: a disclosure arrives in fragments in separate scenes, a disagreement carries through a change of location, a decision about belonging together appears as a practical act—staying to help, leaving before the explanation, returning an item, clearing space on a shelf. End a scene on an unfinished exchange, a completed action, or a changed room; a confession or reversal is available when the accumulated scenes have earned it, never a toll charged by the format.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagax::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script. Named speakers, lines in their mouths, and stage directions confined to what a company can stage: entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, physical business. Let the gap between the spoken line and the simultaneous business carry the public scene—someone says they are fine while setting a glass down too carefully, offers help while standing between the other person and the door, agrees to stay while putting on their coat.\n\nHold the Dostoevsky profile's self-arguing movement as nonspoken authoring material inside the play's active presentation. The focal character agrees aloud while privately calling the agreement cowardice, reverses and names it a necessary kindness, then remembers a past failure that makes the kindness feel like a bribe; the scene partner answers the spoken line, not the hidden argument. Reserve monologue and aside for moments with a genuine dramatic job—establishing a stake no piece of business can show, marking a real shift in the character's relation to the other person—and treat them as performed speech with their own audience and cost, never as the default container for private thought.\n\nLet a plain spoken sentence from the other character break into the spiral without settling it; the focal character covers the interruption, or betrays it in the next piece of business. Shape disclosure through stage conditions: an entrance cuts a confession short, a disagreement resumes after a scene change, belonging together is decided by who stays, who goes, and what is handed over. Who can leave, who sits and who stands, who touches an object belonging to the other—these choices show attention, memory, and proximity better than any speech about them. No film-camera directions; the stage does its own work. A scene may end on a pause, an exit, or an unfinished line, with a confession or revelation arriving only when the accumulated pressure of the staged scenes has earned it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagay::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Stream of Consciousness\n\nTell the romance from inside one authorized focal mind, structured by association rather than report. Braid what the senses take in, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and a returning concern with this particular person, so the relationship is lived through rather than summarized. The smell of coffee, the sound of a name, the texture of a borrowed scarf pulls the mind out of the present room into an earlier moment with the other person—their sentence, their hesitation, their hand—then a door or the weight of a phone brings it back. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow these turns: a rush where the thought rushes, a break where it breaks.\n\nMake the self-argument the mind's actual movement, not a staged debate. The focal character registers a kindness and at once accuses themselves of wanting it, reverses and calls the accusation pride, remembers refusing help once and feeling clean, then notices the cold from the window and realizes they have been standing still for some time. Keep orientation inside the flow: present event, remembered event, and imagined event stay distinguishable even when the mind itself blurs them, and other people's actions and words remain observable facts that this mind interprets through memory and fear—correctly or not.\n\nLet the other person's real presence interrupt. A plain sentence, a gesture, a change in the room breaks the associative run and returns the mind to the exchange without resolving what it was arguing with itself about. What the relationship comes to here is what the mind returns to, what it avoids, what it admits to itself, and what it finally says or does not say—a physical hesitation, a recalled promise, a sentence at last assembled. Stay inside the one mind the active contract authorizes; no second head opens, and no scene fact dissolves into vagueness to protect a mood.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagaz::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the romance in a chat log: sender-attributed messages a reader can scan, plus only those timestamps, delays, edits, read receipts, attachments, and channel changes that bear real pressure on the two people. Let each person message the way that person actually messages—one drafting paragraphs and deleting them, one answering in fragments, one leaving a message unread for hours and then replying as though no time had passed. These habits are character, and the friction between them is where timing and uncertainty do their work.\n\nKeep three layers distinct: the sent message; the unsent draft as a composed artifact someone wrote, revised, and withheld; and private thought, which is neither. Put the self-arguing pressure chiefly in the drafting layer. The character types an accusation, softens it, deletes the softening, and sends only a question; writes a confession, holds it unsent, and transmits instead a practical note about where to meet. The distance between the withheld draft and the sent line is where shame and pride operate, and the other person answers only what was sent.\n\nLet a short received message unsettle a long private one. 'okay,' 'are you coming,' 'I already ate' can cut under an elaborate self-justification without settling it, and the sender then types a careful reply that hides the interruption or an abrupt one that shows it. Where the story needs what the log cannot hold—the room, the hour, the memory of a previous conversation—set it as clearly separated context around the exchange, and invent no delivery failure or screenshot solely to manufacture subtext. Belonging together is decided here through what is finally sent, what is deleted for good, and what is answered after a long silence; no exchange owes a confession, and none is barred one.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagba::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Visual Novel\n\nPresent the romance in scene units suited to an illustrated frame: a concise line or two establishing where the characters are and what can be seen or heard, attributed dialogue that reads cleanly, and focal narration or inner response marking the viewpoint character's reaction. Each scene first fixes place and persons, then lets attention move among the other person's words, the room, and the focal character's inward reply. Background and expression cues are references that orient the moment—a familiar street, a rearranged room, one particular look—not dials reporting the other person's honesty; the expression can draw the focal character's suspicion or hope, while what the person says and does carries the actual meaning.\n\nRender the self-arguing pressure as that inner response, fitted to the scene's rhythm rather than delivered as a set speech. The focal character privately defends a decision, accuses themselves of a second motive, reverses, then registers the cup being set down or the change in the other's face. Keep each inward turn short enough that the scene keeps its pace, but let it carry the weight: it is why the focal character answers a kind question with a cold one, or stays when every prepared sentence says leave.\n\nGive the plain line its force: the other person's ordinary words can undercut an elaborate justification without finishing it, and the focal character's next reply either conceals or reveals the cut. Keep scene changes legible so a returning location reads as the same place with new meaning. Across scenes, a disclosure can arrive in fragments, a disagreement can survive a change of location, and belonging together can be shown in a practical act—staying to help, returning an object, making room. This style adds no menus, route locks, player choices, or engine commands; a development opens the next conversation rather than a choice screen, and a scene may end on a pause, a line, or a changed room with nothing yet resolved that the two have not resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamagbb::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Web Serial\n\nWrite the romance as serial prose with an easy point of re-entry. Each installment makes three things legible: what is presently underway between the two people, what changes locally in this reply, and what remains open to continue. Recover earlier context only as needed and briefly—one phrase from a past conversation, an object left at someone's flat, a debt still pressing on the present scene—without retelling how it was established.\n\nPace the focal character's self-arguing pressure across installments. A justification built in one reply is cut by a plain sentence in the next, reverses again when the character is alone, and need not complete inside any single installment. Let it accumulate instead: a memory returns with its meaning shifted, a resentment survives an apology, a decision becomes harder because a new obligation or changed circumstance has entered since it was made. The other person keeps a life between replies—work, family, silences with reasons—and their choices, including help and cooperation, arrive as events this mind must read.\n\nLet the relationship advance by earned developments rather than a compulsory turn per reply. Attention, memory, proximity, disclosure, disagreement, and care can each move it; a character may act on something small and only later understand what the act meant. Close an installment on finished work, a request still pending, a changed understanding, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known—no artificial cliffhanger, no forced miniature-chapter shape. Progress can lie in knowledge, work, money, or the relationship itself; this style brings no leveling system, ranking, or progression-fantasy rules. Give the moral pressure room to mean something across time rather than settling it early to fit a reply's length.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahaa::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the relationship as an intimate, turbulent matter that plays out against the long memory of the community and the lovers' own returning attention. The García Márquez gathering sentence is the main device: move from a concrete detail or moment to its accumulated significance across weeks, months, or years, then land on the specific cost or unresolved consequence for the pair. For example, a sentence may pick up a public quarrel, the silence that followed in the household, and the private acknowledgment one character made much later, and let all three layers bear on a present exchange.\n\nThe turbulence lives in behavior, not in a fixed vocabulary of suffering. Show admission, need, withholding, apology, or return as actual choices with visible consequences. If a character admits needing the other, the next exchange should carry that admission: a question asked more carefully, a familiar routine performed with sudden self-consciousness, an ordinary phrase avoided because it now carries too much. Warmth may appear awkwardly or partially, and it never cancels the hurt; it coexists with it and changes its texture.\n\nUse communal memory as pressure and limitation, not as omniscient commentary. What neighbors, relatives, or old acquaintances repeat about the couple may be incomplete, self-serving, or mistaken. The narrator may know more than the community, but the community's version still shapes what the lovers feel permitted to say or do. Keep the levels distinct: a public account of a reconciliation may circulate for years while the actual private cost remains known only to the two of them. When a community memory enters a scene, attach it to a specific person remembering and speaking, or to a concrete social consequence that the lovers must navigate.\n\nAllow timing, obligation, and competing wants to generate complications with lasting aftermath. A promise kept too long, a return after the original need has changed, a decision made at the wrong moment—all can create turbulence without requiring a tragic outcome. The narrative may end with a partial resolution, an open wound, or a fragile reconnection, but it should never force a confession to collapse or a relationship to fail. The recipe is a tonal treatment of romance, not a mandate for suffering.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahab::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the romance as a coherent shared interval with unmistakable dialogue attribution and readable, scannable paragraphs. In every reply, keep the García Márquez movement between personal life, family memory, public events, rumor, and recurring language active inside the scene. Whatever memory or communal knowledge appears must be attached to a speaker or to the focal character's observable reaction; do not slip into an omniscient narrator's aside. A character may repeat what the town says, recall a relative's claim, or notice that a phrase from years ago has returned with a different meaning—and that remembered material becomes something the other character can hear, question, correct, or ignore.\n\nRecurring language works as shared texture. A phrase one character uses may be picked up, altered, or conspicuously avoided, and that shift should be legible through the dialogue itself and through the focal reaction that follows. The change in meaning builds intimacy or friction without turning into a trivia puzzle; the other character may notice it, miss it, or misread it, and their response keeps the scene moving.\n\nThe relationship develops through attention, proximity, disclosure, disagreement, and care in the present interval. Memory and rumor may shape what the characters assume about each other, but the actual connection is built from what they do and say now—a remembered version of a person is not the same as the person present, and that difference can become romantic tension or relief. Use concrete actions and conversational beats to show interest, hesitation, or changing closeness.\n\nEnd each reply at a natural continuation point that follows from the scene. The next turn may pick up a half-finished conversation, a task still in progress, or a shift in how one character looks at the other. Do not force a question, cliffhanger, or choice screen. Keep ongoing world and NPC behavior active, and strictly preserve the active authorship contract for narrative person, action markers, and player control; those settings determine who speaks and how interiority is presented, not this style recipe.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahac::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite this romance through the intimate urban or domestic narrative route. Keep the story close to particular rooms, households, streets, and the people who know each other's routines. The relationship develops through attention to small domestic details—who prepares what, who waits, who notices a change in habit—and through the social expectations that make some disclosures easy and others costly.\n\nAdapt the García Márquez gathering sentence to this route by letting it accumulate the layered knowledge of the household and neighborhood. A single sentence may gather what the family has said over years, what the neighbors assume, and what the lovers privately remember, but it must land on a specific human consequence within the domestic setting: an unspoken debt, a changed routine, a gesture repeated too late. Do not invent literal marvels, and do not transplant García Márquez's familiar town or family. The layered time comes from memory and communal knowledge, not from magical events.\n\nUse family and neighborhood memory as a social field that surrounds the couple. An elder's recollection, a neighbor's repeated story, or a matter the household will not discuss can shape what the lovers believe is permitted. Keep the relevant language, period, and community distinct; do not manufacture a generic regional temperament or import a cast identity from elsewhere. If a phrase or custom recurs, let it carry the weight of shared history rather than becoming an exotic marker.\n\nBuild the romance through disclosure, disagreement, care, and decisions about belonging together. In this route, restraint often carries as much weight as speech: a pause before answering, a task done for someone without being asked, a conversation resumed after days. Timing and obligation create complications with meaningful aftermath, and neither party is a guaranteed reward. The finished passage should feel as if it belongs to one specific household and its particular ways of remembering, not to a general cultural portrait.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahad::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the romance in hard-edged, concrete prose. Use forceful diction and compressed transitions: move quickly from what a character notices to what it costs them. Material wear carries the relationship's history—a door handle polished by use, a jacket mended badly, a table scarred from an argument—and physical proximity registers as pressure, warmth, or risk. Do not soften the prose into comfort.\n\nCompress García Márquez's memory movement rather than deleting it. A remembered phrase or a neighbor's version of events can arrive in a single sharp clause and then be dropped. Recurring language surfaces briefly and changes meaning through repetition, not exposition. The gathering sentence does not expand into a retrospective essay; it gathers two or three sharp details from different times and lands directly on their present consequence.\n\nGround abrasive or rough speech in its speaker's immediate state. A character may be blunt because they are tired, angry, or trying not to say something else. After such speech, show the strain in concrete behavior: a question answered with a tool still in hand, a door closed harder than necessary, a task resumed without looking up. Warmth coexists with pain, but it arrives through specific physical action—a cup set down, a coat handed over—not through generic tenderness.\n\nUse rhythm deliberately. Compressed prose can move in short, hard beats, but when a character's reasoning or emotional realization needs room, allow a longer thought to slow the prose before the next sharp transition. That rhythmic shift marks significance without explanation. Let timing, obligation, and competing wants create complications with meaningful aftermath, and do not make either party a guaranteed reward.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahae::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the romance through documents, each with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Choose the specific document form the scene requires—a letter sent or unsent, a journal entry, a family record, a note left in a book—rather than rotating through formats. What a document knows or conceals derives from who wrote it, for whom, and when. Do not add an omniscient explanation after each record.\n\nLet García Márquez's movement between personal life, family memory, public events, rumor, and recurring language live in the differences between documents. One letter may repeat what the town says; a journal entry may record what the writer could not say aloud; a later record may revise or contradict an earlier account. Recurring language moves from one document to another and changes meaning as the relationship changes. Gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts reveal the memory layers, not editorial commentary.\n\nUse time of composition as a structural force. A document written years after an event may know more than the participants did at the time, or it may still misunderstand them. Distinguish carefully: an unsent letter is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. It shows what the writer chose to set down; what they omitted is visible only in the document's silences and revisions. Do not pretend a record grants direct access to a mind.\n\nBuild the romance through what the documents reveal about attention, memory, disclosure, disagreement, and care. A material trace—a torn page, a date crossed out, an address changed—carries as much weight as a confession. Timing and obligation create complications with meaningful aftermath. Preserve readable continuity between documents without letting the format erase the particular relationship at the center.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahaf::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the romance with attention to chosen kindness, familiar preferences, practical care, ease, and small pleasures. Let García Márquez's movement between personal life, family memory, and recurring language become a source of warmth: a remembered preference honored without being asked, a phrase from early in the relationship returning as a private comfort, a family story told with affection rather than judgment.\n\nUse communal memory as generous knowledge. People around the couple may remember how they first met, what they always order, or the time one of them waited through a storm. That memory creates room for the pair to enjoy each other rather than pressing them toward confession or repair. Keep the community's version affectionate but not omniscient; the couple's private ease remains their own, and the narrator should not explain what they feel beyond what the scene shows.\n\nLet recurring language carry intimacy. A phrase one of them uses may be picked up by the other, repeated with a small variation, or left for the right moment. The repetition feels like shared history, not a test to be passed. Small pleasures—a meal prepared, a walk taken, a task finished together—carry the relationship's development without needing a dramatic turning point.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable or erase established problems. A moment of friction may pass quickly, but it belongs to these particular people and their history. Affection remains sincere without requiring a confession or emotional repair. The romance stays at the center: these are two people choosing to be good to each other, not a household description that forgets why they care.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahag::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the romance in composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. The García Márquez gathering sentence is a formal device: a paragraph may gather what happened across years, what the family remembered, what the town repeated, and what the lovers knew privately, then land on the specific human consequence that anchors the passage. Distinguish the narration from ordinary spoken language. The narrator's considered phrasing frames the scene, but the characters' particular voices and idioms remain their own; do not let everyone speak with the narrator's formality.\n\nUse social context, history, and judgment to frame the relationship where the chosen narrator permits it. A courtship may be understood against the background of a public event, a family obligation, or a change in the community's fortunes. The contrast between what is said in public and what is known privately can organize a passage: a ceremony observed, a letter composed, a conversation held at the edge of a gathering. Do not manufacture an aristocratic setting, archaic vocabulary, or uniformly elaborate speech; the formal architecture is the style, not a required social world.\n\nLet communal memory and recurring language move through the novel's longer rhythms. What neighbors say, what the family chooses not to say, and what a phrase meant at one time and means now can all be gathered into the narrative movement. Keep the levels distinct: the narrator may know more than the community, but the community's version still shapes the lovers' sense of what is possible. Recurring language accrues weight across recurrences, each new use tinged by the last.\n\nBuild the romance through attention, memory, disclosure, disagreement, and decisions about belonging together. The narrative distance permits gravity about timing and obligation, but the relationship itself remains particular and contingent. Chemistry depends on these people, not generic attraction, and neither party is a guaranteed reward. Do not force a tragic conclusion; the novel may end with partial resolution, open questions, or a fragile but real decision to stay.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahah::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nLet a listener's need give the telling its shape. Open with the storyteller and the audience already situated by an occasion: a promise to explain, a night when old history can be spoken, a delay that itself changes what will be heard. Decide what the teller wants from this audience—to be believed, to be remembered, to hold someone's attention a little longer—and let that motive select what the story includes.\n\nThe embedded account should not be a full history. Follow the teller's memory, returning to a particular hour or conversation, and use García Márquez's habit of gathering a sentence across time: begin with a small repeated phrase, an object, or a rumor that once surrounded the lovers, and let it arrive later with a more intimate meaning. Show the romance through what the teller judges worth recounting and what can only be hinted at before the listener.\n\nLet the audience's attention act on the outer scene. A question may ask for the part the narrator avoided; a remembered name may change the temperature between speaker and listener; a pause may let the listener supply an interpretation the teller cannot say aloud. When an embedded scene returns to a detail from the outer story, make the recurrence reveal a shift in understanding or trust, not merely a stylized echo. A repeated phrase can mark a promise, a wound, or a sign of belonging between the lovers without making the story sentimental or provable.\n\nWrite the characters' private experience inside the chosen level. The teller may know more than the account admits; the listener may feel suspicion, longing, relief, or fear that does not reach speech; a lover inside the remembered scene may act from a motive visible to the reader but not to the narrator. Preserve the distinction between what was known then and what is known now, so later love or loss does not erase the earlier uncertainty. The story may end with the listener deciding to stay, speak, leave, or ask again—whatever follows from the evening's actual exchange. The frame slows the telling and makes the relationship legible through memory and audience, without adding magic, a compulsory cliffhanger between levels, or a final moral that resolves every withheld detail.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahai::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the social-realist route. Write in composed, continuous prose that moves between the private space of the two people and the households, workplaces, and public life that press upon them. Let obligations—rent, work schedules, family expectations, legal or institutional time—create the complications through which the relationship must be negotiated. Do not treat the social setting as background decoration; show how a specific decision about meeting, helping, or leaving carries cost for someone beyond the lovers.\n\nUse García Márquez's long sentence as a way to hold a memory and its aftermath together. A paragraph may gather the day they first spoke, the rumor that followed, and the years in which that rumor changed what each could admit. Anchor such sentences in a concrete consequence rather than abstraction: a room that had to be shared with a relative, an application that could not be submitted that week, a street where being seen together carried a name. Let the narrative return to particular objects or phrases—a coat pocket, a tram stop, a word used only between them—until the repetition marks a change in intimacy or constraint.\n\nDevelop the romance through scenes of attention and disagreement. Show one character noticing the other's unguarded moment under pressure, or recognizing a silence at the family table, and let that recognition alter what they risk saying later. Dialogue may be direct and socially exact, with each person's phrasing shaped by region, generation, or occupation as supplied by the setting, without caricature. Keep private interiority available: the focal character's uncertainty, desire, or shame should be written in the prose, not merely implied by dialogue. Let the German-language route set the syntax and distance—sentences may be longer and more civic-minded than the oral frame—while the romance keeps the relationship at the center. No uncanny doubling, absurdist detour, or documentary archive should take over the continuous development of the lovers' choices and their social cost.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahaj::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Gothic-Punk\n\nLet the romance unfold inside a place that remembers old powers: a family known for what it once controlled, a building registered under a name that still matters, a district reshaped by money that arrived before anyone living. Use García Márquez's communal memory by letting neighbors, records, and recycled stories carry that history into the present. The two people do not need to share the same relation to this legacy. One may carry its privileges, the other its costs; or both may be trying to live past a name that will not leave them alone.\n\nGive the setting material detail that changes how an encounter is experienced. A corridor with one functioning lamp, a room where the heating is turned on only for certain guests, a stairwell that makes every arrival audible—these should not merely decorate. Let them make private conversation risky, mark who is expected, or turn a small kindness into a public statement. Describe urban decay and inherited grandeur in the same paragraph so the lovers' intimacy is always pressed against what surrounds it.\n\nWrite the romance through the difference between social masks and trusted company. In public or family spaces, the characters may perform versions required by old obligations: correct speech, careful distance, the appearance of not caring. In the private interval they choose, those masks may relax, but not completely or safely at once. Show what each lets fall, and what remains hidden because trust is still being tested. Use visible interiority to give one focal character's experience of that shift—relief, fear, suspicion, want—without making the other character's motives transparent. The Gothic-Punk atmosphere should make the shared trust feel both necessary and fragile, not make every quiet room a trap or every legacy a curse.\n\nLet the relationship develop through decisions about belonging together under real pressure: seeing each other despite surveillance by relatives, speaking against a family story, covering for a practical failure, or choosing not to use the other's inherited power on his or her behalf. Preserve the moral complexity without requiring betrayal. A caring act can be genuine and still have a price; an old authority can be resisted without turning the lover into a prize. No vampire physiology, faction, power, or blood-bond rule enters the method. The marvelous is absent; the weight of history is enough. The ending follows from the characters' choices about what they can bear and whom they will protect, not from a required revelation or loyalty crisis.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahak::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the Indian oral and epic frame-tale route. Place the outer story in a particular occasion for telling: a wedding party's delay, a courtyard at night, a long return journey, a feast when old stories are expected. Let the storyteller be someone with a stake in the lovers—a relative, friend, neighbor, servant, or rival. Decide what that teller wants from the present audience: to prepare a marriage move, to warn, to plead for help, to define a reputation, or to keep someone from leaving before a message is delivered.\n\nLet the nested accounts make the romance legible through version, omission, and audience reaction. An uncle may tell the version that makes a family choice look inevitable; a sister may interrupt with the detail he forgot; a servant's recollection may supply the small act of care no one else noticed. Keep each teller's speech shaped by the actual language, region, and community supplied in the story context. If the setting is multilingual, choose the appropriate register and code-switching only when the characters would do so; do not impose English or a generic \"Indian\" English.\n\nUse García Márquez's gathering sentence by letting a family rumor be repeated across tellers until it begins to change the lovers' own memory. A phrase from a song, a festival, a journey, or a household task can recur across narrative levels. When it returns inside the remembered romance, it should carry a new emotional meaning for a listener, not simply prove the story consistent. Show the outer audience questioning, supplying, or resisting what they hear; let a listener recognize an omitted moment because they were present at the time.\n\nWrite the romance chiefly through remembered scenes within the nested accounts, but keep focal interiority available for the outer teller and listener when the current narrative level is theirs. The teller may know more than she says; the listener may understand that a particular detail was changed to protect someone. Distinguish then-known facts from later knowledge. A story told to secure agreement does not need to be false; it may simply arrange events around the point the teller needs. The relationship develops through the accumulating versions and the listener's eventual choice, not through sequential direct scenes alone. No universal caste, religion, family structure, or landscape should be imported. The frame closes or turns on the listener's response—what they decide to repeat, ask, or do about the lovers now.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahal::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the diary and reflective miscellany route. Present the story as a personal record made close to the events: dated entries, occasional undated notes, lists, remembered exchanges, and brief observations gathered around the developing relationship. Keep the writer's identity, period, and language situation precise from the supplied setting. This is not a fictionalized author's journal but a character's composed account, addressed to memory or a possible later reader.\n\nLet García Márquez's communal memory become the personal archive's way of returning to others' words. The entry-writer may record what a neighbor said, what the family repeated, what a lover once answered, and then, pages or months later, recall the same phrase as its meaning changes. Use seasonal attention as an actual temporal marker—what could be worn, what the street smelled like, which flowers or rains accompanied a meeting—but do not make a mandatory inventory of Japanese scenery. The season should organize feeling and anticipation: a change in weather or festival time can bring the lovers together or make an absence harder.\n\nDevelop the romance through the difference between the written record and the fuller private experience. In the entry, the writer may state less than she felt, or record a practical detail instead of an emotion, because the diary is a social artifact with its own restraints. In the narration between or around entries, render the writer's unspoken experience through visible interiority: the fear of being found out, the relief of seeing the other alive after a rumor, the body's hesitation that the written sentence omits. Do not simply report entries; show the writer deciding what to include. Dialogue may appear as remembered speech, concise and specific, but its effect should be measured in the later entry's tone and omissions.\n\nLet time pass unevenly. Some dates follow daily; others leap across weeks or years with a single sentence registering a consequence. García Márquez's gathered sentences can compress that passage: begin with the day they met, gather a family's changing opinion, and land on the entry where the writer admits what has already altered. Like the framed oral style, recurrence matters, but here the recurring image belongs to one person's attention—a torn sleeve, a station platform, a dish returned to the same café. Each recurrence shows a shift in the relationship's possibilities without forcing a resolution. Preserve the diary's partiality: the writer may misread the other, and later knowledge should not be silently inserted into an earlier entry as its original motive. The story may end with the writer rereading old entries just before or after a decision, leaving the next choice open according to the actual scene boundary. No fantastic disruption, theatrical exclamation, or compulsory quietness is required; the route's emotional frankness or plain argument may appear where it belongs to this particular writer.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamaham::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the social realism, chronicle, and testimony route, with polyphonic or nonlinear elements used sparingly to preserve competing community versions. Write the relationship inside a concrete community, time, and place supplied by the story. Let the romance develop not only in private scenes but through how others speak of, ignore, protect, or depend upon the two people. Use the actual language or languages of the setting, including local idioms and formal registers where appropriate, without inventing a generic Latin American Spanish.\n\nLet García Márquez's sentence gather public and private consequence. A paragraph may begin with a market rumor, carry the couple's attempt to meet despite a vigil or work schedule, and end with a detail that will be remembered differently years later. Use public events—a strike, festival, flood, migration, election, sudden death—as forces that pressure the relationship without reducing it to social commentary. Show family memory at work: older relatives may repeat a story about an earlier couple, and the present lovers recognize themselves or resist the comparison.\n\nUse polyphony by letting different attributed accounts enter the narration: a neighbor's version of an argument, a shopkeeper's recollection of a purchase, a letter or document that contradicts what the family later claimed. Keep each teller's perspective partial and motivated. Do not make all accounts converge; the reader should see how a shared event becomes different memories. The focal lovers' interiority remains available through visible private prose, though not every character's mind is accessible equally. Let the narrative move between the chronicler-like distance of public events and intimate scenes where one character's attention, fear, or desire becomes immediate.\n\nPreserve the marvelous only if the world already contains it. This style cannot grant a ghost, a healing, or a sign where the active setting excludes one. The central technique here is the social treatment of extraordinary feeling: a love affair may become the subject of rumor, legend, and family apology without becoming supernatural. Repeated phrases and objects should accrue meaning across the community's telling—a borrowed shawl, a song heard at a particular window, a name said differently by two generations—so that recurrence itself becomes the structural memory. The romance advances through decisions about loyalty, flight, work, and care under those collective pressures. The ending may leave some versions unreconciled; the narrator can end with a corroborated consequence without imposing a single moral or resolving every rumor.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahan::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Light Novel\n\nOpen each scene inside its immediate situation: who is present, where they are, and what small need brought them together. Enter the moment from the focal character's position so the reader can follow movement through doors, streets, and rooms without relearning the space. Keep spatial changes explicit and quick: a sentence can carry her from the stairwell to the balcony, but must make the transition legible. End the narrative passage or dialogue unit with a focused reaction beat that belongs to that character—serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to who she is.\n\nUse García Márquez's recurrence as a short-range memory device rather than an extended communal narration. Let a particular phrase, object, or rumor appear in more than one scene: a coin returned at a market stall, a name spoken by a neighbor, a song from a passing car. When the item returns, attach a new emotional consequence. The focal character may notice the recurrence and act on it—walking faster, asking a different question, choosing to sit closer. The reader sees memory change the present without long historical exposition.\n\nDevelop the romance through direct attention and disclosure. Show what one character notices about the other in the current scene: a hesitation before lying, a habit of touching the back of her neck before a difficult question, a change in voice when his mother is mentioned. Use dialogue to press and retreat, but keep reaction beats after each meaningful exchange. Do not let every character be quick-witted; humor or plainness belongs to the particular speaker. Keep private interiority visible and focused—the focal character's fear, hope, or misreading can be one or two sentences before the next action, not a lengthy reflection that halts all movement.\n\nUse paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response so the page remains fast-readable. Scene length may vary, but each paragraph should do one clear job. Compress García Márquez's gathering sentence into a compact image that links past and present: \"The same rain that had closed the school when they were twelve now kept them under the pharmacy awning; she finally said what the puddle had told her to say for years.\" Such compressed memory can replace long exposition. Keep family memory and rumor as background pressure—a relative's phone call, a neighbor's warning—that enters the scene briefly and leaves a consequence for one of the lovers. Let the relationship advance through choices made inside these charged moments, not through narrated years. The response may end at a natural pause, an unfinished statement, or a new uncertainty that arose inside the scene; no compulsory cliffhanger or resolved confession is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahao::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite romance through a lyric syntax built from García Márquez's layered time: let a sentence gather an early meeting, a piece of family rumor, a later change, and a present gesture, but keep the sequence anchored to the couple's particular emotional consequence. Use recurring sounds or images as time-markers for the relationship—not as ornaments, but as elements the lovers themselves revise when they encounter them again. When public talk or family memory enters, let it surround the private association rather than replace it: the narrator may move from what the community said to what one partner remembers feeling, and that movement itself becomes part of the love's texture.\n\nLet shifts of attention follow the focal character's or narrator's understanding rather than a predetermined emotional arc. A scene may linger on an object, a pause, a habit formed because of the other person, but only when that lingering changes what the narrator sees about the relationship. Plain statements should provide contrast, especially as direct dialogue; keep each character's speech individual while the narrative's associative movement carries the unsaid. Authorized interiority may be rendered as a brief associative turn within the lyric flow, but do not let the style grant access to excluded minds.\n\nDevelop the relationship through recorded consequences: attraction may be immediate, but trust, disagreement, care, and decision require choices whose later meaning is shown. Let the lovers disagree about what a shared memory meant, and let that disagreement appear in a recurrence with altered significance rather than in a single explanatory scene. Keep conflict abrupt when the scene requires it; lyric attention should not soften an argument. End at the point where new understanding becomes possible, leaving the next turn open without requiring every feeling to be named.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahap::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the romance unfold through the narrator's awareness of what the relationship has already changed, but ground that awareness in García Márquez's movement between personal life, family memory, and public rumor. A present scene may recall an earlier version of a person, a room, a phrase, or a family judgment, but the memory must affect the present choice or perception rather than merely adorn it. Use small irreversible changes—a gift that stops being used, a nickname that becomes painful, a habit one person drops—to show how time has entered the love. When public talk or family recollection enters, keep it distinct from the lovers' private version; the narrator may know what others said, but cannot automatically know what the lovers felt.\n\nAllow longing and regret to coexist with ordinary pleasure. A meal together may be genuinely warm and still remind one person of what cannot be restored. Do not make the reflective voice equate love with loss; the characters may choose to remain together, and that choice can be shadowed without being cancelled. When a narrator reflects, attach the reflection to a concrete person and moment, not to generalized weariness. Authorized interiority may be rendered directly, but later knowledge must not enter an earlier scene unless the focused character possessed it then; use retrospective passages to mark that distinction.\n\nDevelop trust, vulnerability, and disagreement through remembered occasions rather than abstract summary. An argument may first appear in its later meaning and then in its actual occurrence, so the reader understands what the narrator only learned afterward. Let recurring language or a family saying return with changed emotional weight. Close a response with a moment that holds both the present pleasure and the memory's weight, returning to the relationship's particular opening rather than declaring universal loss.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahaq::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse framed oral storytelling as the composition method, shaped by the supplied world's actual Middle Eastern tradition rather than a generic regional label. The response should sound like an account told to someone: a situated narrator selects incidents, introduces people through what the community says and then revises them through closer knowledge, and admits when a motive was unknown or misread. The teller's relationship to the lovers need not be stated, but the act of telling should imply why this story is being remembered and for whom.\n\nUse the frame to organize disclosure and preserve García Márquez's distinction between public rumor and private consequence. A rumor or family judgment can appear before the private event that explains or contradicts it, but the frame cannot grant access to hidden minds. When a character's thought is narrated, supply the nearest available ground: remembered confession, observed hesitation, a later account, or an inference the teller marks as inference. Keep the lovers' actual private experience distinct from public reputation; closeness may be invisible to others, and a public declaration may be less true than a private gesture.\n\nDevelop the romance through repeated tellings, not through a single summary. A phrase, object, or debt recurs with different meanings before and after a break or discovery. Let the teller pause at an ordinary moment—a meal, a walk, a task—because that is where the relationship changed, not because the scene is ornamental. Humor, family memory, and practical obligation may enter, but they must be drawn from the supplied cultural and social setting. End when the account reaches a turn worth passing on, leaving the relationship's future open rather than claiming to exhaust the lovers' hearts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahar::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect only the details that will return later with changed meaning. A gift, a doorway, a mannerism, a phrase said without knowing its weight: introduce it plainly, then let a later scene reveal its consequence through action or a spare inward thought. Do not explain what the detail means the first time it appears, and do not add a second imagery cluster to soften its effect. Omission should leave something inferable; do not cut out the causal hinge.\n\nWrite romance by choosing acts of attention over descriptions of emotion. Show care through what one person remembers to do or leave undone; show trust through what is not asked; show breach through what changes in a routine. When private thought is necessary, keep it causal: the character reasons toward a decision, notices an implication, or connects a past event to the present. A single extended sentence may serve if it carries an essential chain, but avoid subordinate clauses that merely restate.\n\nUse García Márquez's movement between personal life, family memory, and rumor by compressing them into one or two concrete reversals of reputation or understanding. A rumor may be answered by a small public act, and a family memory may be contradicted by the lovers' private version. Keep dialogue direct and individual; the narrator's spareness should not erase each character's speech. The response can end at the instant a causal sequence completes or turns, without the narrator judging what it means for the whole relationship.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahas::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the romance in sustained scene-first prose. Begin with an action, a piece of dialogue, or a perception that belongs to the immediate moment, then move to memory only when it changes how the focal character reads the present. Let family memory, public rumor, or recurring language enter as part of those transitions—not as separate summary blocks, but as a brief phrase or recollection that alters the scene's meaning. Use paragraphing to make time shifts clear without summarizing a life. A sentence may gather past and present, but it must remain anchored to a human consequence: a choice, a fear, a debt, a change in intimacy.\n\nDevelop trust, vulnerability, and disagreement through ordinary scenes that have aftermaths. A meal, an errand, a missed call, or a family obligation can become a turning point when the characters act differently afterward. Show proximity through small physical and verbal adjustments, not through a single confession scene that explains everything. Let one person misunderstand or withhold; keep the other's knowledge limited to what they can perceive or infer under the active perspective. Authorized inner experience may be rendered directly; do not grant access to excluded minds.\n\nUse García Márquez's public/private distinction naturally: a rumor may precede the scene, then be contradicted by what the couple says or does in private. The prose need not be ornamented, but the narrator's voice may include a distinctive rhythm or recurring image if it arises from the scene. End a response at a moment of completed exchange or a new uncertainty, without forcing a lesson or a clean emotional summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahat::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nAssume the relationship has a history the reader may already love. Write scenes that let that history show in private language, repeated gestures, and the difference between what the pair says around others and what passes between them alone. Do not name a trope to justify a scene. Instead, let the premise reveal itself through what the characters protect, repeat, miss, or cannot stop wanting from each other.\n\nUse García Márquez's movement between private life and collective memory by making rumors or family recollections about the couple part of their world, but keep their actual private version distinct. Let family memory or public gossip enter as a pressure or a mistaken account, then let the private scene answer it. A callback should be a detail from earlier in their relationship—an object, phrase, habit, or place—that now carries a changed feeling. Revisit it when it creates simultaneous comfort and vulnerability, not as a prize for readers who remember.\n\nGive jealousy, affection, comfort, rivalry, or discovery enough space where the premise supports them. Let competing wants and obligations complicate the intimacy without making either person a guaranteed reward. Affection can be unspoken and still effective; a disagreement can end without full resolution; a reunion can be incomplete. Write interiority as actual private experience, not fan commentary. The response may close on a private moment or an unfinished conversation, as long as it advances the particular relationship rather than staging a generic soft epilogue.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahau::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nRetell the romance as a chronicle of acts that the community or family will remember. Open a response with a deliberate declaration—who the lovers were to each other, what they did, what was not known—then anchor it in a concrete scene. Use compact statements to introduce people, then complicate the reputation with particulars. A recurrent phrase or naming should come from an established act or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet.\n\nFor romance, make the memorable deeds small enough to be intimate: a promise kept, a risk taken, a refusal made, a habit endured or adopted. Public reputation may celebrate one version while the lovers' private experience holds another. Show trust through what one person dares to ask or tell; show vulnerability through what they lose by speaking or remaining silent. Do not make courtship a ceremonial performance; allow ordinary dialogue to interrupt the chronicle voice, and let a lover answer an elevated introduction with a practical concern.\n\nKeep the particular source tradition coherent. Oaths, kinship, gifts, journeys, or feuds may organize events if the supplied world provides them, but do not import a pantheon, heroic doom, or formulaic speech. García Márquez's layered time appears when a later act reveals the full consequence of an earlier one. End at a point where the relationship's next turn has become legible to the teller or the pair, not where every feeling has been formally declared.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahav::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the romance as a social/domestic realist account that stays with the couple's household and its immediate participants. Let each movement of the relationship enter through a domestic activity: a meal prepared or cleared, an errand run together or separately, a room entered at an unusual hour, a seasonal task performed or postponed. Keep the narration anchored in particular people, objects, rooms, and obligations rather than in a general community presence.\n\nUse García Márquez's layered time by letting a sentence gather the present with an earlier moment through a physical trace. When a character sets down a basket, closes a door, or refills a cup, the narration may move from that action to the first time the action was shared, then to what a neighbor, relative, or friend later said about it, and return to the present consequence. Anchor every gathering in what someone now understands, doubts, avoids, or finally says. Do not let a gesture become a fixed symbol; its meaning changes because the people and circumstances have changed.\n\nLet the community enter as specific voices attached to specific domestic occasions. A neighbor's comment arrives while one character helps with laundry; a relative's repeated story surfaces during a meal; a family account is corrected while someone tends a garden or repairs a tool. Show who is speaking, what they want from the retelling, and how the couple receives it. The town's version may affect practical decisions, but it is not a reliable summary of private feeling. A character may know a partner's fatigue or reluctance while neighbors interpret the same visible facts as coldness or scandal; keep whose knowledge is being rendered clear.\n\nRetain the Scandinavian route by choosing social/domestic realism rather than folktale, myth, or psychological essay. If the supplied world provides a particular Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish context, let its details arise from that world; do not import snow, isolation, or a generic Nordic temperament. The marvelous is not required in a nonfantastic world. Preserve attention to ordinary social treatment: an extraordinary emotional or social event is handled with the same steady domestic attention as preparing a meal or waiting out a storm.\n\nKeep private interiority available under the active contract, distinct from what the community can know. A familiar room, object, or phrase may acquire new significance through later knowledge, deepening understanding without restarting the same conflict. The scene can end after a completed errand, a remembered phrase, a postponed answer, or a neighbor's partial account. The relationship need not reach a decision in every passage; let practical life continue and let characters change their minds for supported reasons.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
