            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-romance-03",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Romance · 03/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == romance}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Romance. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 3 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-romance; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPamahaw::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Screenplay\n\nWrite the romance in concise scene blocks with clear speaker cues and scene headings when location or time changes. Let the relationship develop through visible returns and audible recurrences, each return anchored to a concrete cue the scene can show: a phrase arrives with a coat pocket, a song returns through a particular window, a gesture done in haste is now performed slowly at the same table where it once interrupted a meal. Each return carries a human consequence rather than decorating the scene.\n\nUse García Márquez's layered time within the scene economy. Present the small present action and let the framing or a repeated object indicate the earlier moment. A character may set down a cup that another person once gave, or pause beside the chair where a previous conversation ended. Do not explain the full history in the action lines; instead, let the later exchange revise what the earlier image suggested. A cut may place the public account beside the private moment it misreads, but do not use the cut to supply knowledge the characters lack.\n\nLet rumor and communal memory enter through what characters say to one another, never through an omniscient narrator's summary. A neighbor can report what the town believes, and the couple can confirm, correct, or ignore it. Show who is speaking and what they want from the retelling; their version is a social force with its own motives and errors, not a reliable account of private feeling. A later scene can return to that report and revise its meaning without erasing the original statement.\n\nKeep required private interiority as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation. The note may record what a character notices, withholds, or fails to say while the visible scene continues. It is not dialogue another character can hear, and it is not automatic voice-over. Another character does not know that private content unless the scene gives them a supported reason to perceive it. When a cut or offscreen sound is used, attach it to a concrete event: a recurring song or a voice from another room, not a supernatural sign.\n\nThe scene can end after a completed exchange, a visible change in attention, or the arrival of a person whose presence alters the moment. Do not require a confession, embrace, or decision in every scene; a request can remain unanswered and a task can continue across scenes. Let the reaction and private note open the next scene's available conversation rather than close it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahax::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Stage Play\n\nWrite the romance as a playable script. Let the relationship become legible through entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and repeated lines. When a phrase returns, keep its exact wording audible and attach it to a concrete prop or position: the line first spoken while setting down a basket may return later when that basket is handed back, or the phrase said at a door may be repeated as someone opens the same door at another hour. The actor can show the change through timing, posture, or handling of the prop.\n\nUse García Márquez's layered time by letting the stage hold a trace that has been established in a previous scene. If a chair was turned during an exit in act one, later a character may find it turned differently and respond; if a cup was moved to the wrong side of a table, a later scene may show someone correcting it or leaving it untouched. Keep these traces economical and specific to the scene's practical life. A character may notice or ignore the trace for a supported reason; another may ask about it or use it as an opening to speak.\n\nLet communal memory enter through what characters say about the couple when they are absent or present. A relative, neighbor, or friend can repeat what the town believes, and the couple can correct, confirm, or let it stand. Show who is speaking, to whom they are speaking, and what they want from the retelling. The public report is a social account with its own motives and errors. A later scene may stage the correction by having a character handle the same object or stand in the same position mentioned in the rumor, without the text explicitly explaining the connection.\n\nPreserve required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation, not as an aside or monologue another character can hear. A monologue or aside may be used only when the chosen scene gives it a dramatic purpose and an intended audience; it is not the default destination for thought. When a private conclusion is available to the reader or audience, let the actor's visible business indicate its weight: a character may count out cutlery more slowly, fold a cloth again, or leave the room by a different door. Another character does not know that private content unless a later supported action reveals it.\n\nLet the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene rather than relying on thematic explanation. A pause can hold a decision, a refusal, or the weight of a remembered phrase, but do not make every pause conceal guilt or signal an unspoken confession. The scene can end after an exit, a completed task, or a line that changes what the characters can now say to each other. Do not require a theatrical revelation or a gathering of witnesses in every scene. Practical life continues: dishes remain to be washed, doors must be locked, and visits must be prepared for.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPamahay::♢ * [STYLE] Romance / García Márquez / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the romance through the associative mental movement of one authorized mind. Begin each movement from a present sensory trigger: the smell of a room, the weight of a cup, the sound of a neighbor's voice, the pressure of a sleeve. Let that trigger open memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern, so that a present touch can recall an earlier touch, a phrase can surface during a meal, and a family story can interrupt a private decision. Keep the movement within the focal mind's actual experience.\n\nUse García Márquez's layered time by letting the mind gather a present moment with an earlier one in a single unbroken movement. A sentence may move from the smell of a room to the day the room was first entered, then to what someone said about it years later, and return to the present with the earlier material pressing on the current choice. Anchor each gathering in a human consequence: what the character now understands, doubts, wants, or cannot say. Rather than summarizing options, the mind may worry the same detail until the present sensation resolves the movement.\n\nLet communal memory enter as material the focal mind has received from others. A rumor, a family account, or a public event can surface as remembered speech, but keep its source and uncertainty available. The character may believe it, resist it, repeat it inwardly, or discover that it no longer fits the person now present. Do not let the mind's associations become a reliable summary of what the community actually knows or what the partner actually feels. Mark inference, memory, or fantasy with small phrases ('she would have said', 'they used to tell', 'perhaps') when the mind moves beyond its own direct knowledge.\n\nKeep the stream within the authorized mind. Other characters' thoughts are not available unless the active contract grants them. Their inner lives may be inferred, remembered, or imagined, but the narration should mark those as inference, memory, or fantasy rather than direct access. Preserve orientation by anchoring each burst of association to checkable scene details: a door that was closed remains closed, a message that was sent remains sent, and a later realization cannot become an earlier motiv